{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705119557,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_afd6eb0f526b4ee7b37b02bb","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"just --list"},"output":"Available recipes:\n    bench NAME     # Design: plans/benchmark-plan.md. Results land in benches/results/<name>.{md,json}.\n    check          # The fast inner loop: skips the docs mega-test, the suite's ~137s long pole. Gate on `just test`.\n    checks         # The repo's mechanical checks, the same surface the Stop hook runs.\n    clippy         # Run clippy with the repo's lint set, the same surface the Stop hook checks.\n    default        # List available recipes.\n    drive          # The autonomous drive loop: a tick every 600s, live colorized output, Ctrl-C to stop. Run ONE.\n    euler-data DIR # `just test`. DIR holds your own copies of the raw data texts; fails loudly, never skips.\n    fmt            # Formatter check over every .toy file from the repo root down (exit 1 on drift).\n    fmt-write      # Rewrite drifted files in place (same exit code as the check).\n    peek           # Watch the current coordinator tick live (detaches with Ctrl-C, tick untouched).\n    slow-test      # this is where the deferred execution claim is re-verified.\n    test           # The final gate: the full test suite. Landing requires this green.\n    tick           # Fire one coordinator tick right now (zero tokens if there is nothing to do).\n","metadata":{"output":"Available recipes:\n    bench NAME     # Design: plans/benchmark-plan.md. Results land in benches/results/<name>.{md,json}.\n    check          # The fast inner loop: skips the docs mega-test, the suite's ~137s long pole. Gate on `just test`.\n    checks         # The repo's mechanical checks, the same surface the Stop hook runs.\n    clippy         # Run clippy with the repo's lint set, the same surface the Stop hook checks.\n    default        # List available recipes.\n    drive          # The autonomous drive loop: a tick every 600s, live colorized output, Ctrl-C to stop. Run ONE.\n    euler-data DIR # `just test`. DIR holds your own copies of the raw data texts; fails loudly, never skips.\n    fmt            # Formatter check over every .toy file from the repo root down (exit 1 on drift).\n    fmt-write      # Rewrite drifted files in place (same exit code as the check).\n    peek           # Watch the current coordinator tick live (detaches with Ctrl-C, tick untouched).\n    slow-test      # this is where the deferred execution claim is re-verified.\n    test           # The final gate: the full test suite. Landing requires this green.\n    tick           # Fire one coordinator tick right now (zero tokens if there is nothing to do).\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"just --list","time":{"start":1788705119536,"end":1788705119549}},"id":"prt_077221d0a001Z6Ueue6o8HmkS9","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_077221115001dW4tHdCXekcay8"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705119578,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_499eca117d69456f855e3c31","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/plans/module-routing-research.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/plans/module-routing-research.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Module routing: a matcher arm dispatching into a submodule file (gh:162)\n2: \n3: Research spike for the board row `module-as-function-routing-design` (gh:162). The question is\n4: the maintainer's note, quoted in the issue:\n5: \n6: > let's capture the idea of running other modules as functions; for instance imagine an http\n7: > router that uses matcher expressions for matching different route groups; it may map onto\n8: > another module that handles a specific subgroup like `FooBar -> $(../route-groups/foo-bar.toy)`\n9: > or something similar\n10: \n11: That note assumes a module system that does not fully exist. This doc first says what \"module\"\n12: means in toylang today, then surveys how other languages route pattern-matched cases to separate\n13: files, then proposes syntax candidates. No code changes; this is a decide-row input.\n14: \n15: ## What \"module\" means in toylang today\n16: \n17: A program is one file: declarations (type aliases, enums, functions) followed by one expression\n18: whose value prints. There are no statements, no `main`, no print call\n19: ([a program](../docs/reference/syntax/programs.md)). The compiler reads exactly one file\n20: (`src/main.rs` reads one path, then `build`/`run`); nothing reaches for a second file by name.\n21: \n22: A second, smaller unit already exists alongside the program: a **module**, which is a file of\n23: declarations with no trailing expression. `prelude.toy` is the one real, checked-in module. The\n24: [DECIDED section in draft.md](../draft.md#decided-a-rudimentary-module-system-one-prelude-file-and-pub)\n25: records the current shape:\n26: \n27: - A module is zero or more `[pub] fn` definitions (and enums), nothing else, parsed by\n28:   `parse::parse_module` rather than `parse::parse`.\n29: - `pub fn` marks an export. Every `pub` definition in `prelude.toy` is merged into every\n30:   compiled program, then pruned to what the body can reach.\n31: - There is no import statement, and a program cannot name what it wants from a module or export\n32:   anything for another file to use. `pub` parsed in an ordinary program changes nothing.\n33: - Non-`pub` is not a working privacy boundary. A `pub` prelude function can only call compiler\n34:   builtins and itself, never a private prelude helper, because a non-`pub` definition is not\n35:   merged into any program at all. Real scoping needs the checker to track which file a\n36:   definition came from and enforce visibility per call site, which does not exist yet.\n37: \n38: So \"module\" already has a precise meaning here: a file of fully-typed, fully-declared function\n39: signatures that some program runs, where nothing yet decides which module or which function. The\n40: maintainer's note generalizes exactly the prelude's arrangement: instead of one always-merged\n41: module, a program names other module files and runs them, and a matcher arm is a natural place\n42: for that naming because matching *is* toylang's dispatch mechanism\n43: ([the match reference](../docs/reference/operators/match.md)).\n44: \n45: The constraint that shapes every candidate below: toylang has no first-class functions and every\n46: signature is declared, so \"running a module as a function\" must be resolved by the checker at\n47: compile time against a static signature, the way any call is. There is no runtime indirection to\n48: hide behind. Any candidate therefore needs the file-origin tracking and visibility enforcement\n49: that the DECIDED section names as missing.\n50: \n51: ## How other systems route pattern-matched cases to separate files\n52: \n53: Two shapes recur; they differ in what the target of a match is.\n54: \n55: **The whole file is the handler.** The match names a location, and the file that lives there is\n56: the unit that runs. Phoenix LiveView routes a path to a module that is the whole page:\n57: `live \"/path\", MyAppWeb.PageLive` ([Phoenix router docs](https://hexdocs.pm/phoenix/Phoenix.Router.html)).\n58: Next.js is the purest form: the filesystem path *is* the route (`pages/users/[id].tsx`), and the\n59: file's default export is the handler. The pattern (path) and the handler (file) are separate\n60: axes: patterns centralize in a table or a directory tree, handlers live one per file, and the\n61: mapping is declarative data rather than arbitrary control flow.\n62: \n63: **The target is a named function inside a module.** The match names a module and one of its\n64: functions. This is the classic MVC router. Phoenix's `Router` maps `get \"/users\", UserController,\n65: :index` to the `index/2` function in `user_controller.ex`\n66: ([controller guide](https://hexdocs.pm/phoenix/controllers.html)). Rails `match '/foo' =>\n67: 'foo#bar'` and Express `app.get('/users', require('./routes/users'))` are the same split, with\n68: the handler reached by a `module#action` string or by requiring a module and calling a method.\n69: The module can serve many routes because each route names a different function in it.\n70: \n71: The one thing all of them share, and the load-bearing idea for toylang: **the match pattern is\n72: declared separately from the code that handles the match**, and the target is a named module in\n73: a separate file. What varies is only whether the whole file is the unit or a named function\n74: within it is.\n75: \n76: jq, toylang's stated inspiration, is the relevant language-side precedent rather than a routing\n77: framework. jq 1.7 added modules: a file of definitions is brought in with `include \"lib\";` or\n78: `import \"lib\" as l;`, and its functions are called qualified, `l::fun`\n79: ([jq manual](https://jqlang.github.io/jq/manual/#module-system)). jq does not dispatch matcher\n80: arms to files, but it is the precedent in the same lineage for \"a file of definitions named from\n81: another file, called by a qualified name.\" It maps onto the Phoenix \"module and function\" shape,\n82: not the whole-file shape.\n83: \n84: ## Syntax candidates\n85: \n86: All three keep the matcher's arm shape unchanged on the left and only extend what an arm's body\n87: may be. The subject arrives through the pipe, so the submodule is applied to `.` in every case.\n88: The sketches use an enum of route names and a `router` function; `Request`/`Response` stand in\n89: for whatever wire types the router actually moves.\n90: \n91: **Candidate 1: `$(path)`, the whole module is a function (the maintainer's sketch).**\n92: \n93: ```toylang\n94: enum Route { FooBar, Baz, Qux }\n95: \n96: fn router(r: Route) -> Response =\n97:     r | FooBar -> $(route-groups/foo-bar.toy)\n98:       or Baz   -> $(route-groups/baz.toy)\n99:       or Qux   -> $(route-groups/qux.toy)\n100: ```\n101: \n102: `route-groups/foo-bar.toy` is a module holding one entry by convention:\n103: \n104: ```toylang\n105: pub fn handle(req: Request) -> Response = ...\n106: ```\n107: \n108: `$(path)` is an expression: the entry function of that module, applied to the subject. The path\n109: resolves relative to the referring file at compile time; the checker loads the module, reads\n110: `handle`'s signature, and checks it against the subject type and the arm's expected type, the\n111: way it checks any call. This is the whole-file model (Phoenix LiveView, Next.js page). `$` is\n112: free in toylang source today. The cost is an entry-point convention (`handle`): a module with\n113: several exported functions can only be dispatched through the one the convention names.\n114: \n115: **Candidate 2: `path::fn`, a named function in a module (Phoenix Controller#action, jq 1.7).**\n116: \n117: ```toylang\n118: fn router(r: Route) -> Response =\n119:     r | FooBar -> route-groups/foo-bar.toy::handle\n120:       or Baz   -> route-groups/baz.toy::handle\n121: ```\n122: \n123: The arm names both the file and one exported function, so one module can serve several arms and\n124: no entry convention is needed. This is the most general of the three and the closest to\n125: mainstream routers and to jq's `import ... as l; l::fun`. It costs more syntax: a `::` namespace\n126: separator, and it implies being able to name a module, which brushes against the DECIDED\n127: no-import-statement stance more than the other two.\n128: \n129: **Candidate 3: `@path`, a dedicated routing-arm form (convention routing).**\n130: \n131: ```toylang\n132: fn router(r: Route) -> Response =\n133:     r | FooBar -> @route-groups/foo-bar.toy\n134:       or Baz   -> @route-groups/baz.toy\n135: ```\n136: \n137: `@path` is a new arm-body kind reserved for module dispatch, distinct from ordinary function\n138: application. The checker treats it specially: the path must resolve to a module whose entry\n139: signature accepts the subject's type and returns what the arm expects, and the path is pinned at\n140: compile time the way a Next.js route is part of the program. Making file-dispatch a first-class\n141: arm form lets the checker give routing-specific errors and keeps `@` from being confused with a\n142: normal call. It is the whole-file model again, but spelled as a routing primitive rather than a\n143: call. `@` is free in toylang today. Its redundancy with Candidate 1 is the argument against it:\n144: if `$(path)` already says \"run this module on the subject,\" a second spelling needs to earn its\n145: own token.\n146: \n147: ## What all three depend on\n148: \n149: All three resolve a second file, read a definition from it, and check that definition against a\n150: call site. That is the machinery the DECIDED section explicitly says does not exist yet: the\n151: checker does not track which file a definition came from and cannot enforce visibility per call\n152: site. Until that exists, no candidate works even for the prelude's own next step (a `pub`\n153: function using a private helper). The candidates are syntax on top of that missing capability,\n154: not a substitute for it. Whatever is chosen, the file-origin/visibility work lands first.\n155: \n156: Each also inherits the prelude's reachability rule: only the submodule functions a program\n157: actually reaches get emitted, so an unused `handle` is pruned the way an unused prelude function\n158: is today.\n159: \n160: ## Open questions\n161: \n162: - Entry-point convention (whole-file forms) vs. naming the function (qualified form): which\n163:   matches how a toylang module file is actually written?\n164: - Whether a submodule dispatch target is a full typed function call or something looser; the\n165:   static-signature check is what keeps eight backends agreeing, so it should stay strict.\n166: - Whether \"module\" should grow aliases or stay declarations-only, and whether a route submodule\n167:   ever needs its own submodules (the http-router example suggests it might, recursively).\n168: \n169: ## Provenance\n170: \n171: Human-authored: the maintainer's note (gh:162) and the synthesis of what the candidates share.\n172: Derived: the DECIDED module section of draft.md and the toylang reference docs; Phoenix, Next.js,\n173: Rails, Express, and jq 1.7 behavior from their cited documentation. Agent-invented: the three\n174: candidate spellings and the framing of the whole-file vs. named-function axis.\n175: \n176: Note on scope: the task brief names `plans/module-routing-research.md` as the deliverable and\n177: also carries a generic \"do not touch plans/\" constraint. The named deliverable path controls\n178: here, matching every other research spike in `plans/`; this is recorded so the conflict is not\n179: lost. No other file in `plans/` is modified.\n\n(End of file - total 179 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Module routing: a matcher arm dispatching into a submodule file (gh:162)\n\nResearch spike for the board row `module-as-function-routing-design` (gh:162). The question is\nthe maintainer's note, quoted in the issue:\n\n> let's capture the idea of running other modules as functions; for instance imagine an http\n> router that uses matcher expressions for matching different route groups; it may map onto\n> another module that handles a specific subgroup like `FooBar -> $(../route-groups/foo-bar.toy)`\n> or something similar\n\nThat note assumes a module system that does not fully exist. This doc first says what \"module\"\nmeans in toylang today, then surveys how other languages route pattern-matched cases to separate\nfiles, then proposes syntax candidates. No code changes; this is a decide-row input.\n\n## What \"module\" means in toylang today\n\nA program is one file: declarations (type aliases, enums, functions) followed by one expression\nwhose value prints. There are no statements, no `main`, no print call\n([a program](../docs/reference/syntax/programs.md)). The compiler reads exactly one file\n(`src/main.rs` reads one path, then `build`/`run`); nothing reaches for a second file by name.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/plans/module-routing-research.md","text":"# Module routing: a matcher arm dispatching into a submodule file (gh:162)\n\nResearch spike for the board row `module-as-function-routing-design` (gh:162). The question is\nthe maintainer's note, quoted in the issue:\n\n> let's capture the idea of running other modules as functions; for instance imagine an http\n> router that uses matcher expressions for matching different route groups; it may map onto\n> another module that handles a specific subgroup like `FooBar -> $(../route-groups/foo-bar.toy)`\n> or something similar\n\nThat note assumes a module system that does not fully exist. This doc first says what \"module\"\nmeans in toylang today, then surveys how other languages route pattern-matched cases to separate\nfiles, then proposes syntax candidates. No code changes; this is a decide-row input.\n\n## What \"module\" means in toylang today\n\nA program is one file: declarations (type aliases, enums, functions) followed by one expression\nwhose value prints. There are no statements, no `main`, no print call\n([a program](../docs/reference/syntax/programs.md)). The compiler reads exactly one file\n(`src/main.rs` reads one path, then `build`/`run`); nothing reaches for a second file by name.\n\nA second, smaller unit already exists alongside the program: a **module**, which is a file of\ndeclarations with no trailing expression. `prelude.toy` is the one real, checked-in module. The\n[DECIDED section in draft.md](../draft.md#decided-a-rudimentary-module-system-one-prelude-file-and-pub)\nrecords the current shape:\n\n- A module is zero or more `[pub] fn` definitions (and enums), nothing else, parsed by\n  `parse::parse_module` rather than `parse::parse`.\n- `pub fn` marks an export. Every `pub` definition in `prelude.toy` is merged into every\n  compiled program, then pruned to what the body can reach.\n- There is no import statement, and a program cannot name what it wants from a module or export\n  anything for another file to use. `pub` parsed in an ordinary program changes nothing.\n- Non-`pub` is not a working privacy boundary. A `pub` prelude function can only call compiler\n  builtins and itself, never a private prelude helper, because a non-`pub` definition is not\n  merged into any program at all. Real scoping needs the checker to track which file a\n  definition came from and enforce visibility per call site, which does not exist yet.\n\nSo \"module\" already has a precise meaning here: a file of fully-typed, fully-declared function\nsignatures that some program runs, where nothing yet decides which module or which function. The\nmaintainer's note generalizes exactly the prelude's arrangement: instead of one always-merged\nmodule, a program names other module files and runs them, and a matcher arm is a natural place\nfor that naming because matching *is* toylang's dispatch mechanism\n([the match reference](../docs/reference/operators/match.md)).\n\nThe constraint that shapes every candidate below: toylang has no first-class functions and every\nsignature is declared, so \"running a module as a function\" must be resolved by the checker at\ncompile time against a static signature, the way any call is. There is no runtime indirection to\nhide behind. Any candidate therefore needs the file-origin tracking and visibility enforcement\nthat the DECIDED section names as missing.\n\n## How other systems route pattern-matched cases to separate files\n\nTwo shapes recur; they differ in what the target of a match is.\n\n**The whole file is the handler.** The match names a location, and the file that lives there is\nthe unit that runs. Phoenix LiveView routes a path to a module that is the whole page:\n`live \"/path\", MyAppWeb.PageLive` ([Phoenix router docs](https://hexdocs.pm/phoenix/Phoenix.Router.html)).\nNext.js is the purest form: the filesystem path *is* the route (`pages/users/[id].tsx`), and the\nfile's default export is the handler. The pattern (path) and the handler (file) are separate\naxes: patterns centralize in a table or a directory tree, handlers live one per file, and the\nmapping is declarative data rather than arbitrary control flow.\n\n**The target is a named function inside a module.** The match names a module and one of its\nfunctions. This is the classic MVC router. Phoenix's `Router` maps `get \"/users\", UserController,\n:index` to the `index/2` function in `user_controller.ex`\n([controller guide](https://hexdocs.pm/phoenix/controllers.html)). Rails `match '/foo' =>\n'foo#bar'` and Express `app.get('/users', require('./routes/users'))` are the same split, with\nthe handler reached by a `module#action` string or by requiring a module and calling a method.\nThe module can serve many routes because each route names a different function in it.\n\nThe one thing all of them share, and the load-bearing idea for toylang: **the match pattern is\ndeclared separately from the code that handles the match**, and the target is a named module in\na separate file. What varies is only whether the whole file is the unit or a named function\nwithin it is.\n\njq, toylang's stated inspiration, is the relevant language-side precedent rather than a routing\nframework. jq 1.7 added modules: a file of definitions is brought in with `include \"lib\";` or\n`import \"lib\" as l;`, and its functions are called qualified, `l::fun`\n([jq manual](https://jqlang.github.io/jq/manual/#module-system)). jq does not dispatch matcher\narms to files, but it is the precedent in the same lineage for \"a file of definitions named from\nanother file, called by a qualified name.\" It maps onto the Phoenix \"module and function\" shape,\nnot the whole-file shape.\n\n## Syntax candidates\n\nAll three keep the matcher's arm shape unchanged on the left and only extend what an arm's body\nmay be. The subject arrives through the pipe, so the submodule is applied to `.` in every case.\nThe sketches use an enum of route names and a `router` function; `Request`/`Response` stand in\nfor whatever wire types the router actually moves.\n\n**Candidate 1: `$(path)`, the whole module is a function (the maintainer's sketch).**\n\n```toylang\nenum Route { FooBar, Baz, Qux }\n\nfn router(r: Route) -> Response =\n    r | FooBar -> $(route-groups/foo-bar.toy)\n      or Baz   -> $(route-groups/baz.toy)\n      or Qux   -> $(route-groups/qux.toy)\n```\n\n`route-groups/foo-bar.toy` is a module holding one entry by convention:\n\n```toylang\npub fn handle(req: Request) -> Response = ...\n```\n\n`$(path)` is an expression: the entry function of that module, applied to the subject. The path\nresolves relative to the referring file at compile time; the checker loads the module, reads\n`handle`'s signature, and checks it against the subject type and the arm's expected type, the\nway it checks any call. This is the whole-file model (Phoenix LiveView, Next.js page). `$` is\nfree in toylang source today. The cost is an entry-point convention (`handle`): a module with\nseveral exported functions can only be dispatched through the one the convention names.\n\n**Candidate 2: `path::fn`, a named function in a module (Phoenix Controller#action, jq 1.7).**\n\n```toylang\nfn router(r: Route) -> Response =\n    r | FooBar -> route-groups/foo-bar.toy::handle\n      or Baz   -> route-groups/baz.toy::handle\n```\n\nThe arm names both the file and one exported function, so one module can serve several arms and\nno entry convention is needed. This is the most general of the three and the closest to\nmainstream routers and to jq's `import ... as l; l::fun`. It costs more syntax: a `::` namespace\nseparator, and it implies being able to name a module, which brushes against the DECIDED\nno-import-statement stance more than the other two.\n\n**Candidate 3: `@path`, a dedicated routing-arm form (convention routing).**\n\n```toylang\nfn router(r: Route) -> Response =\n    r | FooBar -> @route-groups/foo-bar.toy\n      or Baz   -> @route-groups/baz.toy\n```\n\n`@path` is a new arm-body kind reserved for module dispatch, distinct from ordinary function\napplication. The checker treats it specially: the path must resolve to a module whose entry\nsignature accepts the subject's type and returns what the arm expects, and the path is pinned at\ncompile time the way a Next.js route is part of the program. Making file-dispatch a first-class\narm form lets the checker give routing-specific errors and keeps `@` from being confused with a\nnormal call. It is the whole-file model again, but spelled as a routing primitive rather than a\ncall. `@` is free in toylang today. Its redundancy with Candidate 1 is the argument against it:\nif `$(path)` already says \"run this module on the subject,\" a second spelling needs to earn its\nown token.\n\n## What all three depend on\n\nAll three resolve a second file, read a definition from it, and check that definition against a\ncall site. That is the machinery the DECIDED section explicitly says does not exist yet: the\nchecker does not track which file a definition came from and cannot enforce visibility per call\nsite. Until that exists, no candidate works even for the prelude's own next step (a `pub`\nfunction using a private helper). The candidates are syntax on top of that missing capability,\nnot a substitute for it. Whatever is chosen, the file-origin/visibility work lands first.\n\nEach also inherits the prelude's reachability rule: only the submodule functions a program\nactually reaches get emitted, so an unused `handle` is pruned the way an unused prelude function\nis today.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Entry-point convention (whole-file forms) vs. naming the function (qualified form): which\n  matches how a toylang module file is actually written?\n- Whether a submodule dispatch target is a full typed function call or something looser; the\n  static-signature check is what keeps eight backends agreeing, so it should stay strict.\n- Whether \"module\" should grow aliases or stay declarations-only, and whether a route submodule\n  ever needs its own submodules (the http-router example suggests it might, recursively).\n\n## Provenance\n\nHuman-authored: the maintainer's note (gh:162) and the synthesis of what the candidates share.\nDerived: the DECIDED module section of draft.md and the toylang reference docs; Phoenix, Next.js,\nRails, Express, and jq 1.7 behavior from their cited documentation. Agent-invented: the three\ncandidate spellings and the framing of the whole-file vs. named-function axis.\n\nNote on scope: the task brief names `plans/module-routing-research.md` as the deliverable and\nalso carries a generic \"do not touch plans/\" constraint. The named deliverable path controls\nhere, matching every other research spike in `plans/`; this is recorded so the conflict is not\nlost. No other file in `plans/` is modified.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":179,"totalLines":179,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/module-routing-research.md","time":{"start":1788705119492,"end":1788705119570}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me read the plans/module-routing-research.md for the full design, and explore the repository structure.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_077221cf10010HW4bsaWJS5kWg","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_077221115001dW4tHdCXekcay8"}}
