{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705136159,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_b532345a97674024bb36ba50","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/parse.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/parse.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: use winnow::error::ParserError;\n2: use winnow::prelude::*;\n3: use winnow::stream::{LocatingSlice, Location, Stream};\n4: use winnow::token::take_while;\n5: \n6: use crate::ast::{\n7:     Alias, BinOp, Def, EnumDecl, Expr, FieldsPattern, File, ImplDecl, ImplMethod, LogicOp,\n8:     MatchArm, Module, Param, ParamShape, Pattern, Span, TraitDecl, TraitMethodSig, TypeExpr,\n9:     Variant,\n10: };\n11: use crate::error::Error;\n12: use crate::ty;\n13: \n14: /// The stream this whole module parses over: a source string with byte-offset tracking built\n15: /// in, so every sub-parser gets `Span`s for free from `current_token_start` rather than threading\n16: /// position by hand.\n17: type Input<'i> = LocatingSlice<&'i str>;\n18: \n19: /// Lets every winnow combinator used below (`take_while`, and anything built on it) report\n20: /// failure as this crate's own `Error` directly -- there is no separate winnow error type to\n21: /// translate out of. `from_input` is winnow's fallback for a failure it synthesises itself\n22: /// rather than one this module constructs explicitly; nothing here is expected to hit it; the\n23: /// hand-written parsers below always build their own `Error` with a specific message instead.\n24: impl<'i> ParserError<Input<'i>> for Error {\n25:     type Inner = Error;\n26: \n27:     fn from_input(input: &Input<'i>) -> Error {\n28:         let at = input.current_token_start();\n29:         Error::new(Span::new(at, at), \"expected a token\".to_string())\n30:     }\n31: \n32:     fn into_inner(self) -> Result<Error, Error> {\n33:         Ok(self)\n34:     }\n35: }\n36: \n37: #[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]\n38: enum Tok {\n39:     Str(String),\n40:     Int(i64),\n41:     Float(f64),\n42:     Ident(String),\n43:     Fn,\n44:     Pub,\n45:     Type,\n46:     Enum,\n47:     Trait,\n48:     Impl,\n49:     Let,\n50:     Stdin,\n51:     Dsv,\n52:     Csv,\n53:     Tsv,\n54:     And,\n55:     Or,\n56:     Not,\n57:     Plus,\n58:     Minus,\n59:     Star,\n60:     Slash,\n61:     Percent,\n62:     Pipe,\n63:     PipeGt,\n64:     Comma,\n65:     Dot,\n66:     Eq,\n67:     EqEq,\n68:     Ne,\n69:     Bang,\n70:     Lt,\n71:     Le,\n72:     Gt,\n73:     Ge,\n74:     LParen,\n75:     RParen,\n76:     LBracket,\n77:     RBracket,\n78:     LBrace,\n79:     RBrace,\n80:     Colon,\n81:     Semicolon,\n82:     Arrow,\n83:     Eof,\n84: }\n85: \n86: impl std::fmt::Display for Tok {\n87:     fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {\n88:         let s = match self {\n89:             Tok::Str(_) => return write!(f, \"a string\"),\n90:             Tok::Int(n) => return write!(f, \"`{n}`\"),\n91:             Tok::Float(n) => return write!(f, \"`{n}`\"),\n92:             Tok::Ident(name) => return write!(f, \"`{name}`\"),\n93:             Tok::Fn => \"`fn`\",\n94:             Tok::Pub => \"`pub`\",\n95:             Tok::Type => \"`type`\",\n96:             Tok::Enum => \"`enum`\",\n97:             Tok::Trait => \"`trait`\",\n98:             Tok::Impl => \"`impl`\",\n99:             Tok::Let => \"`let`\",\n100:             Tok::Stdin => \"`stdin`\",\n101:             Tok::Dsv => \"`dsv`\",\n102:             Tok::Csv => \"`csv`\",\n103:             Tok::Tsv => \"`tsv`\",\n104:             Tok::And => \"`and`\",\n105:             Tok::Or => \"`or`\",\n106:             Tok::Not => \"`not`\",\n107:             Tok::Plus => \"`+`\",\n108:             Tok::Minus => \"`-`\",\n109:             Tok::Star => \"`*`\",\n110:             Tok::Slash => \"`/`\",\n111:             Tok::Percent => \"`%`\",\n112:             Tok::Pipe => \"`|`\",\n113:             Tok::PipeGt => \"`|>`\",\n114:             Tok::Comma => \"`,`\",\n115:             Tok::Dot => \"`.`\",\n116:             Tok::Eq => \"`=`\",\n117:             Tok::EqEq => \"`==`\",\n118:             Tok::Ne => \"`!=`\",\n119:             Tok::Bang => \"`!`\",\n120:             Tok::Lt => \"`<`\",\n121:             Tok::Le => \"`<=`\",\n122:             Tok::Gt => \"`>`\",\n123:             Tok::Ge => \"`>=`\",\n124:             Tok::LParen => \"`(`\",\n125:             Tok::RParen => \"`)`\",\n126:             Tok::LBracket => \"`[`\",\n127:             Tok::RBracket => \"`]`\",\n128:             Tok::LBrace => \"`{`\",\n129:             Tok::RBrace => \"`}`\",\n130:             Tok::Colon => \"`:`\",\n131:             Tok::Semicolon => \"`;`\",\n132:             Tok::Arrow => \"`->`\",\n133:             Tok::Eof => \"end of program\",\n134:         };\n135:         write!(f, \"{s}\")\n136:     }\n137: }\n138: \n139: /// Zero or more ASCII spaces/tabs/newlines, then a `#`-to-newline comment if one follows,\n140: /// repeated: a run of trivia can mix any number of either in any order.\n141: fn skip_trivia(input: &mut Input) {\n142:     loop {\n143:         take_while::<_, _, Error>(0.., |c: char| c.is_ascii_whitespace())\n144:             .parse_next(input)\n145:             .expect(\"a lower bound of zero never fails\");\n146:         if input.peek_token() != Some('#') {\n147:             return;\n148:         }\n149:         input.next_token();\n150:         while !matches!(input.peek_token(), None | Some('\\n')) {\n151:             input.next_token();\n152:         }\n153:     }\n154: }\n155: \n156: /// Skips trivia, then reads exactly one token from the front of `input`. Called fresh for every\n157: /// `peek`/`advance` rather than once up front into a `Vec`, so a lexical error (a bad\n158: /// escape, an out-of-range integer) surfaces at the point parsing actually reaches it instead of\n159: /// always winning over a parse error earlier in the file the way a separate up-front pass would.\n160: fn read_tok<'i>(input: &mut Input<'i>) -> Result<(Tok, Span), Error> {\n161:     skip_trivia(input);\n162:     let start = input.current_token_start();\n163:     let Some(c) = input.peek_token() else {\n164:         return Ok((Tok::Eof, Span::new(start, start)));\n165:     };\n166: \n167:     let tok = match c {\n168:         '\"' => Tok::Str(read_string(input)?),\n169:         '0'..='9' => read_number(input, start)?,\n170:         c if c.is_ascii_alphabetic() || c == '_' => {\n171:             let word =\n172:                 take_while::<_, _, Error>(1.., |c: char| c.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || c == '_')\n173:                     .parse_next(input)\n174:                     .expect(\"a letter or underscore was just confirmed to follow\");\n175:             match word {\n176:                 \"fn\" => Tok::Fn,\n177:                 \"pub\" => Tok::Pub,\n178:                 \"type\" => Tok::Type,\n179:                 \"enum\" => Tok::Enum,\n180:                 \"trait\" => Tok::Trait,\n181:                 \"impl\" => Tok::Impl,\n182:                 \"let\" => Tok::Let,\n183:                 \"stdin\" => Tok::Stdin,\n184:                 \"dsv\" => Tok::Dsv,\n185:                 \"csv\" => Tok::Csv,\n186:                 \"tsv\" => Tok::Tsv,\n187:                 \"and\" => Tok::And,\n188:                 // Two operators sharing one spelling, told apart by position: the arm separator\n189:                 // at a chain's top level, Bool disjunction everywhere else. `Cursor::or_separates`\n190:                 // is which one is being read (kantord/toylang#96).\n191:                 \"or\" => Tok::Or,\n192:                 \"not\" => Tok::Not,\n193:                 name => Tok::Ident(name.to_string()),\n194:             }\n195:         }\n196:         // `-` is subtraction and negation now, so a digit after it is no longer a negative\n197:         // literal: `a -1` would otherwise not be `a - 1`.\n198:         '-' => read_two_char(input, '>', Tok::Minus, Tok::Arrow),\n199:         '!' => read_two_char(input, '=', Tok::Bang, Tok::Ne),\n200:         '=' | '<' | '>' => match c {\n201:             '=' => read_two_char(input, '=', Tok::Eq, Tok::EqEq),\n202:             '<' => read_two_char(input, '=', Tok::Lt, Tok::Le),\n203:             _ => read_two_char(input, '=', Tok::Gt, Tok::Ge),\n204:         },\n205:         '+' => single(input, Tok::Plus),\n206:         '*' => single(input, Tok::Star),\n207:         // `//` is not a token anymore: match arms compose with `or` now, so a second slash is\n208:         // just a `/` where no expression can start, which is the parse error migration needs.\n209:         '/' => single(input, Tok::Slash),\n210:         '%' => single(input, Tok::Percent),\n211:         '|' => read_two_char(input, '>', Tok::Pipe, Tok::PipeGt),\n212:         ',' => single(input, Tok::Comma),\n213:         '.' => single(input, Tok::Dot),\n214:         '(' => single(input, Tok::LParen),\n215:         ')' => single(input, Tok::RParen),\n216:         '[' => single(input, Tok::LBracket),\n217:         ']' => single(input, Tok::RBracket),\n218:         '{' => single(input, Tok::LBrace),\n219:         '}' => single(input, Tok::RBrace),\n220:         ':' => single(input, Tok::Colon),\n221:         ';' => single(input, Tok::Semicolon),\n222:         other => {\n223:             return Err(Error::new(\n224:                 Span::new(start, start + 1),\n225:                 format!(\"unexpected character `{other}`\"),\n226:             ));\n227:         }\n228:     };\n229:     Ok((tok, Span::new(start, input.current_token_start())))\n230: }\n231: \n232: /// A number literal: the integer digits that opened it, then an optional `.digits` fraction\n233: /// and an optional `e`-exponent. Either makes it a Float (ADR 0007); without both it stays the\n234: /// Int it always was. A `.` or `e` only joins the literal when what follows makes a number, so\n235: /// `1.5` and `1e3` are one token while `1.`, `1.x`, and `1e` keep the dot or letter for the\n236: /// parser.\n237: fn read_number<'i>(input: &mut Input<'i>, start: usize) -> Result<Tok, Error> {\n238:     let digits = take_while::<_, _, Error>(1.., |c: char| c.is_ascii_digit())\n239:         .parse_next(input)\n240:         .expect(\"a digit was just confirmed to follow\");\n241:     let mut text = digits.to_string();\n242:     let mut is_float = false;\n243: \n244:     if let Some(frac) = read_fraction(input) {\n245:         text.push('.');\n246:         text.push_str(&frac);\n247:         is_float = true;\n248:     }\n249:     if let Some(exp) = read_exponent(input) {\n250:         text.push('e');\n251:         text.push_str(&exp);\n252:         is_float = true;\n253:     }\n254: \n255:     let end = input.current_token_start();\n256:     if is_float {\n257:         let value = text.parse::<f64>().map_err(|_| {\n258:             Error::new(\n259:                 Span::new(start, end),\n260:                 format!(\"float `{text}` is out of range\"),\n261:             )\n262:         })?;\n263:         Ok(Tok::Float(value))\n264:     } else {\n265:         let n = digits.parse::<i64>().map_err(|_| {\n266:             Error::new(\n267:                 Span::new(start, end),\n268:                 format!(\"integer `{digits}` is out of range\"),\n269:             )\n270:         })?;\n271:         Ok(Tok::Int(n))\n272:     }\n273: }\n274: \n275: /// The `.digits` fraction of a number literal, `None` when the dot is not followed by a digit\n276: /// so it is left for the parser (`1.` and `1.x` are not floats).\n277: fn read_fraction<'i>(input: &mut Input<'i>) -> Option<String> {\n278:     if input.peek_token() != Some('.') {\n279:         return None;\n280:     }\n281:     let cp = input.checkpoint();\n282:     input.next_token();\n283:     if matches!(input.peek_token(), Some(c) if c.is_ascii_digit()) {\n284:         let frac = take_while::<_, _, Error>(1.., |c: char| c.is_ascii_digit())\n285:             .parse_next(input)\n286:             .expect(\"a digit was just confirmed to follow\");\n287:         Some(frac.to_string())\n288:     } else {\n289:         input.reset(&cp);\n290:         None\n291:     }\n292: }\n293: \n294: /// The `e`-exponent of a number literal, `None` when the exponent has no digits after it so\n295: /// the `e` is left for the parser (a bare trailing `e` is not a float).\n296: fn read_exponent<'i>(input: &mut Input<'i>) -> Option<String> {\n297:     if !matches!(input.peek_token(), Some('e') | Some('E')) {\n298:         return None;\n299:     }\n300:     let cp = input.checkpoint();\n301:     input.next_token();\n302:     let mut exp = String::new();\n303:     if matches!(input.peek_token(), Some('+') | Some('-')) {\n304:         exp.push(input.next_token().expect(\"a sign was just seen\"));\n305:     }\n306:     if matches!(input.peek_token(), Some(c) if c.is_ascii_digit()) {\n307:         let exd = take_while::<_, _, Error>(1.., |c: char| c.is_ascii_digit())\n308:             .parse_next(input)\n309:             .expect(\"a digit was just confirmed to follow\");\n310:         exp.push_str(exd);\n311:         Some(exp)\n312:     } else {\n313:         input.reset(&cp);\n314:         None\n315:     }\n316: }\n317: \n318: fn single(input: &mut Input, tok: Tok) -> Tok {\n319:     input.next_token();\n320:     tok\n321: }\n322: \n323: /// `-`, `!`, `=`, `<`, `>`, `|` all may start a two-character token: if the char after the\n324: /// first is `second`, the compound token stands; otherwise the bare operator does. `single` is\n325: /// for tokens that have no compound form at all.\n326: fn read_two_char(input: &mut Input, second: char, bare: Tok, compound: Tok) -> Tok {\n327:     input.next_token();\n328:     if input.peek_token() == Some(second) {\n329:         input.next_token();\n330:         compound\n331:     } else {\n332:         bare\n333:     }\n334: }\n335: \n336: /// Returns the unescaped contents of a string literal, having consumed through the closing\n337: /// quote. `input` is positioned on the opening `\"` on entry.\n338: fn read_string(input: &mut Input) -> Result<String, Error> {\n339:     let open = input.current_token_start();\n340:     input.next_token();\n341:     let mut text = String::new();\n342:     loop {\n343:         let pos = input.current_token_start();\n344:         match input.peek_token() {\n345:             None => return Err(Error::new(Span::new(open, pos), \"unterminated string\")),\n346:             Some('\"') => {\n347:                 input.next_token();\n348:                 return Ok(text);\n349:             }\n350:             // Escapes are JSON's set minus \\u, which waits for a real value model.\n351:             Some('\\\\') => {\n352:                 let backslash = pos;\n353:                 input.next_token();\n354:                 let Some(esc) = input.peek_token() else {\n355:                     return Err(Error::new(\n356:                         Span::new(backslash, backslash + 1),\n357:                         \"unterminated escape sequence\",\n358:                     ));\n359:                 };\n360:                 text.push(match esc {\n361:                     '\"' => '\"',\n362:                     '\\\\' => '\\\\',\n363:                     '/' => '/',\n364:                     'n' => '\\n',\n365:                     't' => '\\t',\n366:                     'r' => '\\r',\n367:                     other => {\n368:                         return Err(Error::new(\n369:                             Span::new(backslash, backslash + 2),\n370:                             format!(\"unknown escape sequence `\\\\{other}`\"),\n371:                         ));\n372:                     }\n373:                 });\n374:                 input.next_token();\n375:             }\n376:             Some(c) => {\n377:                 text.push(c);\n378:                 input.next_token();\n379:             }\n380:         }\n381:     }\n382: }\n383: \n384: /// Which node an infix token builds. `and` and `or` are `ast::LogicOp`, not `BinOp`, so the\n385: /// table has to name the kind as well as the binding power.\n386: enum Infix {\n387:     Bin(BinOp),\n388:     Logic(LogicOp),\n389: }\n390: \n391: /// Left and right binding power. Left below right makes the operator left-associative.\n392: ///\n393: /// `|` sits below comparison so that `a | select(. >= 2)` splits at the pipe, which is the\n394: /// ordering jq uses and the reason this table exists rather than a nest of functions.\n395: fn infix_power(tok: &Tok) -> Option<(Infix, u8, u8)> {\n396:     let (op, left, right) = match tok {\n397:         Tok::Or => (Infix::Logic(LogicOp::Or), OR_LEFT, OR_RIGHT),\n398:         Tok::And => (Infix::Logic(LogicOp::And), 6, 7),\n399:         Tok::EqEq => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Eq), 8, 9),\n400:         Tok::Ne => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Ne), 8, 9),\n401:         Tok::Lt => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Lt), 8, 9),\n402:         Tok::Le => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Le), 8, 9),\n403:         Tok::Gt => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Gt), 8, 9),\n404:         Tok::Ge => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Ge), 8, 9),\n405:         Tok::Plus => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Add), 10, 11),\n406:         Tok::Minus => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Sub), 10, 11),\n407:         Tok::Star => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Mul), 12, 13),\n408:         Tok::Slash => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Div), 12, 13),\n409:         Tok::Percent => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Rem), 12, 13),\n410:         _ => return None,\n411:     };\n412:     Some((op, left, right))\n413: }\n414: \n415: const PIPE_LEFT: u8 = 1;\n416: const PIPE_RIGHT: u8 = 2;\n417: \n418: /// Where a match arm's body and its guard operand are read and printed: between `|` (1/2) and\n419: /// the Bool connectives (`or` at 4/5). An arm body read here stops at the separator `or` and\n420: /// `->`, which is what lets a chain be one pipe stage; a guard operand read here still gets the\n421: /// Bool `or` above it, so `a == 1 or b == 2 -> x` is one two-clause guard.\n422: const COND_POWER: u8 = 3;\n423: \n424: /// Bool `or`, the loosest operator a Bool expression is built from: `a == 1 or b == 2` is one\n425: /// disjunction of two comparisons. Its *other* reading, the match-arm separator, has no power\n426: /// at all -- it is not an operator there but the chain's own punctuation, and it binds looser\n427: /// than everything, which is exactly why it cannot be one table entry with this (draft.md, the\n428: /// match-arms decision).\n429: const OR_LEFT: u8 = 4;\n430: const OR_RIGHT: u8 = 5;\n431: \n432: /// `not` sits directly below comparison, so `not a == b` negates the comparison rather than\n433: /// `a`, and above `and`, so `not a and b` negates only `a`. Python's ordering; the alternative,\n434: /// binding it as tight as unary `-`, makes every use of it against a comparison need parens.\n435: const NOT_POWER: u8 = 8;\n436: \n437: pub fn parse(src: &str) -> Result<File, Error> {\n438:     let mut p = Cursor {\n439:         input: LocatingSlice::new(src),\n440:         src,\n441:         declined_cross_line: None,\n442:         or_separates: false,\n443:     };\n444: \n445:     // Declarations in any order and any mix, since no kind can refer to another's position:\n446:     // aliases and enums are resolved before any signature is read.\n447:     let mut defs = Vec::new();\n448:     let mut aliases = Vec::new();\n449:     let mut enums = Vec::new();\n450:     let mut traits = Vec::new();\n451:     let mut impls = Vec::new();\n452:     loop {\n453:         let (tok, _) = p.peek()?;\n454:         match tok {\n455:             Tok::Pub => {\n456:                 p.advance()?;\n457:                 match p.peek()?.0 {\n458:                     Tok::Enum => enums.push(p.enum_decl(true)?),\n459:                     Tok::Trait => traits.push(p.trait_decl(true)?),\n460:                     _ => defs.push(p.def(true)?),\n461:                 }\n462:             }\n463:             Tok::Fn => defs.push(p.def(false)?),\n464:             Tok::Type => aliases.push(p.alias()?),\n465:             Tok::Enum => enums.push(p.enum_decl(false)?),\n466:             Tok::Trait => traits.push(p.trait_decl(false)?),\n467:             Tok::Impl => impls.push(p.impl_decl()?),\n468:             _ => break,\n469:         }\n470:     }\n471: \n472:     let body = p.tail_pipe()?;\n473:     let (rest, rest_span) = p.peek()?;\n474:     if rest != Tok::Eof {\n475:         return Err(p.unexpected(rest_span, format!(\"expected end of program, found {rest}\")));\n476:     }\n477:     Ok(File {\n478:         aliases,\n479:         enums,\n480:         traits,\n481:         impls,\n482:         defs,\n483:         body,\n484:     })\n485: }\n486: \n487: /// A module is declarations only, with no trailing expression -- there is nothing here to run,\n488: /// only names for a program to import. `pub` marks which ones a program actually receives.\n489: pub fn parse_module(src: &str) -> Result<Module, Error> {\n490:     let mut p = Cursor {\n491:         input: LocatingSlice::new(src),\n492:         src,\n493:         declined_cross_line: None,\n494:         or_separates: false,\n495:     };\n496:     let mut defs = Vec::new();\n497:     let mut enums = Vec::new();\n498:     loop {\n499:         let (tok, span) = p.peek()?;\n500:         match tok {\n501:             Tok::Pub => {\n502:                 p.advance()?;\n503:                 match p.peek()?.0 {\n504:                     Tok::Enum => enums.push(p.enum_decl(true)?),\n505:                     _ => defs.push(p.def(true)?),\n506:                 }\n507:             }\n508:             Tok::Fn => defs.push(p.def(false)?),\n509:             Tok::Enum => enums.push(p.enum_decl(false)?),\n510:             Tok::Eof => break,\n511:             other => {\n512:                 return Err(Error::new(\n513:                     span,\n514:                     format!(\"expected `fn` or end of module, found {other}\"),\n515:                 ));\n516:             }\n517:         }\n518:     }\n519:     Ok(Module { defs, enums })\n520: }\n521: \n522: struct Cursor<'i> {\n523:     input: Input<'i>,\n524:     /// The full source, kept alongside the advancing `input` so the same-line rule can look at\n525:     /// the trivia between two tokens it already has spans for.\n526:     src: &'i str,\n527:     /// Where a call was declined because its argument started on a later line: the argument\n528:     /// token's start, and the callee's name. Declining is usually right -- a definition's body\n529:     /// and the program's body sit adjacent with nothing between them, and the line break is what\n530:     /// keeps one from swallowing the other -- but when the parse then fails at exactly the\n531:     /// declined token, the intended reading was probably the call, and the error can name the\n532:     /// parens spelling that works across lines.\n533:     declined_cross_line: Option<(usize, String)>,\n534:     /// Which of `or`'s two readings is in force here: the match-arm separator (true), or Bool\n535:     /// disjunction (false). True only inside an arm's body, where the separator has to win so\n536:     /// that `a -> b or c` is two arms; every fresh expression position below that -- a\n537:     /// parenthesized group, a call argument, an arm's own guard -- puts it back to false, since\n538:     /// nothing there is a chain's top level. This is the split kantord/toylang#96 set out to\n539:     /// prove clean, and the one place a token's meaning depends on where it is read.\n540:     or_separates: bool,\n541: }\n542: \n543: impl<'i> Cursor<'i> {\n544:     /// Reads the next token without consuming it. `Input` is `Copy` (a source `&str` plus an\n545:     /// offset), so looking ahead is just tokenizing a throwaway copy of the cursor.\n546:     fn peek(&self) -> Result<(Tok, Span), Error> {\n547:         let mut probe = self.input;\n548:         read_tok(&mut probe)\n549:     }\n550: \n551:     fn advance(&mut self) -> Result<(Tok, Span), Error> {\n552:         read_tok(&mut self.input)\n553:     }\n554: \n555:     fn eat(&mut self, want: Tok) -> Result<Span, Error> {\n556:         let (tok, span) = self.peek()?;\n557:         if tok != want {\n558:             return Err(self.unexpected(span, format!(\"expected {want}, found {tok}\")));\n559:         }\n560:         self.advance()?;\n561:         Ok(span)\n562:     }\n563: \n564:     /// An \"unexpected token\" error, extended with the parens spelling when the failing token is\n565:     /// one a call declined to take as a cross-line argument (see `declined_cross_line`): the\n566:     /// token had somewhere to go, and only the line break kept it from going there.\n567:     fn unexpected(&self, span: Span, msg: String) -> Error {\n568:         if let Some((at, callee)) = &self.declined_cross_line\n569:             && *at == span.start\n570:         {\n571:             return Error::new(\n572:                 span,\n573:                 format!(\n574:                     \"{msg}; an argument must start on the same line as its function -- \\\n575:                      write `{callee}(...)` to call across lines\"\n576:                 ),\n577:             );\n578:         }\n579:         Error::new(span, msg)\n580:     }\n581: \n582:     /// Whether nothing but spaces and tabs sits between two byte offsets. The same-line rule for\n583:     /// call arguments: an argument, bare or parenthesized, must start on the same line as its\n584:     /// function, which is what lets a definition's body end next to the program's body without\n585:     /// either reaching across the line break and swallowing the other.\n586:     fn same_line(&self, from: usize, to: usize) -> bool {\n587:         !self.src[from..to].contains('\\n')\n588:     }\n589: \n590:     /// The same-line rule applied at one call site: true when the argument starting at\n591:     /// `arg_span` is on `callee_span`'s line. A cross-line decline is recorded (see\n592:     /// `declined_cross_line`) so that if the parse then fails at that token, the error can say\n593:     /// why the call was not taken.\n594:     fn takes_argument(&mut self, callee: &str, callee_span: Span, arg_span: Span) -> bool {\n595:         if self.same_line(callee_span.end, arg_span.start) {\n596:             return true;\n597:         }\n598:         self.declined_cross_line = Some((arg_span.start, callee.to_string()));\n599:         false\n600:     }\n601: \n602:     /// Runs `f` with `or` reading as `separates` says, restoring the caller's reading after.\n603:     fn with_or<T>(\n604:         &mut self,\n605:         separates: bool,\n606:         f: impl FnOnce(&mut Self) -> Result<T, Error>,\n607:     ) -> Result<T, Error> {\n608:         let outer = std::mem::replace(&mut self.or_separates, separates);\n609:         let out = f(self);\n610:         self.or_separates = outer;\n611:         out\n612:     }\n613: \n614:     fn eat_ident(&mut self, what: &str) -> Result<(String, Span), Error> {\n615:         match self.advance()? {\n616:             (Tok::Ident(n), span) => Ok((n, span)),\n617:             (other, span) => Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"expected {what}, found {other}\"))),\n618:         }\n619:     }\n620: \n621:     /// What the parens hold in a function signature: nothing, `name: Type`, or\n622:     /// `{a, b, ..}: Type`, the destructuring spelling reusing a match arm's brace pattern.\n623:     fn param(&mut self) -> Result<Option<Param>, Error> {\n624:         let (next, _) = self.peek()?;\n625:         if next == Tok::RParen {\n626:             return Ok(None);\n627:         }\n628:         let shape = if next == Tok::LBrace {\n629:             ParamShape::Fields(self.fields_pattern()?)\n630:         } else {\n631:             let (param_name, param_span) = self.eat_ident(\"a name\")?;\n632:             ParamShape::Name(param_name, param_span)\n633:         };\n634:         // Both spellings are followed by `: Type`; the Name case checks for the `:` before\n635:         // parsing anything, and the Fields case must consume it too or the type parser meets it\n636:         // first.\n637:         let (colon, _) = self.peek()?;\n638:         if colon != Tok::Colon {\n639:             return Err(Error::new(\n640:                 shape.span(),\n641:                 match &shape {\n642:                     ParamShape::Name(name, _) => {\n643:                         format!(\"parameter `{name}` needs a type annotation\")\n644:                     }\n645:                     ParamShape::Fields(_) => {\n646:                         \"a destructured parameter needs a type annotation\".to_string()\n647:                     }\n648:                 },\n649:             ));\n650:         }\n651:         self.advance()?;\n652:         let param_ty = self.type_expr()?;\n653:         Ok(Some(Param {\n654:             span: shape.span().to(param_ty.span()),\n655:             shape,\n656:             ty: param_ty,\n657:         }))\n658:     }\n659: \n660:     /// `fn name(param: Type) -> Type = body`, `fn name() -> Type = body`, or the hoisted\n661:     /// `fn name = body` (gh:152) with no parameter list and no return annotation.\n662:     ///\n663:     /// The annotated forms require both annotations by the grammar rather than by the checker,\n664:     /// which is what makes the message point at the missing annotation instead of at an\n665:     /// inference failure. The hoisted form is the opposite: `=` follows the name directly, and\n666:     /// the whole signature -- parameter and return -- is inferred from the body, whose first\n667:     /// token must be a match call that names the enum the implicit `.` parameter matches.\n668:     fn def(&mut self, is_pub: bool) -> Result<Def, Error> {\n669:         let start = self.eat(Tok::Fn)?;\n670:         let (name, _) = self.eat_ident(\"a name\")?;\n671:         let (next, _) = self.peek()?;\n672:         if next == Tok::Eq {\n673:             self.advance()?;\n674:             let body = self.def_body()?;\n675:             return Ok(Def {\n676:                 span: start.to(body.span()),\n677:                 name,\n678:                 param: None,\n679:                 ret: None,\n680:                 body,\n681:                 is_pub,\n682:                 origin: crate::ast::Origin::Program,\n683:                 hoisted: true,\n684:             });\n685:         }\n686:         self.eat(Tok::LParen)?;\n687: \n688:         let param = self.param()?;\n689: \n690:         let close = self.eat(Tok::RParen)?;\n691:         let (arrow, _) = self.peek()?;\n692:         if arrow != Tok::Arrow {\n693:             return Err(Error::new(\n694:                 close,\n695:                 format!(\"function `{name}` needs a return type\"),\n696:             ));\n697:         }\n698:         self.advance()?;\n699:         let ret = self.type_expr()?;\n700: \n701:         self.eat(Tok::Eq)?;\n702:         let body = self.def_body()?;\n703:         Ok(Def {\n704:             span: start.to(body.span()),\n705:             name,\n706:             param,\n707:             ret: Some(ret),\n708:             body,\n709:             is_pub,\n710:             origin: crate::ast::Origin::Program,\n711:             hoisted: false,\n712:         })\n713:     }\n714: \n715:     /// `fn name(param: Type) -> Type`,the signature spine a trait method and an impl method\n716:     /// share. `what` names the kind in the return-type error;the caller decides whether a\n717:     /// `= body` follows the signature. A function's own spine stays inline in `def`.\n718:     fn fn_signature(\n719:         &mut self,\n720:         what: &str,\n721:     ) -> Result<(String, Option<Param>, TypeExpr, Span), Error> {\n722:         let start = self.eat(Tok::Fn)?;\n723:         let (name, _) = self.eat_ident(\"a name\")?;\n724:         self.eat(Tok::LParen)?;\n725: \n726:         let param = self.param()?;\n727: \n728:         let close = self.eat(Tok::RParen)?;\n729:         let (arrow, _) = self.peek()?;\n730:         if arrow != Tok::Arrow {\n731:             return Err(Error::new(\n732:                 close,\n733:                 format!(\"{what} `{name}` needs a return type\"),\n734:             ));\n735:         }\n736:         self.advance()?;\n737:         let ret = self.type_expr()?;\n738:         let span = start.to(ret.span());\n739:         Ok((name, param, ret, span))\n740:     }\n741: \n742:     /// `trait Name { fn sig(param: Type) -> Type, ... }`:a named collection of method\n743:     /// signatures with no bodies;an `impl` block provides those per concrete type. `pub` marks\n744:     /// it for a module. The body-less `fn` spine is the only thing inside the braces, so no\n745:     /// separator token is needed: each method starts with its own `fn`.\n746:     fn trait_decl(&mut self, is_pub: bool) -> Result<TraitDecl, Error> {\n747:         let start = self.eat(Tok::Trait)?;\n748:         let (name, _) = self.eat_ident(\"a trait name\")?;\n749:         self.eat(Tok::LBrace)?;\n750:         let mut methods = Vec::new();\n751:         let (first, _) = self.peek()?;\n752:         if first != Tok::RBrace {\n753:             loop {\n754:                 let (mname, param, ret, span) = self.fn_signature(\"trait method\")?;\n755:                 methods.push(TraitMethodSig {\n756:                     name: mname,\n757:                     param,\n758:                     ret,\n759:                     span,\n760:                 });\n761:                 if self.peek()?.0 == Tok::RBrace {\n762:                     break;\n763:                 }\n764:             }\n765:         }\n766:         let close = self.eat(Tok::RBrace)?;\n767:         Ok(TraitDecl {\n768:             name,\n769:             methods,\n770:             span: start.to(close),\n771:             is_pub,\n772:         })\n773:     }\n774: \n775:     /// `impl Trait for Type { fn sig(param: Type) -> Type = body, ... }`:concrete bodies\n776:     /// for one trait's methods, one block per (trait, type) pair. Like a trait's, each method\n777:     /// starts with its own `fn`, so the methods need no separator token between them.\n778:     fn impl_decl(&mut self) -> Result<ImplDecl, Error> {\n779:         let start = self.eat(Tok::Impl)?;\n780:         let (trait_name, _) = self.eat_ident(\"a trait name\")?;\n781:         let (link, link_span) = self.eat_ident(\"`for`\")?;\n782:         if link != \"for\" {\n783:             return Err(Error::new(\n784:                 link_span,\n785:                 format!(\n786:                     \"expected `for` between the trait name and its target type, found `{link}`\"\n787:                 ),\n788:             ));\n789:         }\n790:         let ty = self.type_expr()?;\n791:         self.eat(Tok::LBrace)?;\n792:         let mut methods = Vec::new();\n793:         let (first, _) = self.peek()?;\n794:         if first != Tok::RBrace {\n795:             loop {\n796:                 let (mname, param, ret, sig_span) = self.fn_signature(\"impl method\")?;\n797:                 self.eat(Tok::Eq)?;\n798:                 let body = self.def_body()?;\n799:                 let span = sig_span.to(body.span());\n800:                 methods.push(ImplMethod {\n801:                     name: mname,\n802:                     param,\n803:                     ret,\n804:                     body,\n805:                     span,\n806:                 });\n807:                 if self.peek()?.0 == Tok::RBrace {\n808:                     break;\n809:                 }\n810:             }\n811:         }\n812:         let close = self.eat(Tok::RBrace)?;\n813:         Ok(ImplDecl {\n814:             trait_name,\n815:             ty,\n816:             methods,\n817:             span: start.to(close),\n818:         })\n819:     }\n820: \n821:     /// A function's body. An ordinary expression, or -- when the `let` keyword opens it -- the\n822:     /// local-binding block the ruling on #87 settled on: `let <name> = <expr>` one per line,\n823:     /// then the expression that ends the block, with no `in` keyword. The line that is not a\n824:     /// `let` is the value, so each binding's value has to fit on its own line or the next line\n825:     /// cannot be told apart from the block's result.\n826:     fn def_body(&mut self) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n827:         let (first, _) = self.peek()?;\n828:         if first != Tok::Let {\n829:             return self.tail_pipe();\n830:         }\n831:         let first_let = self.advance()?.1;\n832:         let mut bindings = Vec::new();\n833:         let mut binding_start = first_let.start;\n834: \n835:         loop {\n836:             let (name, _) = self.eat_ident(\"a binding name\")?;\n837:             self.eat(Tok::Eq)?;\n838:             let value = self.expr(0)?;\n839:             if !self.same_line(binding_start, value.span().end) {\n840:                 return Err(Error::new(\n841:                     value.span(),\n842:                     \"a `let` binding must fit on its line; write the whole `let <name> = <expr>` \\\n843:                      on one line and let the next line be another `let` or the block's value\"\n844:                         .to_string(),\n845:                 ));\n846:             }\n847:             bindings.push((name, value));\n848:             if self.peek()?.0 == Tok::Let {\n849:                 binding_start = self.advance()?.1.start;\n850: \n851:                 continue;\n852:             }\n853:             break;\n854:         }\n855:         let body = self.expr(0)?;\n856:         let span = first_let.to(body.span());\n857:         Ok(Expr::Let {\n858:             bindings,\n859:             body: Box::new(body),\n860:             span,\n861:         })\n862:     }\n863: \n864:     /// `Str`, `Int`, `Bool`, `Vec<T>`, `Stream<T>`, or any declared name -- with\n865:     /// `<...>` arguments when the name is a generic enum's. The parser accepts arguments on\n866:     /// every name and lets resolution hold the arity, so `Pair` and `Str<Int>` fail with an\n867:     /// error that knows what `Pair` and `Str` are rather than a parse error that does not.\n868:     fn type_expr(&mut self) -> Result<TypeExpr, Error> {\n869:         let (tok, span) = self.advance()?;\n870:         if tok == Tok::LBrace {\n871:             return self.record_type(span);\n872:         }\n873:         let name = match tok {\n874:             Tok::Ident(n) => n,\n875:             other => return Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"expected a type, found {other}\"))),\n876:         };\n877:         if self.peek()?.0 != Tok::Lt {\n878:             return Ok(TypeExpr::Named {\n879:                 name,\n880:                 args: Vec::new(),\n881:                 span,\n882:             });\n883:         }\n884:         self.advance()?;\n885:         let mut args = Vec::new();\n886:         loop {\n887:             args.push(self.type_expr()?);\n888:             let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n889:             if sep != Tok::Comma {\n890:                 break;\n891:             }\n892:             self.advance()?;\n893:         }\n894:         let close = self.eat(Tok::Gt)?;\n895:         let span = span.to(close);\n896:         // The two built-in constructors keep their own nodes; their arity is the grammar's.\n897:         if ty::takes_type_arg(&name) {\n898:             if args.len() != 1 {\n899:                 return Err(Error::new(\n900:                     span,\n901:                     format!(\"`{name}` takes one type argument, found {}\", args.len()),\n902:                 ));\n903:             }\n904:             let elem = Box::new(args.remove(0));\n905:             return Ok(match name.as_str() {\n906:                 \"Vec\" => TypeExpr::Vec { elem, span },\n907:                 _ => TypeExpr::Stream { elem, span },\n908:             });\n909:         }\n910:         Ok(TypeExpr::Named { name, args, span })\n911:     }\n912: \n913:     /// A call's argument when it opens with `(` or `{`: a parenthesized expression, or a record\n914:     /// literal standing bare because `{` cannot start any other expression. The closing token\n915:     /// marks the argument's end unambiguously, which is what makes the parens form the\n916:     /// disambiguator wherever the bare form's own stopping rules would read a program\n917:     /// differently.\n918:     fn argument(&mut self) -> Result<(Expr, Span), Error> {\n919:         let (tok, span) = self.peek()?;\n920:         if tok == Tok::LBrace {\n921:             self.advance()?;\n922:             let lit = self.record_lit(span)?;\n923:             let lit_span = lit.span();\n924:             return Ok((lit, lit_span));\n925:         }\n926:         self.eat(Tok::LParen)?;\n927:         let inner = self.expr(0)?;\n928:         let close = self.eat(Tok::RParen)?;\n929:         Ok((inner, close))\n930:     }\n931: \n932:     /// `{name: expr, age: expr}`, the value form of the brace that `record_type` reads in type\n933:     /// position.\n934:     fn record_lit(&mut self, open: Span) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n935:         let mut fields = Vec::new();\n936:         let (first, _) = self.peek()?;\n937:         if first != Tok::RBrace {\n938:             loop {\n939:                 let (name, name_span) = self.eat_ident(\"a field name\")?;\n940:                 self.eat(Tok::Colon)?;\n941:                 fields.push((name, name_span, self.expr(0)?));\n942:                 let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n943:                 if sep != Tok::Comma {\n944:                     break;\n945:                 }\n946:                 self.advance()?;\n947:             }\n948:         }\n949:         let close = self.eat(Tok::RBrace)?;\n950:         Ok(Expr::RecordLit {\n951:             fields,\n952:             span: open.to(close),\n953:         })\n954:     }\n955: \n956:     /// `enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int}, celsius(Int) }`, optionally with type\n957:     /// parameters: `enum Opt<T> { some(T), none }`.\n958:     ///\n959:     /// A variant is a name, optionally followed by its payload type. The payload rule is any\n960:     /// single type, spelled the way a call spells its argument: a record type directly in\n961:     /// braces, or any type in parens.\n962:     fn enum_decl(&mut self, is_pub: bool) -> Result<EnumDecl, Error> {\n963:         let start = self.eat(Tok::Enum)?;\n964:         let (name, _) = self.eat_ident(\"an enum name\")?;\n965:         let mut params = Vec::new();\n966:         if self.peek()?.0 == Tok::Lt {\n967:             self.advance()?;\n968:             loop {\n969:                 params.push(self.eat_ident(\"a type parameter\")?);\n970:                 let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n971:                 if sep != Tok::Comma {\n972:                     break;\n973:                 }\n974:                 self.advance()?;\n975:             }\n976:             self.eat(Tok::Gt)?;\n977:         }\n978:         self.eat(Tok::LBrace)?;\n979:         let mut variants = Vec::new();\n980:         let (first, _) = self.peek()?;\n981:         if first != Tok::RBrace {\n982:             loop {\n983:                 let (vname, vspan) = self.eat_ident(\"a variant name\")?;\n984:                 let payload = match self.peek()? {\n985:                     (Tok::LBrace, open) => {\n986:                         self.advance()?;\n987:                         Some(self.record_type(open)?)\n988:                     }\n989:                     (Tok::LParen, _) => {\n990:                         self.advance()?;\n991:                         let ty = self.type_expr()?;\n992:                         self.eat(Tok::RParen)?;\n993:                         Some(ty)\n994:                     }\n995:                     _ => None,\n996:                 };\n997:                 variants.push(Variant {\n998:                     name: vname,\n999:                     span: vspan,\n1000:                     payload,\n1001:                 });\n1002:                 let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n1003:                 if sep != Tok::Comma {\n1004:                     break;\n1005:                 }\n1006:                 self.advance()?;\n1007:             }\n1008:         }\n1009:         let close = self.eat(Tok::RBrace)?;\n1010:         Ok(EnumDecl {\n1011:             name,\n1012:             params,\n1013:             variants,\n1014:             span: start.to(close),\n1015:             is_pub,\n1016:         })\n1017:     }\n1018: \n1019:     /// `type Db = {users: Vec<User>}`\n1020:     fn alias(&mut self) -> Result<Alias, Error> {\n1021:         let start = self.eat(Tok::Type)?;\n1022:         let (name, _) = self.eat_ident(\"a type name\")?;\n1023:         self.eat(Tok::Eq)?;\n1024:         let ty = self.type_expr()?;\n1025:         let span = start.to(ty.span());\n1026:         Ok(Alias { name, ty, span })\n1027:     }\n1028: \n1029:     fn record_type(&mut self, open: Span) -> Result<TypeExpr, Error> {\n1030:         let mut fields = Vec::new();\n1031:         let (first, _) = self.peek()?;\n1032:         if first != Tok::RBrace {\n1033:             loop {\n1034:                 let (fname, _) = self.eat_ident(\"a field name\")?;\n1035:                 self.eat(Tok::Colon)?;\n1036:                 fields.push((fname, self.type_expr()?));\n1037:                 let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n1038:                 if sep != Tok::Comma {\n1039:                     break;\n1040:                 }\n1041:                 self.advance()?;\n1042:             }\n1043:         }\n1044:         let close = self.eat(Tok::RBrace)?;\n1045:         Ok(TypeExpr::Record {\n1046:             fields,\n1047:             span: open.to(close),\n1048:         })\n1049:     }\n1050: \n1051:     /// Whether the tokens ahead begin a match arm: `name ->`, `name{a, b} ->`, or `any() ->`.\n1052:     /// The one place this has to look carefully is a brace: a record *pattern* holds bare names\n1053:     /// (and `..`), so the first `:` proves the braces are a constructor's record literal\n1054:     /// argument instead. A lexical error while probing is not an arm; the ordinary path will\n1055:     /// surface it.\n1056:     fn arm_starts_here(&self) -> bool {\n1057:         let mut probe = self.input;\n1058:         let mut next = || read_tok(&mut probe).map(|(t, _)| t);\n1059:         let Ok(Tok::Ident(name)) = next() else {\n1060:             return false;\n1061:         };\n1062:         match next() {\n1063:             Ok(Tok::Arrow) => true,\n1064:             Ok(Tok::LParen) if name == \"any\" => {\n1065:                 matches!(next(), Ok(Tok::RParen)) && matches!(next(), Ok(Tok::Arrow))\n1066:             }\n1067:             Ok(Tok::LBrace) => {\n1068:                 loop {\n1069:                     match next() {\n1070:                         Ok(Tok::Ident(_) | Tok::Comma | Tok::Dot) => continue,\n1071:                         Ok(Tok::RBrace) => break,\n1072:                         _ => return false,\n1073:                     }\n1074:                 }\n1075:                 matches!(next(), Ok(Tok::Arrow))\n1076:             }\n1077:             _ => false,\n1078:         }\n1079:     }\n1080: \n1081:     /// `left -> body or left -> body or ...`, first match wins; `or` is the one arm composer.\n1082:     /// A left side is a variant pattern (`circle{r}`), `any()`, or a Bool guard expression; the\n1083:     /// chain's final element may instead be a bare expression, the default. The chain reads `.`\n1084:     /// as its subject, so it usually sits to the right of a `|`; each body stops at `|` and\n1085:     /// `or`, which is what lets the chain be one pipe stage.\n1086:     ///\n1087:     /// The two `or`s meet here and nowhere else. A body is read with the separator reading, so\n1088:     /// it ends at the next `or`; an element's own left side is read with the disjunction one, so\n1089:     /// `a -> 1 or b == 2 or c == 3 -> 4` is two arms whose second has a two-clause guard. What\n1090:     /// makes that split decidable is that an element ending at a bare `or` could only ever have\n1091:     /// been a bare default in a non-final position, which was already an error.\n1092:     ///\n1093:     /// `lead` is an expression `expr` had already read when the `->` after it revealed a chain;\n1094:     /// the first element continues from it instead of parsing fresh.\n1095:     fn match_expr(&mut self, lead: Option<Expr>) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n1096:         let mut arms = Vec::new();\n1097:         let mut lead = lead;\n1098:         loop {\n1099:             let arm = match lead.take() {\n1100:                 Some(e) => self.match_arm(Some(e))?,\n1101:                 None => self.match_arm(None)?,\n1102:             };\n1103:             arms.push(arm);\n1104:             let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n1105:             if sep != Tok::Or {\n1106:                 break;\n1107:             }\n1108:             self.advance()?;\n1109:         }\n1110:         let span = arms[0].span.to(arms[arms.len() - 1].span);\n1111:         Ok(Expr::Match { arms, span })\n1112:     }\n1113: \n1114:     /// One `or`-separated element of an arm chain: a variant pattern arm, or (when `lead` is a\n1115:     /// guard the caller already read, or no pattern begins the element) a guard or default arm.\n1116:     /// Shared by a `Match` and a `MatchCall` (gh:152), which differ only in what heads them and\n1117:     /// how the chain ends.\n1118:     fn match_arm(&mut self, lead: Option<Expr>) -> Result<MatchArm, Error> {\n1119:         match lead {\n1120:             Some(e) => self.guard_or_default_arm(e),\n1121:             None if self.arm_starts_here() => {\n1122:                 let pattern = self.pattern()?;\n1123:                 self.eat(Tok::Arrow)?;\n1124:                 let body = self.arm_body()?;\n1125:                 let span = pattern.span().to(body.span());\n1126:                 Ok(MatchArm {\n1127:                     pattern,\n1128:                     body,\n1129:                     span,\n1130:                 })\n1131:             }\n1132:             None => {\n1133:                 let e = self.with_or(false, |p| p.operand(COND_POWER))?;\n1134:                 self.guard_or_default_arm(e)\n1135:             }\n1136:         }\n1137:     }\n1138: \n1139:     /// `Msg(Ping -> \"pong\" or Quit -> \"bye\")` (gh:152): a capitalized type name used as a match\n1140:     /// call. The parens hold the same `or`-separated arms a `Match` carries, over the subject\n1141:     /// `.`; the name is the assertion of what the subject is, and the checker resolves each arm\n1142:     /// against it. A capitalized name is always an enum, never a callable, so `Name(...)` is\n1143:     /// unambiguously a match call rather than a function application.\n1144:     fn match_call(&mut self, enum_name: String, enum_span: Span) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n1145:         self.eat(Tok::LParen)?;\n1146:         let mut arms = Vec::new();\n1147:         loop {\n1148:             arms.push(self.match_arm(None)?);\n1149:             let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n1150:             if sep != Tok::Or {\n1151:                 break;\n1152:             }\n1153:             self.advance()?;\n1154:         }\n1155:         let close = self.eat(Tok::RParen)?;\n1156:         let span = enum_span.to(close);\n1157:         Ok(Expr::MatchCall {\n1158:             enum_name,\n1159:             enum_span,\n1160:             arms,\n1161:             span,\n1162:         })\n1163:     }\n1164: \n1165:     /// An arm's right side: the one position where a bare `or` is the chain's separator rather\n1166:     /// than disjunction, so a Bool `or` written here needs parens (draft.md, the match-arms\n1167:     /// decision).\n1168:     fn arm_body(&mut self) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n1169:         self.with_or(true, |p| p.expr(COND_POWER))\n1170:     }\n1171: \n1172:     /// The element `left` opens: `left -> body` makes `left` a guard, and a bare `left` is the\n1173:     /// default, desugared to a `Default` arm whose body is `left` itself.\n1174:     fn guard_or_default_arm(&mut self, left: Expr) -> Result<MatchArm, Error> {\n1175:         if self.peek()?.0 == Tok::Arrow {\n1176:             self.advance()?;\n1177:             let body = self.arm_body()?;\n1178:             let span = left.span().to(body.span());\n1179:             return Ok(MatchArm {\n1180:                 pattern: Pattern::Guard(left),\n1181:                 body,\n1182:                 span,\n1183:             });\n1184:         }\n1185:         let span = left.span();\n1186:         Ok(MatchArm {\n1187:             pattern: Pattern::Default { span },\n1188:             body: left,\n1189:             span,\n1190:         })\n1191:     }\n1192: \n1193:     fn pattern(&mut self) -> Result<Pattern, Error> {\n1194:         let (name, span) = self.eat_ident(\"a pattern\")?;\n1195:         let (next, _) = self.peek()?;\n1196:         if name == \"any\" && next == Tok::LParen {\n1197:             self.advance()?;\n1198:             let close = self.eat(Tok::RParen)?;\n1199:             return Ok(Pattern::Default {\n1200:                 span: span.to(close),\n1201:             });\n1202:         }\n1203:         if next != Tok::LBrace {\n1204:             return Ok(Pattern::Variant {\n1205:                 name,\n1206:                 span,\n1207:                 fields: None,\n1208:             });\n1209:         }\n1210:         let fields = self.fields_pattern()?;\n1211:         Ok(Pattern::Variant {\n1212:             name,\n1213:             span: span.to(fields.span),\n1214:             fields: Some(fields),\n1215:         })\n1216:     }\n1217: \n1218:     /// `{a, b, ..}`: the brace pattern a match arm and a destructuring parameter share.\n1219:     /// `..` is two Dot tokens, and it ends the list: naming a field after \"and the rest\" would\n1220:     /// make the marker meaningless.\n1221:     fn fields_pattern(&mut self) -> Result<FieldsPattern, Error> {\n1222:         let open = self.eat(Tok::LBrace)?;\n1223:         let mut names = Vec::new();\n1224:         let mut rest = false;\n1225:         let (first, _) = self.peek()?;\n1226:         if first != Tok::RBrace {\n1227:             loop {\n1228:                 if self.peek()?.0 == Tok::Dot {\n1229:                     self.advance()?;\n1230:                     self.eat(Tok::Dot)?;\n1231:                     rest = true;\n1232:                     break;\n1233:                 }\n1234:                 names.push(self.eat_ident(\"a field name\")?);\n1235:                 let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n1236:                 if sep != Tok::Comma {\n1237:                     break;\n1238:                 }\n1239:                 self.advance()?;\n1240:             }\n1241:         }\n1242:         let close = self.eat(Tok::RBrace)?;\n1243:         Ok(FieldsPattern {\n1244:             names,\n1245:             rest,\n1246:             span: open.to(close),\n1247:         })\n1248:     }\n1249: \n1250:     /// Arm chains begin only where an expression begins fresh (a pipe stage, a delimited\n1251:     /// position), and a fresh position is also where `or` goes back to reading as disjunction:\n1252:     /// whatever chain the enclosing arm body belonged to, this expression is not part of it. An\n1253:     /// arm body enters at COND_POWER instead and leaves any `->` or separator `or` it meets for\n1254:     /// the chain that owns it, rather than opening a nested chain that would swallow the rest of\n1255:     /// the outer one.\n1256:     fn expr(&mut self, min_power: u8) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n1257:         if min_power <= PIPE_RIGHT {\n1258:             return self.with_or(false, |p| p.expr_at(min_power, true));\n1259:         }\n1260:         self.expr_at(min_power, false)\n1261:     }\n1262: \n1263:     fn expr_at(&mut self, min_power: u8, fresh: bool) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n1264:         let mut lhs = if fresh && self.arm_starts_here() {\n1265:             self.match_expr(None)?\n1266:         } else {\n1267:             let e = self.operand(min_power)?;\n1268:             let (tok, _) = self.peek()?;\n1269:             // No `Tok::Or` here: a fresh position reads `or` as disjunction, so `operand` has\n1270:             // already taken any that were there, and an arm chain is revealed by the `->` that\n1271:             // follows the whole disjunction (`a == 1 or b == 2 -> ...` is one guard).\n1272:             if fresh && tok == Tok::Arrow {\n1273:                 self.match_expr(Some(e))?\n1274:             } else {\n1275:                 e\n1276:             }\n1277:         };\n1278: \n1279:         loop {\n1280:             let (tok, _) = self.peek()?;\n1281:             if tok != Tok::Pipe || PIPE_LEFT < min_power {\n1282:                 break;\n1283:             }\n1284:             self.advance()?;\n1285:             let rhs = self.expr(PIPE_RIGHT)?;\n1286:             let span = lhs.span().to(rhs.span());\n1287:             lhs = Expr::Pipe {\n1288:                 lhs: Box::new(lhs),\n1289:                 rhs: Box::new(rhs),\n1290:                 span,\n1291:             };\n1292:         }\n1293: \n1294:         Ok(lhs)\n1295:     }\n1296: \n1297:     /// The program's body (or a function's, when it is not a `let` block): an ordinary\n1298:     /// expression, or the tail-pipeline `lhs |> callee` that is the one way a sink is written.\n1299:     /// Parsed only here, at the outermost position, so a nested `|>` is a parse error rather\n1300:     /// than a value -- the checker's sink position rule is this production's, not a search for\n1301:     /// a stray token somewhere inside a larger expression.\n1302:     fn tail_pipe(&mut self) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n1303:         let lhs = self.expr(0)?;\n1304:         let (tok, _) = self.peek()?;\n1305:         if tok != Tok::PipeGt {\n1306:             return Ok(lhs);\n1307:         }\n1308:         self.advance()?;\n1309:         let (callee, callee_span) = self.eat_ident(\"a sink call\")?;\n1310:         let span = lhs.span().to(callee_span);\n1311:         Ok(Expr::TailPipe {\n1312:             lhs: Box::new(lhs),\n1313:             callee,\n1314:             callee_span,\n1315:             span,\n1316:         })\n1317:     }\n1318: \n1319:     fn operand(&mut self, min_power: u8) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n1320:         let mut lhs = self.complement(min_power)?;\n1321: \n1322:         loop {\n1323:             let (tok, _) = self.peek()?;\n1324:             // Where `or` is the arm separator it is not an operator at all, so the table is not\n1325:             // even consulted: the chain the arm belongs to takes the token.\n1326:             if tok == Tok::Or && self.or_separates {\n1327:                 break;\n1328:             }\n1329:             let Some((op, left, right)) = infix_power(&tok) else {\n1330:                 break;\n1331:             };\n1332:             if left < min_power {\n1333:                 break;\n1334:             }\n1335:             self.advance()?;\n1336:             let rhs = self.operand(right)?;\n1337:             let span = lhs.span().to(rhs.span());\n1338:             lhs = match op {\n1339:                 Infix::Bin(op) => Expr::Binary {\n1340:                     op,\n1341:                     lhs: Box::new(lhs),\n1342:                     rhs: Box::new(rhs),\n1343:                     span,\n1344:                 },\n1345:                 Infix::Logic(op) => Expr::Logic {\n1346:                     op,\n1347:                     lhs: Box::new(lhs),\n1348:                     rhs: Box::new(rhs),\n1349:                     span,\n1350:                 },\n1351:             };\n1352:         }\n1353: \n1354:         Ok(lhs)\n1355:     }\n1356: \n1357:     /// `not`, which is prefix but not unary in `-`'s sense: it binds looser than every operator\n1358:     /// in the infix table above comparison, so it reads its own operand at its own power rather\n1359:     /// than taking whatever `unary` returns. A position that binds tighter than `not` (a\n1360:     /// comparison's right side, say) declines it, and parens are what reach in there.\n1361:     fn complement(&mut self, min_power: u8) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n\n(Output capped at 50 KB. Showing lines 1-1361. Use offset=1362 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"use winnow::error::ParserError;\nuse winnow::prelude::*;\nuse winnow::stream::{LocatingSlice, Location, Stream};\nuse winnow::token::take_while;\n\nuse crate::ast::{\n    Alias, BinOp, Def, EnumDecl, Expr, FieldsPattern, File, ImplDecl, ImplMethod, LogicOp,\n    MatchArm, Module, Param, ParamShape, Pattern, Span, TraitDecl, TraitMethodSig, TypeExpr,\n    Variant,\n};\nuse crate::error::Error;\nuse crate::ty;\n\n/// The stream this whole module parses over: a source string with byte-offset tracking built\n/// in, so every sub-parser gets `Span`s for free from `current_token_start` rather than threading\n/// position by hand.\ntype Input<'i> = LocatingSlice<&'i str>;\n\n/// Lets every winnow combinator used below (`take_while`, and anything built on it) report\n/// failure as this crate's own `Error` directly -- there is no separate winnow error type to","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/parse.rs","text":"use winnow::error::ParserError;\nuse winnow::prelude::*;\nuse winnow::stream::{LocatingSlice, Location, Stream};\nuse winnow::token::take_while;\n\nuse crate::ast::{\n    Alias, BinOp, Def, EnumDecl, Expr, FieldsPattern, File, ImplDecl, ImplMethod, LogicOp,\n    MatchArm, Module, Param, ParamShape, Pattern, Span, TraitDecl, TraitMethodSig, TypeExpr,\n    Variant,\n};\nuse crate::error::Error;\nuse crate::ty;\n\n/// The stream this whole module parses over: a source string with byte-offset tracking built\n/// in, so every sub-parser gets `Span`s for free from `current_token_start` rather than threading\n/// position by hand.\ntype Input<'i> = LocatingSlice<&'i str>;\n\n/// Lets every winnow combinator used below (`take_while`, and anything built on it) report\n/// failure as this crate's own `Error` directly -- there is no separate winnow error type to\n/// translate out of. `from_input` is winnow's fallback for a failure it synthesises itself\n/// rather than one this module constructs explicitly; nothing here is expected to hit it; the\n/// hand-written parsers below always build their own `Error` with a specific message instead.\nimpl<'i> ParserError<Input<'i>> for Error {\n    type Inner = Error;\n\n    fn from_input(input: &Input<'i>) -> Error {\n        let at = input.current_token_start();\n        Error::new(Span::new(at, at), \"expected a token\".to_string())\n    }\n\n    fn into_inner(self) -> Result<Error, Error> {\n        Ok(self)\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]\nenum Tok {\n    Str(String),\n    Int(i64),\n    Float(f64),\n    Ident(String),\n    Fn,\n    Pub,\n    Type,\n    Enum,\n    Trait,\n    Impl,\n    Let,\n    Stdin,\n    Dsv,\n    Csv,\n    Tsv,\n    And,\n    Or,\n    Not,\n    Plus,\n    Minus,\n    Star,\n    Slash,\n    Percent,\n    Pipe,\n    PipeGt,\n    Comma,\n    Dot,\n    Eq,\n    EqEq,\n    Ne,\n    Bang,\n    Lt,\n    Le,\n    Gt,\n    Ge,\n    LParen,\n    RParen,\n    LBracket,\n    RBracket,\n    LBrace,\n    RBrace,\n    Colon,\n    Semicolon,\n    Arrow,\n    Eof,\n}\n\nimpl std::fmt::Display for Tok {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {\n        let s = match self {\n            Tok::Str(_) => return write!(f, \"a string\"),\n            Tok::Int(n) => return write!(f, \"`{n}`\"),\n            Tok::Float(n) => return write!(f, \"`{n}`\"),\n            Tok::Ident(name) => return write!(f, \"`{name}`\"),\n            Tok::Fn => \"`fn`\",\n            Tok::Pub => \"`pub`\",\n            Tok::Type => \"`type`\",\n            Tok::Enum => \"`enum`\",\n            Tok::Trait => \"`trait`\",\n            Tok::Impl => \"`impl`\",\n            Tok::Let => \"`let`\",\n            Tok::Stdin => \"`stdin`\",\n            Tok::Dsv => \"`dsv`\",\n            Tok::Csv => \"`csv`\",\n            Tok::Tsv => \"`tsv`\",\n            Tok::And => \"`and`\",\n            Tok::Or => \"`or`\",\n            Tok::Not => \"`not`\",\n            Tok::Plus => \"`+`\",\n            Tok::Minus => \"`-`\",\n            Tok::Star => \"`*`\",\n            Tok::Slash => \"`/`\",\n            Tok::Percent => \"`%`\",\n            Tok::Pipe => \"`|`\",\n            Tok::PipeGt => \"`|>`\",\n            Tok::Comma => \"`,`\",\n            Tok::Dot => \"`.`\",\n            Tok::Eq => \"`=`\",\n            Tok::EqEq => \"`==`\",\n            Tok::Ne => \"`!=`\",\n            Tok::Bang => \"`!`\",\n            Tok::Lt => \"`<`\",\n            Tok::Le => \"`<=`\",\n            Tok::Gt => \"`>`\",\n            Tok::Ge => \"`>=`\",\n            Tok::LParen => \"`(`\",\n            Tok::RParen => \"`)`\",\n            Tok::LBracket => \"`[`\",\n            Tok::RBracket => \"`]`\",\n            Tok::LBrace => \"`{`\",\n            Tok::RBrace => \"`}`\",\n            Tok::Colon => \"`:`\",\n            Tok::Semicolon => \"`;`\",\n            Tok::Arrow => \"`->`\",\n            Tok::Eof => \"end of program\",\n        };\n        write!(f, \"{s}\")\n    }\n}\n\n/// Zero or more ASCII spaces/tabs/newlines, then a `#`-to-newline comment if one follows,\n/// repeated: a run of trivia can mix any number of either in any order.\nfn skip_trivia(input: &mut Input) {\n    loop {\n        take_while::<_, _, Error>(0.., |c: char| c.is_ascii_whitespace())\n            .parse_next(input)\n            .expect(\"a lower bound of zero never fails\");\n        if input.peek_token() != Some('#') {\n            return;\n        }\n        input.next_token();\n        while !matches!(input.peek_token(), None | Some('\\n')) {\n            input.next_token();\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Skips trivia, then reads exactly one token from the front of `input`. Called fresh for every\n/// `peek`/`advance` rather than once up front into a `Vec`, so a lexical error (a bad\n/// escape, an out-of-range integer) surfaces at the point parsing actually reaches it instead of\n/// always winning over a parse error earlier in the file the way a separate up-front pass would.\nfn read_tok<'i>(input: &mut Input<'i>) -> Result<(Tok, Span), Error> {\n    skip_trivia(input);\n    let start = input.current_token_start();\n    let Some(c) = input.peek_token() else {\n        return Ok((Tok::Eof, Span::new(start, start)));\n    };\n\n    let tok = match c {\n        '\"' => Tok::Str(read_string(input)?),\n        '0'..='9' => read_number(input, start)?,\n        c if c.is_ascii_alphabetic() || c == '_' => {\n            let word =\n                take_while::<_, _, Error>(1.., |c: char| c.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || c == '_')\n                    .parse_next(input)\n                    .expect(\"a letter or underscore was just confirmed to follow\");\n            match word {\n                \"fn\" => Tok::Fn,\n                \"pub\" => Tok::Pub,\n                \"type\" => Tok::Type,\n                \"enum\" => Tok::Enum,\n                \"trait\" => Tok::Trait,\n                \"impl\" => Tok::Impl,\n                \"let\" => Tok::Let,\n                \"stdin\" => Tok::Stdin,\n                \"dsv\" => Tok::Dsv,\n                \"csv\" => Tok::Csv,\n                \"tsv\" => Tok::Tsv,\n                \"and\" => Tok::And,\n                // Two operators sharing one spelling, told apart by position: the arm separator\n                // at a chain's top level, Bool disjunction everywhere else. `Cursor::or_separates`\n                // is which one is being read (kantord/toylang#96).\n                \"or\" => Tok::Or,\n                \"not\" => Tok::Not,\n                name => Tok::Ident(name.to_string()),\n            }\n        }\n        // `-` is subtraction and negation now, so a digit after it is no longer a negative\n        // literal: `a -1` would otherwise not be `a - 1`.\n        '-' => read_two_char(input, '>', Tok::Minus, Tok::Arrow),\n        '!' => read_two_char(input, '=', Tok::Bang, Tok::Ne),\n        '=' | '<' | '>' => match c {\n            '=' => read_two_char(input, '=', Tok::Eq, Tok::EqEq),\n            '<' => read_two_char(input, '=', Tok::Lt, Tok::Le),\n            _ => read_two_char(input, '=', Tok::Gt, Tok::Ge),\n        },\n        '+' => single(input, Tok::Plus),\n        '*' => single(input, Tok::Star),\n        // `//` is not a token anymore: match arms compose with `or` now, so a second slash is\n        // just a `/` where no expression can start, which is the parse error migration needs.\n        '/' => single(input, Tok::Slash),\n        '%' => single(input, Tok::Percent),\n        '|' => read_two_char(input, '>', Tok::Pipe, Tok::PipeGt),\n        ',' => single(input, Tok::Comma),\n        '.' => single(input, Tok::Dot),\n        '(' => single(input, Tok::LParen),\n        ')' => single(input, Tok::RParen),\n        '[' => single(input, Tok::LBracket),\n        ']' => single(input, Tok::RBracket),\n        '{' => single(input, Tok::LBrace),\n        '}' => single(input, Tok::RBrace),\n        ':' => single(input, Tok::Colon),\n        ';' => single(input, Tok::Semicolon),\n        other => {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                Span::new(start, start + 1),\n                format!(\"unexpected character `{other}`\"),\n            ));\n        }\n    };\n    Ok((tok, Span::new(start, input.current_token_start())))\n}\n\n/// A number literal: the integer digits that opened it, then an optional `.digits` fraction\n/// and an optional `e`-exponent. Either makes it a Float (ADR 0007); without both it stays the\n/// Int it always was. A `.` or `e` only joins the literal when what follows makes a number, so\n/// `1.5` and `1e3` are one token while `1.`, `1.x`, and `1e` keep the dot or letter for the\n/// parser.\nfn read_number<'i>(input: &mut Input<'i>, start: usize) -> Result<Tok, Error> {\n    let digits = take_while::<_, _, Error>(1.., |c: char| c.is_ascii_digit())\n        .parse_next(input)\n        .expect(\"a digit was just confirmed to follow\");\n    let mut text = digits.to_string();\n    let mut is_float = false;\n\n    if let Some(frac) = read_fraction(input) {\n        text.push('.');\n        text.push_str(&frac);\n        is_float = true;\n    }\n    if let Some(exp) = read_exponent(input) {\n        text.push('e');\n        text.push_str(&exp);\n        is_float = true;\n    }\n\n    let end = input.current_token_start();\n    if is_float {\n        let value = text.parse::<f64>().map_err(|_| {\n            Error::new(\n                Span::new(start, end),\n                format!(\"float `{text}` is out of range\"),\n            )\n        })?;\n        Ok(Tok::Float(value))\n    } else {\n        let n = digits.parse::<i64>().map_err(|_| {\n            Error::new(\n                Span::new(start, end),\n                format!(\"integer `{digits}` is out of range\"),\n            )\n        })?;\n        Ok(Tok::Int(n))\n    }\n}\n\n/// The `.digits` fraction of a number literal, `None` when the dot is not followed by a digit\n/// so it is left for the parser (`1.` and `1.x` are not floats).\nfn read_fraction<'i>(input: &mut Input<'i>) -> Option<String> {\n    if input.peek_token() != Some('.') {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let cp = input.checkpoint();\n    input.next_token();\n    if matches!(input.peek_token(), Some(c) if c.is_ascii_digit()) {\n        let frac = take_while::<_, _, Error>(1.., |c: char| c.is_ascii_digit())\n            .parse_next(input)\n            .expect(\"a digit was just confirmed to follow\");\n        Some(frac.to_string())\n    } else {\n        input.reset(&cp);\n        None\n    }\n}\n\n/// The `e`-exponent of a number literal, `None` when the exponent has no digits after it so\n/// the `e` is left for the parser (a bare trailing `e` is not a float).\nfn read_exponent<'i>(input: &mut Input<'i>) -> Option<String> {\n    if !matches!(input.peek_token(), Some('e') | Some('E')) {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let cp = input.checkpoint();\n    input.next_token();\n    let mut exp = String::new();\n    if matches!(input.peek_token(), Some('+') | Some('-')) {\n        exp.push(input.next_token().expect(\"a sign was just seen\"));\n    }\n    if matches!(input.peek_token(), Some(c) if c.is_ascii_digit()) {\n        let exd = take_while::<_, _, Error>(1.., |c: char| c.is_ascii_digit())\n            .parse_next(input)\n            .expect(\"a digit was just confirmed to follow\");\n        exp.push_str(exd);\n        Some(exp)\n    } else {\n        input.reset(&cp);\n        None\n    }\n}\n\nfn single(input: &mut Input, tok: Tok) -> Tok {\n    input.next_token();\n    tok\n}\n\n/// `-`, `!`, `=`, `<`, `>`, `|` all may start a two-character token: if the char after the\n/// first is `second`, the compound token stands; otherwise the bare operator does. `single` is\n/// for tokens that have no compound form at all.\nfn read_two_char(input: &mut Input, second: char, bare: Tok, compound: Tok) -> Tok {\n    input.next_token();\n    if input.peek_token() == Some(second) {\n        input.next_token();\n        compound\n    } else {\n        bare\n    }\n}\n\n/// Returns the unescaped contents of a string literal, having consumed through the closing\n/// quote. `input` is positioned on the opening `\"` on entry.\nfn read_string(input: &mut Input) -> Result<String, Error> {\n    let open = input.current_token_start();\n    input.next_token();\n    let mut text = String::new();\n    loop {\n        let pos = input.current_token_start();\n        match input.peek_token() {\n            None => return Err(Error::new(Span::new(open, pos), \"unterminated string\")),\n            Some('\"') => {\n                input.next_token();\n                return Ok(text);\n            }\n            // Escapes are JSON's set minus \\u, which waits for a real value model.\n            Some('\\\\') => {\n                let backslash = pos;\n                input.next_token();\n                let Some(esc) = input.peek_token() else {\n                    return Err(Error::new(\n                        Span::new(backslash, backslash + 1),\n                        \"unterminated escape sequence\",\n                    ));\n                };\n                text.push(match esc {\n                    '\"' => '\"',\n                    '\\\\' => '\\\\',\n                    '/' => '/',\n                    'n' => '\\n',\n                    't' => '\\t',\n                    'r' => '\\r',\n                    other => {\n                        return Err(Error::new(\n                            Span::new(backslash, backslash + 2),\n                            format!(\"unknown escape sequence `\\\\{other}`\"),\n                        ));\n                    }\n                });\n                input.next_token();\n            }\n            Some(c) => {\n                text.push(c);\n                input.next_token();\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Which node an infix token builds. `and` and `or` are `ast::LogicOp`, not `BinOp`, so the\n/// table has to name the kind as well as the binding power.\nenum Infix {\n    Bin(BinOp),\n    Logic(LogicOp),\n}\n\n/// Left and right binding power. Left below right makes the operator left-associative.\n///\n/// `|` sits below comparison so that `a | select(. >= 2)` splits at the pipe, which is the\n/// ordering jq uses and the reason this table exists rather than a nest of functions.\nfn infix_power(tok: &Tok) -> Option<(Infix, u8, u8)> {\n    let (op, left, right) = match tok {\n        Tok::Or => (Infix::Logic(LogicOp::Or), OR_LEFT, OR_RIGHT),\n        Tok::And => (Infix::Logic(LogicOp::And), 6, 7),\n        Tok::EqEq => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Eq), 8, 9),\n        Tok::Ne => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Ne), 8, 9),\n        Tok::Lt => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Lt), 8, 9),\n        Tok::Le => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Le), 8, 9),\n        Tok::Gt => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Gt), 8, 9),\n        Tok::Ge => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Ge), 8, 9),\n        Tok::Plus => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Add), 10, 11),\n        Tok::Minus => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Sub), 10, 11),\n        Tok::Star => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Mul), 12, 13),\n        Tok::Slash => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Div), 12, 13),\n        Tok::Percent => (Infix::Bin(BinOp::Rem), 12, 13),\n        _ => return None,\n    };\n    Some((op, left, right))\n}\n\nconst PIPE_LEFT: u8 = 1;\nconst PIPE_RIGHT: u8 = 2;\n\n/// Where a match arm's body and its guard operand are read and printed: between `|` (1/2) and\n/// the Bool connectives (`or` at 4/5). An arm body read here stops at the separator `or` and\n/// `->`, which is what lets a chain be one pipe stage; a guard operand read here still gets the\n/// Bool `or` above it, so `a == 1 or b == 2 -> x` is one two-clause guard.\nconst COND_POWER: u8 = 3;\n\n/// Bool `or`, the loosest operator a Bool expression is built from: `a == 1 or b == 2` is one\n/// disjunction of two comparisons. Its *other* reading, the match-arm separator, has no power\n/// at all -- it is not an operator there but the chain's own punctuation, and it binds looser\n/// than everything, which is exactly why it cannot be one table entry with this (draft.md, the\n/// match-arms decision).\nconst OR_LEFT: u8 = 4;\nconst OR_RIGHT: u8 = 5;\n\n/// `not` sits directly below comparison, so `not a == b` negates the comparison rather than\n/// `a`, and above `and`, so `not a and b` negates only `a`. Python's ordering; the alternative,\n/// binding it as tight as unary `-`, makes every use of it against a comparison need parens.\nconst NOT_POWER: u8 = 8;\n\npub fn parse(src: &str) -> Result<File, Error> {\n    let mut p = Cursor {\n        input: LocatingSlice::new(src),\n        src,\n        declined_cross_line: None,\n        or_separates: false,\n    };\n\n    // Declarations in any order and any mix, since no kind can refer to another's position:\n    // aliases and enums are resolved before any signature is read.\n    let mut defs = Vec::new();\n    let mut aliases = Vec::new();\n    let mut enums = Vec::new();\n    let mut traits = Vec::new();\n    let mut impls = Vec::new();\n    loop {\n        let (tok, _) = p.peek()?;\n        match tok {\n            Tok::Pub => {\n                p.advance()?;\n                match p.peek()?.0 {\n                    Tok::Enum => enums.push(p.enum_decl(true)?),\n                    Tok::Trait => traits.push(p.trait_decl(true)?),\n                    _ => defs.push(p.def(true)?),\n                }\n            }\n            Tok::Fn => defs.push(p.def(false)?),\n            Tok::Type => aliases.push(p.alias()?),\n            Tok::Enum => enums.push(p.enum_decl(false)?),\n            Tok::Trait => traits.push(p.trait_decl(false)?),\n            Tok::Impl => impls.push(p.impl_decl()?),\n            _ => break,\n        }\n    }\n\n    let body = p.tail_pipe()?;\n    let (rest, rest_span) = p.peek()?;\n    if rest != Tok::Eof {\n        return Err(p.unexpected(rest_span, format!(\"expected end of program, found {rest}\")));\n    }\n    Ok(File {\n        aliases,\n        enums,\n        traits,\n        impls,\n        defs,\n        body,\n    })\n}\n\n/// A module is declarations only, with no trailing expression -- there is nothing here to run,\n/// only names for a program to import. `pub` marks which ones a program actually receives.\npub fn parse_module(src: &str) -> Result<Module, Error> {\n    let mut p = Cursor {\n        input: LocatingSlice::new(src),\n        src,\n        declined_cross_line: None,\n        or_separates: false,\n    };\n    let mut defs = Vec::new();\n    let mut enums = Vec::new();\n    loop {\n        let (tok, span) = p.peek()?;\n        match tok {\n            Tok::Pub => {\n                p.advance()?;\n                match p.peek()?.0 {\n                    Tok::Enum => enums.push(p.enum_decl(true)?),\n                    _ => defs.push(p.def(true)?),\n                }\n            }\n            Tok::Fn => defs.push(p.def(false)?),\n            Tok::Enum => enums.push(p.enum_decl(false)?),\n            Tok::Eof => break,\n            other => {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    span,\n                    format!(\"expected `fn` or end of module, found {other}\"),\n                ));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    Ok(Module { defs, enums })\n}\n\nstruct Cursor<'i> {\n    input: Input<'i>,\n    /// The full source, kept alongside the advancing `input` so the same-line rule can look at\n    /// the trivia between two tokens it already has spans for.\n    src: &'i str,\n    /// Where a call was declined because its argument started on a later line: the argument\n    /// token's start, and the callee's name. Declining is usually right -- a definition's body\n    /// and the program's body sit adjacent with nothing between them, and the line break is what\n    /// keeps one from swallowing the other -- but when the parse then fails at exactly the\n    /// declined token, the intended reading was probably the call, and the error can name the\n    /// parens spelling that works across lines.\n    declined_cross_line: Option<(usize, String)>,\n    /// Which of `or`'s two readings is in force here: the match-arm separator (true), or Bool\n    /// disjunction (false). True only inside an arm's body, where the separator has to win so\n    /// that `a -> b or c` is two arms; every fresh expression position below that -- a\n    /// parenthesized group, a call argument, an arm's own guard -- puts it back to false, since\n    /// nothing there is a chain's top level. This is the split kantord/toylang#96 set out to\n    /// prove clean, and the one place a token's meaning depends on where it is read.\n    or_separates: bool,\n}\n\nimpl<'i> Cursor<'i> {\n    /// Reads the next token without consuming it. `Input` is `Copy` (a source `&str` plus an\n    /// offset), so looking ahead is just tokenizing a throwaway copy of the cursor.\n    fn peek(&self) -> Result<(Tok, Span), Error> {\n        let mut probe = self.input;\n        read_tok(&mut probe)\n    }\n\n    fn advance(&mut self) -> Result<(Tok, Span), Error> {\n        read_tok(&mut self.input)\n    }\n\n    fn eat(&mut self, want: Tok) -> Result<Span, Error> {\n        let (tok, span) = self.peek()?;\n        if tok != want {\n            return Err(self.unexpected(span, format!(\"expected {want}, found {tok}\")));\n        }\n        self.advance()?;\n        Ok(span)\n    }\n\n    /// An \"unexpected token\" error, extended with the parens spelling when the failing token is\n    /// one a call declined to take as a cross-line argument (see `declined_cross_line`): the\n    /// token had somewhere to go, and only the line break kept it from going there.\n    fn unexpected(&self, span: Span, msg: String) -> Error {\n        if let Some((at, callee)) = &self.declined_cross_line\n            && *at == span.start\n        {\n            return Error::new(\n                span,\n                format!(\n                    \"{msg}; an argument must start on the same line as its function -- \\\n                     write `{callee}(...)` to call across lines\"\n                ),\n            );\n        }\n        Error::new(span, msg)\n    }\n\n    /// Whether nothing but spaces and tabs sits between two byte offsets. The same-line rule for\n    /// call arguments: an argument, bare or parenthesized, must start on the same line as its\n    /// function, which is what lets a definition's body end next to the program's body without\n    /// either reaching across the line break and swallowing the other.\n    fn same_line(&self, from: usize, to: usize) -> bool {\n        !self.src[from..to].contains('\\n')\n    }\n\n    /// The same-line rule applied at one call site: true when the argument starting at\n    /// `arg_span` is on `callee_span`'s line. A cross-line decline is recorded (see\n    /// `declined_cross_line`) so that if the parse then fails at that token, the error can say\n    /// why the call was not taken.\n    fn takes_argument(&mut self, callee: &str, callee_span: Span, arg_span: Span) -> bool {\n        if self.same_line(callee_span.end, arg_span.start) {\n            return true;\n        }\n        self.declined_cross_line = Some((arg_span.start, callee.to_string()));\n        false\n    }\n\n    /// Runs `f` with `or` reading as `separates` says, restoring the caller's reading after.\n    fn with_or<T>(\n        &mut self,\n        separates: bool,\n        f: impl FnOnce(&mut Self) -> Result<T, Error>,\n    ) -> Result<T, Error> {\n        let outer = std::mem::replace(&mut self.or_separates, separates);\n        let out = f(self);\n        self.or_separates = outer;\n        out\n    }\n\n    fn eat_ident(&mut self, what: &str) -> Result<(String, Span), Error> {\n        match self.advance()? {\n            (Tok::Ident(n), span) => Ok((n, span)),\n            (other, span) => Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"expected {what}, found {other}\"))),\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// What the parens hold in a function signature: nothing, `name: Type`, or\n    /// `{a, b, ..}: Type`, the destructuring spelling reusing a match arm's brace pattern.\n    fn param(&mut self) -> Result<Option<Param>, Error> {\n        let (next, _) = self.peek()?;\n        if next == Tok::RParen {\n            return Ok(None);\n        }\n        let shape = if next == Tok::LBrace {\n            ParamShape::Fields(self.fields_pattern()?)\n        } else {\n            let (param_name, param_span) = self.eat_ident(\"a name\")?;\n            ParamShape::Name(param_name, param_span)\n        };\n        // Both spellings are followed by `: Type`; the Name case checks for the `:` before\n        // parsing anything, and the Fields case must consume it too or the type parser meets it\n        // first.\n        let (colon, _) = self.peek()?;\n        if colon != Tok::Colon {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                shape.span(),\n                match &shape {\n                    ParamShape::Name(name, _) => {\n                        format!(\"parameter `{name}` needs a type annotation\")\n                    }\n                    ParamShape::Fields(_) => {\n                        \"a destructured parameter needs a type annotation\".to_string()\n                    }\n                },\n            ));\n        }\n        self.advance()?;\n        let param_ty = self.type_expr()?;\n        Ok(Some(Param {\n            span: shape.span().to(param_ty.span()),\n            shape,\n            ty: param_ty,\n        }))\n    }\n\n    /// `fn name(param: Type) -> Type = body`, `fn name() -> Type = body`, or the hoisted\n    /// `fn name = body` (gh:152) with no parameter list and no return annotation.\n    ///\n    /// The annotated forms require both annotations by the grammar rather than by the checker,\n    /// which is what makes the message point at the missing annotation instead of at an\n    /// inference failure. The hoisted form is the opposite: `=` follows the name directly, and\n    /// the whole signature -- parameter and return -- is inferred from the body, whose first\n    /// token must be a match call that names the enum the implicit `.` parameter matches.\n    fn def(&mut self, is_pub: bool) -> Result<Def, Error> {\n        let start = self.eat(Tok::Fn)?;\n        let (name, _) = self.eat_ident(\"a name\")?;\n        let (next, _) = self.peek()?;\n        if next == Tok::Eq {\n            self.advance()?;\n            let body = self.def_body()?;\n            return Ok(Def {\n                span: start.to(body.span()),\n                name,\n                param: None,\n                ret: None,\n                body,\n                is_pub,\n                origin: crate::ast::Origin::Program,\n                hoisted: true,\n            });\n        }\n        self.eat(Tok::LParen)?;\n\n        let param = self.param()?;\n\n        let close = self.eat(Tok::RParen)?;\n        let (arrow, _) = self.peek()?;\n        if arrow != Tok::Arrow {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                close,\n                format!(\"function `{name}` needs a return type\"),\n            ));\n        }\n        self.advance()?;\n        let ret = self.type_expr()?;\n\n        self.eat(Tok::Eq)?;\n        let body = self.def_body()?;\n        Ok(Def {\n            span: start.to(body.span()),\n            name,\n            param,\n            ret: Some(ret),\n            body,\n            is_pub,\n            origin: crate::ast::Origin::Program,\n            hoisted: false,\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// `fn name(param: Type) -> Type`,the signature spine a trait method and an impl method\n    /// share. `what` names the kind in the return-type error;the caller decides whether a\n    /// `= body` follows the signature. A function's own spine stays inline in `def`.\n    fn fn_signature(\n        &mut self,\n        what: &str,\n    ) -> Result<(String, Option<Param>, TypeExpr, Span), Error> {\n        let start = self.eat(Tok::Fn)?;\n        let (name, _) = self.eat_ident(\"a name\")?;\n        self.eat(Tok::LParen)?;\n\n        let param = self.param()?;\n\n        let close = self.eat(Tok::RParen)?;\n        let (arrow, _) = self.peek()?;\n        if arrow != Tok::Arrow {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                close,\n                format!(\"{what} `{name}` needs a return type\"),\n            ));\n        }\n        self.advance()?;\n        let ret = self.type_expr()?;\n        let span = start.to(ret.span());\n        Ok((name, param, ret, span))\n    }\n\n    /// `trait Name { fn sig(param: Type) -> Type, ... }`:a named collection of method\n    /// signatures with no bodies;an `impl` block provides those per concrete type. `pub` marks\n    /// it for a module. The body-less `fn` spine is the only thing inside the braces, so no\n    /// separator token is needed: each method starts with its own `fn`.\n    fn trait_decl(&mut self, is_pub: bool) -> Result<TraitDecl, Error> {\n        let start = self.eat(Tok::Trait)?;\n        let (name, _) = self.eat_ident(\"a trait name\")?;\n        self.eat(Tok::LBrace)?;\n        let mut methods = Vec::new();\n        let (first, _) = self.peek()?;\n        if first != Tok::RBrace {\n            loop {\n                let (mname, param, ret, span) = self.fn_signature(\"trait method\")?;\n                methods.push(TraitMethodSig {\n                    name: mname,\n                    param,\n                    ret,\n                    span,\n                });\n                if self.peek()?.0 == Tok::RBrace {\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        let close = self.eat(Tok::RBrace)?;\n        Ok(TraitDecl {\n            name,\n            methods,\n            span: start.to(close),\n            is_pub,\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// `impl Trait for Type { fn sig(param: Type) -> Type = body, ... }`:concrete bodies\n    /// for one trait's methods, one block per (trait, type) pair. Like a trait's, each method\n    /// starts with its own `fn`, so the methods need no separator token between them.\n    fn impl_decl(&mut self) -> Result<ImplDecl, Error> {\n        let start = self.eat(Tok::Impl)?;\n        let (trait_name, _) = self.eat_ident(\"a trait name\")?;\n        let (link, link_span) = self.eat_ident(\"`for`\")?;\n        if link != \"for\" {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                link_span,\n                format!(\n                    \"expected `for` between the trait name and its target type, found `{link}`\"\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n        let ty = self.type_expr()?;\n        self.eat(Tok::LBrace)?;\n        let mut methods = Vec::new();\n        let (first, _) = self.peek()?;\n        if first != Tok::RBrace {\n            loop {\n                let (mname, param, ret, sig_span) = self.fn_signature(\"impl method\")?;\n                self.eat(Tok::Eq)?;\n                let body = self.def_body()?;\n                let span = sig_span.to(body.span());\n                methods.push(ImplMethod {\n                    name: mname,\n                    param,\n                    ret,\n                    body,\n                    span,\n                });\n                if self.peek()?.0 == Tok::RBrace {\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        let close = self.eat(Tok::RBrace)?;\n        Ok(ImplDecl {\n            trait_name,\n            ty,\n            methods,\n            span: start.to(close),\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// A function's body. An ordinary expression, or -- when the `let` keyword opens it -- the\n    /// local-binding block the ruling on #87 settled on: `let <name> = <expr>` one per line,\n    /// then the expression that ends the block, with no `in` keyword. The line that is not a\n    /// `let` is the value, so each binding's value has to fit on its own line or the next line\n    /// cannot be told apart from the block's result.\n    fn def_body(&mut self) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n        let (first, _) = self.peek()?;\n        if first != Tok::Let {\n            return self.tail_pipe();\n        }\n        let first_let = self.advance()?.1;\n        let mut bindings = Vec::new();\n        let mut binding_start = first_let.start;\n\n        loop {\n            let (name, _) = self.eat_ident(\"a binding name\")?;\n            self.eat(Tok::Eq)?;\n            let value = self.expr(0)?;\n            if !self.same_line(binding_start, value.span().end) {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    value.span(),\n                    \"a `let` binding must fit on its line; write the whole `let <name> = <expr>` \\\n                     on one line and let the next line be another `let` or the block's value\"\n                        .to_string(),\n                ));\n            }\n            bindings.push((name, value));\n            if self.peek()?.0 == Tok::Let {\n                binding_start = self.advance()?.1.start;\n\n                continue;\n            }\n            break;\n        }\n        let body = self.expr(0)?;\n        let span = first_let.to(body.span());\n        Ok(Expr::Let {\n            bindings,\n            body: Box::new(body),\n            span,\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// `Str`, `Int`, `Bool`, `Vec<T>`, `Stream<T>`, or any declared name -- with\n    /// `<...>` arguments when the name is a generic enum's. The parser accepts arguments on\n    /// every name and lets resolution hold the arity, so `Pair` and `Str<Int>` fail with an\n    /// error that knows what `Pair` and `Str` are rather than a parse error that does not.\n    fn type_expr(&mut self) -> Result<TypeExpr, Error> {\n        let (tok, span) = self.advance()?;\n        if tok == Tok::LBrace {\n            return self.record_type(span);\n        }\n        let name = match tok {\n            Tok::Ident(n) => n,\n            other => return Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"expected a type, found {other}\"))),\n        };\n        if self.peek()?.0 != Tok::Lt {\n            return Ok(TypeExpr::Named {\n                name,\n                args: Vec::new(),\n                span,\n            });\n        }\n        self.advance()?;\n        let mut args = Vec::new();\n        loop {\n            args.push(self.type_expr()?);\n            let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n            if sep != Tok::Comma {\n                break;\n            }\n            self.advance()?;\n        }\n        let close = self.eat(Tok::Gt)?;\n        let span = span.to(close);\n        // The two built-in constructors keep their own nodes; their arity is the grammar's.\n        if ty::takes_type_arg(&name) {\n            if args.len() != 1 {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    span,\n                    format!(\"`{name}` takes one type argument, found {}\", args.len()),\n                ));\n            }\n            let elem = Box::new(args.remove(0));\n            return Ok(match name.as_str() {\n                \"Vec\" => TypeExpr::Vec { elem, span },\n                _ => TypeExpr::Stream { elem, span },\n            });\n        }\n        Ok(TypeExpr::Named { name, args, span })\n    }\n\n    /// A call's argument when it opens with `(` or `{`: a parenthesized expression, or a record\n    /// literal standing bare because `{` cannot start any other expression. The closing token\n    /// marks the argument's end unambiguously, which is what makes the parens form the\n    /// disambiguator wherever the bare form's own stopping rules would read a program\n    /// differently.\n    fn argument(&mut self) -> Result<(Expr, Span), Error> {\n        let (tok, span) = self.peek()?;\n        if tok == Tok::LBrace {\n            self.advance()?;\n            let lit = self.record_lit(span)?;\n            let lit_span = lit.span();\n            return Ok((lit, lit_span));\n        }\n        self.eat(Tok::LParen)?;\n        let inner = self.expr(0)?;\n        let close = self.eat(Tok::RParen)?;\n        Ok((inner, close))\n    }\n\n    /// `{name: expr, age: expr}`, the value form of the brace that `record_type` reads in type\n    /// position.\n    fn record_lit(&mut self, open: Span) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n        let mut fields = Vec::new();\n        let (first, _) = self.peek()?;\n        if first != Tok::RBrace {\n            loop {\n                let (name, name_span) = self.eat_ident(\"a field name\")?;\n                self.eat(Tok::Colon)?;\n                fields.push((name, name_span, self.expr(0)?));\n                let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n                if sep != Tok::Comma {\n                    break;\n                }\n                self.advance()?;\n            }\n        }\n        let close = self.eat(Tok::RBrace)?;\n        Ok(Expr::RecordLit {\n            fields,\n            span: open.to(close),\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// `enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int}, celsius(Int) }`, optionally with type\n    /// parameters: `enum Opt<T> { some(T), none }`.\n    ///\n    /// A variant is a name, optionally followed by its payload type. The payload rule is any\n    /// single type, spelled the way a call spells its argument: a record type directly in\n    /// braces, or any type in parens.\n    fn enum_decl(&mut self, is_pub: bool) -> Result<EnumDecl, Error> {\n        let start = self.eat(Tok::Enum)?;\n        let (name, _) = self.eat_ident(\"an enum name\")?;\n        let mut params = Vec::new();\n        if self.peek()?.0 == Tok::Lt {\n            self.advance()?;\n            loop {\n                params.push(self.eat_ident(\"a type parameter\")?);\n                let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n                if sep != Tok::Comma {\n                    break;\n                }\n                self.advance()?;\n            }\n            self.eat(Tok::Gt)?;\n        }\n        self.eat(Tok::LBrace)?;\n        let mut variants = Vec::new();\n        let (first, _) = self.peek()?;\n        if first != Tok::RBrace {\n            loop {\n                let (vname, vspan) = self.eat_ident(\"a variant name\")?;\n                let payload = match self.peek()? {\n                    (Tok::LBrace, open) => {\n                        self.advance()?;\n                        Some(self.record_type(open)?)\n                    }\n                    (Tok::LParen, _) => {\n                        self.advance()?;\n                        let ty = self.type_expr()?;\n                        self.eat(Tok::RParen)?;\n                        Some(ty)\n                    }\n                    _ => None,\n                };\n                variants.push(Variant {\n                    name: vname,\n                    span: vspan,\n                    payload,\n                });\n                let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n                if sep != Tok::Comma {\n                    break;\n                }\n                self.advance()?;\n            }\n        }\n        let close = self.eat(Tok::RBrace)?;\n        Ok(EnumDecl {\n            name,\n            params,\n            variants,\n            span: start.to(close),\n            is_pub,\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// `type Db = {users: Vec<User>}`\n    fn alias(&mut self) -> Result<Alias, Error> {\n        let start = self.eat(Tok::Type)?;\n        let (name, _) = self.eat_ident(\"a type name\")?;\n        self.eat(Tok::Eq)?;\n        let ty = self.type_expr()?;\n        let span = start.to(ty.span());\n        Ok(Alias { name, ty, span })\n    }\n\n    fn record_type(&mut self, open: Span) -> Result<TypeExpr, Error> {\n        let mut fields = Vec::new();\n        let (first, _) = self.peek()?;\n        if first != Tok::RBrace {\n            loop {\n                let (fname, _) = self.eat_ident(\"a field name\")?;\n                self.eat(Tok::Colon)?;\n                fields.push((fname, self.type_expr()?));\n                let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n                if sep != Tok::Comma {\n                    break;\n                }\n                self.advance()?;\n            }\n        }\n        let close = self.eat(Tok::RBrace)?;\n        Ok(TypeExpr::Record {\n            fields,\n            span: open.to(close),\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// Whether the tokens ahead begin a match arm: `name ->`, `name{a, b} ->`, or `any() ->`.\n    /// The one place this has to look carefully is a brace: a record *pattern* holds bare names\n    /// (and `..`), so the first `:` proves the braces are a constructor's record literal\n    /// argument instead. A lexical error while probing is not an arm; the ordinary path will\n    /// surface it.\n    fn arm_starts_here(&self) -> bool {\n        let mut probe = self.input;\n        let mut next = || read_tok(&mut probe).map(|(t, _)| t);\n        let Ok(Tok::Ident(name)) = next() else {\n            return false;\n        };\n        match next() {\n            Ok(Tok::Arrow) => true,\n            Ok(Tok::LParen) if name == \"any\" => {\n                matches!(next(), Ok(Tok::RParen)) && matches!(next(), Ok(Tok::Arrow))\n            }\n            Ok(Tok::LBrace) => {\n                loop {\n                    match next() {\n                        Ok(Tok::Ident(_) | Tok::Comma | Tok::Dot) => continue,\n                        Ok(Tok::RBrace) => break,\n                        _ => return false,\n                    }\n                }\n                matches!(next(), Ok(Tok::Arrow))\n            }\n            _ => false,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// `left -> body or left -> body or ...`, first match wins; `or` is the one arm composer.\n    /// A left side is a variant pattern (`circle{r}`), `any()`, or a Bool guard expression; the\n    /// chain's final element may instead be a bare expression, the default. The chain reads `.`\n    /// as its subject, so it usually sits to the right of a `|`; each body stops at `|` and\n    /// `or`, which is what lets the chain be one pipe stage.\n    ///\n    /// The two `or`s meet here and nowhere else. A body is read with the separator reading, so\n    /// it ends at the next `or`; an element's own left side is read with the disjunction one, so\n    /// `a -> 1 or b == 2 or c == 3 -> 4` is two arms whose second has a two-clause guard. What\n    /// makes that split decidable is that an element ending at a bare `or` could only ever have\n    /// been a bare default in a non-final position, which was already an error.\n    ///\n    /// `lead` is an expression `expr` had already read when the `->` after it revealed a chain;\n    /// the first element continues from it instead of parsing fresh.\n    fn match_expr(&mut self, lead: Option<Expr>) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n        let mut arms = Vec::new();\n        let mut lead = lead;\n        loop {\n            let arm = match lead.take() {\n                Some(e) => self.match_arm(Some(e))?,\n                None => self.match_arm(None)?,\n            };\n            arms.push(arm);\n            let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n            if sep != Tok::Or {\n                break;\n            }\n            self.advance()?;\n        }\n        let span = arms[0].span.to(arms[arms.len() - 1].span);\n        Ok(Expr::Match { arms, span })\n    }\n\n    /// One `or`-separated element of an arm chain: a variant pattern arm, or (when `lead` is a\n    /// guard the caller already read, or no pattern begins the element) a guard or default arm.\n    /// Shared by a `Match` and a `MatchCall` (gh:152), which differ only in what heads them and\n    /// how the chain ends.\n    fn match_arm(&mut self, lead: Option<Expr>) -> Result<MatchArm, Error> {\n        match lead {\n            Some(e) => self.guard_or_default_arm(e),\n            None if self.arm_starts_here() => {\n                let pattern = self.pattern()?;\n                self.eat(Tok::Arrow)?;\n                let body = self.arm_body()?;\n                let span = pattern.span().to(body.span());\n                Ok(MatchArm {\n                    pattern,\n                    body,\n                    span,\n                })\n            }\n            None => {\n                let e = self.with_or(false, |p| p.operand(COND_POWER))?;\n                self.guard_or_default_arm(e)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// `Msg(Ping -> \"pong\" or Quit -> \"bye\")` (gh:152): a capitalized type name used as a match\n    /// call. The parens hold the same `or`-separated arms a `Match` carries, over the subject\n    /// `.`; the name is the assertion of what the subject is, and the checker resolves each arm\n    /// against it. A capitalized name is always an enum, never a callable, so `Name(...)` is\n    /// unambiguously a match call rather than a function application.\n    fn match_call(&mut self, enum_name: String, enum_span: Span) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n        self.eat(Tok::LParen)?;\n        let mut arms = Vec::new();\n        loop {\n            arms.push(self.match_arm(None)?);\n            let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n            if sep != Tok::Or {\n                break;\n            }\n            self.advance()?;\n        }\n        let close = self.eat(Tok::RParen)?;\n        let span = enum_span.to(close);\n        Ok(Expr::MatchCall {\n            enum_name,\n            enum_span,\n            arms,\n            span,\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// An arm's right side: the one position where a bare `or` is the chain's separator rather\n    /// than disjunction, so a Bool `or` written here needs parens (draft.md, the match-arms\n    /// decision).\n    fn arm_body(&mut self) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n        self.with_or(true, |p| p.expr(COND_POWER))\n    }\n\n    /// The element `left` opens: `left -> body` makes `left` a guard, and a bare `left` is the\n    /// default, desugared to a `Default` arm whose body is `left` itself.\n    fn guard_or_default_arm(&mut self, left: Expr) -> Result<MatchArm, Error> {\n        if self.peek()?.0 == Tok::Arrow {\n            self.advance()?;\n            let body = self.arm_body()?;\n            let span = left.span().to(body.span());\n            return Ok(MatchArm {\n                pattern: Pattern::Guard(left),\n                body,\n                span,\n            });\n        }\n        let span = left.span();\n        Ok(MatchArm {\n            pattern: Pattern::Default { span },\n            body: left,\n            span,\n        })\n    }\n\n    fn pattern(&mut self) -> Result<Pattern, Error> {\n        let (name, span) = self.eat_ident(\"a pattern\")?;\n        let (next, _) = self.peek()?;\n        if name == \"any\" && next == Tok::LParen {\n            self.advance()?;\n            let close = self.eat(Tok::RParen)?;\n            return Ok(Pattern::Default {\n                span: span.to(close),\n            });\n        }\n        if next != Tok::LBrace {\n            return Ok(Pattern::Variant {\n                name,\n                span,\n                fields: None,\n            });\n        }\n        let fields = self.fields_pattern()?;\n        Ok(Pattern::Variant {\n            name,\n            span: span.to(fields.span),\n            fields: Some(fields),\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// `{a, b, ..}`: the brace pattern a match arm and a destructuring parameter share.\n    /// `..` is two Dot tokens, and it ends the list: naming a field after \"and the rest\" would\n    /// make the marker meaningless.\n    fn fields_pattern(&mut self) -> Result<FieldsPattern, Error> {\n        let open = self.eat(Tok::LBrace)?;\n        let mut names = Vec::new();\n        let mut rest = false;\n        let (first, _) = self.peek()?;\n        if first != Tok::RBrace {\n            loop {\n                if self.peek()?.0 == Tok::Dot {\n                    self.advance()?;\n                    self.eat(Tok::Dot)?;\n                    rest = true;\n                    break;\n                }\n                names.push(self.eat_ident(\"a field name\")?);\n                let (sep, _) = self.peek()?;\n                if sep != Tok::Comma {\n                    break;\n                }\n                self.advance()?;\n            }\n        }\n        let close = self.eat(Tok::RBrace)?;\n        Ok(FieldsPattern {\n            names,\n            rest,\n            span: open.to(close),\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// Arm chains begin only where an expression begins fresh (a pipe stage, a delimited\n    /// position), and a fresh position is also where `or` goes back to reading as disjunction:\n    /// whatever chain the enclosing arm body belonged to, this expression is not part of it. An\n    /// arm body enters at COND_POWER instead and leaves any `->` or separator `or` it meets for\n    /// the chain that owns it, rather than opening a nested chain that would swallow the rest of\n    /// the outer one.\n    fn expr(&mut self, min_power: u8) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n        if min_power <= PIPE_RIGHT {\n            return self.with_or(false, |p| p.expr_at(min_power, true));\n        }\n        self.expr_at(min_power, false)\n    }\n\n    fn expr_at(&mut self, min_power: u8, fresh: bool) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n        let mut lhs = if fresh && self.arm_starts_here() {\n            self.match_expr(None)?\n        } else {\n            let e = self.operand(min_power)?;\n            let (tok, _) = self.peek()?;\n            // No `Tok::Or` here: a fresh position reads `or` as disjunction, so `operand` has\n            // already taken any that were there, and an arm chain is revealed by the `->` that\n            // follows the whole disjunction (`a == 1 or b == 2 -> ...` is one guard).\n            if fresh && tok == Tok::Arrow {\n                self.match_expr(Some(e))?\n            } else {\n                e\n            }\n        };\n\n        loop {\n            let (tok, _) = self.peek()?;\n            if tok != Tok::Pipe || PIPE_LEFT < min_power {\n                break;\n            }\n            self.advance()?;\n            let rhs = self.expr(PIPE_RIGHT)?;\n            let span = lhs.span().to(rhs.span());\n            lhs = Expr::Pipe {\n                lhs: Box::new(lhs),\n                rhs: Box::new(rhs),\n                span,\n            };\n        }\n\n        Ok(lhs)\n    }\n\n    /// The program's body (or a function's, when it is not a `let` block): an ordinary\n    /// expression, or the tail-pipeline `lhs |> callee` that is the one way a sink is written.\n    /// Parsed only here, at the outermost position, so a nested `|>` is a parse error rather\n    /// than a value -- the checker's sink position rule is this production's, not a search for\n    /// a stray token somewhere inside a larger expression.\n    fn tail_pipe(&mut self) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n        let lhs = self.expr(0)?;\n        let (tok, _) = self.peek()?;\n        if tok != Tok::PipeGt {\n            return Ok(lhs);\n        }\n        self.advance()?;\n        let (callee, callee_span) = self.eat_ident(\"a sink call\")?;\n        let span = lhs.span().to(callee_span);\n        Ok(Expr::TailPipe {\n            lhs: Box::new(lhs),\n            callee,\n            callee_span,\n            span,\n        })\n    }\n\n    fn operand(&mut self, min_power: u8) -> Result<Expr, Error> {\n        let mut lhs = self.complement(min_power)?;\n\n        loop {\n            let (tok, _) = self.peek()?;\n            // Where `or` is the arm separator it is not an operator at all, so the table is not\n            // even consulted: the chain the arm belongs to takes the token.\n            if tok == Tok::Or && self.or_separates {\n                break;\n            }\n            let Some((op, left, right)) = infix_power(&tok) else {\n                break;\n            };\n            if left < min_power {\n                break;\n            }\n            self.advance()?;\n            let rhs = self.operand(right)?;\n            let span = lhs.span().to(rhs.span());\n            lhs = match op {\n                Infix::Bin(op) => Expr::Binary {\n                    op,\n                    lhs: Box::new(lhs),\n                    rhs: Box::new(rhs),\n                    span,\n                },\n                Infix::Logic(op) => Expr::Logic {\n                    op,\n                    lhs: Box::new(lhs),\n                    rhs: Box::new(rhs),\n                    span,\n                },\n            };\n        }\n\n        Ok(lhs)\n    }\n\n    /// `not`, which is prefix but not unary in `-`'s sense: it binds looser than every operator\n    /// in the infix table above comparison, so it reads its own operand at its own power rather\n    /// than taking whatever `unary` returns. A position that binds tighter than `not` (a\n    /// comparison's right side, say) declines it, and parens are what reach in there.\n    fn complement(&mut self, min_power: u8) -> Result<Expr, Error> {","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":1361,"totalLines":1362,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/parse.rs","time":{"start":1788705136147,"end":1788705136155}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the basic structure. The Origin enum has `Program` and `Prelude`. Issue 166 landed the file-visibility tracking (Origin enum). Now I need to implement the `@(path)` module-as-function routing-arm syntax.\n\nLet me understand the design:\n- Option C: a dedicated routing-arm form for matcher arms dispatching to a submodule file, spelled `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` (parenthesized).\n- The arm's body is `@(path)`.\n- The whole file is the handler: the entry function of that module, applied to the subject. The path resolves relative to the referring file at compile time; the checker loads the module, reads `handle`'s signature, and checks it against the subject type and the arm's expected type, the way it checks any call. `$` is free in toylang source today. The cost is an entry-point convention (`handle`).\n\nWait, but the issue says `@(path)` module-as-function. The research doc's candidate 3 was bare `@path`, but the maintainer refined it to `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` (parenthesized, because it encloses the path expression).\n\nSo the syntax is: an arm body can be `@(path)` where path is a relative file path (relative to the referring file). The module file has an entry function `handle` with signature `fn handle(req: Request) -> Response`, applied to the subject `.`.\n\nNow I need to understand:\n1. How the parser works for match arms and their bodies.\n\n2. How the checker checks a call (signature resolution).\n3. How `parse_module` works.\n4. How the checker currently handles origin visibility.\n\nLet me read the parse.rs (match arms, module parsing), and check/mod.rs (the checker.\n\nLet me also look at the tests to understand how corpus cases work. And look at how main.rs reads files, since now I need path resolution relative to the referring file.\n\nThe AST nodes for match arm bodies are `MatchArm { pattern, body }` where body is an Expr. Now the new arm body kind `@(path)` needs a new AST node, e.g. `Expr::ModuleDispatch { path: String, span: Span }`.\n\nWait -- the arm body needs to be an Expr. Let me think about the design more carefully.\n\n\n\nLet me read the parse.rs file, focusing on match parsing and module parsing.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_077225dcf0011QiLT6hllTJLF4","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_077224508001SU6QmdjzWiTWh6"}}
