{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705160867,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_549ec0bfb16b4f6ba9859d80","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/check/mod.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/check/mod.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: use std::cell::{Cell, RefCell};\n2: use std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet};\n3: \n4: use crate::ast::{BinOp, Def, Expr, FieldsPattern, File, MatchArm, Origin, Param, ParamShape, Pattern, Span};\n5: use crate::error::Error;\n6: use crate::tir::{self, Kind, LocalId, Tir};\n7: use crate::ty::{self, Sig, Type};\n8: \n9: mod linearity;\n10: mod types;\n11: \n12: use linearity::{StreamBinding, check_linear, field_used, local_used, param_used, prune_unreachable};\n13: use types::{\n14:     TypeEnv, alias_map, constructor_of, enum_map, is_constructor_of, matcher_of, resolve,\n15:     resolve_enum, signatures,\n16: };\n17: \n18: struct Ctx<'a> {\n19:     sigs: &'a HashMap<String, Sig>,\n20:     /// Every declared enum, resolved. Keyed by name, which is the identity.\n21:     enums: &'a HashMap<String, Type>,\n22:     /// Which enums declare each variant name, in declaration order. A bare variant use resolves\n23:     /// through this while exactly one enum claims the name; two claimants are a loud error\n24:     /// naming both, with `Shape.circle` as the qualified way out.\n25:     variant_owners: &'a HashMap<String, Vec<String>>,\n26:     /// Named bindings, innermost last. A function parameter carries `None` for its local (the\n27:     /// backends render a param by name); a `let` binding carries the `LocalId` it was bound to,\n28:     /// so reading the name is reading that local. `let` can stack many of these, and a later one\n29:     /// shadows an earlier, which is what the reverse search in `synth` is for.\n30:     scope: Vec<(String, Type, Option<LocalId>)>,\n31:     /// Names a match arm's record pattern bound, innermost last: reading one reads that field\n32:     /// off the arm's payload local, so nothing beyond ordinary `Field` nodes reaches the\n33:     /// backends. Each entry is the bound name, the payload's record type, and its local.\n34:     arm_fields: Vec<(String, Type, LocalId)>,\n35:     /// What `.` refers to here, if anything: its type and the local holding it.\n36:     subject: Option<(Type, LocalId)>,\n37:     /// The type `input` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used.\n38:     input: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n39:     /// The element type `inputs` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used. A\n40:     /// separate cell from `input`'s: one records a whole value's type, the other one line's.\n41:     inputs: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n42:     /// Whether `lines` has already been read. There is only ever one real stdin, so a second\n43:     /// read is refused rather than silently handed nothing, the way a second pass over an\n44:     /// already-consumed iterator would be in a language that let it compile.\n45:     lines_used: &'a Cell<bool>,\n46:     /// The delimiter `dsv` was read with, filled in the first time it is used. `csv`/`tsv`\n47:     /// arrive here with the delimiter already fixed by the parser. A second read of any kind\n48:     /// is refused the same way a second `lines` is.\n49:     dsv: &'a RefCell<Option<String>>,\n50:     /// Whether checking is inside a mapper's body (`map`'s, or `select`'s predicate), which\n51:     /// runs once per element: a source read there would drain stdin on the first element and\n52:     /// hand every later one nothing, so `lines` and `inputs` are refused in that position.\n53:     in_mapper: bool,\n54:     /// The name of the `fn` whose body is being checked; `None` in the program's own body. A\n55:     /// source is legal only in the program body (see `source_in_fn`), so `lines` and `inputs`\n56:     /// are refused whenever this is set.\n57:     in_fn: Option<&'a str>,\n58:     /// Every user function, keyed by name, with the file it was defined in and whether it is\n59:     /// `pub`. A call to a non-`pub` one from a different file than `file` is refused, which is\n60:     /// the visibility rule (gh:166).\n61:     visibility: &'a HashMap<String, (Origin, bool)>,\n62:     /// Which file the code being checked was written in. The program's body and definitions are\n63:     /// `Program`; the prelude's definitions (checked at build time) are `Prelude`.\n64:     file: Origin,\n65:     next_local: &'a Cell<LocalId>,\n66: }\n67: \n68: impl Ctx<'_> {\n69:     fn with(&self, subject: Option<(Type, LocalId)>) -> Ctx<'_> {\n70:         self.rebuild(self.scope.clone(), subject)\n71:     }\n72: \n73:     fn rebuild(\n74:         &self,\n75:         scope: Vec<(String, Type, Option<LocalId>)>,\n76:         subject: Option<(Type, LocalId)>,\n77:     ) -> Ctx<'_> {\n78:         Ctx {\n79:             sigs: self.sigs,\n80:             enums: self.enums,\n81:             variant_owners: self.variant_owners,\n82:             scope,\n83:             arm_fields: self.arm_fields.clone(),\n84:             subject,\n85:             input: self.input,\n86:             inputs: self.inputs,\n87:             lines_used: self.lines_used,\n88:             dsv: self.dsv,\n89:             in_mapper: self.in_mapper,\n90:             in_fn: self.in_fn,\n91:             visibility: self.visibility,\n92:             file: self.file,\n93:             next_local: self.next_local,\n94:         }\n95:     }\n96: \n97:     fn fresh(&self) -> LocalId {\n98:         let id = self.next_local.get();\n99:         self.next_local.set(id + 1);\n100:         id\n101:     }\n102: }\n103: \n104: pub fn check(file: &File) -> Result<tir::Program, Error> {\n105:     let lines_used = Cell::new(false);\n106:     let aliases = alias_map(&file.aliases)?;\n107:     let env = TypeEnv {\n108:         aliases,\n109:         enums: enum_map(&file.enums)?,\n110:     };\n111:     for e in &file.enums {\n112:         if env.aliases.contains_key(&e.name) {\n113:             return Err(Error::new(\n114:                 e.span,\n115:                 format!(\"type `{}` is defined twice\", e.name),\n116:             ));\n117:         }\n118:     }\n119:     // Resolved eagerly so a broken declaration is an error even when nothing uses it, and so a\n120:     // cycle is found here rather than wherever it happened to be reached from.\n121:     for a in &file.aliases {\n122:         resolve(&a.ty, &env, &mut vec![(a.name.clone(), Vec::new())])?;\n123:     }\n124:     let mut enums: HashMap<String, Type> = HashMap::new();\n125:     for e in &file.enums {\n126:         enums.insert(\n127:             e.name.clone(),\n128:             resolve_enum(e, &env, &mut Vec::new(), None)?,\n129:         );\n130:     }\n131:     let mut variant_owners: HashMap<String, Vec<String>> = HashMap::new();\n132:     for e in &file.enums {\n133:         for v in &e.variants {\n134:             variant_owners\n135:                 .entry(v.name.clone())\n136:                 .or_default()\n137:                 .push(e.name.clone());\n138:             // The lowercase spelling is the constructor, the value built right away (gh:156):\n139:             // a bare lowercase variant name resolves to the same owner so it reaches `construct`,\n140:             // which maps it to the declared variant. A declared name already lowercase (the\n141:             // prelude's `Opt`/`Result`) has the same constructor spelling, so pushing again would\n142:             // make one owner look like two.\n143:             let constructor = constructor_of(&v.name);\n144:             if constructor != v.name {\n145:                 variant_owners\n146:                     .entry(constructor)\n147:                     .or_default()\n148:                     .push(e.name.clone());\n149:             }\n150:         }\n151:     }\n152:     let mut sigs = signatures(&file.defs, &env)?;\n153:     let input = RefCell::new(None);\n154:     let inputs = RefCell::new(None);\n155:     let dsv = RefCell::new(None);\n156:     let next_local = Cell::new(0);\n157:     let visibility: HashMap<String, (Origin, bool)> = file\n158:         .defs\n159:         .iter()\n160:         .map(|d| (d.name.clone(), (d.origin, d.is_pub)))\n161:         .collect();\n162:     // The first context carries `signatures`' provisional hoisted signatures (ret = the matched\n163:     // enum), enough to check their bodies; the inference pass below replaces each provisional\n164:     // return with the body's actual type and rebuilds the context before anything is checked.\n165:     let ctx = Ctx {\n166:         sigs: &sigs,\n167:         enums: &enums,\n168:         variant_owners: &variant_owners,\n169:         scope: Vec::new(),\n170:         arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n171:         subject: None,\n172:         input: &input,\n173:         inputs: &inputs,\n174:         lines_used: &lines_used,\n175:         dsv: &dsv,\n176:         in_mapper: false,\n177:         in_fn: None,\n178:         visibility: &visibility,\n179:         file: Origin::Program,\n180:         next_local: &next_local,\n181:     };\n182:     // Return-type inference for hoisted definitions (`fn name = expr`, gh:152): a hoisted\n183:     // function's signature is not written, so no body -- its own or another's -- may be checked\n184:     // against the provisional return `signatures` seeded. Checking each hoisted body once here\n185:     // fixes the real return, and rebuilding `sigs` first is what lets every later call, a\n186:     // recursive one included, resolve it.\n187:     for (name, sig) in infer_hoisted(&ctx, file.defs.iter())? {\n188:         sigs.insert(name, sig);\n189:     }\n190:     let ctx = Ctx {\n191:         sigs: &sigs,\n192:         enums: &enums,\n193:         variant_owners: &variant_owners,\n194:         scope: Vec::new(),\n195:         arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n196:         subject: None,\n197:         input: &input,\n198:         inputs: &inputs,\n199:         lines_used: &lines_used,\n200:         dsv: &dsv,\n201:         in_mapper: false,\n202:         in_fn: None,\n203:         visibility: &visibility,\n204:         file: Origin::Program,\n205:         next_local: &next_local,\n206:     };\n207: \n208:     // `prelude::inject` prepended prelude.toy's own defs to `file.defs`, which is what let\n209:     // `sigs` above resolve calls into them and is what catches a program that redefines one --\n210:     // but their bodies were already checked once, at build time (`build.rs`, via\n211:     // `prelude::checked`), and prelude.toy cannot have changed since. Rechecking them here would\n212:     // only repeat that work on every single compile.\n213:     let mut funcs = crate::prelude::checked();\n214:     let precompiled_names: HashSet<String> = funcs.iter().map(|f| f.name.clone()).collect();\n215:     let own_defs = file\n216:         .defs\n217:         .iter()\n218:         .filter(|d| !precompiled_names.contains(&d.name));\n219:     funcs.extend(check_defs(own_defs, &ctx)?);\n220: \n221:     let body = check_program_body(&ctx, &file.body)?;\n222:     // A stream cannot be printed, having nothing to show: it is not a value, and collect() is\n223:     // what turns it into one. A function body catches this for free, since its return\n224:     // annotation can never spell Stream and so can never match a body that contains one; the\n225:     // program's own result has no annotation to check against, so it needs asking directly.\n226:     if body.ty.contains_stream() {\n227:         return Err(Error::new(\n228:             file.body.span(),\n229:             \"the program's result contains a stream, which has nothing to print; pass it to \\\n230:              `collect` first\"\n231:                 .to_string(),\n232:         ));\n233:     }\n234:     // A Char has no wire form (the reverse of `input`'s refusal above), so a program cannot\n235:     // hand one to the printer either -- it has to build a Str first.\n236:     if body.ty.contains_char() {\n237:         return Err(Error::new(\n238:             file.body.span(),\n239:             \"the program's result contains a Char, which has no wire form to print\".to_string(),\n240:         ));\n241:     }\n242:     let input = input.into_inner();\n243:     let inputs = inputs.into_inner();\n244:     // Forced by the backends, not chosen: Python's `input` reads all of stdin to EOF before\n245:     // parsing, leaving nothing for anything else to read afterward, and jq needs a different\n246:     // invocation flag for raw lines (`-R -n`) than for parsed JSON values (`-n` alone) -- one\n247:     // process cannot run with both. So all three ways of reading the same real stdin are\n248:     // mutually exclusive, not just `input` and `lines` as before.\n249:     if input.is_some() && lines_used.get() {\n250:         return Err(Error::new(\n251:             file.body.span(),\n252:             \"a program cannot use both `input` and `lines`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n253:              different ways\"\n254:                 .to_string(),\n255:         ));\n256:     }\n257:     if input.is_some() && inputs.is_some() {\n258:         return Err(Error::new(\n259:             file.body.span(),\n260:             \"a program cannot use both `input` and `inputs`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n261:              different ways\"\n262:                 .to_string(),\n263:         ));\n264:     }\n265:     if lines_used.get() && inputs.is_some() {\n266:         return Err(Error::new(\n267:             file.body.span(),\n268:             \"a program cannot use both `lines` and `inputs`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n269:              different ways\"\n270:                 .to_string(),\n271:         ));\n272:     }\n273:     // `dsv` reads the same raw lines `stdin` does, so it joins the same exclusivity: one real\n274:     // stdin, read one way.\n275:     let dsv = dsv.into_inner();\n276:     for (other, name) in [\n277:         (input.is_some(), \"`parse(stdin)`\"),\n278:         (inputs.is_some(), \"`stdin | map(parse(.))`\"),\n279:         (lines_used.get(), \"`stdin`\"),\n280:     ] {\n281:         if dsv.is_some() && other {\n282:             return Err(Error::new(\n283:                 file.body.span(),\n284:                 format!(\n285:                     \"a program cannot use both `dsv` and {name}; they read the same real stdin \\\n286:                      two different ways\"\n287:                 ),\n288:             ));\n289:         }\n290:     }\n291:     Ok(tir::Program {\n292:         funcs: prune_unreachable(funcs, &body),\n293:         body,\n294:         input,\n295:         inputs,\n296:         uses_lines: lines_used.get(),\n297:         dsv,\n298:         enums,\n299:     })\n300: }\n301: \n302: /// What `check_defs` made of a definition's parameter: the TIR param name the backends bind\n303: /// the function's argument to, and, for a destructured one, the locals each named field was\n304: /// lowered to. The param name is a real user name for a plain parameter and a hidden one the body\n305: /// never sees for a destructured one.\n306: struct LoweredParam {\n307:     name: String,\n308:     record: Option<LocalId>,\n309:     field_locals: Vec<(String, Span, LocalId, Type)>,\n310: }\n311: \n312: /// Signatures are collected before any body is checked, so a definition may call one that\n313: /// appears later in `defs`. This is also what recursion needs. No reachability pruning happens\n314: /// here: that needs the whole program's body, which `check_module` never has and `check` only\n315: /// gets once this returns, so both call this and prune afterward, over the combined result.\n316: /// `ctx`'s own per-def fields (`scope`, `subject`, `in_fn`, ...) are ignored -- each def gets its\n317: /// own, built from its signature exactly as `check` always has.\n318: fn check_defs<'a>(\n319:     defs: impl IntoIterator<Item = &'a crate::ast::Def>,\n320:     ctx: &Ctx<'a>,\n321: ) -> Result<Vec<tir::Func>, Error> {\n322:     let mut funcs = Vec::new();\n323:     for def in defs {\n324:         // A hoisted definition (`fn name = expr`, gh:152) is checked by its own path: the\n325:         // signature is inferred rather than written, so none of the annotated-param machinery\n326:         // below applies. `infer_hoisted` already checked it once to fix the return type; this\n327:         // second check produces the `Func` the backends emit.\n328:         if def.hoisted {\n329:             funcs.push(check_hoisted_def(ctx, def)?);\n330:             continue;\n331:         }\n332:         let sig = &ctx.sigs[&def.name];\n333:         // A destructured parameter binds each named field to a fresh local, and its record arrives\n334:         // as a hidden single-name parameter the body never sees: the `Bind` chain below reads it off\n335:         // the backend's function argument, so a user name can never collide with it. The fields'\n336:         // `Some(local)` scope entries are what make `let` shadowing behave (kantord/toylang#144).\n337:         let (scope, lowered) = match (&def.param, &sig.param) {\n338:             (Some(param), Some(param_ty)) => match &param.shape {\n339:                 ParamShape::Name(name, _) => (\n340:                     vec![(name.clone(), param_ty.clone(), None)],\n341:                     LoweredParam {\n342:                         name: name.clone(),\n343:                         record: None,\n344:                         field_locals: Vec::new(),\n345:                     },\n346:                 ),\n347:                 ParamShape::Fields(fields) => {\n348:                     let Type::Record(pfields) = param_ty else {\n349:                         return Err(Error::new(\n350:                             fields.span,\n351:                             format!(\n352:                                 \"a destructuring parameter needs a record type, found {param_ty}\"\n353:                             ),\n354:                         ));\n355:                     };\n356:                     let record = ctx.fresh();\n357:                     let mut scope = Vec::new();\n358:                     let mut field_locals = Vec::new();\n359:                     for (i, (fname, fspan)) in fields.names.iter().enumerate() {\n360:                         if fields.names[..i].iter().any(|(seen,_)| seen == fname) {\n361:                             return Err(Error::new(\n362:                                 *fspan,\n363:                                 format!(\"`{fname}` is bound twice in this pattern\"),\n364:                             ));\n365:                         }\n366:                         let Some((_, fty)) = pfields.iter().find(|(n, _)| n == fname) else {\n367:                             return Err(Error::new(*fspan, format!(\"no field `{fname}` on {param_ty}\")));\n368:                         };\n369:                         let fid = ctx.fresh();\n370:                         scope.push((fname.clone(), fty.clone(), Some(fid)));\n371:                         field_locals.push((fname.clone(), *fspan, fid, fty.clone()));\n372:                     }\n373:                     // Leaving fields out of a destructuring parameter is a forgotten field until\n374:                     // `..` says it was meant, the same rule a match arm's pattern follows.\n375:                     if !fields.rest {\n376:                         let missing: Vec<String> = pfields\n377:                             .iter()\n378:                             .filter(|(n,_)| !fields.names.iter().any(|(m,_)| m == n))\n379:                             .map(|(n,_)| format!(\"`{n}`\"))\n380:                             .collect();\n381:                         if !missing.is_empty() {\n382:                             return Err(Error::new(\n383:                                 fields.span,\n384:                                 format!(\n385:                                     \"a destructuring parameter must name every field of its type \\\n386:                                      or end in `..`; missing {}\",\n387:                                     missing.join(\" and \")\n388:                                 ),\n389:                             ));\n390:                         }\n391:                     }\n392:                     let name = format!(\"__{}_param\", def.name);\n393:                     (\n394:                         scope,\n395:                         LoweredParam {\n396:                             name,\n397:                             record: Some(record),\n398:                             field_locals,\n399:                         },\n400:                     )\n401:                 }\n402:             },\n403:             (None, None) => (\n404:                 Vec::new(),\n405:                 LoweredParam {\n406:                     name: String::new(),\n407:                     record: None,\n408:                     field_locals: Vec::new(),\n409:                 },\n410:             ),\n411:             _ => unreachable!(\"a signature's param mirrors its definition's\"),\n412:         };\n413:         let def_ctx = Ctx {\n414:             sigs: ctx.sigs,\n415:             enums: ctx.enums,\n416:             variant_owners: ctx.variant_owners,\n417:             scope,\n418:             arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n419:             subject: None,\n420:             input: ctx.input,\n421:             inputs: ctx.inputs,\n422:             lines_used: ctx.lines_used,\n423:             dsv: ctx.dsv,\n424:             in_mapper: false,\n425:             in_fn: Some(&def.name),\n426:             visibility: ctx.visibility,\n427:             file: ctx.file,\n428:             next_local: ctx.next_local,\n429:         };\n430:         // The declared return type flows into the body, so a form whose type comes from its\n431:         // position (`[]`, `input`, a variant-naming string) resolves against the annotation. A\n432:         // body that synthesises instead is compared below, where the error can name the\n433:         // function rather than just the two types.\n434:         //\n435:         // A function declared `-> Sink` is the other sink position, so its body must be a sink\n436:         // call -- the tail-pipeline `|>` form or a direct call to a sink; nothing else is a\n437:         // sink, and a body that is not one fails here with the declared-vs-found mismatch below\n438:         // naming the function.\n439:         let mut body = if sig.ret == Type::Sink {\n440:             if matches!(def.body, Expr::TailPipe { .. }) {\n441:                 tail_pipe(&def_ctx, &def.body)?\n442:             } else if let Some(tir) = sink_call(&def_ctx, &def.body)? {\n443:                 tir\n444:             } else {\n445:                 return Err(Error::new(\n446:                     def.body.span(),\n447:                     format!(\n448:                         \"`{}` declares it returns Sink, so its body must be a sink call \\\n449:                          such as `jsonlines(...)` or `x |> jsonlines`\",\n450:                         def.name\n451:                     ),\n452:                 ));\n453:             }\n454:         } else {\n455:             match expect_inner(&def_ctx, &def.body, &sig.ret)? {\n456:                 Expected::Checked(body) => body,\n457:                 Expected::Synthesised(body) => conform(&def_ctx, body, &sig.ret),\n458:             }\n459:         };\n460:         // A destructured parameter's record arrives as a hidden param and is bound to a fresh\n461:         // local, then each named field is projected off it -- the same `Bind` shape `let` uses, so\n462:         // every backend already knows how to run one. Field bindings wrap innermost-first so the\n463:         // record's own bind comes first.\n464:         if let Some(record) = lowered.record {\n465:             let param_ty = sig.param.clone().expect(\"a destructured param's type is Some\");\n466:             for (fname, _, fid, fty) in lowered.field_locals.iter().rev() {\n467:                 let base = Tir::new(param_ty.clone(), Kind::Local(record));\n468:                 body = Tir::new(\n469:                     body.ty.clone(),\n470:                     Kind::Bind {\n471:                         local: *fid,\n472:                         value: Box::new(Tir::new(\n473:                             fty.clone(),\n474:                             Kind::Field {\n475:                                 base: Box::new(base),\n476:                                 name: fname.clone(),\n477:                             },\n478:                         )),\n479:                         body: Box::new(body),\n480:                     },\n481:                 );\n482:             }\n483:             body = Tir::new(\n484:                 body.ty.clone(),\n485:                 Kind::Bind {\n486:                     local: record,\n487:                     value: Box::new(Tir::new(param_ty, Kind::Var(lowered.name.clone()))),\n488:                     body: Box::new(body),\n489:                 },\n490:             );\n491:         }\n492:         if let Some(param) = &def.param {\n493:             check_param(&body, param, &lowered, &sig.param, &def.name, def.body.span())?;\n494:         }\n495:         if body.ty != sig.ret {\n496:             return Err(Error::new(\n497:                 def.body.span(),\n498:                 format!(\n499:                     \"`{}` declares it returns {}, but its body is {}\",\n500:                     def.name, sig.ret, body.ty\n501:                 ),\n502:             ));\n503:         }\n504:         funcs.push(tir::Func {\n505:             name: def.name.clone(),\n506:             param: if def.param.is_some() { Some(lowered.name.clone()) } else { None },\n507:             param_ty: sig.param.clone(),\n508:             body,\n509:         });\n510:     }\n511:     Ok(funcs)\n512: }\n513: \n514: /// Checks a module's own `pub` declarations in isolation -- `prelude.toy`'s, at build time\n515: /// (`build.rs`), before there is any file for them to be merged into and nothing yet calling any\n516: /// of it. Every declaration is kept: reachability is the calling file's question, decided once\n517: /// program and prelude are merged (`check` above, via `prune_unreachable`).\n518: pub fn check_module(module: &crate::ast::Module) -> Result<Vec<tir::Func>, Error> {\n519:     let env = TypeEnv {\n520:         aliases: HashMap::new(),\n521:         enums: enum_map(&module.enums)?,\n522:     };\n523:     let mut enums: HashMap<String, Type> = HashMap::new();\n524:     for e in &module.enums {\n525:         enums.insert(\n526:             e.name.clone(),\n527:             resolve_enum(e, &env, &mut Vec::new(), None)?,\n528:         );\n529:     }\n530:     let mut variant_owners: HashMap<String, Vec<String>> = HashMap::new();\n531:     for e in &module.enums {\n532:         for v in &e.variants {\n533:             variant_owners\n534:                 .entry(v.name.clone())\n535:                 .or_default()\n536:                 .push(e.name.clone());\n537:             let constructor = constructor_of(&v.name);\n538:             if constructor != v.name {\n539:                 variant_owners\n540:                     .entry(constructor)\n541:                     .or_default()\n542:                     .push(e.name.clone());\n543:             }\n544:         }\n545:     }\n546:     let mut sigs = signatures(&module.defs, &env)?;\n547:     let input = RefCell::new(None);\n548:     let inputs = RefCell::new(None);\n549:     let lines_used = Cell::new(false);\n550:     let dsv = RefCell::new(None);\n551:     let next_local = Cell::new(0);\n552:     let visibility: HashMap<String, (Origin, bool)> = module\n553:         .defs\n554:         .iter()\n555:         .map(|d| (d.name.clone(), (d.origin, d.is_pub)))\n556:         .collect();\n557:     let ctx = Ctx {\n558:         sigs: &sigs,\n559:         enums: &enums,\n560:         variant_owners: &variant_owners,\n561:         scope: Vec::new(),\n562:         arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n563:         subject: None,\n564:         input: &input,\n565:         inputs: &inputs,\n566:         lines_used: &lines_used,\n567:         dsv: &dsv,\n568:         in_mapper: false,\n569:         in_fn: None,\n570:         visibility: &visibility,\n571:         file: Origin::Prelude,\n572:         next_local: &next_local,\n573:     };\n574:     for (name, sig) in infer_hoisted(&ctx, module.defs.iter())? {\n575:         sigs.insert(name, sig);\n576:     }\n577:     let ctx = Ctx {\n578:         sigs: &sigs,\n579:         enums: &enums,\n580:         variant_owners: &variant_owners,\n581:         scope: Vec::new(),\n582:         arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n583:         subject: None,\n584:         input: &input,\n585:         inputs: &inputs,\n586:         lines_used: &lines_used,\n587:         dsv: &dsv,\n588:         in_mapper: false,\n589:         in_fn: None,\n590:         visibility: &visibility,\n591:         file: Origin::Prelude,\n592:         next_local: &next_local,\n593:     };\n594:     check_defs(module.defs.iter(), &ctx)\n595: }\n596: \n597: /// A signature may spell Stream now, which un-does the trick the Lines design leaned on (a\n598: /// return annotation could never match a body holding a stream), so what that trick guaranteed\n599: /// for free is checked for real here. Called only when `def` takes a parameter: a nullary\n600: /// function has none of this to check.\n601: fn check_param(\n602:     body: &Tir,\n603:     param: &Param,\n604:     lowered: &LoweredParam,\n605:     param_ty: &Option<Type>,\n606:     func_name: &str,\n607:     body_span: Span,\n608: ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n609:     match &param.shape {\n610:         ParamShape::Name(name, _) => {\n611:             if matches!(param_ty, Some(Type::Stream(_))) {\n612:                 check_linear(\n613:                     body,\n614:                     &StreamBinding::Param(name),\n615:                     &format!(\"`{}`\", name),\n616:                     body_span,\n617:                 )?;\n618:             }\n619:             if !param_used(body, name) {\n620:                 return Err(Error::new(\n621:                     param.span,\n622:                     format!(\n623:                         \"parameter `{}` is never used; delete it from `{}`'s definition and its call sites\",\n624:                         name, func_name\n625:                     ),\n626:                 ));\n627:             }\n628:         }\n629:         // A destructured parameter's record type can never be a Stream (streams cannot live in\n630:         // records), so nothing linear to check; each named field is instead held to the same\n631:         // dead-code rule the plain parameter's name is, echoing what a match arm does to its\n632:         // pattern's fields.\n633:         ParamShape::Fields(fields) => {\n634:             for (fname, fspan) in &fields.names {\n635:                 let (_, _, fid, _) = lowered\n636:                     .field_locals\n637:                     .iter()\n638:                     .find(|(n, _, _, _)| n == fname)\n639:                     .expect(\"the checker lowered every named field\");\n640:                 if !local_used(body, *fid) {\n641:                     let hint = if fields.rest {\n642:                         \"remove it from the pattern\".to_string()\n643:                     } else {\n644:                         \"remove it from the pattern and close it with `..`\".to_string()\n645:                     };\n646:                     return Err(Error::new(\n647:                         *fspan,\n648:                         format!(\"`{fname}` is bound here but never used in the body; {hint}\"),\n649:                     ));\n650:                 }\n651:             }\n652:         }\n653:     }\n654:     Ok(())\n655: }\n656: \n657: /// The program's own body. A sink-shaped body -- the tail-pipeline `lhs |> callee` or a direct\n658: /// sink call (`jsonlines(...)` or a call to a function declared `-> Sink`) -- is the one legal\n659: /// place a `Sink` may be born besides a `Sink`-returning function's body, so it is recognized\n660: /// ahead of `synth`, which refuses a sink everywhere else (the general position rule; see\n661: /// `tail_pipe` and `sink_call`). Any other body is synthesized, and a `Sink` result that slips\n662: /// past recognition can only mean a shape `synth` should have refused.\n663: fn check_program_body(ctx: &Ctx, body: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n664:     if matches!(body, Expr::TailPipe { .. }) {\n665:         return tail_pipe(ctx, body);\n666:     }\n667:     if let Some(tir) = sink_call(ctx, body)? {\n668:         return Ok(tir);\n669:     }\n670:     synth(ctx, body)\n671: }\n672: \n673: /// Whether `body` is a direct sink call -- `jsonlines(...)` or a call to a function declared\n674: /// `-> Sink` -- and, if so, the `Sink`-typed Tir it checks to. The `|>` tail-pipeline is the\n675: /// other way a sink is written (see `tail_pipe`); both are legal in the program's body and a\n676: /// `Sink`-returning function's body, and everywhere else `synth` runs the general position\n677: /// rule and a `Sink` that slips past here is a shape `synth` should have refused.\n678: fn sink_call(ctx: &Ctx, body: &Expr) -> Result<Option<Tir>, Error> {\n679:     let Expr::Call {\n680:         func,\n681:         func_span,\n682:         arg,\n683:         span,\n684:     } = body\n685:     else {\n686:         return Ok(None);\n687:     };\n688:     if func == \"jsonlines\" {\n689:         return Ok(Some(jsonlines_call(ctx, arg, *span)?));\n690:     }\n691:     if ctx.sigs.get(func).is_some_and(|sig| sig.ret == Type::Sink) {\n692:         return Ok(Some(call(ctx, func, *func_span, arg, *span)?));\n693:     }\n694:     Ok(None)\n695: }\n696: \n697: /// `lhs |> callee`, the tail-pipeline marker: `callee` -- a sink -- applied to `lhs`. This is\n698: /// one of the two legal places a `Sink` may be born (the program's body and a `Sink`-returning\n699: /// function's body; the other is a direct sink call, see `sink_call`); everywhere else `synth`\n700: /// runs the general position rule and a `Sink` that slips past here is a shape `synth` should\n701: /// have refused. Only a `Sink`-typed callee is legal, which is the general rule the old\n702: /// hand-checked `jsonlines` position (kantord/toylang#151, #154) folded into: `jsonlines` is\n703: /// the one sink builtin, and every other callee must be a function whose declared return type\n704: /// is `Sink`.\n705: fn tail_pipe(ctx: &Ctx, expr: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n706:     let Expr::TailPipe {\n707:         lhs,\n708:         callee,\n709:         callee_span,\n710:         ..\n711:     } = expr\n712:     else {\n713:         unreachable!(\"tail_pipe is only called on a `|>` node\");\n714:     };\n715:     let value = synth(ctx, lhs)?;\n716:     if callee == \"jsonlines\" {\n717:         return jsonlines_arg(value, lhs.span());\n718:     }\n719:     let Some(sig) = ctx.sigs.get(callee.as_str()) else {\n720:         return Err(Error::new(\n721:             *callee_span,\n722:             format!(\n723:                 \"the callee of `|>` must be a sink, such as `jsonlines` or a function that \\\n724:                  returns Sink; `{callee}` is not one\"\n725:             ),\n726:         ));\n727:     };\n728:     if sig.ret != Type::Sink {\n729:         return Err(Error::new(\n730:             *callee_span,\n731:             format!(\n732:                 \"the callee of `|>` must be a sink, but `{callee}` returns {}\",\n733:                 sig.ret\n734:             ),\n735:         ));\n736:     }\n737:     let Some(param_ty) = &sig.param else {\n738:         return Err(Error::new(\n739:             *callee_span,\n740:             format!(\"`{callee}` takes no argument, so there is nothing for `|>` to pass it\"),\n741:         ));\n742:     };\n743:     if value.ty != *param_ty {\n744:         return Err(Error::new(\n745:             lhs.span(),\n746:             format!(\"`{callee}` needs {param_ty}, found {}\", value.ty),\n747:         ));\n748:     }\n749:     let value = conform(ctx, value, param_ty);\n750:     Ok(Tir::new(\n751:         Type::Sink,\n752:         Kind::Call {\n753:             func: callee.clone(),\n754:             arg: Some(Box::new(value)),\n755:         },\n756:     ))\n757: }\n758: \n759: /// Every function name the language itself provides, and therefore reserves. `str`, `range`,\n760: /// `chars`, and `i64` live in `builtin()`'s fixed table; `jsonlines`, `length`, `flatten`,\n761: /// `tail`, `collect`, `fields`, `sort`, `reverse`, `sum`, `max`, `parse`, `first`, `any`, and\n762: /// `all` are polymorphic and checked from `synth`/`expect_inner`'s own arms; `select` and `map`\n763: /// rebind `.`,and `sort_by`/`max_by` do the same with an orderable projection. All twenty-two are reserved the same way, and the docs harness (tests/docs.rs) reads\n764: /// this list to insist each one has a reference page.\n765: pub const BUILTIN_NAMES: [&str; 22] = [\n766:     \"all\",\n767:     \"any\",\n768:     \"chars\",\n769:     \"collect\",\n770:     \"first\",\n771:     \"length\",\n772:     \"fields\",\n773:     \"flatten\",\n774:     \"i64\",\n775:     \"jsonlines\",\n776:     \"map\",\n777:     \"max\",\n778:     \"max_by\",\n779:     \"parse\",\n780:     \"range\",\n781:     \"reverse\",\n782:     \"select\",\n783:     \"sort\",\n784:     \"sort_by\",\n785:     \"str\",\n786:     \"sum\",\n787:     \"tail\",\n788: ];\n789: \n790: /// Functions the language provides. Unary like every other function, so they need no special\n791: /// call syntax and are looked up before user definitions.\n792: fn builtin(name: &str) -> Option<(tir::Builtin, Sig)> {\n793:     let vec_of = |t: Type| Type::Vec(Box::new(t));\n794:     Some(match name {\n795:         \"str\" => (\n796:             tir::Builtin::IntToStr,\n797:             Sig {\n798:                 param: Some(Type::Int),\n799:                 ret: Type::Str,\n800:             },\n801:         ),\n802:         \"range\" => (\n803:             tir::Builtin::Range,\n804:             Sig {\n805:                 param: Some(Type::Int),\n806:                 ret: Type::Stream(Box::new(Type::Int)),\n807:             },\n808:         ),\n809:         \"chars\" => (\n810:             tir::Builtin::Chars,\n811:             Sig {\n812:                 param: Some(Type::Str),\n813:                 ret: vec_of(Type::Char),\n814:             },\n815:         ),\n816:         // The one bridge between the integer types (kantord/toylang#83). Its argument is an\n817:         // Int, so `i64(9000000000)` is refused like any other too-big Int literal: a wide\n818:         // value enters as a literal only where an Int64 is already expected.\n819:         \"i64\" => (\n820:             tir::Builtin::IntToI64,\n821:             Sig {\n822:                 param: Some(Type::Int),\n823:                 ret: Type::Int64,\n824:             },\n825:         ),\n826:         _ => return None,\n827:     })\n828: }\n829: \n830: /// A chain split by `rebase`: the rebased body, and the stream source it was split from.\n831: struct Rebased {\n832:     body: Tir,\n833:     source: Tir,\n834: }\n835: \n836: /// Split an access chain whose outermost dimension is a stream from its source, rebasing the\n837: /// chain on `param` as a map body: every node's type loses its Stream wrapper, and an Index's\n838: /// stored depth drops by one, since the layer it counted is now the loop.\n839: ///\n840: /// Only Field, Unwrap, and Index appear in a chain, and the source is never one of them: a\n841: /// stream-typed access chain is normalized to a Map right here, so one can never be the base of\n842: /// another.\n843: fn rebase(t: Tir, param: LocalId) -> Rebased {\n844:     fn peel(ty: Type) -> Type {\n845:         match ty {\n846:             Type::Stream(t) => *t,\n847:             other => unreachable!(\n848:                 \"every node inside the stream dimension is stream-typed, found {other}\"\n849:             ),\n850:         }\n851:     }\n852:     fn is_chain(t: &Tir) -> bool {\n853:         matches!(\n854:             t.kind,\n855:             Kind::Field { .. } | Kind::Unwrap { .. } | Kind::Index { .. }\n856:         )\n857:     }\n858:     /// The base of one link in the chain: another link to recurse into, or the stream source\n859:     /// itself, in which case `param` stands in for the per-element value the map body reads.\n860:     fn split_base(base: Tir, param: LocalId) -> Rebased {\n861:         if is_chain(&base) {\n862:             rebase(base, param)\n863:         } else {\n864:             let elem = peel(base.ty.clone());\n865:             Rebased {\n866:                 body: Tir::new(elem, Kind::Local(param)),\n867:                 source: base,\n868:             }\n869:         }\n870:     }\n871:     match t.kind {\n872:         Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n873:             let Rebased { body: base, source } = split_base(*base, param);\n874:             Rebased {\n875:                 body: Tir::new(\n876:                     peel(t.ty),\n877:                     Kind::Field {\n878:                         base: Box::new(base),\n879:                         name,\n880:                     },\n881:                 ),\n882:                 source,\n883:             }\n884:         }\n885:         Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n886:             let Rebased { body: base, source } = split_base(*base, param);\n887:             Rebased {\n888:                 body: Tir::new(\n889:                     peel(t.ty),\n890:                     Kind::Unwrap {\n891:                         base: Box::new(base),\n892:                     },\n893:                 ),\n894:                 source,\n895:             }\n896:         }\n897:         Kind::Index {\n898:             base,\n899:             index,\n900:             depth,\n901:             elem_is_record,\n902:         } => {\n903:             let Rebased { body: base, source } = split_base(*base, param);\n904:             let kind = Kind::Index {\n905:                 base: Box::new(base),\n906:                 index,\n907:                 depth: depth - 1,\n908:                 elem_is_record,\n909:             };\n910:             Rebased {\n911:                 body: Tir::new(peel(t.ty), kind),\n912:                 source,\n913:             }\n914:         }\n915:         other => unreachable!(\n916:             \"only an access chain is rebased, found {:?}\",\n917:             std::mem::discriminant(&other)\n918:         ),\n919:     }\n920: }\n921: \n922: /// The element `select` and `map` rebind `.` to: a Vec's, or a Stream's. Deliberately not\n923: /// `Type::elem`, which stays Vec-only so the reducers (`length`, `jsonlines` today) keep\n924: /// refusing a stream.\n925: fn mapper_elem(subject: &Type) -> Option<Type> {\n926:     match subject {\n927:         Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => Some((**t).clone()),\n928:         _ => None,\n929:     }\n930: }\n931: \n932: /// The context a mapper body (`select`'s predicate, `map`'s body) checks in: `.` rebound to the\n933: /// element a fresh `param` will hold at runtime, with a source read refused since the body runs\n934: /// once per element rather than once.\n935: fn mapper_ctx<'a>(ctx: &'a Ctx, elem: Type, param: LocalId) -> Ctx<'a> {\n936:     let mut inner = ctx.with(Some((elem, param)));\n937:     inner.in_mapper = true;\n938:     inner\n939: }\n940: \n941: /// The one enum a bare variant name refers to, or the error naming every candidate: guessing\n942: /// between two claimants would silently pick a type the program never wrote down.\n943: fn sole_owner<'a>(\n944:     ctx: &'a Ctx,\n945:     variant: &str,\n946:     owners: &[String],\n947:     span: Span,\n948: ) -> Result<&'a Type, Error> {\n949:     if owners.len() > 1 {\n950:         let named: Vec<String> = owners.iter().map(|e| format!(\"`{e}`\")).collect();\n951:         let qualified: Vec<String> = owners.iter().map(|e| format!(\"`{e}.{variant}`\")).collect();\n952:         return Err(Error::new(\n953:             span,\n954:             format!(\n955:                 \"`{variant}` is a variant of {}; qualify it as {}\",\n956:                 named.join(\" and \"),\n957:                 qualified.join(\" or \")\n958:             ),\n959:         ));\n960:     }\n961:     Ok(&ctx.enums[&owners[0]])\n962: }\n963: \n964: /// Every builtin and special-cased call form (`select`, `map`, `length`, ...) is unary; only a\n965: /// user-defined function can be nullary. This unwraps a call's optional argument for those\n966: /// forms, turning a bare `name()` at one of these names into the same \"not a function\" shape as\n967: /// any other arity mismatch would need, rather than a panic.\n968: fn need_arg<'a>(arg: &'a Option<Box<Expr>>, func: &str, span: Span) -> Result<&'a Expr, Error> {\n969:     arg.as_deref()\n970:         .ok_or_else(|| Error::new(span, format!(\"`{func}` needs an argument\")))\n971: }\n972: \n973: /// `expr` is a call to `name` carrying an argument, for `expect_inner`'s `collect`/`map`\n974: /// special cases: a bare `name()` (no argument) is not one of these, and falls through to\n975: /// `synth`, which owns the arity error for a name that is not actually nullary.\n976: fn call_named<'a>(expr: &'a Expr, name: &str) -> Option<&'a Expr> {\n977:     let Expr::Call { func, arg, .. } = expr else {\n978:         return None;\n979:     };\n980:     if func != name {\n981:         return None;\n982:     }\n983:     arg.as_deref()\n984: }\n985: \n986: /// `name`'s variant list at `args`. A thin wrapper over the shared re-derivation\n987: /// (`ty::variants_of`), which the backends reach for too: the checker's own `Type::Enum` in\n988: /// hand carries a placeholder wherever a self-reference sits behind a `Vec`\n989: /// (`types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76), so every consumer that actually needs the\n990: /// variants comes through here instead, one layer at a time, exactly as deep as the program\n991: /// itself ever navigates into the value.\n992: fn enum_variants(ctx: &Ctx, name: &str, args: &[Type]) -> Vec<(String, Option<Type>)> {\n993:     ty::variants_of(ctx.enums, name, args)\n994: }\n995: \n996: /// The prelude's `Opt<T>` at a concrete `T`: what every Opt-producing site -- the collapsing\n997: /// index, `tail`, a partial guard chain -- wraps its result in. Instantiated from the\n998: /// registry template rather than spelled out, so the checker never re-states the prelude's\n999: /// declaration.\n1000: fn opt_of(ctx: &Ctx, inner: Type) -> Type {\n1001:     let template = ctx.enums.get(\"Opt\").expect(\"the prelude declares Opt\");\n1002:     let Type::Enum { args, .. } = template else {\n1003:         unreachable!(\"the registry holds enum types\")\n1004:     };\n1005:     let Type::Param(p) = &args[0] else {\n1006:         unreachable!(\"Opt's template argument is its own parameter\")\n1007:     };\n1008:     ty::substitute(template, &HashMap::from([(p.clone(), inner)]))\n1009: }\n1010: \n1011: /// Match a template type (parameters possibly inside) against a concrete one, binding each\n1012: /// parameter to what it faces. Records match fields by name, not position, the same way type\n1013: /// equality does (kantord/toylang#60); enums match on name and arguments, since the variants\n1014: /// follow from those.\n1015: fn unify(template: &Type, concrete: &Type, map: &mut HashMap<String, Type>) -> bool {\n1016:     match (template, concrete) {\n1017:         (Type::Param(p), c) => match map.get(p) {\n1018:             Some(bound) => bound == c,\n1019:             None => {\n1020:                 map.insert(p.clone(), c.clone());\n1021:                 true\n1022:             }\n1023:         },\n1024:         (Type::Vec(a), Type::Vec(b)) | (Type::Stream(a), Type::Stream(b)) => unify(a, b, map),\n1025:         (Type::Record(a), Type::Record(b)) => {\n1026:             a.len() == b.len()\n1027:                 && a.iter().all(|(n, t)| {\n1028:                     b.iter()\n1029:                         .find(|(bn, _)| bn == n)\n1030:                         .is_some_and(|(_, bt)| unify(t, bt, map))\n1031:                 })\n1032:         }\n1033:         (\n1034:             Type::Enum {\n1035:                 name: n1, args: a1, ..\n1036:             },\n1037:             Type::Enum {\n1038:                 name: n2, args: a2, ..\n1039:             },\n1040:         ) => n1 == n2 && a1.len() == a2.len() && a1.iter().zip(a2).all(|(x, y)| unify(x, y, map)),\n1041:         _ => template == concrete,\n1042:     }\n1043: }\n1044: \n1045: /// Build one variant of `enum_ty`, checking that the payload written matches the payload\n1046: /// declared: a unit variant takes none, a payload variant requires one.\n1047: ///\n1048: /// `enum_ty` is a registry template for a generic enum reached by name, or a concrete type\n1049: /// when the position already knows one (an expectation, or a written annotation). A template\n1050: /// instantiates from the expectation when `expected` names the same enum; otherwise the\n1051: /// payload's synthesised type binds the parameters, and a variant that leaves any parameter\n1052: /// open -- every unit variant of a generic enum does -- is refused, the `[]` answer.\n1053: fn construct(\n1054:     ctx: &Ctx,\n1055:     enum_ty: &Type,\n1056:     variant: &str,\n1057:     variant_span: Span,\n1058:     payload: Option<&Expr>,\n1059:     expected: Option<&Type>,\n1060:     from_string: bool,\n1061: ) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n1062:     let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = enum_ty else {\n1063:         unreachable!(\"construct is only called with an enum type\")\n1064:     };\n1065:     let is_template = args.iter().any(|a| matches!(a, Type::Param(_)));\n1066:     let instantiated;\n1067:     let enum_ty = if !is_template {\n1068:         enum_ty\n1069:     } else if let Some(want @ Type::Enum { name: wanted, .. }) = expected\n1070:         && wanted == name\n1071:     {\n1072:         want\n1073:     } else {\n1074:         instantiated = infer_instantiation(ctx, enum_ty, variant, variant_span, payload)?;\n1075:         return Ok(instantiated);\n1076:     };\n1077:     let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = enum_ty else {\n1078:         unreachable!(\"construct is only called with an enum type\")\n1079:     };\n1080:     let variants = enum_variants(ctx, name, args);\n1081:     // A capitalized name is the matcher, legal only in a pattern; a value is built with the\n1082:     // lowercase constructor, which resolves to the declared (capitalized) variant. A string\n1083:     // literal naming a variant is the wire's spelling, so it is exempt -- see the caller.\n1084:     if !from_string && variant.chars().next().is_some_and(char::is_uppercase) {\n1085:         return Err(Error::new(\n1086:             variant_span,\n1087:             format!(\n1088:                 \"a constructor starts with a lowercase letter; `{variant}` is the matcher and \\\n1089:                  matches only in a pattern, so write `{}` to build the value\",\n1090:                 constructor_of(variant)\n1091:             ),\n1092:         ));\n1093:     }\n1094:     let Some((variant_name, declared)) = variants\n1095:         .iter()\n1096:         .find(|(n, _)| n == variant || is_constructor_of(n, variant))\n1097:     else {\n1098:         return Err(Error::new(\n1099:             variant_span,\n1100:             format!(\"`{name}` has no variant `{variant}`\"),\n1101:         ));\n1102:     };\n1103:     let payload = match (declared, payload) {\n1104:         (None, None) => None,\n1105:         (Some(want), Some(expr)) => Some(Box::new(expect(ctx, expr, want)?)),\n1106:         (None, Some(expr)) => {\n1107:             return Err(Error::new(\n1108:                 expr.span(),\n1109:                 format!(\"`{variant}` is a unit variant of `{name}` and takes no payload\"),\n1110:             ));\n1111:         }\n1112:         (Some(want), None) => {\n1113:             // The hint mirrors the declaration's two spellings: braces for a record payload,\n1114:             // parens for any other type.\n1115:             let spelled = match want {\n1116:                 Type::Record(_) => format!(\"{variant}{{...}}\"),\n1117:                 _ => format!(\"{variant}(...)\"),\n1118:             };\n1119:             return Err(Error::new(\n1120:                 variant_span,\n1121:                 format!(\n1122:                     \"`{variant}` of `{name}` carries a payload of {want}, so it is written `{spelled}`\"\n1123:                 ),\n1124:             ));\n1125:         }\n1126:     };\n1127:     Ok(Tir::new(\n1128:         enum_ty.clone(),\n1129:         Kind::EnumLit {\n1130:             // The wire carries the matcher's name, the declared variant (`Circle`), so a backend\n1131:             // reader and printer can agree on it without lowering; the lowercase `variant` is the\n1132:             // constructor, the source spelling that builds the value (gh:156).\n1133:             variant: variant_name.to_string(),\n1134:             payload,\n1135:         },\n1136:     ))\n1137: }\n1138: \n1139: /// A generic enum's constructor met with no expectation: the payload is synthesised and its\n1140: /// type binds the parameters. The template's own display (`Pair<T>`) does the talking in the\n1141: /// errors, since it shows the reader exactly which names are still open.\n1142: fn infer_instantiation(\n1143:     ctx: &Ctx,\n1144:     template: &Type,\n1145:     variant: &str,\n1146:     variant_span: Span,\n1147:     payload: Option<&Expr>,\n1148: ) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n1149:     let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = template else {\n1150:         unreachable!(\"construct is only called with an enum type\")\n1151:     };\n1152:     let variants = enum_variants(ctx, name, args);\n1153:     if variant.chars().next().is_some_and(char::is_uppercase) {\n1154:         return Err(Error::new(\n1155:             variant_span,\n1156:             format!(\n1157:                 \"a constructor starts with a lowercase letter; `{variant}` is the matcher and \\\n1158:                  matches only in a pattern, so write `{}` to build the value\",\n1159:                 constructor_of(variant)\n1160:             ),\n1161:         ));\n1162:     }\n1163:     let Some((variant_name, declared)) = variants\n1164:         .iter()\n1165:         .find(|(n, _)| n == variant || is_constructor_of(n, variant))\n1166:     else {\n1167:         return Err(Error::new(\n1168:             variant_span,\n1169:             format!(\"`{name}` has no variant `{variant}`\"),\n1170:         ));\n1171:     };\n1172:     let (declared, expr) = match (declared, payload) {\n1173:         (Some(declared), Some(expr)) => (declared, expr),\n1174:         (None, Some(expr)) => {\n1175:             return Err(Error::new(\n1176:                 expr.span(),\n1177:                 format!(\"`{variant}` is a unit variant of `{name}` and takes no payload\"),\n1178:             ));\n1179:         }\n1180:         (Some(want), None) => {\n1181:             let spelled = match want {\n1182:                 Type::Record(_) => format!(\"{variant}{{...}}\"),\n1183:                 _ => format!(\"{variant}(...)\"),\n1184:             };\n1185:             return Err(Error::new(\n1186:                 variant_span,\n1187:                 format!(\n1188:                     \"`{variant}` of `{name}` carries a payload of {want}, so it is written `{spelled}`\"\n1189:                 ),\n1190:             ));\n1191:         }\n1192:         (None, None) => {\n1193:             return Err(Error::new(\n1194:                 variant_span,\n1195:                 format!(\n1196:                     \"cannot tell what `{template}` a bare `{variant}` builds; only a position \\\n1197:                      that expects one can say\"\n1198:                 ),\n1199:             ));\n1200:         }\n1201:     };\n1202:     let built = synth(ctx, expr)?;\n1203:     let mut bindings = HashMap::new();\n1204:     if !unify(declared, &built.ty, &mut bindings) {\n1205:         return Err(Error::new(\n1206:             expr.span(),\n1207:             format!(\"expected {declared}, found {}\", built.ty),\n1208:         ));\n1209:     }\n1210:     if let Type::Enum { args, .. } = template\n1211:         && let Some(open) = args.iter().find_map(|a| match a {\n1212:             Type::Param(p) if !bindings.contains_key(p) => Some(p),\n1213:             _ => None,\n1214:         })\n1215:     {\n1216:         return Err(Error::new(\n1217:             variant_span,\n1218:             format!(\n1219:                 \"`{variant}`'s payload does not say what `{template}`'s `{open}` is; only a \\\n1220:                  position that expects one can\"\n1221:             ),\n1222:         ));\n1223:     }\n1224:     Ok(Tir::new(\n1225:         ty::substitute(template, &bindings),\n1226:         Kind::EnumLit {\n1227:             variant: variant_name.to_string(),\n1228:             payload: Some(Box::new(built)),\n1229:         },\n1230:     ))\n1231: }\n1232: \n1233: /// A source spelled inside a `fn` body. The rule exists because the mapper-body check alone had\n1234: /// a cross-function hole: a function reading a source, called from a mapper's body, re-read\n1235: /// stdin once per element with nothing at the call site to refuse. Refusing sources in every\n1236: /// `fn` closes that everywhere; the alternative, tracking source consumption per function\n1237: /// transitively, was rejected as an invisible effect on every signature.\n1238: fn source_in_fn(span: Span, source: &str, func: &str) -> Error {\n1239:     Error::new(\n1240:         span,\n1241:         format!(\n1242:             \"`{source}` cannot be read inside `fn {func}`; a source is legal only in the \\\n1243:              program's own body, so take the stream through a `Stream` parameter\"\n1244:         ),\n1245:     )\n1246: }\n1247: \n1248: /// The one exit a stream has: `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`. `want_elem` is `Some` in the checking\n1249: /// direction, needed because `collect(inputs)` has no type to synthesise until the wanted\n1250: /// Vec<T> pushes a Stream<T> expectation down onto `inputs`; it is `None` in the synthesising\n1251: /// direction, where the argument's own type says what T is.\n1252: fn collect(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr, want_elem: Option<&Type>) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n1253:     let (elem, arg) = match want_elem {\n1254:         Some(elem) => (\n1255:             elem.clone(),\n1256:             expect(ctx, arg, &Type::Stream(Box::new(elem.clone())))?,\n1257:         ),\n1258:         None => {\n1259:             let arg_span = arg.span();\n1260:             let arg = synth(ctx, arg)?;\n1261:             let Type::Stream(elem) = &arg.ty else {\n1262:                 return Err(Error::new(\n1263:                     arg_span,\n1264:                     format!(\"`collect` needs a stream, found {}\", arg.ty),\n1265:                 ));\n1266:             };\n1267:             (elem.as_ref().clone(), arg)\n1268:         }\n1269:     };\n1270:     Ok(Tir::new(\n1271:         Type::Vec(Box::new(elem)),\n1272:         Kind::Builtin {\n1273:             which: tir::Builtin::Collect,\n1274:             arg: Box::new(arg),\n1275:         },\n\n(Output capped at 50 KB. Showing lines 1-1275. Use offset=1276 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"use std::cell::{Cell, RefCell};\nuse std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet};\n\nuse crate::ast::{BinOp, Def, Expr, FieldsPattern, File, MatchArm, Origin, Param, ParamShape, Pattern, Span};\nuse crate::error::Error;\nuse crate::tir::{self, Kind, LocalId, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Sig, Type};\n\nmod linearity;\nmod types;\n\nuse linearity::{StreamBinding, check_linear, field_used, local_used, param_used, prune_unreachable};\nuse types::{\n    TypeEnv, alias_map, constructor_of, enum_map, is_constructor_of, matcher_of, resolve,\n    resolve_enum, signatures,\n};\n\nstruct Ctx<'a> {\n    sigs: &'a HashMap<String, Sig>,\n    /// Every declared enum, resolved. Keyed by name, which is the identity.","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/check/mod.rs","text":"use std::cell::{Cell, RefCell};\nuse std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet};\n\nuse crate::ast::{BinOp, Def, Expr, FieldsPattern, File, MatchArm, Origin, Param, ParamShape, Pattern, Span};\nuse crate::error::Error;\nuse crate::tir::{self, Kind, LocalId, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Sig, Type};\n\nmod linearity;\nmod types;\n\nuse linearity::{StreamBinding, check_linear, field_used, local_used, param_used, prune_unreachable};\nuse types::{\n    TypeEnv, alias_map, constructor_of, enum_map, is_constructor_of, matcher_of, resolve,\n    resolve_enum, signatures,\n};\n\nstruct Ctx<'a> {\n    sigs: &'a HashMap<String, Sig>,\n    /// Every declared enum, resolved. Keyed by name, which is the identity.\n    enums: &'a HashMap<String, Type>,\n    /// Which enums declare each variant name, in declaration order. A bare variant use resolves\n    /// through this while exactly one enum claims the name; two claimants are a loud error\n    /// naming both, with `Shape.circle` as the qualified way out.\n    variant_owners: &'a HashMap<String, Vec<String>>,\n    /// Named bindings, innermost last. A function parameter carries `None` for its local (the\n    /// backends render a param by name); a `let` binding carries the `LocalId` it was bound to,\n    /// so reading the name is reading that local. `let` can stack many of these, and a later one\n    /// shadows an earlier, which is what the reverse search in `synth` is for.\n    scope: Vec<(String, Type, Option<LocalId>)>,\n    /// Names a match arm's record pattern bound, innermost last: reading one reads that field\n    /// off the arm's payload local, so nothing beyond ordinary `Field` nodes reaches the\n    /// backends. Each entry is the bound name, the payload's record type, and its local.\n    arm_fields: Vec<(String, Type, LocalId)>,\n    /// What `.` refers to here, if anything: its type and the local holding it.\n    subject: Option<(Type, LocalId)>,\n    /// The type `input` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used.\n    input: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n    /// The element type `inputs` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used. A\n    /// separate cell from `input`'s: one records a whole value's type, the other one line's.\n    inputs: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n    /// Whether `lines` has already been read. There is only ever one real stdin, so a second\n    /// read is refused rather than silently handed nothing, the way a second pass over an\n    /// already-consumed iterator would be in a language that let it compile.\n    lines_used: &'a Cell<bool>,\n    /// The delimiter `dsv` was read with, filled in the first time it is used. `csv`/`tsv`\n    /// arrive here with the delimiter already fixed by the parser. A second read of any kind\n    /// is refused the same way a second `lines` is.\n    dsv: &'a RefCell<Option<String>>,\n    /// Whether checking is inside a mapper's body (`map`'s, or `select`'s predicate), which\n    /// runs once per element: a source read there would drain stdin on the first element and\n    /// hand every later one nothing, so `lines` and `inputs` are refused in that position.\n    in_mapper: bool,\n    /// The name of the `fn` whose body is being checked; `None` in the program's own body. A\n    /// source is legal only in the program body (see `source_in_fn`), so `lines` and `inputs`\n    /// are refused whenever this is set.\n    in_fn: Option<&'a str>,\n    /// Every user function, keyed by name, with the file it was defined in and whether it is\n    /// `pub`. A call to a non-`pub` one from a different file than `file` is refused, which is\n    /// the visibility rule (gh:166).\n    visibility: &'a HashMap<String, (Origin, bool)>,\n    /// Which file the code being checked was written in. The program's body and definitions are\n    /// `Program`; the prelude's definitions (checked at build time) are `Prelude`.\n    file: Origin,\n    next_local: &'a Cell<LocalId>,\n}\n\nimpl Ctx<'_> {\n    fn with(&self, subject: Option<(Type, LocalId)>) -> Ctx<'_> {\n        self.rebuild(self.scope.clone(), subject)\n    }\n\n    fn rebuild(\n        &self,\n        scope: Vec<(String, Type, Option<LocalId>)>,\n        subject: Option<(Type, LocalId)>,\n    ) -> Ctx<'_> {\n        Ctx {\n            sigs: self.sigs,\n            enums: self.enums,\n            variant_owners: self.variant_owners,\n            scope,\n            arm_fields: self.arm_fields.clone(),\n            subject,\n            input: self.input,\n            inputs: self.inputs,\n            lines_used: self.lines_used,\n            dsv: self.dsv,\n            in_mapper: self.in_mapper,\n            in_fn: self.in_fn,\n            visibility: self.visibility,\n            file: self.file,\n            next_local: self.next_local,\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn fresh(&self) -> LocalId {\n        let id = self.next_local.get();\n        self.next_local.set(id + 1);\n        id\n    }\n}\n\npub fn check(file: &File) -> Result<tir::Program, Error> {\n    let lines_used = Cell::new(false);\n    let aliases = alias_map(&file.aliases)?;\n    let env = TypeEnv {\n        aliases,\n        enums: enum_map(&file.enums)?,\n    };\n    for e in &file.enums {\n        if env.aliases.contains_key(&e.name) {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                e.span,\n                format!(\"type `{}` is defined twice\", e.name),\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n    // Resolved eagerly so a broken declaration is an error even when nothing uses it, and so a\n    // cycle is found here rather than wherever it happened to be reached from.\n    for a in &file.aliases {\n        resolve(&a.ty, &env, &mut vec![(a.name.clone(), Vec::new())])?;\n    }\n    let mut enums: HashMap<String, Type> = HashMap::new();\n    for e in &file.enums {\n        enums.insert(\n            e.name.clone(),\n            resolve_enum(e, &env, &mut Vec::new(), None)?,\n        );\n    }\n    let mut variant_owners: HashMap<String, Vec<String>> = HashMap::new();\n    for e in &file.enums {\n        for v in &e.variants {\n            variant_owners\n                .entry(v.name.clone())\n                .or_default()\n                .push(e.name.clone());\n            // The lowercase spelling is the constructor, the value built right away (gh:156):\n            // a bare lowercase variant name resolves to the same owner so it reaches `construct`,\n            // which maps it to the declared variant. A declared name already lowercase (the\n            // prelude's `Opt`/`Result`) has the same constructor spelling, so pushing again would\n            // make one owner look like two.\n            let constructor = constructor_of(&v.name);\n            if constructor != v.name {\n                variant_owners\n                    .entry(constructor)\n                    .or_default()\n                    .push(e.name.clone());\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    let mut sigs = signatures(&file.defs, &env)?;\n    let input = RefCell::new(None);\n    let inputs = RefCell::new(None);\n    let dsv = RefCell::new(None);\n    let next_local = Cell::new(0);\n    let visibility: HashMap<String, (Origin, bool)> = file\n        .defs\n        .iter()\n        .map(|d| (d.name.clone(), (d.origin, d.is_pub)))\n        .collect();\n    // The first context carries `signatures`' provisional hoisted signatures (ret = the matched\n    // enum), enough to check their bodies; the inference pass below replaces each provisional\n    // return with the body's actual type and rebuilds the context before anything is checked.\n    let ctx = Ctx {\n        sigs: &sigs,\n        enums: &enums,\n        variant_owners: &variant_owners,\n        scope: Vec::new(),\n        arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n        subject: None,\n        input: &input,\n        inputs: &inputs,\n        lines_used: &lines_used,\n        dsv: &dsv,\n        in_mapper: false,\n        in_fn: None,\n        visibility: &visibility,\n        file: Origin::Program,\n        next_local: &next_local,\n    };\n    // Return-type inference for hoisted definitions (`fn name = expr`, gh:152): a hoisted\n    // function's signature is not written, so no body -- its own or another's -- may be checked\n    // against the provisional return `signatures` seeded. Checking each hoisted body once here\n    // fixes the real return, and rebuilding `sigs` first is what lets every later call, a\n    // recursive one included, resolve it.\n    for (name, sig) in infer_hoisted(&ctx, file.defs.iter())? {\n        sigs.insert(name, sig);\n    }\n    let ctx = Ctx {\n        sigs: &sigs,\n        enums: &enums,\n        variant_owners: &variant_owners,\n        scope: Vec::new(),\n        arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n        subject: None,\n        input: &input,\n        inputs: &inputs,\n        lines_used: &lines_used,\n        dsv: &dsv,\n        in_mapper: false,\n        in_fn: None,\n        visibility: &visibility,\n        file: Origin::Program,\n        next_local: &next_local,\n    };\n\n    // `prelude::inject` prepended prelude.toy's own defs to `file.defs`, which is what let\n    // `sigs` above resolve calls into them and is what catches a program that redefines one --\n    // but their bodies were already checked once, at build time (`build.rs`, via\n    // `prelude::checked`), and prelude.toy cannot have changed since. Rechecking them here would\n    // only repeat that work on every single compile.\n    let mut funcs = crate::prelude::checked();\n    let precompiled_names: HashSet<String> = funcs.iter().map(|f| f.name.clone()).collect();\n    let own_defs = file\n        .defs\n        .iter()\n        .filter(|d| !precompiled_names.contains(&d.name));\n    funcs.extend(check_defs(own_defs, &ctx)?);\n\n    let body = check_program_body(&ctx, &file.body)?;\n    // A stream cannot be printed, having nothing to show: it is not a value, and collect() is\n    // what turns it into one. A function body catches this for free, since its return\n    // annotation can never spell Stream and so can never match a body that contains one; the\n    // program's own result has no annotation to check against, so it needs asking directly.\n    if body.ty.contains_stream() {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            file.body.span(),\n            \"the program's result contains a stream, which has nothing to print; pass it to \\\n             `collect` first\"\n                .to_string(),\n        ));\n    }\n    // A Char has no wire form (the reverse of `input`'s refusal above), so a program cannot\n    // hand one to the printer either -- it has to build a Str first.\n    if body.ty.contains_char() {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            file.body.span(),\n            \"the program's result contains a Char, which has no wire form to print\".to_string(),\n        ));\n    }\n    let input = input.into_inner();\n    let inputs = inputs.into_inner();\n    // Forced by the backends, not chosen: Python's `input` reads all of stdin to EOF before\n    // parsing, leaving nothing for anything else to read afterward, and jq needs a different\n    // invocation flag for raw lines (`-R -n`) than for parsed JSON values (`-n` alone) -- one\n    // process cannot run with both. So all three ways of reading the same real stdin are\n    // mutually exclusive, not just `input` and `lines` as before.\n    if input.is_some() && lines_used.get() {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            file.body.span(),\n            \"a program cannot use both `input` and `lines`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n             different ways\"\n                .to_string(),\n        ));\n    }\n    if input.is_some() && inputs.is_some() {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            file.body.span(),\n            \"a program cannot use both `input` and `inputs`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n             different ways\"\n                .to_string(),\n        ));\n    }\n    if lines_used.get() && inputs.is_some() {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            file.body.span(),\n            \"a program cannot use both `lines` and `inputs`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n             different ways\"\n                .to_string(),\n        ));\n    }\n    // `dsv` reads the same raw lines `stdin` does, so it joins the same exclusivity: one real\n    // stdin, read one way.\n    let dsv = dsv.into_inner();\n    for (other, name) in [\n        (input.is_some(), \"`parse(stdin)`\"),\n        (inputs.is_some(), \"`stdin | map(parse(.))`\"),\n        (lines_used.get(), \"`stdin`\"),\n    ] {\n        if dsv.is_some() && other {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                file.body.span(),\n                format!(\n                    \"a program cannot use both `dsv` and {name}; they read the same real stdin \\\n                     two different ways\"\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n    Ok(tir::Program {\n        funcs: prune_unreachable(funcs, &body),\n        body,\n        input,\n        inputs,\n        uses_lines: lines_used.get(),\n        dsv,\n        enums,\n    })\n}\n\n/// What `check_defs` made of a definition's parameter: the TIR param name the backends bind\n/// the function's argument to, and, for a destructured one, the locals each named field was\n/// lowered to. The param name is a real user name for a plain parameter and a hidden one the body\n/// never sees for a destructured one.\nstruct LoweredParam {\n    name: String,\n    record: Option<LocalId>,\n    field_locals: Vec<(String, Span, LocalId, Type)>,\n}\n\n/// Signatures are collected before any body is checked, so a definition may call one that\n/// appears later in `defs`. This is also what recursion needs. No reachability pruning happens\n/// here: that needs the whole program's body, which `check_module` never has and `check` only\n/// gets once this returns, so both call this and prune afterward, over the combined result.\n/// `ctx`'s own per-def fields (`scope`, `subject`, `in_fn`, ...) are ignored -- each def gets its\n/// own, built from its signature exactly as `check` always has.\nfn check_defs<'a>(\n    defs: impl IntoIterator<Item = &'a crate::ast::Def>,\n    ctx: &Ctx<'a>,\n) -> Result<Vec<tir::Func>, Error> {\n    let mut funcs = Vec::new();\n    for def in defs {\n        // A hoisted definition (`fn name = expr`, gh:152) is checked by its own path: the\n        // signature is inferred rather than written, so none of the annotated-param machinery\n        // below applies. `infer_hoisted` already checked it once to fix the return type; this\n        // second check produces the `Func` the backends emit.\n        if def.hoisted {\n            funcs.push(check_hoisted_def(ctx, def)?);\n            continue;\n        }\n        let sig = &ctx.sigs[&def.name];\n        // A destructured parameter binds each named field to a fresh local, and its record arrives\n        // as a hidden single-name parameter the body never sees: the `Bind` chain below reads it off\n        // the backend's function argument, so a user name can never collide with it. The fields'\n        // `Some(local)` scope entries are what make `let` shadowing behave (kantord/toylang#144).\n        let (scope, lowered) = match (&def.param, &sig.param) {\n            (Some(param), Some(param_ty)) => match &param.shape {\n                ParamShape::Name(name, _) => (\n                    vec![(name.clone(), param_ty.clone(), None)],\n                    LoweredParam {\n                        name: name.clone(),\n                        record: None,\n                        field_locals: Vec::new(),\n                    },\n                ),\n                ParamShape::Fields(fields) => {\n                    let Type::Record(pfields) = param_ty else {\n                        return Err(Error::new(\n                            fields.span,\n                            format!(\n                                \"a destructuring parameter needs a record type, found {param_ty}\"\n                            ),\n                        ));\n                    };\n                    let record = ctx.fresh();\n                    let mut scope = Vec::new();\n                    let mut field_locals = Vec::new();\n                    for (i, (fname, fspan)) in fields.names.iter().enumerate() {\n                        if fields.names[..i].iter().any(|(seen,_)| seen == fname) {\n                            return Err(Error::new(\n                                *fspan,\n                                format!(\"`{fname}` is bound twice in this pattern\"),\n                            ));\n                        }\n                        let Some((_, fty)) = pfields.iter().find(|(n, _)| n == fname) else {\n                            return Err(Error::new(*fspan, format!(\"no field `{fname}` on {param_ty}\")));\n                        };\n                        let fid = ctx.fresh();\n                        scope.push((fname.clone(), fty.clone(), Some(fid)));\n                        field_locals.push((fname.clone(), *fspan, fid, fty.clone()));\n                    }\n                    // Leaving fields out of a destructuring parameter is a forgotten field until\n                    // `..` says it was meant, the same rule a match arm's pattern follows.\n                    if !fields.rest {\n                        let missing: Vec<String> = pfields\n                            .iter()\n                            .filter(|(n,_)| !fields.names.iter().any(|(m,_)| m == n))\n                            .map(|(n,_)| format!(\"`{n}`\"))\n                            .collect();\n                        if !missing.is_empty() {\n                            return Err(Error::new(\n                                fields.span,\n                                format!(\n                                    \"a destructuring parameter must name every field of its type \\\n                                     or end in `..`; missing {}\",\n                                    missing.join(\" and \")\n                                ),\n                            ));\n                        }\n                    }\n                    let name = format!(\"__{}_param\", def.name);\n                    (\n                        scope,\n                        LoweredParam {\n                            name,\n                            record: Some(record),\n                            field_locals,\n                        },\n                    )\n                }\n            },\n            (None, None) => (\n                Vec::new(),\n                LoweredParam {\n                    name: String::new(),\n                    record: None,\n                    field_locals: Vec::new(),\n                },\n            ),\n            _ => unreachable!(\"a signature's param mirrors its definition's\"),\n        };\n        let def_ctx = Ctx {\n            sigs: ctx.sigs,\n            enums: ctx.enums,\n            variant_owners: ctx.variant_owners,\n            scope,\n            arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n            subject: None,\n            input: ctx.input,\n            inputs: ctx.inputs,\n            lines_used: ctx.lines_used,\n            dsv: ctx.dsv,\n            in_mapper: false,\n            in_fn: Some(&def.name),\n            visibility: ctx.visibility,\n            file: ctx.file,\n            next_local: ctx.next_local,\n        };\n        // The declared return type flows into the body, so a form whose type comes from its\n        // position (`[]`, `input`, a variant-naming string) resolves against the annotation. A\n        // body that synthesises instead is compared below, where the error can name the\n        // function rather than just the two types.\n        //\n        // A function declared `-> Sink` is the other sink position, so its body must be a sink\n        // call -- the tail-pipeline `|>` form or a direct call to a sink; nothing else is a\n        // sink, and a body that is not one fails here with the declared-vs-found mismatch below\n        // naming the function.\n        let mut body = if sig.ret == Type::Sink {\n            if matches!(def.body, Expr::TailPipe { .. }) {\n                tail_pipe(&def_ctx, &def.body)?\n            } else if let Some(tir) = sink_call(&def_ctx, &def.body)? {\n                tir\n            } else {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    def.body.span(),\n                    format!(\n                        \"`{}` declares it returns Sink, so its body must be a sink call \\\n                         such as `jsonlines(...)` or `x |> jsonlines`\",\n                        def.name\n                    ),\n                ));\n            }\n        } else {\n            match expect_inner(&def_ctx, &def.body, &sig.ret)? {\n                Expected::Checked(body) => body,\n                Expected::Synthesised(body) => conform(&def_ctx, body, &sig.ret),\n            }\n        };\n        // A destructured parameter's record arrives as a hidden param and is bound to a fresh\n        // local, then each named field is projected off it -- the same `Bind` shape `let` uses, so\n        // every backend already knows how to run one. Field bindings wrap innermost-first so the\n        // record's own bind comes first.\n        if let Some(record) = lowered.record {\n            let param_ty = sig.param.clone().expect(\"a destructured param's type is Some\");\n            for (fname, _, fid, fty) in lowered.field_locals.iter().rev() {\n                let base = Tir::new(param_ty.clone(), Kind::Local(record));\n                body = Tir::new(\n                    body.ty.clone(),\n                    Kind::Bind {\n                        local: *fid,\n                        value: Box::new(Tir::new(\n                            fty.clone(),\n                            Kind::Field {\n                                base: Box::new(base),\n                                name: fname.clone(),\n                            },\n                        )),\n                        body: Box::new(body),\n                    },\n                );\n            }\n            body = Tir::new(\n                body.ty.clone(),\n                Kind::Bind {\n                    local: record,\n                    value: Box::new(Tir::new(param_ty, Kind::Var(lowered.name.clone()))),\n                    body: Box::new(body),\n                },\n            );\n        }\n        if let Some(param) = &def.param {\n            check_param(&body, param, &lowered, &sig.param, &def.name, def.body.span())?;\n        }\n        if body.ty != sig.ret {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                def.body.span(),\n                format!(\n                    \"`{}` declares it returns {}, but its body is {}\",\n                    def.name, sig.ret, body.ty\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n        funcs.push(tir::Func {\n            name: def.name.clone(),\n            param: if def.param.is_some() { Some(lowered.name.clone()) } else { None },\n            param_ty: sig.param.clone(),\n            body,\n        });\n    }\n    Ok(funcs)\n}\n\n/// Checks a module's own `pub` declarations in isolation -- `prelude.toy`'s, at build time\n/// (`build.rs`), before there is any file for them to be merged into and nothing yet calling any\n/// of it. Every declaration is kept: reachability is the calling file's question, decided once\n/// program and prelude are merged (`check` above, via `prune_unreachable`).\npub fn check_module(module: &crate::ast::Module) -> Result<Vec<tir::Func>, Error> {\n    let env = TypeEnv {\n        aliases: HashMap::new(),\n        enums: enum_map(&module.enums)?,\n    };\n    let mut enums: HashMap<String, Type> = HashMap::new();\n    for e in &module.enums {\n        enums.insert(\n            e.name.clone(),\n            resolve_enum(e, &env, &mut Vec::new(), None)?,\n        );\n    }\n    let mut variant_owners: HashMap<String, Vec<String>> = HashMap::new();\n    for e in &module.enums {\n        for v in &e.variants {\n            variant_owners\n                .entry(v.name.clone())\n                .or_default()\n                .push(e.name.clone());\n            let constructor = constructor_of(&v.name);\n            if constructor != v.name {\n                variant_owners\n                    .entry(constructor)\n                    .or_default()\n                    .push(e.name.clone());\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    let mut sigs = signatures(&module.defs, &env)?;\n    let input = RefCell::new(None);\n    let inputs = RefCell::new(None);\n    let lines_used = Cell::new(false);\n    let dsv = RefCell::new(None);\n    let next_local = Cell::new(0);\n    let visibility: HashMap<String, (Origin, bool)> = module\n        .defs\n        .iter()\n        .map(|d| (d.name.clone(), (d.origin, d.is_pub)))\n        .collect();\n    let ctx = Ctx {\n        sigs: &sigs,\n        enums: &enums,\n        variant_owners: &variant_owners,\n        scope: Vec::new(),\n        arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n        subject: None,\n        input: &input,\n        inputs: &inputs,\n        lines_used: &lines_used,\n        dsv: &dsv,\n        in_mapper: false,\n        in_fn: None,\n        visibility: &visibility,\n        file: Origin::Prelude,\n        next_local: &next_local,\n    };\n    for (name, sig) in infer_hoisted(&ctx, module.defs.iter())? {\n        sigs.insert(name, sig);\n    }\n    let ctx = Ctx {\n        sigs: &sigs,\n        enums: &enums,\n        variant_owners: &variant_owners,\n        scope: Vec::new(),\n        arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n        subject: None,\n        input: &input,\n        inputs: &inputs,\n        lines_used: &lines_used,\n        dsv: &dsv,\n        in_mapper: false,\n        in_fn: None,\n        visibility: &visibility,\n        file: Origin::Prelude,\n        next_local: &next_local,\n    };\n    check_defs(module.defs.iter(), &ctx)\n}\n\n/// A signature may spell Stream now, which un-does the trick the Lines design leaned on (a\n/// return annotation could never match a body holding a stream), so what that trick guaranteed\n/// for free is checked for real here. Called only when `def` takes a parameter: a nullary\n/// function has none of this to check.\nfn check_param(\n    body: &Tir,\n    param: &Param,\n    lowered: &LoweredParam,\n    param_ty: &Option<Type>,\n    func_name: &str,\n    body_span: Span,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    match &param.shape {\n        ParamShape::Name(name, _) => {\n            if matches!(param_ty, Some(Type::Stream(_))) {\n                check_linear(\n                    body,\n                    &StreamBinding::Param(name),\n                    &format!(\"`{}`\", name),\n                    body_span,\n                )?;\n            }\n            if !param_used(body, name) {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    param.span,\n                    format!(\n                        \"parameter `{}` is never used; delete it from `{}`'s definition and its call sites\",\n                        name, func_name\n                    ),\n                ));\n            }\n        }\n        // A destructured parameter's record type can never be a Stream (streams cannot live in\n        // records), so nothing linear to check; each named field is instead held to the same\n        // dead-code rule the plain parameter's name is, echoing what a match arm does to its\n        // pattern's fields.\n        ParamShape::Fields(fields) => {\n            for (fname, fspan) in &fields.names {\n                let (_, _, fid, _) = lowered\n                    .field_locals\n                    .iter()\n                    .find(|(n, _, _, _)| n == fname)\n                    .expect(\"the checker lowered every named field\");\n                if !local_used(body, *fid) {\n                    let hint = if fields.rest {\n                        \"remove it from the pattern\".to_string()\n                    } else {\n                        \"remove it from the pattern and close it with `..`\".to_string()\n                    };\n                    return Err(Error::new(\n                        *fspan,\n                        format!(\"`{fname}` is bound here but never used in the body; {hint}\"),\n                    ));\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n/// The program's own body. A sink-shaped body -- the tail-pipeline `lhs |> callee` or a direct\n/// sink call (`jsonlines(...)` or a call to a function declared `-> Sink`) -- is the one legal\n/// place a `Sink` may be born besides a `Sink`-returning function's body, so it is recognized\n/// ahead of `synth`, which refuses a sink everywhere else (the general position rule; see\n/// `tail_pipe` and `sink_call`). Any other body is synthesized, and a `Sink` result that slips\n/// past recognition can only mean a shape `synth` should have refused.\nfn check_program_body(ctx: &Ctx, body: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    if matches!(body, Expr::TailPipe { .. }) {\n        return tail_pipe(ctx, body);\n    }\n    if let Some(tir) = sink_call(ctx, body)? {\n        return Ok(tir);\n    }\n    synth(ctx, body)\n}\n\n/// Whether `body` is a direct sink call -- `jsonlines(...)` or a call to a function declared\n/// `-> Sink` -- and, if so, the `Sink`-typed Tir it checks to. The `|>` tail-pipeline is the\n/// other way a sink is written (see `tail_pipe`); both are legal in the program's body and a\n/// `Sink`-returning function's body, and everywhere else `synth` runs the general position\n/// rule and a `Sink` that slips past here is a shape `synth` should have refused.\nfn sink_call(ctx: &Ctx, body: &Expr) -> Result<Option<Tir>, Error> {\n    let Expr::Call {\n        func,\n        func_span,\n        arg,\n        span,\n    } = body\n    else {\n        return Ok(None);\n    };\n    if func == \"jsonlines\" {\n        return Ok(Some(jsonlines_call(ctx, arg, *span)?));\n    }\n    if ctx.sigs.get(func).is_some_and(|sig| sig.ret == Type::Sink) {\n        return Ok(Some(call(ctx, func, *func_span, arg, *span)?));\n    }\n    Ok(None)\n}\n\n/// `lhs |> callee`, the tail-pipeline marker: `callee` -- a sink -- applied to `lhs`. This is\n/// one of the two legal places a `Sink` may be born (the program's body and a `Sink`-returning\n/// function's body; the other is a direct sink call, see `sink_call`); everywhere else `synth`\n/// runs the general position rule and a `Sink` that slips past here is a shape `synth` should\n/// have refused. Only a `Sink`-typed callee is legal, which is the general rule the old\n/// hand-checked `jsonlines` position (kantord/toylang#151, #154) folded into: `jsonlines` is\n/// the one sink builtin, and every other callee must be a function whose declared return type\n/// is `Sink`.\nfn tail_pipe(ctx: &Ctx, expr: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let Expr::TailPipe {\n        lhs,\n        callee,\n        callee_span,\n        ..\n    } = expr\n    else {\n        unreachable!(\"tail_pipe is only called on a `|>` node\");\n    };\n    let value = synth(ctx, lhs)?;\n    if callee == \"jsonlines\" {\n        return jsonlines_arg(value, lhs.span());\n    }\n    let Some(sig) = ctx.sigs.get(callee.as_str()) else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            *callee_span,\n            format!(\n                \"the callee of `|>` must be a sink, such as `jsonlines` or a function that \\\n                 returns Sink; `{callee}` is not one\"\n            ),\n        ));\n    };\n    if sig.ret != Type::Sink {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            *callee_span,\n            format!(\n                \"the callee of `|>` must be a sink, but `{callee}` returns {}\",\n                sig.ret\n            ),\n        ));\n    }\n    let Some(param_ty) = &sig.param else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            *callee_span,\n            format!(\"`{callee}` takes no argument, so there is nothing for `|>` to pass it\"),\n        ));\n    };\n    if value.ty != *param_ty {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            lhs.span(),\n            format!(\"`{callee}` needs {param_ty}, found {}\", value.ty),\n        ));\n    }\n    let value = conform(ctx, value, param_ty);\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        Type::Sink,\n        Kind::Call {\n            func: callee.clone(),\n            arg: Some(Box::new(value)),\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\n/// Every function name the language itself provides, and therefore reserves. `str`, `range`,\n/// `chars`, and `i64` live in `builtin()`'s fixed table; `jsonlines`, `length`, `flatten`,\n/// `tail`, `collect`, `fields`, `sort`, `reverse`, `sum`, `max`, `parse`, `first`, `any`, and\n/// `all` are polymorphic and checked from `synth`/`expect_inner`'s own arms; `select` and `map`\n/// rebind `.`,and `sort_by`/`max_by` do the same with an orderable projection. All twenty-two are reserved the same way, and the docs harness (tests/docs.rs) reads\n/// this list to insist each one has a reference page.\npub const BUILTIN_NAMES: [&str; 22] = [\n    \"all\",\n    \"any\",\n    \"chars\",\n    \"collect\",\n    \"first\",\n    \"length\",\n    \"fields\",\n    \"flatten\",\n    \"i64\",\n    \"jsonlines\",\n    \"map\",\n    \"max\",\n    \"max_by\",\n    \"parse\",\n    \"range\",\n    \"reverse\",\n    \"select\",\n    \"sort\",\n    \"sort_by\",\n    \"str\",\n    \"sum\",\n    \"tail\",\n];\n\n/// Functions the language provides. Unary like every other function, so they need no special\n/// call syntax and are looked up before user definitions.\nfn builtin(name: &str) -> Option<(tir::Builtin, Sig)> {\n    let vec_of = |t: Type| Type::Vec(Box::new(t));\n    Some(match name {\n        \"str\" => (\n            tir::Builtin::IntToStr,\n            Sig {\n                param: Some(Type::Int),\n                ret: Type::Str,\n            },\n        ),\n        \"range\" => (\n            tir::Builtin::Range,\n            Sig {\n                param: Some(Type::Int),\n                ret: Type::Stream(Box::new(Type::Int)),\n            },\n        ),\n        \"chars\" => (\n            tir::Builtin::Chars,\n            Sig {\n                param: Some(Type::Str),\n                ret: vec_of(Type::Char),\n            },\n        ),\n        // The one bridge between the integer types (kantord/toylang#83). Its argument is an\n        // Int, so `i64(9000000000)` is refused like any other too-big Int literal: a wide\n        // value enters as a literal only where an Int64 is already expected.\n        \"i64\" => (\n            tir::Builtin::IntToI64,\n            Sig {\n                param: Some(Type::Int),\n                ret: Type::Int64,\n            },\n        ),\n        _ => return None,\n    })\n}\n\n/// A chain split by `rebase`: the rebased body, and the stream source it was split from.\nstruct Rebased {\n    body: Tir,\n    source: Tir,\n}\n\n/// Split an access chain whose outermost dimension is a stream from its source, rebasing the\n/// chain on `param` as a map body: every node's type loses its Stream wrapper, and an Index's\n/// stored depth drops by one, since the layer it counted is now the loop.\n///\n/// Only Field, Unwrap, and Index appear in a chain, and the source is never one of them: a\n/// stream-typed access chain is normalized to a Map right here, so one can never be the base of\n/// another.\nfn rebase(t: Tir, param: LocalId) -> Rebased {\n    fn peel(ty: Type) -> Type {\n        match ty {\n            Type::Stream(t) => *t,\n            other => unreachable!(\n                \"every node inside the stream dimension is stream-typed, found {other}\"\n            ),\n        }\n    }\n    fn is_chain(t: &Tir) -> bool {\n        matches!(\n            t.kind,\n            Kind::Field { .. } | Kind::Unwrap { .. } | Kind::Index { .. }\n        )\n    }\n    /// The base of one link in the chain: another link to recurse into, or the stream source\n    /// itself, in which case `param` stands in for the per-element value the map body reads.\n    fn split_base(base: Tir, param: LocalId) -> Rebased {\n        if is_chain(&base) {\n            rebase(base, param)\n        } else {\n            let elem = peel(base.ty.clone());\n            Rebased {\n                body: Tir::new(elem, Kind::Local(param)),\n                source: base,\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    match t.kind {\n        Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n            let Rebased { body: base, source } = split_base(*base, param);\n            Rebased {\n                body: Tir::new(\n                    peel(t.ty),\n                    Kind::Field {\n                        base: Box::new(base),\n                        name,\n                    },\n                ),\n                source,\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n            let Rebased { body: base, source } = split_base(*base, param);\n            Rebased {\n                body: Tir::new(\n                    peel(t.ty),\n                    Kind::Unwrap {\n                        base: Box::new(base),\n                    },\n                ),\n                source,\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Index {\n            base,\n            index,\n            depth,\n            elem_is_record,\n        } => {\n            let Rebased { body: base, source } = split_base(*base, param);\n            let kind = Kind::Index {\n                base: Box::new(base),\n                index,\n                depth: depth - 1,\n                elem_is_record,\n            };\n            Rebased {\n                body: Tir::new(peel(t.ty), kind),\n                source,\n            }\n        }\n        other => unreachable!(\n            \"only an access chain is rebased, found {:?}\",\n            std::mem::discriminant(&other)\n        ),\n    }\n}\n\n/// The element `select` and `map` rebind `.` to: a Vec's, or a Stream's. Deliberately not\n/// `Type::elem`, which stays Vec-only so the reducers (`length`, `jsonlines` today) keep\n/// refusing a stream.\nfn mapper_elem(subject: &Type) -> Option<Type> {\n    match subject {\n        Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => Some((**t).clone()),\n        _ => None,\n    }\n}\n\n/// The context a mapper body (`select`'s predicate, `map`'s body) checks in: `.` rebound to the\n/// element a fresh `param` will hold at runtime, with a source read refused since the body runs\n/// once per element rather than once.\nfn mapper_ctx<'a>(ctx: &'a Ctx, elem: Type, param: LocalId) -> Ctx<'a> {\n    let mut inner = ctx.with(Some((elem, param)));\n    inner.in_mapper = true;\n    inner\n}\n\n/// The one enum a bare variant name refers to, or the error naming every candidate: guessing\n/// between two claimants would silently pick a type the program never wrote down.\nfn sole_owner<'a>(\n    ctx: &'a Ctx,\n    variant: &str,\n    owners: &[String],\n    span: Span,\n) -> Result<&'a Type, Error> {\n    if owners.len() > 1 {\n        let named: Vec<String> = owners.iter().map(|e| format!(\"`{e}`\")).collect();\n        let qualified: Vec<String> = owners.iter().map(|e| format!(\"`{e}.{variant}`\")).collect();\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            span,\n            format!(\n                \"`{variant}` is a variant of {}; qualify it as {}\",\n                named.join(\" and \"),\n                qualified.join(\" or \")\n            ),\n        ));\n    }\n    Ok(&ctx.enums[&owners[0]])\n}\n\n/// Every builtin and special-cased call form (`select`, `map`, `length`, ...) is unary; only a\n/// user-defined function can be nullary. This unwraps a call's optional argument for those\n/// forms, turning a bare `name()` at one of these names into the same \"not a function\" shape as\n/// any other arity mismatch would need, rather than a panic.\nfn need_arg<'a>(arg: &'a Option<Box<Expr>>, func: &str, span: Span) -> Result<&'a Expr, Error> {\n    arg.as_deref()\n        .ok_or_else(|| Error::new(span, format!(\"`{func}` needs an argument\")))\n}\n\n/// `expr` is a call to `name` carrying an argument, for `expect_inner`'s `collect`/`map`\n/// special cases: a bare `name()` (no argument) is not one of these, and falls through to\n/// `synth`, which owns the arity error for a name that is not actually nullary.\nfn call_named<'a>(expr: &'a Expr, name: &str) -> Option<&'a Expr> {\n    let Expr::Call { func, arg, .. } = expr else {\n        return None;\n    };\n    if func != name {\n        return None;\n    }\n    arg.as_deref()\n}\n\n/// `name`'s variant list at `args`. A thin wrapper over the shared re-derivation\n/// (`ty::variants_of`), which the backends reach for too: the checker's own `Type::Enum` in\n/// hand carries a placeholder wherever a self-reference sits behind a `Vec`\n/// (`types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76), so every consumer that actually needs the\n/// variants comes through here instead, one layer at a time, exactly as deep as the program\n/// itself ever navigates into the value.\nfn enum_variants(ctx: &Ctx, name: &str, args: &[Type]) -> Vec<(String, Option<Type>)> {\n    ty::variants_of(ctx.enums, name, args)\n}\n\n/// The prelude's `Opt<T>` at a concrete `T`: what every Opt-producing site -- the collapsing\n/// index, `tail`, a partial guard chain -- wraps its result in. Instantiated from the\n/// registry template rather than spelled out, so the checker never re-states the prelude's\n/// declaration.\nfn opt_of(ctx: &Ctx, inner: Type) -> Type {\n    let template = ctx.enums.get(\"Opt\").expect(\"the prelude declares Opt\");\n    let Type::Enum { args, .. } = template else {\n        unreachable!(\"the registry holds enum types\")\n    };\n    let Type::Param(p) = &args[0] else {\n        unreachable!(\"Opt's template argument is its own parameter\")\n    };\n    ty::substitute(template, &HashMap::from([(p.clone(), inner)]))\n}\n\n/// Match a template type (parameters possibly inside) against a concrete one, binding each\n/// parameter to what it faces. Records match fields by name, not position, the same way type\n/// equality does (kantord/toylang#60); enums match on name and arguments, since the variants\n/// follow from those.\nfn unify(template: &Type, concrete: &Type, map: &mut HashMap<String, Type>) -> bool {\n    match (template, concrete) {\n        (Type::Param(p), c) => match map.get(p) {\n            Some(bound) => bound == c,\n            None => {\n                map.insert(p.clone(), c.clone());\n                true\n            }\n        },\n        (Type::Vec(a), Type::Vec(b)) | (Type::Stream(a), Type::Stream(b)) => unify(a, b, map),\n        (Type::Record(a), Type::Record(b)) => {\n            a.len() == b.len()\n                && a.iter().all(|(n, t)| {\n                    b.iter()\n                        .find(|(bn, _)| bn == n)\n                        .is_some_and(|(_, bt)| unify(t, bt, map))\n                })\n        }\n        (\n            Type::Enum {\n                name: n1, args: a1, ..\n            },\n            Type::Enum {\n                name: n2, args: a2, ..\n            },\n        ) => n1 == n2 && a1.len() == a2.len() && a1.iter().zip(a2).all(|(x, y)| unify(x, y, map)),\n        _ => template == concrete,\n    }\n}\n\n/// Build one variant of `enum_ty`, checking that the payload written matches the payload\n/// declared: a unit variant takes none, a payload variant requires one.\n///\n/// `enum_ty` is a registry template for a generic enum reached by name, or a concrete type\n/// when the position already knows one (an expectation, or a written annotation). A template\n/// instantiates from the expectation when `expected` names the same enum; otherwise the\n/// payload's synthesised type binds the parameters, and a variant that leaves any parameter\n/// open -- every unit variant of a generic enum does -- is refused, the `[]` answer.\nfn construct(\n    ctx: &Ctx,\n    enum_ty: &Type,\n    variant: &str,\n    variant_span: Span,\n    payload: Option<&Expr>,\n    expected: Option<&Type>,\n    from_string: bool,\n) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = enum_ty else {\n        unreachable!(\"construct is only called with an enum type\")\n    };\n    let is_template = args.iter().any(|a| matches!(a, Type::Param(_)));\n    let instantiated;\n    let enum_ty = if !is_template {\n        enum_ty\n    } else if let Some(want @ Type::Enum { name: wanted, .. }) = expected\n        && wanted == name\n    {\n        want\n    } else {\n        instantiated = infer_instantiation(ctx, enum_ty, variant, variant_span, payload)?;\n        return Ok(instantiated);\n    };\n    let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = enum_ty else {\n        unreachable!(\"construct is only called with an enum type\")\n    };\n    let variants = enum_variants(ctx, name, args);\n    // A capitalized name is the matcher, legal only in a pattern; a value is built with the\n    // lowercase constructor, which resolves to the declared (capitalized) variant. A string\n    // literal naming a variant is the wire's spelling, so it is exempt -- see the caller.\n    if !from_string && variant.chars().next().is_some_and(char::is_uppercase) {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            variant_span,\n            format!(\n                \"a constructor starts with a lowercase letter; `{variant}` is the matcher and \\\n                 matches only in a pattern, so write `{}` to build the value\",\n                constructor_of(variant)\n            ),\n        ));\n    }\n    let Some((variant_name, declared)) = variants\n        .iter()\n        .find(|(n, _)| n == variant || is_constructor_of(n, variant))\n    else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            variant_span,\n            format!(\"`{name}` has no variant `{variant}`\"),\n        ));\n    };\n    let payload = match (declared, payload) {\n        (None, None) => None,\n        (Some(want), Some(expr)) => Some(Box::new(expect(ctx, expr, want)?)),\n        (None, Some(expr)) => {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                expr.span(),\n                format!(\"`{variant}` is a unit variant of `{name}` and takes no payload\"),\n            ));\n        }\n        (Some(want), None) => {\n            // The hint mirrors the declaration's two spellings: braces for a record payload,\n            // parens for any other type.\n            let spelled = match want {\n                Type::Record(_) => format!(\"{variant}{{...}}\"),\n                _ => format!(\"{variant}(...)\"),\n            };\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                variant_span,\n                format!(\n                    \"`{variant}` of `{name}` carries a payload of {want}, so it is written `{spelled}`\"\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n    };\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        enum_ty.clone(),\n        Kind::EnumLit {\n            // The wire carries the matcher's name, the declared variant (`Circle`), so a backend\n            // reader and printer can agree on it without lowering; the lowercase `variant` is the\n            // constructor, the source spelling that builds the value (gh:156).\n            variant: variant_name.to_string(),\n            payload,\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\n/// A generic enum's constructor met with no expectation: the payload is synthesised and its\n/// type binds the parameters. The template's own display (`Pair<T>`) does the talking in the\n/// errors, since it shows the reader exactly which names are still open.\nfn infer_instantiation(\n    ctx: &Ctx,\n    template: &Type,\n    variant: &str,\n    variant_span: Span,\n    payload: Option<&Expr>,\n) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = template else {\n        unreachable!(\"construct is only called with an enum type\")\n    };\n    let variants = enum_variants(ctx, name, args);\n    if variant.chars().next().is_some_and(char::is_uppercase) {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            variant_span,\n            format!(\n                \"a constructor starts with a lowercase letter; `{variant}` is the matcher and \\\n                 matches only in a pattern, so write `{}` to build the value\",\n                constructor_of(variant)\n            ),\n        ));\n    }\n    let Some((variant_name, declared)) = variants\n        .iter()\n        .find(|(n, _)| n == variant || is_constructor_of(n, variant))\n    else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            variant_span,\n            format!(\"`{name}` has no variant `{variant}`\"),\n        ));\n    };\n    let (declared, expr) = match (declared, payload) {\n        (Some(declared), Some(expr)) => (declared, expr),\n        (None, Some(expr)) => {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                expr.span(),\n                format!(\"`{variant}` is a unit variant of `{name}` and takes no payload\"),\n            ));\n        }\n        (Some(want), None) => {\n            let spelled = match want {\n                Type::Record(_) => format!(\"{variant}{{...}}\"),\n                _ => format!(\"{variant}(...)\"),\n            };\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                variant_span,\n                format!(\n                    \"`{variant}` of `{name}` carries a payload of {want}, so it is written `{spelled}`\"\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n        (None, None) => {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                variant_span,\n                format!(\n                    \"cannot tell what `{template}` a bare `{variant}` builds; only a position \\\n                     that expects one can say\"\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n    };\n    let built = synth(ctx, expr)?;\n    let mut bindings = HashMap::new();\n    if !unify(declared, &built.ty, &mut bindings) {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            expr.span(),\n            format!(\"expected {declared}, found {}\", built.ty),\n        ));\n    }\n    if let Type::Enum { args, .. } = template\n        && let Some(open) = args.iter().find_map(|a| match a {\n            Type::Param(p) if !bindings.contains_key(p) => Some(p),\n            _ => None,\n        })\n    {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            variant_span,\n            format!(\n                \"`{variant}`'s payload does not say what `{template}`'s `{open}` is; only a \\\n                 position that expects one can\"\n            ),\n        ));\n    }\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        ty::substitute(template, &bindings),\n        Kind::EnumLit {\n            variant: variant_name.to_string(),\n            payload: Some(Box::new(built)),\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\n/// A source spelled inside a `fn` body. The rule exists because the mapper-body check alone had\n/// a cross-function hole: a function reading a source, called from a mapper's body, re-read\n/// stdin once per element with nothing at the call site to refuse. Refusing sources in every\n/// `fn` closes that everywhere; the alternative, tracking source consumption per function\n/// transitively, was rejected as an invisible effect on every signature.\nfn source_in_fn(span: Span, source: &str, func: &str) -> Error {\n    Error::new(\n        span,\n        format!(\n            \"`{source}` cannot be read inside `fn {func}`; a source is legal only in the \\\n             program's own body, so take the stream through a `Stream` parameter\"\n        ),\n    )\n}\n\n/// The one exit a stream has: `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`. `want_elem` is `Some` in the checking\n/// direction, needed because `collect(inputs)` has no type to synthesise until the wanted\n/// Vec<T> pushes a Stream<T> expectation down onto `inputs`; it is `None` in the synthesising\n/// direction, where the argument's own type says what T is.\nfn collect(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr, want_elem: Option<&Type>) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let (elem, arg) = match want_elem {\n        Some(elem) => (\n            elem.clone(),\n            expect(ctx, arg, &Type::Stream(Box::new(elem.clone())))?,\n        ),\n        None => {\n            let arg_span = arg.span();\n            let arg = synth(ctx, arg)?;\n            let Type::Stream(elem) = &arg.ty else {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    arg_span,\n                    format!(\"`collect` needs a stream, found {}\", arg.ty),\n                ));\n            };\n            (elem.as_ref().clone(), arg)\n        }\n    };\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        Type::Vec(Box::new(elem)),\n        Kind::Builtin {\n            which: tir::Builtin::Collect,\n            arg: Box::new(arg),\n        },","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":1275,"totalLines":1276,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/check/mod.rs","time":{"start":1788705160209,"end":1788705160215}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the compile pipeline: `compile(src)` parses, injects prelude, checks. There's no path info. For module-as-function routing, we need the path of the referring file to resolve relative paths. \n\nHmm. Let me think about the scope. The design says: \"The path resolves relative to the referring file at compile time; the checker loads the module, reads `handle`'s signature, and checks it against the subject type and the arm's expected type.\"\n\nSo we need:\n1. A new AST node for the `@(path)` arm body.\n2. Parser support.\n3. Checker support: resolve path, load module, parse as module, find `handle`, check signature against subject type (param type) and arm's expected type (return type). Also visibility: entry function must be `pub`? The module's `handle` should be callable from outside. Since a submodule's `handle` is being called from another file, it needs to be `pub` -- the visibility rule: non-pub is callable only from its own file.\n4. Emission: the backends need to emit code for this. Since the module's function is checked statically and compiled, the checker can inline or lower the module dispatch to a call to `handle`? Or, since the module functions are compiled the same way prelude functions are...\n\nHmm, wait. How does prelude injection work? `prelude::inject` merges the prelude's defs into the file, then the checker checks everything and prunes unreachable. So the backends emit the program body + reachable functions as one file.\n\nFor a module dispatch, the natural implementation: when the checker resolves `@(path)`, it loads the module, parses it, finds the `pub fn handle`, and checks that a call to `handle` from the current file is valid. Then, at emission time, the module's defs need to be available. \n\nHow do we get them into the emitted program? One approach: treat the submodule like the prelude: inject its defs into the file at compile time, and the dispatch node becomes an ordinary call to `handle`. But the checker needs to know the param/ret types to check the call. Actually if we inject the submodule defs into the file, the existing call-checking machinery handles it. The dispatch node `@(path)` could be lowered by the checker to a `Call` to the function named `handle`... but there could be multiple modules each with `handle`, causing name collisions.\n\nHmm. Let me think about how the checker works, how functions are resolved, and how calls are checked. Let me read check/mod.rs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_07722bc0e001FdZXW4wGbv0RmE","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_077229a39001mSIPTzjHsLkwUe"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788705160887,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"id":"prt_07722beaf00158x7c35PTl8yWX","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"307d242a582f94e0a9fe102c3e65441553e7eb4b","messageID":"msg_077229a39001mSIPTzjHsLkwUe","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":67330,"input":11399,"output":20,"reasoning":615,"cache":{"write":0,"read":55296}},"cost":0.001185935436}}
