{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705205918,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_78ea2035f7424074a569839a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls plans | head -80"},"output":"benchmark-plan.md\nbenchmark-suite-spike.md\nbenchmark-tooling-spike.md\nboard-archive.yaml\nboard.yaml\nchecker-structure.md\ncrate-simplification.md\ndense-tensor-design-research.md\ndocs-site.md\ndraft-split.md\nemuto-survey.md\nenums.md\nerlang-target-research.md\neuler-ergonomics.md\nfloat-format-research.md\nincidents\nissue-93-privileged-agent-prompt.md\nlanguage-oddities.md\nlanguage-precedents.md\nmatcher-parser-spike.md\nmini-parser-spike.md\nmodule-routing-research.md\nmutation-semantics-spike.md\nopencode-rollout.md\nopt-as-enum.md\nprelude-inventory.md\nprompt-efficiency-review.md\nprompt-experiment.md\nprototype_1_5.md\nprototype_1_5_step_1.md\nprototype_1_5_step_2.md\nprototype_1_5_step_3.md\nprototype_1_5_step_4.md\nprototype_1_5_step_5.md\nprototype_1_5_step_6.md\nprototype_1.md\nprototype_1_step_1.md\nprototype_1_step_2.md\nprototype_1_step_3.md\nprototype_1_step_4.md\nprototype_1_step_5.md\nquality-practices.md\nquestions.md\nselect-materialization-research.md\nsignature-matching-deeper-research.md\nstdout-stderr-effect-model-research.md\nstreams.md\nstring-type-spike.md\ntrait-interface-research.md\ntype-flow.md\nworker-pool.md\n","metadata":{"output":"benchmark-plan.md\nbenchmark-suite-spike.md\nbenchmark-tooling-spike.md\nboard-archive.yaml\nboard.yaml\nchecker-structure.md\ncrate-simplification.md\ndense-tensor-design-research.md\ndocs-site.md\ndraft-split.md\nemuto-survey.md\nenums.md\nerlang-target-research.md\neuler-ergonomics.md\nfloat-format-research.md\nincidents\nissue-93-privileged-agent-prompt.md\nlanguage-oddities.md\nlanguage-precedents.md\nmatcher-parser-spike.md\nmini-parser-spike.md\nmodule-routing-research.md\nmutation-semantics-spike.md\nopencode-rollout.md\nopt-as-enum.md\nprelude-inventory.md\nprompt-efficiency-review.md\nprompt-experiment.md\nprototype_1_5.md\nprototype_1_5_step_1.md\nprototype_1_5_step_2.md\nprototype_1_5_step_3.md\nprototype_1_5_step_4.md\nprototype_1_5_step_5.md\nprototype_1_5_step_6.md\nprototype_1.md\nprototype_1_step_1.md\nprototype_1_step_2.md\nprototype_1_step_3.md\nprototype_1_step_4.md\nprototype_1_step_5.md\nquality-practices.md\nquestions.md\nselect-materialization-research.md\nsignature-matching-deeper-research.md\nstdout-stderr-effect-model-research.md\nstreams.md\nstring-type-spike.md\ntrait-interface-research.md\ntype-flow.md\nworker-pool.md\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls plans | head -80","time":{"start":1788705205914,"end":1788705205916}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I've seen enough of the checker's structure. Let me now think about the implementation strategy.\n\nKey challenge: The compiler's `compile(src)` takes only a source string -- no file path. But the module dispatch needs to resolve relative paths. So I need to thread the file path into the pipeline.\n\nLet me look at how the checker handles `expect_inner` and `synth`. Also, I need to see `check::types.rs` for `signatures`, which collects signatures from defs.\n\nNow let me think about the design holistically.\n\nThe design from the research doc:\n- Candidate C: `@path` in an arm body, resolved by the checker specially.\n- The maintainer refined: `@(path)` (parenthesized.\n\n\"The path resolves relative to the referring file at compile time; the checker loads the module, reads `handle`'s signature, and checks it against the subject type and the arm's expected type, the way it checks any call.\"\n\nSo the checker, when it encounters the `@(path)` node in an arm body:\n1. Needs the referring file's directory (to resolve the relative path).\n2. Loads the module file, parses it as a module.\n\n3. Finds the entry function `handle` (must be `pub fn handle`).\n4. Checks the visibility rule: since the module file is a different file, `handle` must be `pub` (a non-pub handle can only be called from its own file).\n5. Checks the signature against the subject type (the arm's `.` type) and the arm's expected body type (the return type).\n6. Emits:the module's `handle` body must be compiled (like the prelude's defs), and a `Kind::Call` to `handle` emitted.\n\nBut name collisions: each module's `handle` is called `handle`. If a program dispatches to two modules, both have `handle`, and the emitted code has two functions named `handle`. So we need to rename them per-module.\n\n\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me think about the approach that best fits the codebase's existing structure.\n\n\n\nOption A: Inline the module's entry function at the dispatch site. That is, when the checker sees `@(path)`, it loads the module file, gets the `handle` definition, and inlines its body (checked against the subject/expected types) directly into the arm. This is simple: no new function naming, no name collisions, no emission changes. The dispatch node becomes just the checked body of the module's `handle`. \n\nBut: the module's `handle` may call other functions in its module (private helpers, other pub functions). Inlining just the entry function's body would require inlining its callees recursively, or emitting those as functions. If the module's `handle` calls a private helper, and we inline handle's body, that helper call needs to resolve. Since the helper is a function in the module file, we could... inline transitively. That could explode.\n\n\n\nOption B: Compile the module's functions into the program's `funcs` list with unique names, and lower the dispatch to a call. This is like how the prelude works: inject defs into the file, check them, emit them. But we'd need unique names for each module's functions (since two modules can both define `handle`, `helper`, etc.).\n\nHmm, wait. Let me look at how the program is emitted: `tir::Func { name, param, param_ty, body }`. Functions are named; a call is `Kind::Call { func, arg }` referring to the func name. If two modules each contribute a `handle`, we need to distinguish.\n\nThe cleanest: when loading a module, prefix all its function names with the module path (e.g., `route-groups/baz.toy::handle` or a sanitized version). Then the dispatch lowers to a call to that prefixed name.\n\n\n\nBut wait, there's a deeper question: does this feature need to work through all seven backends? The corpus runs on every backend. The module dispatch would be emitted as a call to a function, which is already supported. The new machinery is all compile-time (checker-side): loading the module, resolving names, checking signatures, and emitting the module's functions as part of the program's funcs. The backends don't need to know about module dispatch at all if we lower it to an ordinary call to an ordinary function name that the module's defs were compiled under.\n\nThat's the elegant approach: \n1. `parse` produces `Expr::Module { path, span }` for `@(path)`.\n2. The checker, when it sees this node (in `synth_inner` or in arm body position), needs the referring file path to resolve the module path relative. Hmm, but `check` takes a `&File`, no path. \n\nSo I need to thread the referring file path into the AST or into the check. Let me think about where the path should live.\n\nThe AST: `Expr::Module { path: String, span }` -- the path as written (`route-groups/baz.toy`). For resolution relative to the referring file, we need to know which file this expression came from. \n\nWhere does the \"referring file\" come from? The program file, or a module file (if modules can dispatch to modules recursively). \n\nSo actually the natural place: an AST-level field on the `Expr::Module` node: the resolved absolute path (computed at parse time or at a resolution pass). But the parser doesn't know the file path either...\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. The compiler `compile(src)` is called from main.rs after reading a file, and from tests. To resolve relative paths, we need the referring file's path. \n\nApproach: add a `path: Option<PathBuf>` parameter to `compile` and thread through to the checker. The parser produces `Expr::Module { path: String (as written), span }`; the checker resolves it relative to `ctx.file_path` (the file currently being checked). Each module file loaded also has its own path (for recursive dispatch).\n\nBut wait -- is recursive dispatch in scope? The research doc's open question: \"Whether a route submodule ever needs its own submodules (the http-router example suggests it might, recursively).\" Not ruled. And the issue doesn't explicitly rule recursive. But keep scope minimal: the task is to implement the basic form. I'll design it so recursion works naturally but not force it. Actually, if we lower the module dispatch to a call to the module's functions compiled into the program, then the module's own body can also contain module dispatches (recursive), since we check the module's defs with the same machinery. That would work if the checker has the module's file path for resolving nested relative paths.\n\n\n\nLet me think about the simplest complete implementation:\n\n1. AST: add `Expr::Module { path: String, span: Span }`.\n\n2. Parser: read `@` as a token, then `(`, then read a raw path until `)`, then `)`. Only valid in an arm body? Or anywhere? The research doc says \"a new arm-body kind reserved for module dispatch\". I'll parse it at the atom level (`atom()`), so it can appear anywhere syntactically, but the checker enforces it only appears... hmm, actually the checker doesn't need to enforce position if `synth_inner` handles it. But semantically it's \"applied to the subject\", like `select`/`map` need a subject. So the checker's handler for `Expr::Module` should require `ctx.subject` is `Some`, just like `map`/`select`/`match_chain` do. That gives the \"must follow `|`\" rule for free, consistent with matches.\n\nBut wait, the arm body: an arm's `.` is the subject (for a payload arm, the payload; for a guard/default arm, the subject. So `@(path)` as an arm body dispatches on the arm's `.`, which is exactly what we want.\n\nActually hold on, in a match arm, the body's `.` is... let me check. In `match_chain`, for a variant arm, `arm_ctx = ctx.with(Some((pty, pid)))` -- `.` is the payload. For unit variants, `ctx.with(None)` -- `.` is not bound. For guard/default, `.` stays the subject. So if `@(path)` requires a subject, a unit-variant arm body `Foo -> @(baz.toy)` would error (\"`.` is not bound here\" via the subject requirement. Hmm, that means dispatch from a unit variant arm can't work -- but that's fine semantically: there's no value to pass to `handle` if the variant has no payload. Wait, actually the subject is the enum value itself (the whole enum. Hmm.\n\nLet me re-read: In toylang, a match's subject is the enum value flowing through the pipe. A variant arm `FooBar -> body` rebinds `.` in the body to... for a unit variant, `.` is NOT bound (None). For a payload variant, `.` is the payload.\n\nHmm, that's interesting. So for the router example:\n\n```\nenum Route { FooBar, Baz, Qux }\nfn router(r: Route) -> Response =\n    r | FooBar -> @(route-groups/foo-bar.toy)\n      or Baz   -> @(route-groups/baz.toy)\n```\n\n`FooBar` is a unit variant, so `.` in the body is None -- not bound. So the dispatch would need a subject, but there is none. Hmm.\n\nThat suggests `@(path)` as the arm body would NOT work for unit variants under the current `.` semantics...\n\nBut wait -- maybe the intended semantics is different. Let me re-read the research doc:\n\n\"The subject arrives through the pipe, so the submodule is applied to `.` in every case.\"\n\nAnd: \"`$(path)` is an expression:the entry function of that module, applied to the subject.\"\n\nHmm, so `@(path)` applies the module's entry function to `.` -- the subject. In a unit-variant arm, `.` is not bound... So the router example in the doc with unit variants wouldn't typecheck. But the doc's example was a sketch.\n\n\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. In the doc's example:\n\n```\nenum Route { FooBar, Baz, Qux }\nfn router(r: Route) -> Response =\n    r | FooBar -> @(route-groups/foo-bar.toy)   ...\n```\n\nHmm. Maybe the intent: the whole enum value `r` is passed to `handle`, not just the payload. I.e., the dispatch is \"run this module on the subject\" where the subject is the enum value the match chain received. So a unit variant arm would dispatch `handle` on the whole `Route` value?\n\nBut that contradicts how match arms rebind `.`. Hmm.\n\nActually wait. Let me re-read `variant_arm`: a payload variant \"rebinds `.` to a fresh payload local\". A unit variant arm gets `ctx.with(None)`. So `.` is only the payload for payload variants.\n\n\n\nHmm, so under the current semantics, for `FooBar -> @(path)`, the body's `.` is unbound, and the dispatch can't happen. That would make the router example not compile. Unless the design intends that the dispatch happens on the arm's `.`, which for payload variants is the payload and for unit variants is nothing (error.\n\nHmm, but this is the maintainer's example. Let me look for more design docs. Let me search plans/ for the round's rulings, or the board row. The issue says \"Ruled in the module-routing-and-erlang-target grilling round (question module-as-function-routing-design): option C, a dedicated routing-arm form\". Let me look for the round notes.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_077236e8b001F48Hf8PQVp25xG","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_0772314fc001SbOXhCBztpJKa5"}}
