{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705219568,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_526b289331294101a24848ca","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/emit_toylang.rs","limit":300,"offset":1},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/emit_toylang.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The formatter's own backend: re-renders a parsed file as toylang source, in one canonical\n2: //! style. Unlike the other `emit_*` backends this walks `ast::File`, not `tir::Program` -- a\n3: //! formatter has to work on a program that does not type-check, and it must not print the\n4: //! prelude that `prelude::inject` would otherwise splice in.\n5: //!\n6: //! Two things drive the design. First, minimal-but-correct parenthesization: the AST does not\n7: //! record which parens the source actually used, so every paren in the output is one this module\n8: //! decided it needed, by comparing a node's own operator power against the power the position it\n9: //! sits in requires -- the same table `parse.rs::infix_power` parses with, walked in reverse.\n10: //! Second, the maintainer's own sample (docs/examples/euler/01-multiples-of-3-and-5.md) is the\n11: //! only ground truth for layout: a function whose signature-plus-body fits on one line stays on\n12: //! one line; otherwise the body moves to its own indented line, and a binary chain that still\n13: //! does not fit breaks at its outermost operator, trailing the operator on the first line.\n14: //! Match-arm chains extend that same \"trailing operator, one extra indent\" rule by analogy,\n15: //! since nothing in the sample pins their layout directly. A pipeline that does not fit is the\n16: //! one exception: it breaks one stage per line, with `|` leading each continuation line so the\n17: //! pipes form a vertical column (maintainer directive, issue #101), rather than trailing like\n18: //! the other chains.\n19: //!\n20: //! Two style choices worth naming since nothing in the grammar forces them: calls are always\n21: //! written with explicit parens (`f(x)`, never the bare `f x` or brace-shorthand `f{...}`),\n22: //! matching both calls in the sample; and the wrap width is 80 columns, backed out from the\n23: //! sample itself -- the one line it left alone is 44 columns, and the two it broke are 89 and\n24: //! 118.\n25: //!\n26: //! One thing the AST cannot carry: comments. `parse::parse` throws every `#...` away as trivia\n27: //! (`skip_trivia`), so `emit` alone can only ever produce a program with none. `format_source`\n28: //! covers the one shape that actually appears in this repository -- a file's leading banner,\n29: //! read back off the raw text rather than the tree -- and nothing else; a comment anywhere else\n30: //! does not survive formatting, which is why every fragment that puts one somewhere else is\n31: //! marked a syntax-spelling exception rather than swept.\n32: \n33: use crate::ast::{\n34:     Alias, BinOp, Def, EnumDecl, Expr, FieldsPattern, File, LogicOp, MatchArm, Module, Param,\n35:     ParamShape, Pattern, TypeExpr, Variant,\n36: };\n37: \n38: const WIDTH: usize = 80;\n39: const INDENT: usize = 4;\n40: \n41: // Mirrors parse.rs's precedence table exactly (`infix_power`, `PIPE_LEFT`/`PIPE_RIGHT`,\n42: // `COND_POWER`, `OR_LEFT`/`OR_RIGHT`, `NOT_POWER`): the numbers a paren decision here has to\n43: // answer to are the parser's, not this module's own invention.\n44: const PIPE_LEFT: u8 = 1;\n45: const PIPE_RIGHT: u8 = 2;\n46: const COND_POWER: u8 = 3;\n47: const NOT_POWER: u8 = 8;\n48: \n49: /// A match arm's body is printed at a power just above `or`'s, which is how the parser's second\n50: /// reading of `or` -- the arm separator, which has no power at all -- reaches this table: a bare\n51: /// disjunction in an arm body is the one expression whose parens the powers alone would not ask\n52: /// for, and the one the parser would otherwise read as two arms.\n53: const ARM_BODY: u8 = 5;\n54: \n55: fn bin_power(op: BinOp) -> (u8, u8) {\n56:     match op {\n57:         BinOp::Eq | BinOp::Ne | BinOp::Lt | BinOp::Le | BinOp::Gt | BinOp::Ge => (8, 9),\n58:         BinOp::Add | BinOp::Sub => (10, 11),\n59:         BinOp::Mul | BinOp::Div | BinOp::Rem => (12, 13),\n60:     }\n61: }\n62: \n63: fn logic_power(op: LogicOp) -> (u8, u8) {\n64:     match op {\n65:         LogicOp::Or => (4, 5),\n66:         LogicOp::And => (6, 7),\n67:     }\n68: }\n69: \n70: /// Where a child expression sits, in terms of what the parser would have accepted bare at that\n71: /// spot -- everything `needs_parens` and the wrapping printer need to know to reproduce the tree\n72: /// exactly, and nothing else.\n73: #[derive(Clone, Copy)]\n74: enum Ctx {\n75:     /// A fresh `self.expr(m)` call: File/Def body, a call argument (always real parens here, so\n76:     /// always reset to `Expr(0)` inside them), `Index`'s bracketed expression, a match arm's\n77:     /// body. The one context where a bare `Pipe` and, when `m` is loose enough, a bare `Match`\n78:     /// are both reachable.\n79:     Expr(u8),\n80:     /// A `self.operand(m)` call: a `Binary` child, or `Pattern::Guard`'s expression. `Pipe` and\n81:     /// `Match` are never reachable bare here -- only `expr()` parses those.\n82:     Operand(u8),\n83:     /// The base of a postfix chain (`Field`/`Index`/`Project`/`Unwrap`), or a `Call`/`Variant`'s\n84:     /// argument print used only when NOT already inside real parens. Nothing compound is\n85:     /// reachable bare here -- not even `Neg`.\n86:     Atom,\n87:     /// `Neg`'s base: like `Atom`, except a nested `Neg` is reachable bare (`- -a`).\n88:     Unary,\n89: }\n90: \n91: /// The left binding power of whatever operator `e` is, for the three contexts that decide by\n92: /// power alone. `None` for everything else, which each context rules on by kind.\n93: fn left_power(e: &Expr) -> Option<u8> {\n94:     match e {\n95:         Expr::Binary { op, .. } => Some(bin_power(*op).0),\n96:         Expr::Logic { op, .. } => Some(logic_power(*op).0),\n97:         Expr::Not { .. } => Some(NOT_POWER),\n98:         _ => None,\n99:     }\n100: }\n101: \n102: fn needs_parens(e: &Expr, ctx: Ctx) -> bool {\n103:     // A `let` block is only ever a function body, never a sub-expression, so no child position\n104:     // can hold one; treating it as needing parens everywhere is the safe dead arm.\n105:     if matches!(e, Expr::Let { .. }) {\n106:         return true;\n107:     }\n108:     match ctx {\n109:         Ctx::Atom => matches!(\n110:             e,\n111:             Expr::Binary { .. }\n112:                 | Expr::Logic { .. }\n113:                 | Expr::Not { .. }\n114:                 | Expr::Pipe { .. }\n115:                 | Expr::Match { .. }\n116:                 | Expr::Neg { .. }\n117:         ),\n118:         Ctx::Unary => {\n119:             matches!(\n120:                 e,\n121:                 Expr::Binary { .. }\n122:                     | Expr::Logic { .. }\n123:                     | Expr::Not { .. }\n124:                     | Expr::Pipe { .. }\n125:                     | Expr::Match { .. }\n126:             )\n127:         }\n128:         Ctx::Operand(m) => match e {\n129:             Expr::Pipe { .. } | Expr::Match { .. } => true,\n130:             _ => left_power(e).is_some_and(|p| p < m),\n131:         },\n132:         Ctx::Expr(m) => match e {\n133:             Expr::Pipe { .. } => PIPE_LEFT < m,\n134:             Expr::Match { .. } => m > PIPE_RIGHT,\n135:             _ => left_power(e).is_some_and(|p| p < m),\n136:         },\n137:     }\n138: }\n139: \n140: fn fits(s: &str, indent: usize) -> bool {\n141:     indent + s.chars().count() <= WIDTH\n142: }\n143: \n144: fn pad(n: usize) -> String {\n145:     \" \".repeat(n)\n146: }\n147: \n148: pub fn emit(file: &File) -> String {\n149:     let mut out = String::new();\n150:     for decl in decls_in_source_order(&file.aliases, &file.enums, &file.defs) {\n151:         out.push_str(&decl);\n152:         out.push_str(\"\\n\\n\");\n153:     }\n154:     out.push_str(&print_expr_wrapped(&file.body, Ctx::Expr(0), 0));\n155:     out.push('\\n');\n156:     out\n157: }\n158: \n159: /// A module is the declarations alone: no trailing expression to separate them from, so they end\n160: /// the file rather than each being followed by a blank line the way `emit` writes them.\n161: pub fn emit_module(module: &Module) -> String {\n162:     let decls = decls_in_source_order(&[], &module.enums, &module.defs);\n163:     if decls.is_empty() {\n164:         return String::new();\n165:     }\n166:     format!(\"{}\\n\", decls.join(\"\\n\\n\"))\n167: }\n168: \n169: /// Every declaration rendered, back in the order it was written.\n170: ///\n171: /// The AST groups declarations by kind, losing their interleaving in the source; sorting by span\n172: /// start puts them back, which is what makes the output idempotent -- reformatting an\n173: /// already-sorted file is a no-op re-sort.\n174: fn decls_in_source_order(aliases: &[Alias], enums: &[EnumDecl], defs: &[Def]) -> Vec<String> {\n175:     enum Item<'a> {\n176:         Alias(&'a Alias),\n177:         Enum(&'a EnumDecl),\n178:         Def(&'a Def),\n179:     }\n180: \n181:     let mut items: Vec<(usize, Item)> = Vec::new();\n182:     for a in aliases {\n183:         items.push((a.span.start, Item::Alias(a)));\n184:     }\n185:     for e in enums {\n186:         items.push((e.span.start, Item::Enum(e)));\n187:     }\n188:     for d in defs {\n189:         items.push((d.span.start, Item::Def(d)));\n190:     }\n191:     items.sort_by_key(|(start, _)| *start);\n192: \n193:     items\n194:         .iter()\n195:         .map(|(_, item)| match item {\n196:             Item::Alias(a) => print_alias(a),\n197:             Item::Enum(e) => print_enum(e),\n198:             Item::Def(d) => print_def(d),\n199:         })\n200:         .collect()\n201: }\n202: \n203: /// Parses `src` and formats it, then reattaches the one piece of source text `emit` cannot\n204: /// reconstruct from the tree: a leading run of `#` comment (and blank) lines, copied back\n205: /// verbatim ahead of the formatted body.\n206: ///\n207: /// Two file shapes reach a formatter that walks a project: a program, and a module --\n208: /// declarations with no trailing expression, which `parse` rejects outright and which\n209: /// `prelude.toy` is the one instance of here. Which one a file is only shows up in the parsing,\n210: /// so both are tried. When neither parse succeeds, the error reported is whichever got further\n211: /// into the file, so a typo halfway down a module is not reported as a missing program body.\n212: pub fn format_source(src: &str) -> Result<String, crate::error::Error> {\n213:     let banner = leading_comment(src);\n214:     let as_program = match crate::parse::parse(src) {\n215:         Ok(file) => return Ok(format!(\"{banner}{}\", emit(&file))),\n216:         Err(e) => e,\n217:     };\n218:     match crate::parse::parse_module(src) {\n219:         Ok(module) => Ok(format!(\"{banner}{}\", emit_module(&module))),\n220:         Err(as_module) if as_module.span.start > as_program.span.start => Err(as_module),\n221:         Err(_) => Err(as_program),\n222:     }\n223: }\n224: \n225: fn leading_comment(src: &str) -> String {\n226:     let mut banner = String::new();\n227:     for line in src.lines() {\n228:         let trimmed = line.trim_start();\n229:         if trimmed.starts_with('#') {\n230:             banner.push_str(line.trim_end());\n231:             banner.push('\\n');\n232:         } else if trimmed.is_empty() {\n233:             if banner.is_empty() {\n234:                 continue;\n235:             }\n236:             break;\n237:         } else {\n238:             break;\n239:         }\n240:     }\n241:     banner\n242: }\n243: \n244: fn print_alias(a: &Alias) -> String {\n245:     format!(\"type {} = {}\", a.name, print_type(&a.ty))\n246: }\n247: \n248: fn print_param(p: &Option<Param>) -> String {\n249:     match p {\n250:         None => String::new(),\n251:         Some(p) => match &p.shape {\n252:             ParamShape::Name(name, _) => format!(\"{name}: {}\", print_type(&p.ty)),\n253:             ParamShape::Fields(f) => {\n254:                 format!(\"{{{}}}: {}\", print_fields_pattern(f), print_type(&p.ty))\n255:             }\n256:         },\n257:     }\n258: }\n259: \n260: fn print_def(d: &Def) -> String {\n261:     let pub_prefix = if d.is_pub { \"pub \" } else { \"\" };\n262:     // A hoisted definition (`fn name = Msg(...)`, gh:152) writes no signature: parameter and\n263:     // return are both inferred from the body, so neither has a spelling to print.\n264:     if d.hoisted {\n265:         let one_line = format!(\n266:             \"{pub_prefix}fn {} = {}\",\n267:             d.name,\n268:             print_expr_compact(&d.body, Ctx::Expr(0))\n269:         );\n270:         if fits(&one_line, 0) {\n271:             return one_line;\n272:         }\n273:         let body = print_expr_wrapped(&d.body, Ctx::Expr(0), INDENT);\n274:         return format!(\"{pub_prefix}fn {} =\\n{}{body}\", d.name, pad(INDENT));\n275:     }\n276:     let ret = match &d.ret {\n277:         Some(ret) => print_type(ret),\n278:         // A definition that is not hoisted always writes its return type, so a `None` here is\n279:         // a parser/checker invariant, never a legal program.\n280:         None => unreachable!(\"a non-hoisted definition always writes a return type\"),\n281:     };\n282:     let sig = format!(\n283:         \"{pub_prefix}fn {}({}) -> {ret}\",\n284:         d.name,\n285:         print_param(&d.param),\n286:     );\n287:     // A `let` block is line-structured, so it has no one-line form: the signature, then one\n288:     // `let` line per binding, then the value, each indented one level.\n289:     if let Expr::Let { bindings, body, .. } = &d.body {\n290:         let mut lines: Vec<String> = bindings\n291:             .iter()\n292:             .map(|(n, v)| format!(\"let {n} = {}\", print_expr_compact(v, Ctx::Expr(0))))\n293:             .collect();\n294:         lines.push(print_expr_wrapped(body, Ctx::Expr(0), INDENT));\n295:         let mut out = format!(\"{sig} =\\n\");\n296:         for line in lines {\n297:             out.push_str(&pad(INDENT));\n298:             out.push_str(&line);\n299:             out.push('\\n');\n300:         }\n\n(Showing lines 1-300 of 743. Use offset=301 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The formatter's own backend: re-renders a parsed file as toylang source, in one canonical\n//! style. Unlike the other `emit_*` backends this walks `ast::File`, not `tir::Program` -- a\n//! formatter has to work on a program that does not type-check, and it must not print the\n//! prelude that `prelude::inject` would otherwise splice in.\n//!\n//! Two things drive the design. First, minimal-but-correct parenthesization: the AST does not\n//! record which parens the source actually used, so every paren in the output is one this module\n//! decided it needed, by comparing a node's own operator power against the power the position it\n//! sits in requires -- the same table `parse.rs::infix_power` parses with, walked in reverse.\n//! Second, the maintainer's own sample (docs/examples/euler/01-multiples-of-3-and-5.md) is the\n//! only ground truth for layout: a function whose signature-plus-body fits on one line stays on\n//! one line; otherwise the body moves to its own indented line, and a binary chain that still\n//! does not fit breaks at its outermost operator, trailing the operator on the first line.\n//! Match-arm chains extend that same \"trailing operator, one extra indent\" rule by analogy,\n//! since nothing in the sample pins their layout directly. A pipeline that does not fit is the\n//! one exception: it breaks one stage per line, with `|` leading each continuation line so the\n//! pipes form a vertical column (maintainer directive, issue #101), rather than trailing like\n//! the other chains.\n//!\n//! Two style choices worth naming since nothing in the grammar forces them: calls are always","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/emit_toylang.rs","text":"//! The formatter's own backend: re-renders a parsed file as toylang source, in one canonical\n//! style. Unlike the other `emit_*` backends this walks `ast::File`, not `tir::Program` -- a\n//! formatter has to work on a program that does not type-check, and it must not print the\n//! prelude that `prelude::inject` would otherwise splice in.\n//!\n//! Two things drive the design. First, minimal-but-correct parenthesization: the AST does not\n//! record which parens the source actually used, so every paren in the output is one this module\n//! decided it needed, by comparing a node's own operator power against the power the position it\n//! sits in requires -- the same table `parse.rs::infix_power` parses with, walked in reverse.\n//! Second, the maintainer's own sample (docs/examples/euler/01-multiples-of-3-and-5.md) is the\n//! only ground truth for layout: a function whose signature-plus-body fits on one line stays on\n//! one line; otherwise the body moves to its own indented line, and a binary chain that still\n//! does not fit breaks at its outermost operator, trailing the operator on the first line.\n//! Match-arm chains extend that same \"trailing operator, one extra indent\" rule by analogy,\n//! since nothing in the sample pins their layout directly. A pipeline that does not fit is the\n//! one exception: it breaks one stage per line, with `|` leading each continuation line so the\n//! pipes form a vertical column (maintainer directive, issue #101), rather than trailing like\n//! the other chains.\n//!\n//! Two style choices worth naming since nothing in the grammar forces them: calls are always\n//! written with explicit parens (`f(x)`, never the bare `f x` or brace-shorthand `f{...}`),\n//! matching both calls in the sample; and the wrap width is 80 columns, backed out from the\n//! sample itself -- the one line it left alone is 44 columns, and the two it broke are 89 and\n//! 118.\n//!\n//! One thing the AST cannot carry: comments. `parse::parse` throws every `#...` away as trivia\n//! (`skip_trivia`), so `emit` alone can only ever produce a program with none. `format_source`\n//! covers the one shape that actually appears in this repository -- a file's leading banner,\n//! read back off the raw text rather than the tree -- and nothing else; a comment anywhere else\n//! does not survive formatting, which is why every fragment that puts one somewhere else is\n//! marked a syntax-spelling exception rather than swept.\n\nuse crate::ast::{\n    Alias, BinOp, Def, EnumDecl, Expr, FieldsPattern, File, LogicOp, MatchArm, Module, Param,\n    ParamShape, Pattern, TypeExpr, Variant,\n};\n\nconst WIDTH: usize = 80;\nconst INDENT: usize = 4;\n\n// Mirrors parse.rs's precedence table exactly (`infix_power`, `PIPE_LEFT`/`PIPE_RIGHT`,\n// `COND_POWER`, `OR_LEFT`/`OR_RIGHT`, `NOT_POWER`): the numbers a paren decision here has to\n// answer to are the parser's, not this module's own invention.\nconst PIPE_LEFT: u8 = 1;\nconst PIPE_RIGHT: u8 = 2;\nconst COND_POWER: u8 = 3;\nconst NOT_POWER: u8 = 8;\n\n/// A match arm's body is printed at a power just above `or`'s, which is how the parser's second\n/// reading of `or` -- the arm separator, which has no power at all -- reaches this table: a bare\n/// disjunction in an arm body is the one expression whose parens the powers alone would not ask\n/// for, and the one the parser would otherwise read as two arms.\nconst ARM_BODY: u8 = 5;\n\nfn bin_power(op: BinOp) -> (u8, u8) {\n    match op {\n        BinOp::Eq | BinOp::Ne | BinOp::Lt | BinOp::Le | BinOp::Gt | BinOp::Ge => (8, 9),\n        BinOp::Add | BinOp::Sub => (10, 11),\n        BinOp::Mul | BinOp::Div | BinOp::Rem => (12, 13),\n    }\n}\n\nfn logic_power(op: LogicOp) -> (u8, u8) {\n    match op {\n        LogicOp::Or => (4, 5),\n        LogicOp::And => (6, 7),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Where a child expression sits, in terms of what the parser would have accepted bare at that\n/// spot -- everything `needs_parens` and the wrapping printer need to know to reproduce the tree\n/// exactly, and nothing else.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\nenum Ctx {\n    /// A fresh `self.expr(m)` call: File/Def body, a call argument (always real parens here, so\n    /// always reset to `Expr(0)` inside them), `Index`'s bracketed expression, a match arm's\n    /// body. The one context where a bare `Pipe` and, when `m` is loose enough, a bare `Match`\n    /// are both reachable.\n    Expr(u8),\n    /// A `self.operand(m)` call: a `Binary` child, or `Pattern::Guard`'s expression. `Pipe` and\n    /// `Match` are never reachable bare here -- only `expr()` parses those.\n    Operand(u8),\n    /// The base of a postfix chain (`Field`/`Index`/`Project`/`Unwrap`), or a `Call`/`Variant`'s\n    /// argument print used only when NOT already inside real parens. Nothing compound is\n    /// reachable bare here -- not even `Neg`.\n    Atom,\n    /// `Neg`'s base: like `Atom`, except a nested `Neg` is reachable bare (`- -a`).\n    Unary,\n}\n\n/// The left binding power of whatever operator `e` is, for the three contexts that decide by\n/// power alone. `None` for everything else, which each context rules on by kind.\nfn left_power(e: &Expr) -> Option<u8> {\n    match e {\n        Expr::Binary { op, .. } => Some(bin_power(*op).0),\n        Expr::Logic { op, .. } => Some(logic_power(*op).0),\n        Expr::Not { .. } => Some(NOT_POWER),\n        _ => None,\n    }\n}\n\nfn needs_parens(e: &Expr, ctx: Ctx) -> bool {\n    // A `let` block is only ever a function body, never a sub-expression, so no child position\n    // can hold one; treating it as needing parens everywhere is the safe dead arm.\n    if matches!(e, Expr::Let { .. }) {\n        return true;\n    }\n    match ctx {\n        Ctx::Atom => matches!(\n            e,\n            Expr::Binary { .. }\n                | Expr::Logic { .. }\n                | Expr::Not { .. }\n                | Expr::Pipe { .. }\n                | Expr::Match { .. }\n                | Expr::Neg { .. }\n        ),\n        Ctx::Unary => {\n            matches!(\n                e,\n                Expr::Binary { .. }\n                    | Expr::Logic { .. }\n                    | Expr::Not { .. }\n                    | Expr::Pipe { .. }\n                    | Expr::Match { .. }\n            )\n        }\n        Ctx::Operand(m) => match e {\n            Expr::Pipe { .. } | Expr::Match { .. } => true,\n            _ => left_power(e).is_some_and(|p| p < m),\n        },\n        Ctx::Expr(m) => match e {\n            Expr::Pipe { .. } => PIPE_LEFT < m,\n            Expr::Match { .. } => m > PIPE_RIGHT,\n            _ => left_power(e).is_some_and(|p| p < m),\n        },\n    }\n}\n\nfn fits(s: &str, indent: usize) -> bool {\n    indent + s.chars().count() <= WIDTH\n}\n\nfn pad(n: usize) -> String {\n    \" \".repeat(n)\n}\n\npub fn emit(file: &File) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    for decl in decls_in_source_order(&file.aliases, &file.enums, &file.defs) {\n        out.push_str(&decl);\n        out.push_str(\"\\n\\n\");\n    }\n    out.push_str(&print_expr_wrapped(&file.body, Ctx::Expr(0), 0));\n    out.push('\\n');\n    out\n}\n\n/// A module is the declarations alone: no trailing expression to separate them from, so they end\n/// the file rather than each being followed by a blank line the way `emit` writes them.\npub fn emit_module(module: &Module) -> String {\n    let decls = decls_in_source_order(&[], &module.enums, &module.defs);\n    if decls.is_empty() {\n        return String::new();\n    }\n    format!(\"{}\\n\", decls.join(\"\\n\\n\"))\n}\n\n/// Every declaration rendered, back in the order it was written.\n///\n/// The AST groups declarations by kind, losing their interleaving in the source; sorting by span\n/// start puts them back, which is what makes the output idempotent -- reformatting an\n/// already-sorted file is a no-op re-sort.\nfn decls_in_source_order(aliases: &[Alias], enums: &[EnumDecl], defs: &[Def]) -> Vec<String> {\n    enum Item<'a> {\n        Alias(&'a Alias),\n        Enum(&'a EnumDecl),\n        Def(&'a Def),\n    }\n\n    let mut items: Vec<(usize, Item)> = Vec::new();\n    for a in aliases {\n        items.push((a.span.start, Item::Alias(a)));\n    }\n    for e in enums {\n        items.push((e.span.start, Item::Enum(e)));\n    }\n    for d in defs {\n        items.push((d.span.start, Item::Def(d)));\n    }\n    items.sort_by_key(|(start, _)| *start);\n\n    items\n        .iter()\n        .map(|(_, item)| match item {\n            Item::Alias(a) => print_alias(a),\n            Item::Enum(e) => print_enum(e),\n            Item::Def(d) => print_def(d),\n        })\n        .collect()\n}\n\n/// Parses `src` and formats it, then reattaches the one piece of source text `emit` cannot\n/// reconstruct from the tree: a leading run of `#` comment (and blank) lines, copied back\n/// verbatim ahead of the formatted body.\n///\n/// Two file shapes reach a formatter that walks a project: a program, and a module --\n/// declarations with no trailing expression, which `parse` rejects outright and which\n/// `prelude.toy` is the one instance of here. Which one a file is only shows up in the parsing,\n/// so both are tried. When neither parse succeeds, the error reported is whichever got further\n/// into the file, so a typo halfway down a module is not reported as a missing program body.\npub fn format_source(src: &str) -> Result<String, crate::error::Error> {\n    let banner = leading_comment(src);\n    let as_program = match crate::parse::parse(src) {\n        Ok(file) => return Ok(format!(\"{banner}{}\", emit(&file))),\n        Err(e) => e,\n    };\n    match crate::parse::parse_module(src) {\n        Ok(module) => Ok(format!(\"{banner}{}\", emit_module(&module))),\n        Err(as_module) if as_module.span.start > as_program.span.start => Err(as_module),\n        Err(_) => Err(as_program),\n    }\n}\n\nfn leading_comment(src: &str) -> String {\n    let mut banner = String::new();\n    for line in src.lines() {\n        let trimmed = line.trim_start();\n        if trimmed.starts_with('#') {\n            banner.push_str(line.trim_end());\n            banner.push('\\n');\n        } else if trimmed.is_empty() {\n            if banner.is_empty() {\n                continue;\n            }\n            break;\n        } else {\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n    banner\n}\n\nfn print_alias(a: &Alias) -> String {\n    format!(\"type {} = {}\", a.name, print_type(&a.ty))\n}\n\nfn print_param(p: &Option<Param>) -> String {\n    match p {\n        None => String::new(),\n        Some(p) => match &p.shape {\n            ParamShape::Name(name, _) => format!(\"{name}: {}\", print_type(&p.ty)),\n            ParamShape::Fields(f) => {\n                format!(\"{{{}}}: {}\", print_fields_pattern(f), print_type(&p.ty))\n            }\n        },\n    }\n}\n\nfn print_def(d: &Def) -> String {\n    let pub_prefix = if d.is_pub { \"pub \" } else { \"\" };\n    // A hoisted definition (`fn name = Msg(...)`, gh:152) writes no signature: parameter and\n    // return are both inferred from the body, so neither has a spelling to print.\n    if d.hoisted {\n        let one_line = format!(\n            \"{pub_prefix}fn {} = {}\",\n            d.name,\n            print_expr_compact(&d.body, Ctx::Expr(0))\n        );\n        if fits(&one_line, 0) {\n            return one_line;\n        }\n        let body = print_expr_wrapped(&d.body, Ctx::Expr(0), INDENT);\n        return format!(\"{pub_prefix}fn {} =\\n{}{body}\", d.name, pad(INDENT));\n    }\n    let ret = match &d.ret {\n        Some(ret) => print_type(ret),\n        // A definition that is not hoisted always writes its return type, so a `None` here is\n        // a parser/checker invariant, never a legal program.\n        None => unreachable!(\"a non-hoisted definition always writes a return type\"),\n    };\n    let sig = format!(\n        \"{pub_prefix}fn {}({}) -> {ret}\",\n        d.name,\n        print_param(&d.param),\n    );\n    // A `let` block is line-structured, so it has no one-line form: the signature, then one\n    // `let` line per binding, then the value, each indented one level.\n    if let Expr::Let { bindings, body, .. } = &d.body {\n        let mut lines: Vec<String> = bindings\n            .iter()\n            .map(|(n, v)| format!(\"let {n} = {}\", print_expr_compact(v, Ctx::Expr(0))))\n            .collect();\n        lines.push(print_expr_wrapped(body, Ctx::Expr(0), INDENT));\n        let mut out = format!(\"{sig} =\\n\");\n        for line in lines {\n            out.push_str(&pad(INDENT));\n            out.push_str(&line);\n            out.push('\\n');\n        }","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":300,"totalLines":743,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_toylang.rs","time":{"start":1788705219564,"end":1788705219567}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I need to understand `needs_parens` too, to add the new node there if necessary. Let me read the beginning of emit_toylang.rs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_07723a3ea0014Q0wxg9qIdV7H2","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_077239d98001sILX9VFLEbvs6x"}}
