{"type":"text","timestamp":1788705225470,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"id":"prt_07723b4f8001G1OA36CrJOW5y3","messageID":"msg_07723ab1a001Akhrk1httj6I3B","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","type":"text","text":"\n\n\n\n","time":{"start":1788705223928,"end":1788705225469}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705225473,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_18201afe40ca43eb9f4dc18d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/tests/corpus.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/tests/corpus.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The agreement harness.\n2: //!\n3: //! One corpus, every backend. This is the check a single-backend suite cannot express: two\n4: //! backends producing different answers is a failure in its own right, separate from either of\n5: //! them producing a wrong one. The step_*.rs suites keep their own job, which is pinning error\n6: //! messages and emitted code; this is about behaviour, and behaviour is what has to be identical\n7: //! across targets.\n8: \n9: mod support;\n10: \n11: /// The agreement check for one slice of the corpus: every `of`th case by index. Splitting by\n12: /// index, not by backend, is the point -- `agreement_failures` runs every backend internally, so\n13: /// the case is the unit of work a parallel runner can hand to a thread, and each shard keeps the\n14: /// same bar a single test always had.\n15: fn assert_shard_agrees(shard: usize, of: usize) {\n16:     let cases = support::cases();\n17:     assert!(\n18:         !cases.is_empty(),\n19:         \"the corpus is empty, so this test proves nothing\"\n20:     );\n21: \n22:     let mut failures = Vec::new();\n23: \n24:     for (i, case) in cases.iter().enumerate() {\n25:         if i % of != shard {\n26:             continue;\n27:         }\n28:         failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n29:             &case.name,\n30:             &case.program,\n31:             case.input.as_deref(),\n32:             &case.expect,\n33:         ));\n34:     }\n35: \n36:     assert!(\n37:         failures.is_empty(),\n38:         \"{} corpus programs failed in shard {shard}/{of}:\\n{}\",\n39:         failures.len(),\n40:         failures.join(\"\\n\")\n41:     );\n42: }\n43: \n44: #[test]\n45: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_0() {\n46:     assert_shard_agrees(0, 8);\n47: }\n48: #[test]\n49: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_1() {\n50:     assert_shard_agrees(1, 8);\n51: }\n52: #[test]\n53: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_2() {\n54:     assert_shard_agrees(2, 8);\n55: }\n56: #[test]\n57: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_3() {\n58:     assert_shard_agrees(3, 8);\n59: }\n60: #[test]\n61: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_4() {\n62:     assert_shard_agrees(4, 8);\n63: }\n64: #[test]\n65: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_5() {\n66:     assert_shard_agrees(5, 8);\n67: }\n68: #[test]\n69: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_6() {\n70:     assert_shard_agrees(6, 8);\n71: }\n72: #[test]\n73: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_7() {\n74:     assert_shard_agrees(7, 8);\n75: }\n76: \n77: /// What a program prints is not always the whole claim. A case that asks for it gets the code a\n78: /// backend emitted pinned as well, which is how a property invisible in the output -- a name\n79: /// that has to be declared, a type that has to be spelled -- stays observed.\n80: #[test]\n81: fn emitted_code_matches_the_snapshot() {\n82:     let mut asked = 0;\n83:     for case in support::cases() {\n84:         let program =\n85:             toylang::compile(&case.program).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", case.name));\n86:         for backend in case.snapshot {\n87:             let emitted = backend.emit(&program).unwrap_or_else(|e| {\n88:                 panic!(\"{}: {} could not emit: {e}\", case.name, backend.name())\n89:             });\n90:             insta::assert_snapshot!(format!(\"{}__{}\", case.name, backend.name()), emitted);\n91:             asked += 1;\n92:         }\n93:     }\n94:     assert!(\n95:         asked > 0,\n96:         \"no case asks for a snapshot, so this test proves nothing\"\n97:     );\n98: }\n\n(End of file - total 98 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The agreement harness.\n//!\n//! One corpus, every backend. This is the check a single-backend suite cannot express: two\n//! backends producing different answers is a failure in its own right, separate from either of\n//! them producing a wrong one. The step_*.rs suites keep their own job, which is pinning error\n//! messages and emitted code; this is about behaviour, and behaviour is what has to be identical\n//! across targets.\n\nmod support;\n\n/// The agreement check for one slice of the corpus: every `of`th case by index. Splitting by\n/// index, not by backend, is the point -- `agreement_failures` runs every backend internally, so\n/// the case is the unit of work a parallel runner can hand to a thread, and each shard keeps the\n/// same bar a single test always had.\nfn assert_shard_agrees(shard: usize, of: usize) {\n    let cases = support::cases();\n    assert!(\n        !cases.is_empty(),\n        \"the corpus is empty, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/tests/corpus.rs","text":"//! The agreement harness.\n//!\n//! One corpus, every backend. This is the check a single-backend suite cannot express: two\n//! backends producing different answers is a failure in its own right, separate from either of\n//! them producing a wrong one. The step_*.rs suites keep their own job, which is pinning error\n//! messages and emitted code; this is about behaviour, and behaviour is what has to be identical\n//! across targets.\n\nmod support;\n\n/// The agreement check for one slice of the corpus: every `of`th case by index. Splitting by\n/// index, not by backend, is the point -- `agreement_failures` runs every backend internally, so\n/// the case is the unit of work a parallel runner can hand to a thread, and each shard keeps the\n/// same bar a single test always had.\nfn assert_shard_agrees(shard: usize, of: usize) {\n    let cases = support::cases();\n    assert!(\n        !cases.is_empty(),\n        \"the corpus is empty, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );\n\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n\n    for (i, case) in cases.iter().enumerate() {\n        if i % of != shard {\n            continue;\n        }\n        failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n            &case.name,\n            &case.program,\n            case.input.as_deref(),\n            &case.expect,\n        ));\n    }\n\n    assert!(\n        failures.is_empty(),\n        \"{} corpus programs failed in shard {shard}/{of}:\\n{}\",\n        failures.len(),\n        failures.join(\"\\n\")\n    );\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_0() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(0, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_1() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(1, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_2() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(2, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_3() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(3, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_4() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(4, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_5() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(5, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_6() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(6, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_7() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(7, 8);\n}\n\n/// What a program prints is not always the whole claim. A case that asks for it gets the code a\n/// backend emitted pinned as well, which is how a property invisible in the output -- a name\n/// that has to be declared, a type that has to be spelled -- stays observed.\n#[test]\nfn emitted_code_matches_the_snapshot() {\n    let mut asked = 0;\n    for case in support::cases() {\n        let program =\n            toylang::compile(&case.program).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", case.name));\n        for backend in case.snapshot {\n            let emitted = backend.emit(&program).unwrap_or_else(|e| {\n                panic!(\"{}: {} could not emit: {e}\", case.name, backend.name())\n            });\n            insta::assert_snapshot!(format!(\"{}__{}\", case.name, backend.name()), emitted);\n            asked += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    assert!(\n        asked > 0,\n        \"no case asks for a snapshot, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":98,"totalLines":98,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/corpus.rs","time":{"start":1788705225468,"end":1788705225472}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me read a corpus case and the tests/corpus.rs harness, to understand how to add a case for the module dispatch. Note: corpus cases are single files. For module dispatch, the case needs the program file plus the module file(s). How would that work in the corpus harness?\n\nLet me look at tests/corpus.rs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_07723baf8001i9LmA9NjJpPWrJ","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_07723ab1a001Akhrk1httj6I3B"}}
