{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705237428,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_2dc907e030d14713bdd9b9df","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls examples docs/reference/operators 2>/dev/null"},"output":"docs/reference/operators:\narithmetic.md\nboolean.md\ncomparison.md\nconditional.md\nmatch.md\npipe.md\nprojection.md\nspecs.md\nunwrap.md\n\nexamples:\nadults.toy\nfilter.toy\nfizzbuzz.toy\ngreet_fn.toy\ngreet.toy\nhello.toy\nshapes.toy\n","metadata":{"output":"docs/reference/operators:\narithmetic.md\nboolean.md\ncomparison.md\nconditional.md\nmatch.md\npipe.md\nprojection.md\nspecs.md\nunwrap.md\n\nexamples:\nadults.toy\nfilter.toy\nfizzbuzz.toy\ngreet_fn.toy\ngreet.toy\nhello.toy\nshapes.toy\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls examples docs/reference/operators 2>/dev/null","time":{"start":1788705237424,"end":1788705237427}},"id":"prt_07723e9a6001aaNw30fidTLcaH","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_07723c012001e7PmvusKjeV2sw"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705237433,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_bf88a7ddd05147cea7d269cf","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"route-groups|module dispatch|@\\(\" tests/ examples/ docs/ src/ --glob '!tests/snapshots/**' | head -30"},"output":"(no output)","metadata":{"output":"(no output)","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n \"route-groups|module dispatch|@\\(\" tests/ examples/ docs/ src/ --glob '!tests/snapshots/**' | head -30","time":{"start":1788705237408,"end":1788705237431}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The corpus harness runs `toylang::run_on(program, input, backend)` which calls `compile(src)` -- no file path. So for corpus cases with module dispatch, the module files need to be found. The program text is in-memory; there's no file path.\n\nThis is a real design challenge: how does the module dispatch resolve relative paths when the program comes from a string? Options:\n- The corpus harness could write the case's program to a temp file and compile from there. But that changes the harness significantly.\n- Alternatively, the `@(path)` path resolution could be based on a \"module root\" search: search for the file relative to the current working directory. But corpus cases are loaded from `tests/corpus/`...\n\nHmm. Let me think about how main.rs runs: `toylang run FILE`. The program file path is known there. For tests, the compile happens from a string. \n\nMaybe the approach: `compile` gains an optional base path. `main.rs` passes the file's parent directory. The corpus harness, for cases that use module dispatch, needs the module files to be loadable. \n\nBut AGENTS.md says \"Add a case to tests/corpus/ with program/input/output per AGENTS.md and verify it with `just check`, not a scratch file plus a manual run\". So a corpus case for module dispatch must work in the harness. The harness calls `run_on(program, ...)` with no path. So the module dispatch must resolve somehow from just the program string.\n\nHmm, what would be the natural resolution? Maybe the path is resolved relative to the current working directory (the repo root when tests run via cargo nextest).. So `@(tests/corpus/route/foo.toy)`? That seems ugly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the intended design: the path is resolved relative to the referring file, and for tests, the harness could... hmm.\n\n\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe the cleaner design: the `compile` API takes an optional base directory. `main.rs` passes the directory of the file being compiled. The corpus harness, when a case's program contains `@(`, would need a base. But the harness currently has no path concept.\n\nWait -- actually, let me reconsider the corpus harness. It reads the case file from `tests/corpus/<name>.yaml`. The program is inline in YAML. For module dispatch, we could create the module files as sibling `.toy` files under tests/corpus/, and the case's `@(path)` refers to them relative to... what? The harness doesn't know the case's file path at compile time (only at load time).\n\nOption: The corpus harness passes the case's directory as the base path to `run_on`. That's a change to the harness signature (`run_on(src, stdin, backend)`), but it's contained. Or `compile` could accept an optional `base: Option<&Path>`.\n\n\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider whether the module dispatch really needs file-path resolution at all, or whether there's a simpler model.\n\n\n\nAlternative model: `@(path)` path is resolved against a fixed \"module search path\" or the file system relative to the current working directory. Given toylang is a single-file compiler (`src/main.rs reads one path`), the natural thing: `@(path)` resolves relative to the directory of the file being compiled. That requires knowing the file path. \n\nFor the corpus, there's no file path. So the simplest workable approach for tests: change `run_on`/`compile` to accept an optional base directory, defaulting to `.` (CWD). The corpus harness passes `tests/corpus` as the base (since module files would live there next to the case). Actually wait, `dir()` is `tests/corpus` and cases are `tests/corpus/<name>.yaml`. So module files could be `tests/corpus/route-groups/baz.toy` etc. The harness would pass `tests/corpus` as the base path. Then `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` resolves to `tests/corpus/route-groups/baz.toy`.\n\nBut that changes the public API of `run_on`/`compile`. Hmm. Let me think about how invasive that is.\n\nActually, maybe a less invasive approach: `compile` signature unchanged; the module resolution uses a relative-to-CWD resolution. The corpus harness already runs with CWD = crate root (`CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR`), so a module file at `tests/corpus/route-groups/baz.toy` would be reachable as `@(tests/corpus/route-groups/baz.toy)`. Ugly but works with zero API change.\n\nHmm, but that's ugly and the \"relative to the referring file\" semantic is the design. Let me not shortcut.\n\n\n\nLet me look at how `main.rs` reads the file and what path info is available: `on_file` has `path: &str`. So `toylang::compile(&src)` is called without path. `run(&src, backend)` too. So currently there is genuinely no path in the library API.\n\nThe design doc says \"The path resolves relative to the referring file at compile time\". This requires threading a path through. Let me check if any test or doc references \"route-groups\" already. Also check examples/.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_07723e99c001MadfusA1CRTSQh","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_07723c012001e7PmvusKjeV2sw"}}
