{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705239358,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_083a88e9c5b94ac8a00d24a3","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls docs/reference docs/reference/syntax docs/reference/functions 2>/dev/null"},"output":"docs/reference:\nbuiltins\noperators\nprelude\nsources\nsyntax\ntypes\n\ndocs/reference/syntax:\nfunctions.md\nprograms.md\n","metadata":{"output":"docs/reference:\nbuiltins\noperators\nprelude\nsources\nsyntax\ntypes\n\ndocs/reference/syntax:\nfunctions.md\nprograms.md\n","exit":2,"truncated":false},"title":"ls docs/reference docs/reference/syntax docs/reference/functions 2>/dev/null","time":{"start":1788705239352,"end":1788705239356}},"id":"prt_07723f12a001Wt0XTCXWOe7TAK","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_07723e9e4001QqDfWldniZD3ss"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705239364,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_8156482d8f294972b86bc29a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/docs/reference/operators/match.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/docs/reference/operators/match.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Match\n2: \n3: A match is a chain of produce-or-decline arms over the subject `.`: the subject arrives\n4: through a pipe, arms compose with `or`, and the first arm that matches wins. An arm's left\n5: side is a variant pattern of an [enum](../types/enum.md) subject, or a Bool guard over any\n6: subject.\n7: \n8: ```case\n9: enum_match\n10: ```\n11: \n12: Four arm shapes:\n13: \n14: - `point -> ...`: a unit variant, by name.\n15: - `circle{r} -> r * r`: a payload variant with a record pattern, binding fields fresh.\n16: - `text -> .body`: a bare payload arm; `.` rebinds to the payload, so a scalar payload is\n17:   used whole and a record payload is projected into.\n18: - `. % 3 == 0 -> \"Fizz\"`: a guard arm, matching when the Bool is true; `.` stays the\n19:   subject in both the guard and the body.\n20: \n21: The chain's final element may be a bare expression, the default; `any() -> ...` is the\n22: default spelled as an arm, standing in for every variant nothing named:\n23: \n24: ```case\n25: enum_match_default\n26: ```\n27: \n28: A guard chain with a bare default is the whole of conditional logic -- FizzBuzz as guard\n29: arms:\n30: \n31: ```case\n32: fizzbuzz_arms\n33: ```\n34: \n35: Totality is a hybrid. A chain with variant patterns is closed-world: every variant handled,\n36: or a default at the end, and missing one is a compile error naming it (the\n37: [enum page](../types/enum.md) shows it). Guards do not count toward that coverage. A pure\n38: guard chain is open-world and may be honestly partial: with no default, declining every arm\n39: yields `Opt` -- the same answer indexing gives to reaching past what's there:\n40: \n41: ```case\n42: match_partial_guards\n43: ```\n44: \n45: A declared `Opt<T>` return reaches the arms as `T`: the chain's own partiality is what\n46: supplies the `Opt`, so an arm never has to spell it. An arm whose body is itself `Opt`-typed\n47: doubles the wrapping instead of colliding with it -- `none` (no arm matched) and `some(none)`\n48: (matched, and found nothing) stay distinct values, even though both print `null`.\n49: \n50: `or` is also Bool disjunction, and which one a given `or` is depends on where it sits: an\n51: arm's left side is still being read, so `or` there joins guard clauses, while an arm's body is\n52: finished, so `or` there ends it and starts the next arm. The\n53: [boolean operators page](boolean.md) has both readings side by side; the only rule that costs\n54: anything is that a Bool `or` written directly in an arm body needs parens.\n55: \n56: Matching is decoding, not control flow bolted on: a match is how a value whose shape is\n57: unknowable until runtime becomes typed data again.\n\n(End of file - total 57 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Match\n\nA match is a chain of produce-or-decline arms over the subject `.`: the subject arrives\nthrough a pipe, arms compose with `or`, and the first arm that matches wins. An arm's left\nside is a variant pattern of an [enum](../types/enum.md) subject, or a Bool guard over any\nsubject.\n\n```case\nenum_match\n```\n\nFour arm shapes:\n\n- `point -> ...`: a unit variant, by name.\n- `circle{r} -> r * r`: a payload variant with a record pattern, binding fields fresh.\n- `text -> .body`: a bare payload arm; `.` rebinds to the payload, so a scalar payload is\n  used whole and a record payload is projected into.\n- `. % 3 == 0 -> \"Fizz\"`: a guard arm, matching when the Bool is true; `.` stays the\n  subject in both the guard and the body.\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/docs/reference/operators/match.md","text":"# Match\n\nA match is a chain of produce-or-decline arms over the subject `.`: the subject arrives\nthrough a pipe, arms compose with `or`, and the first arm that matches wins. An arm's left\nside is a variant pattern of an [enum](../types/enum.md) subject, or a Bool guard over any\nsubject.\n\n```case\nenum_match\n```\n\nFour arm shapes:\n\n- `point -> ...`: a unit variant, by name.\n- `circle{r} -> r * r`: a payload variant with a record pattern, binding fields fresh.\n- `text -> .body`: a bare payload arm; `.` rebinds to the payload, so a scalar payload is\n  used whole and a record payload is projected into.\n- `. % 3 == 0 -> \"Fizz\"`: a guard arm, matching when the Bool is true; `.` stays the\n  subject in both the guard and the body.\n\nThe chain's final element may be a bare expression, the default; `any() -> ...` is the\ndefault spelled as an arm, standing in for every variant nothing named:\n\n```case\nenum_match_default\n```\n\nA guard chain with a bare default is the whole of conditional logic -- FizzBuzz as guard\narms:\n\n```case\nfizzbuzz_arms\n```\n\nTotality is a hybrid. A chain with variant patterns is closed-world: every variant handled,\nor a default at the end, and missing one is a compile error naming it (the\n[enum page](../types/enum.md) shows it). Guards do not count toward that coverage. A pure\nguard chain is open-world and may be honestly partial: with no default, declining every arm\nyields `Opt` -- the same answer indexing gives to reaching past what's there:\n\n```case\nmatch_partial_guards\n```\n\nA declared `Opt<T>` return reaches the arms as `T`: the chain's own partiality is what\nsupplies the `Opt`, so an arm never has to spell it. An arm whose body is itself `Opt`-typed\ndoubles the wrapping instead of colliding with it -- `none` (no arm matched) and `some(none)`\n(matched, and found nothing) stay distinct values, even though both print `null`.\n\n`or` is also Bool disjunction, and which one a given `or` is depends on where it sits: an\narm's left side is still being read, so `or` there joins guard clauses, while an arm's body is\nfinished, so `or` there ends it and starts the next arm. The\n[boolean operators page](boolean.md) has both readings side by side; the only rule that costs\nanything is that a Bool `or` written directly in an arm body needs parens.\n\nMatching is decoding, not control flow bolted on: a match is how a value whose shape is\nunknowable until runtime becomes typed data again.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":57,"totalLines":57,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/reference/operators/match.md","time":{"start":1788705239334,"end":1788705239361}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me look at the docs/reference/operators/match.md to understand match and how docs are tested (tests/docs.rs demands a reference page per builtin, per operator maybe). Also let me check the fmt tree and tag_corpus, since the new node needs tags and possibly docs.\n\nLet me read docs/reference/operators/match.md.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_07723f121001di1Rl9x4qvQbfV","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_07723e9e4001QqDfWldniZD3ss"}}
