{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705241759,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_2f7aaf856e664d13843adf20","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"case|corpus|fragment\" tests/docs.rs | head -40"},"output":"1://! The docs harness: every code fragment in `docs/**/*.md` is a real program.\n3://! A fragment is a `toylang` fence, an `input` fence if the program reads stdin,\n5://! same seven-backend agreement check as the corpus, because a docs fragment is a corpus case\n7://! being turned away. A page can also embed an existing corpus case by id with a `case` fence\n13://! There is no way for a fragment to opt out of that. A `fixture` fence used to name a\n14://! gitignored path for stdin that could not be committed, skipping the fragment when the file\n21://! names lowercase, which the gh:156 casing checker now refuses, so `every_fragment_is_a_real_program`\n22://! gates those fragments to the checker's refusal instead of their stale expectation fence. It is\n26://! `toylang slow` is the one deliberate exception, and it is not a skip. A `slow` fragment is\n27://! an `output` fragment that the default run only type-checks and emits on every backend -- it\n32://! measured cost, not impatience, so a fragment only earns it by being slow in fact.\n40:/// What a fragment claims happens to its program.\n70:/// Fills in a pending fragment's stdin, from an `input` fence: refuses a second one, and a\n71:/// fence with no fragment open to receive it.\n75:        Some(Pending { at: prev, .. }) => panic!(\"{at}: fragment at {prev} already has an input\"),\n81:fn every_fragment_is_a_real_program() {\n82:    let mut fragments = Vec::new();\n85:        extract(&page, &text, &mut fragments, &mut embedded);\n88:        !fragments.is_empty(),\n89:        \"the docs have no fragments, so this test proves nothing\"\n92:        fragments.iter().any(|f| f.slow),\n93:        \"no docs fragment is marked `slow`, so the tier proves nothing; delete it\"\n98:    for f in &fragments {\n103:        failures.extend(fragment_failures(f));\n109:                \"MISSING {at}: embeds corpus case `{id}`, which does not exist\"\n116:        \"{} of {} docs fragments failed:\\n{}\",\n118:        fragments.len(),\n124:/// `slow` fragment on every backend without running it.\n129:/// The default check for a `slow` fragment. Running it is what costs minutes, so the default\n150:/// The failures one fragment accumulates. A gated fragment (see `capital_variant_gate`) is\n153:fn fragment_failures(f: &Fragment) -> Vec<String> {\n188:/// The fragments still spelling lowercase variant names the gh:156 casing checker now refuses.\n211:/// whichever of `output`, `refuses`, or `error` the fragment declared it wanted. That pins the\n212:/// fragment as pending the migration without asserting the stale expectation fence it cannot\n213:/// currently meet, and it means the gate cannot silently cover a broken fragment: compiling, or\n233:/// fails here, the same way a corpus case without fresh tags fails tag_corpus.\n294:/// Pulls the fragments out of one page. Malformed structure -- an `input` with no program, a\n295:/// program left without an expectation -- is a panic naming the line, like a malformed corpus\n296:/// case: a fence that asks for nothing looks exactly like a fence that passes.\n300:    fragments: &mut Vec<Fragment>,\n","metadata":{"output":"1://! The docs harness: every code fragment in `docs/**/*.md` is a real program.\n3://! A fragment is a `toylang` fence, an `input` fence if the program reads stdin,\n5://! same seven-backend agreement check as the corpus, because a docs fragment is a corpus case\n7://! being turned away. A page can also embed an existing corpus case by id with a `case` fence\n13://! There is no way for a fragment to opt out of that. A `fixture` fence used to name a\n14://! gitignored path for stdin that could not be committed, skipping the fragment when the file\n21: