<Str> = collect(s)\\n\\nf(stdin)\").is_ok()\ntests/streams.rs:228:        assert!(toylang::compile(\"collect(stdin)\").is_ok());\ntests/streams.rs:229:        assert!(toylang::compile(\"collect stdin\").is_ok());\ntests/streaming.rs:138:    let program = toylang::compile(program).expect(\"compiles\");\ntests/streaming.rs:148:    let program = toylang::compile(program).expect(\"compiles\");\ntests/streaming.rs:168:    let program = toylang::compile(program).expect(\"compiles\");\ntests/streaming.rs:199:    let program = toylang::compile(program).expect(\"compiles\");\ntests/step_dsv.rs:5:    toylang::compile(src).map(|_| ()).unwrap_err().to_string()\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n \"toylang::compile|::compile\\(|compile\\(&|run_on\\(|toylang::run\\b|fn compile\" tests/ src/ benches/ | rg -v \"\\.compile_error|compile_to_object|rustc|\\.rs:.*//\" | head -50","time":{"start":1788705249962,"end":1788705249965}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The docs harness embeds corpus cases by id via `case` fences. So for module dispatch, I could add a corpus case (and a docs fragment with the `case` fence or an inline fragment.\n\nBut the core challenge remains: `run_on(program, input, backend)` has no base path for resolving `@(path)`. \n\nLet me think about this more carefully. What is the minimal, idiomatic design that fits this codebase?\n\nGiven the constraint that corpus cases are single YAML files with inline programs, and the harness compiles from strings, I think the intended approach is:\n\n**The `@(path)` path is resolved relative to the file being compiled.** The `compile` entry point needs the referring file's directory. For main.rs, that's available. For the corpus, we need a way.\n\nOptions for the corpus harness:\n1. Write case programs to a temp file and compile with a base. Too invasive.\n2. Add an optional base dir parameter to `compile`/`run_on`; corpus harness passes `tests/corpus`. Module files live under tests/corpus (e.g. `tests/corpus/route-groups/baz.toy`), referenced as `@(route-groups/baz.toy)`.\n3. Resolve relative to CWD and have module files under the repo root. Cases would spell `@(tests/corpus/route-groups/baz.toy)`.\n\nHmm. Wait, actually, let me reconsider. Do I even need to make the corpus harness work with real files? Maybe the module dispatch can be tested via the corpus with a `refuses` case (compile error), no file needed. And a \"happy path\" test could be a different kind of test (a test in Rust that writes module files to a temp dir and compiles a program with a base path). But AGENTS.md says add cases to tests/corpus/ for new syntax...\n\nLet me look at how existing tests that need files work. E.g. `tests/euler_real_data.rs` or the `fmt_tree`. Also check whether there's a precedent for passing a path into the library.\n\nLet me look at the check for the visibility (gh:166) -- the prerequisite that \"is already landed\". The branch history shows the `Origin` enum already exists. The task says build on that.\n\nOK. Let me think about the shape of the solution once more.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the whole approach. Maybe the design intent for `@(path)` is much simpler than I'm making it: \n\n**Maybe the module file is resolved at *compile time* relative to the *referring file*, but for the actual compile pipeline (the one used by tests), the module files are loaded through a dedicated mechanism. \n\nLet me look at how `prelude.toy` is loaded: `include_str!(\"../prelude.toy\")`. So prelude is compile-time-embedded. \n\nHmm, maybe the natural design: `@(path)` is resolved relative to the referring file **at compile time** meaning the checker loads the module file from disk (relative to the referring file's directory). And for the corpus harness, we need a base.\n\nI think the cleanest: add an optional `base` directory parameter to `compile` and `run_on` (and the few callers). `main.rs` passes `Path::new(path).parent()`; tests/support passes `tests/corpus` directory (so `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` resolves within tests/corpus/). Actually wait, hmm. `run_on` is called by `agreement_failures` with the program string. The Case struct doesn't carry a directory, but all cases live in one dir. So we could just pass `dir()` there.\n\nLet me look at all callers of `compile` and `run_on` to size this change.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_077241a9d001sFLluw89wRFvHk","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_07723fabc0010VQUgT3YRd2hCI"}}
