{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705326388,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_fb932806bd544e87a855ff35","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"handle|module|routing|@\\(|subject|payload|Route\" plans/erlang-target-research.md | head -40"},"output":"75:payload variant is a tuple of tag plus payload.\n","metadata":{"output":"75:payload variant is a tuple of tag plus payload.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n \"handle|module|routing|@\\(|subject|payload|Route\" plans/erlang-target-research.md | head -40","time":{"start":1788705326380,"end":1788705326383}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"There are many callers of `compile` in tests. Adding a parameter to `compile` would touch many tests. Better: add a **new** function or an optional context, keeping `compile(src)` signature unchanged.\n\nOption: keep `compile(src)` unchanged, and add a separate entry `compile_with_base(src, base_dir)` used by main.rs and the corpus harness. Or: change `compile` to take an optional base: `compile(src)` delegates to `compile_from(src, None)`. But the existing callers pass no base, and their programs never use `@(`, so it's fine. \n\nActually the simplest API: \n\n```rust\npub fn compile(src: &str) -> Result<Program, Error> {\n    compile_in(src, None)\n}\npub fn compile_in(src: &str, base: Option<&Path>) -> Result<Program, Error> { ... }\n```\n\nAnd `run_on` similarly needs the base (since it compiles internally...). Hmm, `run_on(src,...)` calls `compile(src)` internally. So `run_on` needs the base too, for the corpus harness and main.rs.\n\nSo I'll thread an optional base directory:\n- `compile_in(src, base)` / `run_on_in(src, stdin, backend, base)`...\n- `main.rs`: pass `Path::new(path).parent()`, so `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` resolves relative to the program file's directory.\n- `tests/support::agreement_failures`: pass the corpus directory.\n- Keep `compile`/`run_on` delegating with `None` base (existing behavior unchanged for programs without `@(`...)).\n\nHmm, but wait. Actually, let me reconsider: is this the right resolution model? \"The path resolves relative to the referring file at compile time\". For a program file `examples/router.toy`, `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` resolves to `examples/route-groups/baz.toy`. Yes.\n\nNow the bigger question: how does the checker handle a module dispatch? Let me design that carefully.\n\n## The checker design\n\nWhen `synth_inner` (or `expect_inner`) meets `Expr::Module { path, span }`:\n1. Require a subject (`ctx.subject` must be `Some`). If not: error \"`@(...)` needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\" (like `map`/`select`/match).\n2. Resolve the path relative to the current file's directory. Current file = `ctx.file` origin + a base dir. Hmm, the `file` field is an `Origin` enum (Program/Prelude), not a path. For recursion (a module dispatching to another module), we need per-file base dirs. \n\nThis is getting complicated because the module's own functions need to be checked within their own file context. Let me think about whether to support recursive module dispatch now.\n\nThe board row needs: [file-visibility-tracking-build]. The research doc's \"What all three depend on\" section: \"the checker does not track which file a definition came from and cannot enforce visibility per call site. Until that exists, no candidate works even for the prelude's own next step.\" So gh:166 gave us Origin tracking. \n\nNow for module dispatch, the module's functions are loaded from a file, checked, and emitted. Recursive dispatch (module dispatching to submodule) would be natural but adds complexity (each module's defs need checking in their own file context, with their own base dir, and their own enums/aliases/visibility map.\n\n\n\nLet me think about the SIMPLEST implementation that genuinely solves the problem and is honest about scope. \n\n**Simplest approach that works:** when the checker meets `@(path)`, it:\n1. Resolves and loads the module file, parses it as a `Module`.\n2. Prefixes all the module's def names (and enum names? etc.) with a unique per-path prefix to avoid collisions with the program's names and other modules'. E.g. `route-groups/baz.toy` -> prefix `@route-groups/baz.toy@` or something.\n\nHmm, wait. But then the module's defs need to be checked, which requires building a `Ctx` for them (their enums, aliases, visibility, sigs...). And their bodies may reference their own private helpers (origin = the module file). And their bodies may use their own enums declared in the module file. And their bodies may themselves dispatch `@(...)` recursively (then need the module's file path as base).\n\nThis is essentially: for each referenced module, run a check like `check_module` but with reachability to only what the dispatch uses, and emit its funcs into the program.\n\n\n\nAnd there's the entry-point convention: the module's `handle` function. `@(path)` dispatches to the module's `pub fn handle`, applied to the subject. So the dispatch node lowers to a `Kind::Call { func: <prefixed-handle-name>, arg: Some(subject) }`.\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety: the entry function must be `pub` (visibility), and a module with no `handle` is an error, and a non-pub handle is an error.\n\n\n\nThen for emission: all the loaded modules' funcs are added to `funcs`, and reachability pruning keeps only what's used. \n\nBut there's a naming problem for the emitted `Kind::Call`: func names must be unique across all funcs. So prefix each module's def names with a mangled path, e.g. `route_groups__baz_toy__handle`... but the `tir::Func.name` is used in emitted code (e.g., a function name in generated source); mangling with special chars could break codegen. Use a safe mangling (sanitize path: replace non-alphanumeric with `_`).\n\nHmm, wait. But actually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether the module's defs should be merged into `file.defs` before checking (like prelude injection). That would let the existing machinery handle everything (sigs, visibility, funcs...). The problem was name collisions between modules. But if we rename each module's defs with a per-module prefix at AST level (mutate def.name), then merge them all into file.defs, then `check::check` processes them naturally! The `@(path)` node would lower to a call to the prefixed handle name.\n\n\n\nThat's actually elegant:\n1. After parsing (and before/during checking), scan the AST for `Expr::Module { path }` nodes.\n2. For each distinct path (in resolution order), resolve relative to the referring file, load, parse, and -- recursively -- collect its module's defs with a prefix applied to their names. Also its enums... hmm, enums. If a module declares an enum, its name could collide with the program's. Prefix enum names too? But enum names appear in types inside the module's defs... this gets messy.\n\n\n\nHmm. Wait. Let me check: does `parse_module` collect enums? Yes, `Module { defs, enums }`. And `check_module` checks defs against the module's own enums. The prelude: `prelude::module()` returns enums filtered to pub, and `inject` merges them into the file's enums. So the prelude's enum names (Opt, Result) are global and cannot collide with a program's. \n\nFor modules, same story: merging the module's enums into the program's enums. Name collisions would be flagged by the existing duplicate detection (\"type X is defined twice\"). Good enough for now? If two modules both declare `Route`, collision error. Hmm, that's a limitation but acceptable for the first cut (the prelude has the same constraint. But wait -- actually, for routing, each route-group module would likely declare its own types... collisions plausible. But keep scope: the design doc doesn't specify module-local type namespaces. The prelude precedent is global merging. I'll follow the prelude precedent: merge module enums into the program (flagged on collision), and merge module defs (renamed to avoid collisions, since def names like `handle` WILL collide across modules).\n\nHmm, but wait -- if defs are renamed but enums aren't, that's asymmetric. Why rename defs? Because every module has a `handle` by convention. Renaming defs with a per-module prefix solves that. Enums aren't renamed because... hmm, they could collide too. \n\nAlternatively: don't rename anything; require that modules don't collide with each other or the program. But `handle` appears in every module -- inevitable collision. So defs must be namespaced.\n\nLet me think differently. What if each module's functions get a fresh namespace naturally via... no, there's no namespace mechanism in tir::Func names. They're flat strings.\n\nOK so prefixing def names is the pragmatic path. Let me design it:\n\n- Module path `route-groups/baz.toy`, loaded at compile time. Give it a name-prefix derived from the path, e.g. sanitized: `route_groups_baz` (replace `-`, `/`, `.` with `_`... actually `.` should become... hmm, path `route-groups/baz.toy` -> stem `baz`? Use full relative path sanitized: `route_groups_baz_toy_`). \n- Each def in the module gets renamed `{prefix}{name}`, e.g. `route_groups_baz_toy_handle`.\n- The `@(path)` node resolves to a call to the prefixed `handle`.\n- The module's enums are merged without renaming (collision = error, like the prelude).\n\nHmm, but actually wait. There's an even simpler angle. What if we DON'T merge module defs into file.defs, but instead handle them entirely inside the checker: when `@(path)` is met, load the module, check its `handle` against the subject/expected types, and emit a call to... a mangled name, and append the module's checked funcs to the program's funcs list. This is what I earlier called \"Option B\". It requires: checking the module's defs (check_module-like), which needs its own enums/sigs/visibility/ctx, and per-def `in_fn` context etc. But `check::check_module` already does most of this (checks a module's pub defs in isolation)! \n\nSo: when `@(path)` is met:\n1. Resolve + load + parse module.\n2. Rename its defs with the module prefix.\n3. `check::check_module(&module)` -> `Vec<tir::Func>` (already checked!). But wait, check_module checks ALL defs, including private ones (which are the module's own helpers,and needed since handle may call them.. Good, it checks all defs with visibility enforced within the module's own file context.\n. Then the module's funcs (renamed) are appended to the program's funcs.\n\nActually hold on: `check_module` returns funcs for all defs (each def is emitted as a Func). The entry `handle` is among them (renamed). And the dispatch lowers to `Kind::Call { func: <renamed handle>, arg: subject }`. Reachability pruning then keeps only the funcs reachable from the program body (including the module's helpers transitively. \n\nThat's clean! The module's defs are checked once, in their own module context (own enums, visibility), and emitted as ordinary funcs. \n\nBut wait: `check_module`'s defs get `origin: Origin::Prelude`?? Let me check. In `parse_module`, defs get `origin: crate::ast::Origin::Program` (from `def()`, hardcoded). And `check_module` sets `visibility` from defs' origin... Let me re-read `check_module`: it builds `visibility` from `module.defs` origin, and `ctx.file = Origin::Prelude`. Hmm, so `check_module` assumes the module is the prelude (sets `file: Origin::Prelude`). For our submodules, origin should distinguish them. But for visibility purposes within a module: non-pub defs must be callable from within the module. `check_module` sets `file: Prelude` and visibility entries have `origin: Program` (from parse... wait, `def()` sets `origin: Origin::Program`! Hmm, so in `check_module`, defs have origin Program (from the parser, and `file` is Prelude. That means a non-pub def (origin Program) called from `file=Prelude` would be REFUSED by the call-site visibility rule! But prelude's private helpers must be callable from within prelude... \n\nWait, how does the prelude's private helper work then? Let me check `prelude::module()`: it sets `def.origin = Origin::Prelude` after parsing. So prelude defs have origin Prelude, matching `check_module`'s `file: Prelude`. But `check_module` is called at build time via build.rs on the prelude module AFTER prelude::module() set origins... hmm, let me check build.rs.\n\n\n\nActually wait, for `check_module`, where do the defs come from? `build.rs` likely calls `prelude::module()` which parses `prelude.toy` AND sets origins to Prelude. So defs in check_module have origin Prelude, matching file: Prelude. Good.\n\n\n\nBut `check_module` sets `ctx.file = Origin::Prelude` hardcoded. For a general submodule, I'd want `ctx.file` to be its own origin. The `Origin` enum only has Program and Prelude. For submodules, I could... add a variant `Origin::Module`?? But then visibility comparisons: a module's private def (origin Module) called from file Module matches. But two different modules would both be `Origin::Module`... that breaks per-file privacy between modules (a module A's private helper could be called from module B's defs if both are Origin::Module, if they were ever checked together... but each module is checked separately with its own ctx, so the origin is consistent within one module's check. The cross-module boundary is enforced at the `@(path)` call site by requiring `handle` be `pub`. So within-module privacy works if each module is checked in isolation. `Origin::Module` as a single value works for all modules since each check is separate. But hmm, the visibility check `if *origin != ctx.file` — within a module's own check, defs have origin Module and file is Module → private calls allowed. Good.\n\nBut wait, there's a subtlety with the prelude injection: the program's compile merges prelude defs into file.defs, and `check::check` uses `Origin::Program` as file. The prelude defs have origin Prelude. A private prelude helper (origin Prelude) called from the program (file Program) is refused. \n\nFor submodules, we're NOT merging them into file.defs (Option B: separate check_module per module). So each module's defs are checked with origin Module and file Module. Good.\n\nBut hold on, actually, could I avoid adding `Origin::Module` by having check_module take the origin as a parameter? `check_module` currently hardcodes Prelude. I could add a parameter `origin: Origin` or make `check_module` generic. Actually, since each module check is self-contained, adding a variant `Origin::Module` to the enum is simplest, and `check_module` could take an origin param. Hmm, but the existing `check_module` sets Prelude hardcoded. Let me check who calls `check_module`: build.rs (for the prelude).. I could parameterize it: `check_module(module, origin)` with build.rs passing Prelude. That's clean.\n\n\n\nNow, the dispatch node in synth: `Expr::Module { path, span }`:\n```rust\nExpr::Module { path, span } => {\n    let Some((subject_ty, sid)) = ctx.subject.clone() else {\n        return Err(... \"`@(...)` needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\");\n    };\n    let entry = ctx.module_dispatch(path, span)?;   // returns the (checked funcs, entry name, entry sig)\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nBut the problem: the module's funcs need to be added to the program's `funcs` list. But `synth` returns a `Tir`, not funcs. The funcs list is assembled in `check()` from `check_defs(...)` + prelude::checked(). Where do module funcs get added?\n\nOption: the module funcs are collected during checking into a shared buffer (e.g. `RefCell<Vec<tir::Func>>` in Ctx, or a `Rc<RefCell<...>>>` shared with the outer `check`). Then `check()` appends them. Since funcs are built once per module load, and multiple `@(path)` sites can reference the same module (same path), we'd want to cache (load+check once per path. And the funcs for a module are the same regardless of call site. So a shared cache keyed by resolved path. \n\nLet me design: `Ctx` gets a new field:\n```rust\nmodules: &'a RefCell<HashMap<String, (Vec<tir::Func>, String /* entry name */, Sig)>>,\n```\nkeyed by resolved absolute path string. When a dispatch is met:\n- If path already in cache: reuse the cached entry name+sig (and don't recheck/reload; funcs already in the shared buffer).\n- Else: load file from `base_dir.join(path)`, parse as module, rename defs with prefix, check via a module-check, get funcs; insert into cache and into the shared funcs collection.\n\n\n\nHmm, wait. But the funcs collection needs to live somewhere that `check()` can read after. The `Ctx` lives inside `check()`. I could make `check()` own the modules buffer and thread it as a field in Ctx. Then after `check_program_body`, append all collected module funcs to `funcs`. \n\nBut there's a wrinkle: `check_module` returns checked funcs, but the module's defs may reference the program's... no, modules are standalone files (declarations only; no imports).. So module funcs are self-contained (modulo nested `@(` dispatches inside the module's own bodies!... Hmm. If a module's `handle` body contains `@(sub.toy)`, that needs the module's file dir as base and its own module-resolution... recursion!).\n\nDo I support nested dispatch? The design doc open question: \"Whether a route submodule ever needs its own submodules\". Not ruled. Recursion would work naturally with the cache design if the module-check context passes the module's base dir. But check_module doesn't support dispatch at all (its ctx has no modules field...). \n\nScope decision: implement top-level dispatch only (a program file dispatching to submodule files); a module's own body with `@(` would be... refused? or naturally an error \"not supported yet\"?). Hmm, but wait -- how would a module's `handle` even parse `@(`? The parser accepts it anywhere. The module check (check_module) would meet `Expr::Module` in a def body and need to handle it. If I give check_module's ctx a modules field too... it's the same code path. Actually, if the module-loading is recursive through the same `modules` cache, recursion comes almost for free. The catch is just: check_module needs the same ctx fields (modules cache, base dir, next_local)..\n\n The `next_local` ids must be globally unique across program + all modules (since funcs are emitted together with locals ids shared... do local ids need global uniqueness? Tir::Local ids are per-func or global? Let me check: `next_local` is a `Cell<LocalId>` shared across the whole check. In check_defs, each def's ctx has the same `next_local`. So local ids are globally unique across the program. If modules are checked with a fresh next_local per module, collisions possible in emitted code? LocalIds appear in Func bodies; each Func's body is self-contained, and a LocalId only needs uniqueness within one func? Let me check how backends emit Local references... Actually, `Kind::Local(id)` inside a Bind/body is scoped to the func. But let me check if anything keys globally on LocalId. The `prune_unreachable` and `linearity` work per func/body. Likely LocalIds only need uniqueness within one Func. But to be safe, share one `next_local` across everything (pass the same Cell into module checks. Easy since ctx has it.\n\n.\n\nOK, here's my refined plan:\n\n###AST\nAdd:\n```rust\nExpr::Module {\n    path: String,\n    span: Span,\n},\n```\nto the `Expr` enum + `span()` + `needs_parens`/`left_power` (it's an atom; no parens needed in atom contexts; it's self-delimited `@(path)` so parens not needed in any context. Add to `needs_parens`? An atom-like node needs no parens anywhere. Not adding it to the matches lists (they only list compound nodes needing parens; `@(...)` is atomic like `Var`/`Call`... Actually `Call` isn't in the parens lists either (it's printed `f(x)`). So `@(path)` printed as-is; no needs_parens change needed unless it appears in Atom context (nothing compound → false). Good.\n\n###Parser\n- Add `Tok::At` token (read `@` char.\n- In `atom()`, on `Tok::At`: expect `(`, read the path as... hmm, what characters? The path is `route-groups/baz.toy` which contains letters, `-`, `/`, `.`. So I need a custom read: read until `)` (or whitespace?)... Actually simplest: read a run of non-`)` non-whitespace chars, then eat `)`. But a path with spaces? Not supported. Error on empty path, or on `)` directly.\n- Actually, per the maintainer's refinement `@(route-groups/baz.toy)`, the parens enclose the path expression. I'll parse: `@` then `(` then path (chars until `)` or whitespace/newline; error if empty or contains whitespace) then `)`.\n\nWait, but should `@` only be valid as an arm body? The research doc calls it \"a dedicated routing-arm form\". If I parse it in `atom()`, it's allowed anywhere syntactically. The checker enforces it needs a subject (like map/select/match), so `1 + @(x.toy)` would be a parse+check error (\"needs a subject\"). Actually `@(x.toy)` in `1 + @(...)` — the checker would error \"needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\". Hmm, but is that the right restriction? The design says it's a \"routing-arm form\". Perhaps it should ONLY be legal as an arm body (right of `->`). Should the parser enforce that? That'd be more faithful to \"dedicated routing-arm form\". But implementing position-checking in the parser adds complexity (tracking whether we're in an arm body position... the `arm_body` already sets `or_separates = true`; I could gate `@(` to parse only when `or_separates`... but `or_separates` is also true for nested positions? No — `with_or` resets to false for nested expr positions. Actually `arm_body` calls `self.with_or(true, |p| p.expr(COND_POWER))`, and any nested position (call args, parens, etc.) resets to false. So `or_separates == true` exactly identifies the top level of an arm body! But careful: `expr` in arm body enters `expr_at(min_power, fresh)`... it stays true at the top level. Hmm, but a guard's `or`... a guard is parsed with `with_or(false,...)` in match_arm (None => operand(COND_POWER) with or=false. So guard clauses aren't or_separates. So gating `@(` parse on `or_separates == true` means it's only legal at the top level of an arm body. That matches \"routing-arm form\". But it also means `@(path)` can't appear nested in an arm body (e.g. `map(@(x.toy))`... which semantically couldn't work anyway since it needs a subject). Fine.\n\nHmm, but actually -- what about a program body that is directly a dispatch? No: dispatch needs a subject (the pipe. The example `r | FooBar -> @(route-groups/foo-bar.toy)` has it in an arm body. OK.\n\n \n\nBut wait, there's another subtlety: an arm body like `FooBar -> @(route-groups/foo-bar.toy)` — is `@(` parsed via `arm_body` -> `expr(COND_POWER)` -> `expr_at`... -> `operand` -> ... -> `atom`. If I gate in `atom()` on `or_separates`, that works. But what about an arm body `FooBar -> [@(x.toy)]`? or_separates resets to false inside VecLit (`self.expr(0)`... wait, `expr()` at PIPE_RIGHT... `expr(0)` calls `with_or(false,...)`. So nested positions have false. Good.\n\n\n\nActually, hold on. Is gating it to arm-body-only correct per the design? \"a new arm-body kind reserved for module dispatch\". Yes. And the checker then doesn't need the subject rule? Well, an arm body can be a guard/default arm too, where `.` is bound. E.g. `any() -> @(baz.toy)` (default arm, `.` = subject. Fine. So the subject requirement in the checker still applies (dispatch needs `.`).\n\nHmm wait, actually: do we want `@(path)` to require a subject at all? What if `@(path)` is used where `.` is the payload (payload arm)? Then handle receives the payload. What about unit variant arms (`FooBar -> @(...)` where `.` is unbound)? Then handle would receive... nothing. Error (\"`.` is not bound here\" — the same error Subject nodes give). Hmm, but the router example in the doc has `FooBar -> @(route-groups/foo-bar.toy)` with unit variants! Under my design, that errors (\"`.` is not bound here\"). So the canonical example wouldn't compile!\n\nHmm. Let me re-read the design doc example:\n\n```\nfn router(r: Route) -> Response =\n    r | FooBar -> $(route-groups/foo-bar.toy)\n      or Baz   -> $(route-groups/baz.toy)\n```\n\nThe `r` is the subject (a Route enum), `FooBar` is a unit variant, so `.` in the arm body is not bound. So under the current semantics, the dispatch can't receive the subject. That means either:\n(a) The intended semantics: `@(path)` applies the module to the *whole subject* (the enum value `r`), NOT the rebound payload. I.e., dispatch doesn't care about the arm's `.` rebinding; it receives the chain's subject (the match's subject...\n\nHmm, but the design doc says: \"The subject arrives through the pipe, so the submodule is applied to `.` in every case.\" `.` = \"the subject\". In a variant arm, `.` is rebound to the payload (per match.md docs: \"`text -> .body`: a bare payload arm; `.` rebinds to the payload\"). For a unit variant, `.` is not bound.\n\nHmm, so under a strict reading, the doc's own example (unit variants) wouldn't work. But maybe the point is: for a unit variant arm, the arm's subject for dispatch purposes is still the chain's subject (the whole Route value)? That seems inconsistent with match semantics...\n\nLet me look at the round's actual ruling notes. The board title says: \"maintainer refined the spelling from the round''s bare `@path` to parenthesized `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` because it encloses the path expression.\" No semantics detail. The research doc's candidate C text: \"The checker treats it specially:the path must resolve to a module whose entry signature accepts the subject's type and returns what the arm expects\".\n\n\"The subject's type\" — the arm's `.` type. For a unit variant arm, `.` has no type...\n\nHmm. I think there's ambiguity here, and this is exactly the kind of thing to decide. But let me think about what makes a coherent language.\n\nThe purpose: an http router. `Route` enum: `FooBar`, `Baz`, `Qux`. Each route dispatches the request to a handler module. What does the handler need? The request (the payload). For unit-variant routes, there's no request payload — just dispatch (maybe handle takes nothing? But handle needs a subject per the design: \"applied to the subject\"...\n\nAlternatively, maybe the route enum carries the request:\n\n```\nenum Route {\n    foo(Request), \n    bar(Request), \n    ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm. But the doc's sketch uses unit variants...\n\n\n\nOK. Given the ambiguity, let me make a pragmatic design decision and record it:\n\n**Design decision:** `@(path)` applies the module's `handle` to the arm's `.` — whatever `.` is bound to in the arm body (the payload for a payload arm, the subject for guard/default arms). For a unit variant arm, `.` is not bound, so `@(path)` there is an error (\"`.` is not bound here\") — matching how any other subject-reading expression (`map`, `select`, `match`... actually those need a subject via pipe, not `.`; `.` reading happens via Subject nodes... \n\nWait, actually, hmm. Let me reconsider: maybe `@(path)` should NOT read `.` at all, but rather apply to the *match's subject* (the value piped into the match chain)? No — for a guard arm, `.` is the subject; for a payload arm, `.` is the payload. The doc says \"applied to `.`\". So it reads `.`.\n\nSo the canonical example with unit variants would only work if... hmm. Let me reconsider whether `.` is really unbound in unit variant arms. \n\n`variant_arm` for a unit variant: `Ok((None, ctx.with(None)))`. `ctx.with(None)` → subject = None. So `.` is not bound. Yes.\n\n\n\nAlternatively — maybe the intended router shape has payload variants, and the doc's sketch was just loose. Or maybe the dispatcher applies to the whole subject regardless of the arm. I need to make a decision.\n\n \n\nLet me look at the erlang-target-research.md doc, since it's from the same round — maybe it contains the round notes with more detail on the module routing question.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_07725451b001zlv1ct2Z5IDfj7","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_077241ac3001M1x51bIDSCV9Yx"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705326388,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_7a39c6c71e334fd5bdc79ba2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"routing|module-as-function|entry\" draft.md | head -40"},"output":"344:the entry two up deliberately separated the same three things.\n361:A prediction from that entry has half come true from the other direction. It said the free\n365:`Foo {name: \"ada\", age: 36}` is the shape that entry wanted from `Foo(name: \"ada\", age: 36)`,\n383:Spiewak) is the same idea again, and it is also what [the regex-over-types entry](plans/questions.md#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams) is already\n961:consume anything to know what it did. Collapse is not: finding entry three means passing the\n1376:The `Vec` literal cannot do this, because an entry is where an element type comes from and an\n1512:Split on `\\n` only. Strip it from each yielded string. No trailing empty entry when input ends in\n1765:`prelude.toy` is parsed as a module -- `parse::parse_module`, a second entry point next to\n","metadata":{"output":"344:the entry two up deliberately separated the same three things.\n361:A prediction from that entry has half come true from the other direction. It said the free\n365:`Foo {name: \"ada\", age: 36}` is the shape that entry wanted from `Foo(name: \"ada\", age: 36)`,\n383:Spiewak) is the same idea again, and it is also what [the regex-over-types entry](plans/questions.md#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams) is already\n961:consume anything to know what it did. Collapse is not: finding entry three means passing the\n1376:The `Vec` literal cannot do this, because an entry is where an element type comes from and an\n1512:Split on `\\n` only. Strip it from each yielded string. No trailing empty entry when input ends in\n1765:`prelude.toy` is parsed as a module -- `parse::parse_module`, a second entry point next to\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n \"routing|module-as-function|entry\" draft.md | head -40","time":{"start":1788705326384,"end":1788705326386}},"id":"prt_077254524001OlHMM2i917jqbS","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_077241ac3001M1x51bIDSCV9Yx"}}
