{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705340246,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_43d5cb74311042248075e261","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"pub struct Program|pub enums|enums:\" src/tir.rs | head"},"output":"303:pub struct Program {\n321:    pub enums: Enums,\n527:fn reachable_enums(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, seen: &mut Vec<Type>, found: &mut Vec<Type>) {\n","metadata":{"output":"303:pub struct Program {\n321:    pub enums: Enums,\n527:fn reachable_enums(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, seen: &mut Vec<Type>, found: &mut Vec<Type>) {\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n \"pub struct Program|pub enums|enums:\" src/tir.rs | head","time":{"start":1788705340241,"end":1788705340244}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK. Now I have a good understanding. Let me now decide on the architecture concretely.\n\nLet me re-examine the scope and what's simplest:\n\n**Key realization:** The `@(path)` dispatch can be lowered at *parse/check time* to a call to a renamed `handle` function, and the module's funcs are checked and appended to the program's funcs. The backends need no changes.\n\n\n\n**Module funcs checking:** Use `check::check_module`. But it needs:\n- `module.enums` — the module's own enums (merged into program enums too, since the module's defs typecheck against them, AND the program body doesn't need them... wait, do the module's types leak into the program? The `handle` signature has types; the program checks the call against the sig. The sig types (e.g., `Request`) are module-local. The program's match arm body expects a `Response`. If `Request`/`Response` are types declared in the module, the program never names them. So they don't need merging. Only the sig is used at the call site. \n\nHmm, wait, but check_module needs the module's enums resolved (its own enum_map).. And the resulting funcs' bodies use those types. The program's `enums` registry... `tir::Program { enums }` — enums are used by `input::validate` and printing. The module funcs' bodies may reference enum types declared in the module; those enums must be in `program.enums` for... what? The backends need the enum variants to print/codegen. Actually, `ty::Type::Enum { name, args, variants }` carries its variants inline in the type itself (see ty.rs: `variants` field in Type::Enum!). Let me check: in `resolve_enum`, the Type::Enum struct has `variants` inline. So the types in funcs carry their own variant lists. The `program.enums` is a HashMap used for `input::validate` (parsing input values) and... let me check what `program.enums` is used for in backends. Probably codegen for enum values: `EnumLit` printing etc. Actually, if the variants are inline in Type::Enum, why does Program carry enums? Let me check `tir::Program`.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_077257b3f001wbuHhOgQttYgR4","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_077255f63001bKeFjtdV5naqxy"}}
