{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788705345686,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"id":"prt_077259094001e2uj9O4Bn3FLW0","messageID":"msg_077257b69001LwG8eQw6VQgNvL","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","snapshot":"307d242a582f94e0a9fe102c3e65441553e7eb4b","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705361281,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_6c7816998ac74b0fb6c52945","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"pub fn variants|pub fn variants_of|pub enum Type|Enum \\{\" src/ty.rs | head"},"output":"14:pub enum Type {\n80:    Enum {\n147:            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n178:            Type::Enum { name, args, .. } if name == \"Opt\" && args.len() == 1 => Some(&args[0]),\n193:            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n213:            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n231:            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n250:            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n264:        matches!(self, Type::Record(_) | Type::Enum { .. })\n292:            Type::Enum { name, args, .. } => {\n","metadata":{"output":"14:pub enum Type {\n80:    Enum {\n147:            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n178:            Type::Enum { name, args, .. } if name == \"Opt\" && args.len() == 1 => Some(&args[0]),\n193:            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n213:            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n231:            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n250:            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n264:        matches!(self, Type::Record(_) | Type::Enum { .. })\n292:            Type::Enum { name, args, .. } => {\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n \"pub fn variants|pub fn variants_of|pub enum Type|Enum \\{\" src/ty.rs | head","time":{"start":1788705361250,"end":1788705361253}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The backends use `program.enums` to look up variants by name. So the module's enums must be merged into `program.enums` for the backends to handle the module funcs' bodies. So I DO need to merge module enums into the program's enums.\n\nOK so merging module enums into the program's enum registry is necessary. Collisions are errors (like prelude).\n\n\n\nNow let me settle the architecture concretely. Given the complexity, let me reconsider merging module defs into file.defs (prelude-style injection) vs. checking modules separately.\n\n**Approach A — inject-and-merge:** When `@(path)` is met in the checker, load the module, rename its defs + enums with a per-module prefix, and merge into the file's defs/enums... but that mutation mid-check is messy (check::check already built sigs/visibility/enums/variant_owners from the original file.defs...). Actually, a pre-pass: scan the AST for `Expr::Module`, load all reachable modules recursively, rename their defs/enums with prefixes, merge into file.defs/enums, and rewrite the `Expr::Module` nodes into `Expr::Call { func: prefixed_handle }`. Then run check::check normally! \n\nThat's attractive: ONE pass before check, converting everything into existing machinery. The checker changes: none for the core! Visibility: after renaming+merging, the module's private helpers have origin... hmm, origin. The merged defs' origin would be `Program` (from parse::def()). Then a private helper (origin Program) called from... the module's own `handle` (also origin Program... wait, file = Program for everything after merge.) So private helpers would be callable from the program body too! That breaks privacy. Unless we set the module defs' origin to something non-Program (e.g., a new `Origin::Module`),and the call-site check `*origin != ctx.file` refuses them from the program (file=Program). Within the module's own defs... the `handle` body checking: `file` is Program for the whole check. So `handle` calling a private helper: origin Module != file Program → refused. Privacy would be TOO strict — even the module's own handle couldn't call its private helpers!\n\nSo Approach A breaks the within-module private-helper ability. Because the check runs all defs under one `file` context. The prelude's private helpers work because prelude defs are checked separately (check_module with file=Prelude,) at build time, and only the pub ones... wait no. Hmm, prelude's private helpers are merged into file.defs at inject. So during `check::check`, file = Program, and prelude defs have origin Prelude. So a prelude `handle`... there is no handle. The prelude's `join_lines` is pub. It calls no private helper today. The design note says a pub prelude fn CANNOT yet call a private helper (origin Prelude vs file Program). So currently private prelude helpers are NOT callable from pub prelude fns during check::check! \n\nWait, let me re-read prelude.rs doc: \"a non-`pub` one is kept and can serve as a private helper, callable only from within this file (the checker enforces that, by origin, at each call site).\" And build.rs comment: \"Mark prelude origin the way prelude::module() does, so a pub function may call a private helper without tripping the checker's cross-file visibility rule.\" \n\nSo how does a pub prelude fn call a private helper? The prelude defs are checked at BUILD TIME via `check::check_module` (file=Prelude, defs origin=Prelude). So within prelude, private calls are allowed (origin==file). The `prelude::checked()` funcs are reused at compile time WITHOUT rechecking. So the private helper relationship is baked into the checked Funcs. Good.\n\n\n\nSo for modules: the right analog: check each module ONCE, in isolation, with its own file context (origin=Module-like, file=same), and reuse the resulting funcs. That's Approach B — separate check per module. \n\nSo Approach B it is:\n\n**Approach B — separate module checks:**\n- Add `Origin::Module` variant? Or reuse... Actually, do we need a new Origin variant? check_module hardcodes `file: Origin::Prelude` and sets defs origins... wait, check_module doesn't set defs origins; the caller (build.rs/prelude::module() sets them to Prelude. So `check_module` just needs the defs' origins to match its `file` setting. Currently check_module sets file: Prelude. If I parameterize check_module with an `origin: Origin` param and set file: origin, the caller sets defs origins to that origin, everything works. For prelude: origin=Prelude (unchanged). For submodules: origin=... hmm, what value? I could add `Origin::Module` — fine. Or reuse Program?? No, Program must remain the program's. Add `Origin::Module`.\n\n- In `check::check`, add a module cache in Ctx: `modules: &'a RefCell<ModuleCache>` where ModuleCache holds: for each resolved absolute path: the module's checked funcs (with prefixed names), the handle's renamed entry name, the entry Sig, and whether... Also need the module's enums merged into program.enums. But program.enums is built in check() BEFORE checking the body. Module enums are discovered during body checking. So I need to extend program.enums after. Since `check()` returns `Program { enums }` built at the end, I can collect module enums into the same HashMap during body checking (thread it in Ctx as `&RefCell<...>` or collect after). But `enums` is used during body checking for... `opt_of`, `variant_owners`, `enum_variants` — those are prebuilt. For the program body, module enums aren't visible (the program can't name them — no imports!). The module enums are only used within module funcs (already checked separately) and for backends' printing. So during body check, module enums aren't needed in `ctx.enums`; just collect them for the final Program.enums. \n\nWait, but there's a subtlety: the module funcs' bodies contain `Type::Enum { name, args, variants }`. The types carry variants inline? Let me verify: `resolve_enum` builds `Type::Enum` with variants. Yes (the `variants` field in Type::Enum). So the module funcs are self-contained type-wise. The `program.enums` is used by backends to look up `ty::variants(&program.enums, ty)` — hmm, that takes an enum NAME and looks up variants from the registry. Let me check `ty::variants`.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_07725cd4c001gwMdiSBGC8Wnb3","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_077257b69001LwG8eQw6VQgNvL"}}
