{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705363046,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_fde75f4d46d044eca2111275","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/ty.rs","limit":120,"offset":1},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/ty.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: use std::collections::HashMap;\n2: \n3: /// Every enum a program declares, resolved once and keyed by name, which is an enum's identity.\n4: ///\n5: /// A `Type::Enum` in hand is not always enough to work from. Where an enum's payload reaches\n6: /// back to itself through a `Vec`, that occurrence carries a placeholder in place of the real\n7: /// variant list (`check::types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76), since expanding it is what\n8: /// has no end. So nothing reads `variants` off a type directly: everything that needs the list\n9: /// asks `variants` below, which re-derives it here, one layer at a time and exactly as deep as\n10: /// the program itself navigates (kantord/toylang#94).\n11: pub type Enums = HashMap<String, Type>;\n12: \n13: #[derive(Debug, Clone)]\n14: pub enum Type {\n15:     Str,\n16:     Int,\n17:     /// A signed 64-bit integer that wraps, `Int`'s rules at twice the width (kantord/toylang#83,\n18:     /// extending ADR 0006). Deliberately a separate type rather than a widening of `Int`:\n19:     /// nothing converts implicitly, so mixing the two demands an explicit `i64(x)`. Literals\n20:     /// carry no suffix -- one that fits `Int` is `Int`, and any literal resolves as `Int64`\n21:     /// wherever one is expected, the `[]` rule applied to numbers. Barred from `input`, like\n22:     /// `Opt`: not because a 64-bit integer has no JSON spelling but because two backends\n23:     /// cannot yet read one faithfully (JS parses numbers into doubles), and that codec design\n24:     /// has not been done.\n25:     Int64,\n26:     /// A double-precision IEEE 754 binary64 float, exactly JavaScript's number (ADR 0007):\n27:     /// no alternative width, no decimal type. Deliberately a separate type from `Int`, and\n28:     /// nothing converts implicitly: a decimal point is the spelling that makes a literal a\n29:     /// Float, and mixing the two in one operator is an error rather than a silent promotion.\n30:     /// Unlike the integer widths, arithmetic on a Float is total -- division by zero returns\n31:     /// Infinity, the IEEE answer, where `Int` stops the program (the Q37 ruling in\n32:     /// plans/questions.md, kantord/toylang#149).\n33:     Float,\n34:     Bool,\n35:     /// A single Unicode scalar value (kantord/toylang#75): never a surrogate half, so a\n36:     /// codepoint outside the Basic Multilingual Plane is one `Char`, on every backend, even\n37:     /// where a backend's own strings need a surrogate pair to spell it. Produced only by\n38:     /// `chars`, which is also the only way one is compared against a boundary -- there is no\n39:     /// literal syntax, so a program spells `'a'` as `chars(\"a\")[0]!`. Barred from `input` (no\n40:     /// wire form to read one from) and from the program's own printed result (no wire form to\n41:     /// write one either); everywhere else -- function signatures, records, a `Vec<Char>` --\n42:     /// it is an ordinary type.\n43:     Char,\n44:     Vec(Box<Type>),\n45:     /// Fields in declaration order. Order is not part of the type's identity (kantord/toylang#60:\n46:     /// `{a: Int, b: Int}` and `{b: Int, a: Int}` are one type, so `PartialEq` below compares the\n47:     /// field set, not the spelling), but it survives as metadata on every concrete value: a\n48:     /// literal's own written order is what it prints in on every backend and what the\n49:     /// native/Go column layouts key on. Whichever type a value is checked against is the order\n50:     /// it ends up carrying (`check::reorder_record` rebuilds a value that arrives shuffled), so\n51:     /// two differently-ordered values only coexist when nothing ever placed them in the same\n52:     /// checked position.\n53:     Record(Vec<(String, Type)>),\n54:     /// Effect-layer multiplicity: the entries arrive one at a time as evaluation proceeds, and\n55:     /// no stream object ever exists as a value (ADR 0001). Spellable in function signatures,\n56:     /// and only there -- the type grammar refuses it inside a Vec, a record, or another Stream\n57:     /// -- born at a source (`lines` is `Stream<Str>`), consumed exactly once per binding, and\n58:     /// exiting only through `collect`.\n59:     Stream(Box<Type>),\n60:     /// A sink: an expression that writes rather than producing a value, of which the only\n61:     /// instance today is the `jsonlines` builtin (gh:138 ruling). Second-class by construction,\n62:     /// the same way a `Stream` is -- a sink is not a value, so it never sits inside a Vec, a\n63:     /// record, a Stream, or an enum payload, and it is never a parameter -- with the position\n64:     /// rules stated once generally in the checker rather than hand-checked per builtin: a\n65:     /// `Sink` is legal only as the program's outermost expression or as a `Sink`-returning\n66:     /// function's body. A function may declare it as its return type and nothing else.\n67:     Sink,\n68:     /// A declared enum: nominal, so the name is the identity. A name (and, for a generic enum,\n69:     /// its arguments) determines the variants within a program, so `PartialEq` below compares\n70:     /// only those and not `variants` itself -- which matters because a self-referential enum's\n71:     /// own payload carries a placeholder here in place of the real list\n72:     /// (`check::types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76) rather than the list every other\n73:     /// occurrence of the same name carries. A variant's payload is `None` for a unit variant,\n74:     /// in declaration order.\n75:     ///\n76:     /// The list riding along is a convenience, not the source of truth: read it and a\n77:     /// placeholder reads as an enum with no variants at all, which is how a nested recursive\n78:     /// value used to reach every backend's printer as something it could not render\n79:     /// (kantord/toylang#94). Ask `variants` instead.\n80:     Enum {\n81:         name: String,\n82:         /// The type arguments this instantiation was built with, in declaration order; empty\n83:         /// for an enum declared without parameters. Part of the identity: `Pair<Int>` and\n84:         /// `Pair<Str>` are different types even though both are `Pair`.\n85:         args: Vec<Type>,\n86:         variants: Vec<(String, Option<Type>)>,\n87:     },\n88:     /// A type parameter inside a generic enum's registry template, standing for whatever a\n89:     /// use site will supply. Never the type of any checked expression: instantiation\n90:     /// substitutes every one away before a type leaves resolution.\n91:     Param(String),\n92: }\n93: \n94: /// Names reserved for the built-in type constructors, on top of `Str`/`Int`/`Bool`\n95: /// (`Type::from_name`): a program cannot declare an alias or enum under any of these.\n96: const RESERVED_TYPE_NAMES: [&str; 2] = [\"Vec\", \"Stream\"];\n97: \n98: /// Whether `name` is a built-in type and so cannot be redefined as an alias or an enum.\n99: pub fn is_builtin_type_name(name: &str) -> bool {\n100:     Type::from_name(name).is_some() || RESERVED_TYPE_NAMES.contains(&name)\n101: }\n102: \n103: /// Whether `name` is one of the built-in type constructors with its own `TypeExpr` node.\n104: /// `Opt` is no longer here: it is the prelude's enum, reached through the ordinary generic\n105: /// path like any declared name.\n106: pub fn takes_type_arg(name: &str) -> bool {\n107:     matches!(name, \"Vec\" | \"Stream\")\n108: }\n109: \n110: impl Type {\n111:     pub fn from_name(name: &str) -> Option<Type> {\n112:         match name {\n113:             \"Str\" => Some(Type::Str),\n114:             \"Int\" => Some(Type::Int),\n115:             \"Int64\" => Some(Type::Int64),\n116:             \"Float\" => Some(Type::Float),\n117:             \"Bool\" => Some(Type::Bool),\n118:             \"Char\" => Some(Type::Char),\n119:             \"Sink\" => Some(Type::Sink),\n120:             _ => None,\n\n(Showing lines 1-120 of 496. Use offset=121 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\n/// Every enum a program declares, resolved once and keyed by name, which is an enum's identity.\n///\n/// A `Type::Enum` in hand is not always enough to work from. Where an enum's payload reaches\n/// back to itself through a `Vec`, that occurrence carries a placeholder in place of the real\n/// variant list (`check::types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76), since expanding it is what\n/// has no end. So nothing reads `variants` off a type directly: everything that needs the list\n/// asks `variants` below, which re-derives it here, one layer at a time and exactly as deep as\n/// the program itself navigates (kantord/toylang#94).\npub type Enums = HashMap<String, Type>;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\npub enum Type {\n    Str,\n    Int,\n    /// A signed 64-bit integer that wraps, `Int`'s rules at twice the width (kantord/toylang#83,\n    /// extending ADR 0006). Deliberately a separate type rather than a widening of `Int`:\n    /// nothing converts implicitly, so mixing the two demands an explicit `i64(x)`. Literals\n    /// carry no suffix -- one that fits `Int` is `Int`, and any literal resolves as `Int64`","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/ty.rs","text":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\n/// Every enum a program declares, resolved once and keyed by name, which is an enum's identity.\n///\n/// A `Type::Enum` in hand is not always enough to work from. Where an enum's payload reaches\n/// back to itself through a `Vec`, that occurrence carries a placeholder in place of the real\n/// variant list (`check::types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76), since expanding it is what\n/// has no end. So nothing reads `variants` off a type directly: everything that needs the list\n/// asks `variants` below, which re-derives it here, one layer at a time and exactly as deep as\n/// the program itself navigates (kantord/toylang#94).\npub type Enums = HashMap<String, Type>;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\npub enum Type {\n    Str,\n    Int,\n    /// A signed 64-bit integer that wraps, `Int`'s rules at twice the width (kantord/toylang#83,\n    /// extending ADR 0006). Deliberately a separate type rather than a widening of `Int`:\n    /// nothing converts implicitly, so mixing the two demands an explicit `i64(x)`. Literals\n    /// carry no suffix -- one that fits `Int` is `Int`, and any literal resolves as `Int64`\n    /// wherever one is expected, the `[]` rule applied to numbers. Barred from `input`, like\n    /// `Opt`: not because a 64-bit integer has no JSON spelling but because two backends\n    /// cannot yet read one faithfully (JS parses numbers into doubles), and that codec design\n    /// has not been done.\n    Int64,\n    /// A double-precision IEEE 754 binary64 float, exactly JavaScript's number (ADR 0007):\n    /// no alternative width, no decimal type. Deliberately a separate type from `Int`, and\n    /// nothing converts implicitly: a decimal point is the spelling that makes a literal a\n    /// Float, and mixing the two in one operator is an error rather than a silent promotion.\n    /// Unlike the integer widths, arithmetic on a Float is total -- division by zero returns\n    /// Infinity, the IEEE answer, where `Int` stops the program (the Q37 ruling in\n    /// plans/questions.md, kantord/toylang#149).\n    Float,\n    Bool,\n    /// A single Unicode scalar value (kantord/toylang#75): never a surrogate half, so a\n    /// codepoint outside the Basic Multilingual Plane is one `Char`, on every backend, even\n    /// where a backend's own strings need a surrogate pair to spell it. Produced only by\n    /// `chars`, which is also the only way one is compared against a boundary -- there is no\n    /// literal syntax, so a program spells `'a'` as `chars(\"a\")[0]!`. Barred from `input` (no\n    /// wire form to read one from) and from the program's own printed result (no wire form to\n    /// write one either); everywhere else -- function signatures, records, a `Vec<Char>` --\n    /// it is an ordinary type.\n    Char,\n    Vec(Box<Type>),\n    /// Fields in declaration order. Order is not part of the type's identity (kantord/toylang#60:\n    /// `{a: Int, b: Int}` and `{b: Int, a: Int}` are one type, so `PartialEq` below compares the\n    /// field set, not the spelling), but it survives as metadata on every concrete value: a\n    /// literal's own written order is what it prints in on every backend and what the\n    /// native/Go column layouts key on. Whichever type a value is checked against is the order\n    /// it ends up carrying (`check::reorder_record` rebuilds a value that arrives shuffled), so\n    /// two differently-ordered values only coexist when nothing ever placed them in the same\n    /// checked position.\n    Record(Vec<(String, Type)>),\n    /// Effect-layer multiplicity: the entries arrive one at a time as evaluation proceeds, and\n    /// no stream object ever exists as a value (ADR 0001). Spellable in function signatures,\n    /// and only there -- the type grammar refuses it inside a Vec, a record, or another Stream\n    /// -- born at a source (`lines` is `Stream<Str>`), consumed exactly once per binding, and\n    /// exiting only through `collect`.\n    Stream(Box<Type>),\n    /// A sink: an expression that writes rather than producing a value, of which the only\n    /// instance today is the `jsonlines` builtin (gh:138 ruling). Second-class by construction,\n    /// the same way a `Stream` is -- a sink is not a value, so it never sits inside a Vec, a\n    /// record, a Stream, or an enum payload, and it is never a parameter -- with the position\n    /// rules stated once generally in the checker rather than hand-checked per builtin: a\n    /// `Sink` is legal only as the program's outermost expression or as a `Sink`-returning\n    /// function's body. A function may declare it as its return type and nothing else.\n    Sink,\n    /// A declared enum: nominal, so the name is the identity. A name (and, for a generic enum,\n    /// its arguments) determines the variants within a program, so `PartialEq` below compares\n    /// only those and not `variants` itself -- which matters because a self-referential enum's\n    /// own payload carries a placeholder here in place of the real list\n    /// (`check::types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76) rather than the list every other\n    /// occurrence of the same name carries. A variant's payload is `None` for a unit variant,\n    /// in declaration order.\n    ///\n    /// The list riding along is a convenience, not the source of truth: read it and a\n    /// placeholder reads as an enum with no variants at all, which is how a nested recursive\n    /// value used to reach every backend's printer as something it could not render\n    /// (kantord/toylang#94). Ask `variants` instead.\n    Enum {\n        name: String,\n        /// The type arguments this instantiation was built with, in declaration order; empty\n        /// for an enum declared without parameters. Part of the identity: `Pair<Int>` and\n        /// `Pair<Str>` are different types even though both are `Pair`.\n        args: Vec<Type>,\n        variants: Vec<(String, Option<Type>)>,\n    },\n    /// A type parameter inside a generic enum's registry template, standing for whatever a\n    /// use site will supply. Never the type of any checked expression: instantiation\n    /// substitutes every one away before a type leaves resolution.\n    Param(String),\n}\n\n/// Names reserved for the built-in type constructors, on top of `Str`/`Int`/`Bool`\n/// (`Type::from_name`): a program cannot declare an alias or enum under any of these.\nconst RESERVED_TYPE_NAMES: [&str; 2] = [\"Vec\", \"Stream\"];\n\n/// Whether `name` is a built-in type and so cannot be redefined as an alias or an enum.\npub fn is_builtin_type_name(name: &str) -> bool {\n    Type::from_name(name).is_some() || RESERVED_TYPE_NAMES.contains(&name)\n}\n\n/// Whether `name` is one of the built-in type constructors with its own `TypeExpr` node.\n/// `Opt` is no longer here: it is the prelude's enum, reached through the ordinary generic\n/// path like any declared name.\npub fn takes_type_arg(name: &str) -> bool {\n    matches!(name, \"Vec\" | \"Stream\")\n}\n\nimpl Type {\n    pub fn from_name(name: &str) -> Option<Type> {\n        match name {\n            \"Str\" => Some(Type::Str),\n            \"Int\" => Some(Type::Int),\n            \"Int64\" => Some(Type::Int64),\n            \"Float\" => Some(Type::Float),\n            \"Bool\" => Some(Type::Bool),\n            \"Char\" => Some(Type::Char),\n            \"Sink\" => Some(Type::Sink),\n            _ => None,","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":120,"totalLines":496,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/ty.rs","time":{"start":1788705363036,"end":1788705363043}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_07725d45a001Lik56CBlEqq5q1","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_07725cdcb001egMKLcLrNW2fTZ"}}
