construct(ctx, want, text, *span, None, None, true).map(Expected::Checked);\n3736:        return built.map(Expected::Checked);\n3763:            return map_call(ctx, arg, *span, Some(elem)).map(Expected::Checked);\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n \"fn expect|fn expect_inner|fn conform|Expected\" src/check/mod.rs | head -30","time":{"start":1788705387659,"end":1788705387663}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK. I've now got a full picture. Let me decide on the final architecture, keeping scope tight and honesty about what's implemented.\n\n## Final design\n\n### 1. AST\n\nAdd `Expr::Module { path: String, span: Span }`.\n\n### 2. Parser\n\n- Add `Tok::At` token (`@`).\n- In `atom()`, on `Tok::At`: parse `@` `(` `<path>` `)` where path is a run of chars that aren't `)` or whitespace. Errors:\n  - `@` followed by anything other than `(`: \"expected `(` after `@`...\"` Actually, since we gate on or_separates, maybe simpler. Hmm, do I gate parse position? Let me reconsider.\n\n\n\nActually, gating the parse on `or_separates` adds coupling that's subtle. Alternative: parse `@(...)` in `atom()` unconditionally; the checker enforces subject availability. But then `@(x.toy)` could appear nested (e.g., `1 + @(x.toy)`) and would check as a dispatch needing a subject → \"needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\". Hmm, but also `@(x.toy)` as a whole program body → same error. That's fine.\n\nBut the doc calls it a \"routing-arm form\". Position-gating in the parser would be more faithful. Hmm. But actually, `or_separates` gating is fragile: what about an arm body that is `@(x.toy)` directly? `arm_body` calls `expr(COND_POWER)`; `expr` for COND_POWER (>PIPE_RIGHT) calls `expr_at(min_power, false)` → `operand` → ... → `atom`. `or_separates` is still true at that point (set by arm_body's with_or(true). Good. But inside a VecLit item in an arm body, `expr(0)` resets it false. So gating works: `@(` only parses at arm-body top level. \n\nHmm, wait, is that right though? `expr_at` with fresh=false (since COND_POWER > PIPE_RIGHT) → operand... The `or_separates` flag remains true through the operand chain (no with_or reset until a nested expr(0). So yes. \n\nBut hmm — actually, do I even want the parser to reject `@(x.toy)` outside arm bodies? Consider a guard arm: `x > 0 -> @(foo.toy)`... wait that's an arm body too. And a function body: `fn f(x: Int) -> Int = @(x.toy)` — is that legal? A dispatch in a function body. Semantically it'd need a subject; inside a fn body `.` is not bound → subject None → error. So refusing it at parse time vs. check time gives different error messages. Not important.\n\n\n\nDecision: parse `@(...)` in `atom()` unconditionally, and let the checker enforce that it needs a subject (consistency with `map`/`select`/match which parse anywhere and check the subject. This is less parser coupling and matches the codebase's pattern (select/map/match parse as ordinary calls/nodes and the checker restricts position. Hmm, but actually `match` is restricted... no, `match` is parsed whenever arms start. And needs a subject in checker. Same pattern.\n\n\n\nActually, hmm, wait. Let me reconsider once more. There's a subtle issue: if `@(path)` parses in atom() unconditionally, then `@(path)` could be a pipe stage: `r | @(route-groups/baz.toy)`. Is that legal? It would dispatch the module on the subject `.`... `@(x.toy)` with subject=r. Hmm, semantically that's exactly \"run this module on the subject\". The doc says it's an arm body form, but the semantics \"applied to the subject\" would make `r | @(baz.toy)` a perfectly sensible spelling too. Should I allow it? The design doc says \"a dedicated routing-arm form\". Keeping it arm-body-only in the checker? How would the checker distinguish arm-body context? It can't easily...\n\n\n\nOK here's the pragmatic line: allow `@(path)` anywhere an expression parses (atom), and the checker requires a subject (like map/select/match. So `r | @(baz.toy)` works, and `r | FooBar -> @(baz.toy)` works, and `1 + @(baz.toy)` fails with \"needs a subject\". This is the least parser surgery and most consistent with the existing \"the checker owns position rules\" philosophy (see sink position rule, source-in-fn, mapper rules...). I'll note this in the commit message/provenance as an agent decision extending the ruled form to pipe positions. Hmm, wait — but is that overreach? \"Ruled: option C, a dedicated routing-arm form\". Extending it to `r | @(x.toy)` is beyond the ruling... but it's a natural consequence of \"applied to the subject\". Actually, I worry about scope creep. The task: \"Implement `@(path)` module-as-function routing-arm syntax\". The minimum: it works as an arm body. Allowing `r | @(x.toy)` too is harmless extra (same machinery, no extra code — it falls out of \"needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\"). It actually does NOT add code. So fine.\n\n\n\n### 3. Compile plumbing (base dir)\n\nAdd:\n```rust\npub fn compile(src: &str) -> Result<Program, Error> { compile_from(src, None }\npub fn compile_from(src: &str, base: Option<&Path>) -> Result<Program, Error> {\n    let mut file = parse::parse(src)?;\n    prelude::inject(&mut file);\n    check::check(&file, base)\n}\n```\n\nWait, `check::check` currently takes `&File`. I'll add the base dir param. Hmm, but the module dispatch needs the base dir at the point the dispatch is met. Since `@(path)` nodes resolve at check time, thread the base into `Ctx`. Let me define:\n\n`check::check(file: &File, base: Option<&Path>)`. Hmm, but actually the base needs to be a PathBuf (owned, or a &Path). Ctx can hold `Option<&Path>`... but modules' dirs differ per module (recursion — module A dispatches relative to module A's dir). So each module check gets its own base. For recursion, when checking a module's defs (via the module machinery), the base is the module file's parent dir. \n\nLet me design the module machinery concretely now.\n\n\n\n### 4. Module machinery (checker)\n\nNew struct in check/mod.rs:\n```rust\n/// The modules a program dispatches into, keyed by resolved absolute path. A module is\n/// loaded and checked at most once per compile.\n\nstruct LoadedModule {\n    /// The checked functions of the module, names prefixed so no two modules collide.\n\n    funcs: Vec<tir::Func>,\n    /// The entry function's renamed name.\n\n    entry: String,\n    /// The entry function's signature, for checking the dispatch site.\n\n    sig: Sig,\n    /// The enums the module declared, for the program's registry so backends can print them.\n\n    enums: Vec<(String, Type)>,   // hmm, or HashMap\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but `LoadedModule` needs to be mutable in place (insert into cache)... Actually, the cache: `RefCell<HashMap<PathBuf, Rc<LoadedModule>>>`. Since funcs are shared, use Rc or just clone. Since each dispatch site needs (entry name, sig,,and the funcs need appending once per module... Let me collect funcs separately: `modules_funcs: RefCell<Vec<tir::Func>>` in Ctx, plus a `module_cache: RefCell<HashMap<PathBuf, (String, Sig)>>` (entry name+sig per path). On first dispatch to a path: load+check, append funcs to modules_funcs, cache entry name+sig. On subsequent: reuse. And `module_enums: RefCell<Vec<...>>` collect enums.\n\nHmm, wait — multiple fields getting unwieldy. Let me use one struct:\n\n```rust\nstruct Modules<'a> {\n    /// Keyed by the absolute path a `@(path)` resolved to.\n    loaded: HashMap<PathBuf, Entry>,\n    /// The checked functions of every loaded module, names already prefixed.\n\n    funcs: Vec<tir::Func>,\n    /// The enums every loaded module declared, for the program's registry.\n    enums: Vec<(String, Type)>,\n}\nstruct Entry {\n    /// The renamed entry function the dispatch lowers to a call of.\n    func: String,\n    /// The entry's signature, checked against the subject and the position's expectation.\n    sig: Sig,\n}\n```\n\n`Ctx` gets `modules: &'a RefCell<Modules>`. Hmm, `&RefCell<Modules>` is fine (the whole Ctx is already borrowed. But Ctx's lifetimes... `Modules` contains Vec<tir::Func> (owned. Fine.\n\n\n\nThe dispatch handler (in synth_inner or a helper):\n```rust\nExpr::Module { path, span } => module_dispatch(ctx, path, *span),\n```\n\n```rust\nfn module_dispatch(ctx: &Ctx, path: &str, span: Span) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let Some((subject_ty, sid)) = ctx.subject.clone() else {\n        return Err(Error::new(span, \"`@(...)` needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\"));\n    };\n    let entry = resolve_module(ctx, path, span)?;   // Entry { func, sig }\n    // The entry must accept the subject's type and return what the position expects. Since a\n    // match arm's body is checked with an expectation when later arms exist, the return\n    // type is whatever the position needs; check via call-like rules.\n    let Some(param_ty) = &entry.sig.param else {\n        return Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"`@({path})`'s entry function takes no argument, so there is nothing to pass the subject to\")));\n    };\n    if &subject_ty != param_ty {\n        return Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"`@({path})` needs {param_ty}, found {subject_ty}\")));\n    }\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        entry.sig.ret.clone(),\n        Kind::Call { func: entry.func.clone(), arg: Some(Box::new(Tir::new(subject_ty, Kind::Local(sid))))) },\n    ))\n}\n```\n\nWait, but the expected-return check: how do match arms check body expectations? In `match_chain`, arms' bodies are either synthesized (first arm sets the result type) or expected against the first arm's type. `@(...)` as an arm body would be handled by `synth` (which calls the dispatch and returns the entry's ret. In `match_chain`, the arm body is `synth(&arm_ctx, &arm.body)` for the first arm, then `expect(&arm_ctx, &arm.body, t)` for later arms. `expect` calls `synth_inner` or `expect_inner`... The dispatch in synth_inner returns the entry ret, and the caller (`expect`/`synth` wrapper) checks it against want. Hmm, wait — `expect` calls `synth_inner`? Let me check `expect` and `expect_inner`.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_077263480001ZtWuaofVww9i3d","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_07725da48001FQRNLmhJEnL7sJ"}}
