{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705389659,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_98411f8c41e74d97ac8866b0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/check/mod.rs","limit":180,"offset":3694},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/check/mod.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n3694: fn expect_inner(ctx: &Ctx, expr: &Expr, want: &Type) -> Result<Expected, Error> {\n3695:     // The forms whose type comes from their position rather than their contents. `parse`\n3696:     // is resolved by what it is checked against: `parse(stdin)` is the checked\n3697:     // `Stream<Str> -> T` overload (one value read from stdin), and `stdin | map(parse(.))`\n3698:     // is the checked spelling `inputs` retired into. Both are lowered to the existing `Input`/\n3699:     // `Inputs` nodes, whose single-read slots and element-type fixing the backends already\n3700:     // rely on.\n3701:     if let Expr::Call {\n3702:         func, arg, span, ..\n3703:     } = expr\n3704:         && func == \"parse\"\n3705:     {\n3706:         let arg = need_arg(arg, \"parse\", *span)?;\n3707:         return parse_call(ctx, arg, *span, want).map(Expected::Checked);\n3708:     }\n3709: \n3710:     if let Some(span) = inputs_shape(expr) {\n3711:         return inputs_read(ctx, span, want).map(Expected::Checked);\n3712:     }\n3713: \n3714:     // `collect(inputs)` in a Vec-wanted position: the honest eager spelling the decision\n3715:     // records. `collect` is otherwise synthesised argument-first, but `inputs` has no type of\n3716:     // its own until checked, so the wanted Vec<T> is pushed back through as Stream<T> here.\n3717:     if let Some(arg) = call_named(expr, \"collect\")\n3718:         && let Some(elem) = want.elem()\n3719:     {\n3720:         return collect(ctx, arg, Some(elem)).map(Expected::Checked);\n3721:     }\n3722: \n3723:     // A string literal where an enum is wanted is the variant it names, the same spelling the\n3724:     // wire format already gives a unit variant. `construct` owns the errors: a payload variant\n3725:     // gets the how-to-write-it hint, and an unknown name is named against the enum.\n3726:     if let Expr::Str { text, span } = expr\n3727:         && matches!(want, Type::Enum { .. })\n3728:     {\n3729:         return construct(ctx, want, text, *span, None, None, true).map(Expected::Checked);\n3730:     }\n3731: \n3732:     // An Int64-expected literal and a wanted enum's variant are the same kind of arm: a form\n3733:     // resolved by its position, built by its own helper, `None` falling through.\n3734:     if let Some(built) = int64_resolved(ctx, expr, want).or_else(|| wanted_variant(ctx, expr, want))\n3735:     {\n3736:         return built.map(Expected::Checked);\n3737:     }\n3738: \n3739:     // The pipe's value is the right side's, so the expectation flows into the right side --\n3740:     // the only road into the subject-fed forms, which exist only after `|`.\n3741:     if let Expr::Pipe { lhs, rhs, .. } = expr {\n3742:         return pipe(ctx, lhs, rhs, Some(want));\n3743:     }\n3744: \n3745:     // A `let` block's value is its final expression, so the expectation flows into that. The\n3746:     // bindings themselves synthesise, since a local binding has no position to resolve one from.\n3747:     if let Expr::Let { bindings, body, .. } = expr {\n3748:         return let_bind(ctx, bindings, body, Some(want));\n3749:     }\n3750: \n3751:     // A `map` whose position expects a Vec (over a Vec subject) or a Stream (over a Stream\n3752:     // subject) pushes the expected element type into its body. Any other want, or no subject\n3753:     // at all, falls through to synthesis, which owns those errors.\n3754:     if let Expr::Call { span, .. } = expr\n3755:         && let Some(arg) = call_named(expr, \"map\")\n3756:     {\n3757:         let want_elem = match (ctx.subject.as_ref().map(|(ty, _)| ty), want) {\n3758:             (Some(Type::Vec(_)), Type::Vec(elem)) => Some(elem.as_ref()),\n3759:             (Some(Type::Stream(_)), Type::Stream(elem)) => Some(elem.as_ref()),\n3760:             _ => None,\n3761:         };\n3762:         if let Some(elem) = want_elem {\n3763:             return map_call(ctx, arg, *span, Some(elem)).map(Expected::Checked);\n3764:         }\n3765:     }\n3766: \n3767:     // Every arm of a total match chain receives the expectation the same way; a partial\n3768:     // chain's peel is `match_arm_want`'s call.\n3769:     if let Expr::Match { arms, span } = expr\n3770:         && !want.contains_stream()\n3771:         && let Some(push) = match_arm_want(arms, want)\n3772:     {\n3773:         return match_chain(ctx, arms, *span, Some(push)).map(Expected::Checked);\n3774:     }\n3775: \n3776:     // A record literal checked against a record type pushes each field's expected type into\n3777:     // its value. Matching is by name, not position (kantord/toylang#60: {a: Int, b: Int} and\n3778:     // {b: Int, a: Int} are one type), so a mis-fielded literal -- wrong names, wrong count --\n3779:     // falls back to synthesis, where the existing errors say what is wrong. The built fields\n3780:     // are written in `want`'s order regardless of how the literal spelled them: declaration\n3781:     // order is what the printer and the columnar layouts key on, and both read it off the\n3782:     // type, not the source text.\n3783:     if let Expr::RecordLit { fields, .. } = expr\n3784:         && let Type::Record(want_fields) = want\n3785:         && fields.len() == want_fields.len()\n3786:         && want_fields\n3787:             .iter()\n3788:             .all(|(wanted, _)| fields.iter().any(|(name, _, _)| name == wanted))\n3789:     {\n3790:         let mut built = Vec::new();\n3791:         for (wanted, field_ty) in want_fields {\n3792:             let (name, _, value) = fields.iter().find(|(name, _, _)| name == wanted).unwrap();\n3793:             let field = expect(ctx, value, field_ty).map_err(|e| {\n3794:                 stream_refusal(ctx, value, || {\n3795:                     format!(\"`{name}` cannot hold a stream, which has nothing to store\")\n3796:                 })\n3797:                 .unwrap_or(e)\n3798:             })?;\n3799:             built.push((name.clone(), field));\n3800:         }\n3801:         return Ok(Expected::Checked(Tir::new(\n3802:             want.clone(),\n3803:             Kind::RecordLit { fields: built },\n3804:         )));\n3805:     }\n3806: \n3807:     // A Vec literal where a Vec is wanted takes its element type from the position, which is\n3808:     // what lets `[]` -- refused outright under synthesis -- resolve wherever something already\n3809:     // says what it holds.\n3810:     if let Expr::VecLit { items, .. } = expr\n3811:         && let Type::Vec(elem) = want\n3812:     {\n3813:         let mut out = Vec::new();\n3814:         for item in items {\n3815:             let item = expect(ctx, item, elem).map_err(|e| {\n3816:                 stream_refusal(ctx, item, || {\n3817:                     \"a Vec cannot hold a stream, which has nothing to store\".to_string()\n3818:                 })\n3819:                 .unwrap_or(e)\n3820:             })?;\n3821:             out.push(item);\n3822:         }\n3823:         return Ok(Expected::Checked(Tir::new(want.clone(), Kind::VecLit(out))));\n3824:     }\n3825: \n3826:     synth(ctx, expr).map(Expected::Synthesised)\n3827: }\n3828: \n3829: /// The synth arms refuse a stream inside a record or Vec with a message that teaches why\n3830: /// (nothing can store or re-read one); the checked fast paths above would otherwise demote\n3831: /// that refusal to a bare \"expected T, found Stream<T>\" mismatch. When a pushed field or\n3832: /// element fails, ask synthesis whether a stream was the reason and keep the lesson.\n3833: fn stream_refusal(ctx: &Ctx, value: &Expr, msg: impl FnOnce() -> String) -> Option<Error> {\n3834:     let found = synth(ctx, value).ok()?;\n3835:     found\n3836:         .ty\n3837:         .contains_stream()\n3838:         .then(|| Error::new(value.span(), msg()))\n3839: }\n\n(End of file - total 3839 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"fn expect_inner(ctx: &Ctx, expr: &Expr, want: &Type) -> Result<Expected, Error> {\n    // The forms whose type comes from their position rather than their contents. `parse`\n    // is resolved by what it is checked against: `parse(stdin)` is the checked\n    // `Stream<Str> -> T` overload (one value read from stdin), and `stdin | map(parse(.))`\n    // is the checked spelling `inputs` retired into. Both are lowered to the existing `Input`/\n    // `Inputs` nodes, whose single-read slots and element-type fixing the backends already\n    // rely on.\n    if let Expr::Call {\n        func, arg, span, ..\n    } = expr\n        && func == \"parse\"\n    {\n        let arg = need_arg(arg, \"parse\", *span)?;\n        return parse_call(ctx, arg, *span, want).map(Expected::Checked);\n    }\n\n    if let Some(span) = inputs_shape(expr) {\n        return inputs_read(ctx, span, want).map(Expected::Checked);\n    }\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/check/mod.rs","text":"fn expect_inner(ctx: &Ctx, expr: &Expr, want: &Type) -> Result<Expected, Error> {\n    // The forms whose type comes from their position rather than their contents. `parse`\n    // is resolved by what it is checked against: `parse(stdin)` is the checked\n    // `Stream<Str> -> T` overload (one value read from stdin), and `stdin | map(parse(.))`\n    // is the checked spelling `inputs` retired into. Both are lowered to the existing `Input`/\n    // `Inputs` nodes, whose single-read slots and element-type fixing the backends already\n    // rely on.\n    if let Expr::Call {\n        func, arg, span, ..\n    } = expr\n        && func == \"parse\"\n    {\n        let arg = need_arg(arg, \"parse\", *span)?;\n        return parse_call(ctx, arg, *span, want).map(Expected::Checked);\n    }\n\n    if let Some(span) = inputs_shape(expr) {\n        return inputs_read(ctx, span, want).map(Expected::Checked);\n    }\n\n    // `collect(inputs)` in a Vec-wanted position: the honest eager spelling the decision\n    // records. `collect` is otherwise synthesised argument-first, but `inputs` has no type of\n    // its own until checked, so the wanted Vec<T> is pushed back through as Stream<T> here.\n    if let Some(arg) = call_named(expr, \"collect\")\n        && let Some(elem) = want.elem()\n    {\n        return collect(ctx, arg, Some(elem)).map(Expected::Checked);\n    }\n\n    // A string literal where an enum is wanted is the variant it names, the same spelling the\n    // wire format already gives a unit variant. `construct` owns the errors: a payload variant\n    // gets the how-to-write-it hint, and an unknown name is named against the enum.\n    if let Expr::Str { text, span } = expr\n        && matches!(want, Type::Enum { .. })\n    {\n        return construct(ctx, want, text, *span, None, None, true).map(Expected::Checked);\n    }\n\n    // An Int64-expected literal and a wanted enum's variant are the same kind of arm: a form\n    // resolved by its position, built by its own helper, `None` falling through.\n    if let Some(built) = int64_resolved(ctx, expr, want).or_else(|| wanted_variant(ctx, expr, want))\n    {\n        return built.map(Expected::Checked);\n    }\n\n    // The pipe's value is the right side's, so the expectation flows into the right side --\n    // the only road into the subject-fed forms, which exist only after `|`.\n    if let Expr::Pipe { lhs, rhs, .. } = expr {\n        return pipe(ctx, lhs, rhs, Some(want));\n    }\n\n    // A `let` block's value is its final expression, so the expectation flows into that. The\n    // bindings themselves synthesise, since a local binding has no position to resolve one from.\n    if let Expr::Let { bindings, body, .. } = expr {\n        return let_bind(ctx, bindings, body, Some(want));\n    }\n\n    // A `map` whose position expects a Vec (over a Vec subject) or a Stream (over a Stream\n    // subject) pushes the expected element type into its body. Any other want, or no subject\n    // at all, falls through to synthesis, which owns those errors.\n    if let Expr::Call { span, .. } = expr\n        && let Some(arg) = call_named(expr, \"map\")\n    {\n        let want_elem = match (ctx.subject.as_ref().map(|(ty, _)| ty), want) {\n            (Some(Type::Vec(_)), Type::Vec(elem)) => Some(elem.as_ref()),\n            (Some(Type::Stream(_)), Type::Stream(elem)) => Some(elem.as_ref()),\n            _ => None,\n        };\n        if let Some(elem) = want_elem {\n            return map_call(ctx, arg, *span, Some(elem)).map(Expected::Checked);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Every arm of a total match chain receives the expectation the same way; a partial\n    // chain's peel is `match_arm_want`'s call.\n    if let Expr::Match { arms, span } = expr\n        && !want.contains_stream()\n        && let Some(push) = match_arm_want(arms, want)\n    {\n        return match_chain(ctx, arms, *span, Some(push)).map(Expected::Checked);\n    }\n\n    // A record literal checked against a record type pushes each field's expected type into\n    // its value. Matching is by name, not position (kantord/toylang#60: {a: Int, b: Int} and\n    // {b: Int, a: Int} are one type), so a mis-fielded literal -- wrong names, wrong count --\n    // falls back to synthesis, where the existing errors say what is wrong. The built fields\n    // are written in `want`'s order regardless of how the literal spelled them: declaration\n    // order is what the printer and the columnar layouts key on, and both read it off the\n    // type, not the source text.\n    if let Expr::RecordLit { fields, .. } = expr\n        && let Type::Record(want_fields) = want\n        && fields.len() == want_fields.len()\n        && want_fields\n            .iter()\n            .all(|(wanted, _)| fields.iter().any(|(name, _, _)| name == wanted))\n    {\n        let mut built = Vec::new();\n        for (wanted, field_ty) in want_fields {\n            let (name, _, value) = fields.iter().find(|(name, _, _)| name == wanted).unwrap();\n            let field = expect(ctx, value, field_ty).map_err(|e| {\n                stream_refusal(ctx, value, || {\n                    format!(\"`{name}` cannot hold a stream, which has nothing to store\")\n                })\n                .unwrap_or(e)\n            })?;\n            built.push((name.clone(), field));\n        }\n        return Ok(Expected::Checked(Tir::new(\n            want.clone(),\n            Kind::RecordLit { fields: built },\n        )));\n    }\n\n    // A Vec literal where a Vec is wanted takes its element type from the position, which is\n    // what lets `[]` -- refused outright under synthesis -- resolve wherever something already\n    // says what it holds.\n    if let Expr::VecLit { items, .. } = expr\n        && let Type::Vec(elem) = want\n    {\n        let mut out = Vec::new();\n        for item in items {\n            let item = expect(ctx, item, elem).map_err(|e| {\n                stream_refusal(ctx, item, || {\n                    \"a Vec cannot hold a stream, which has nothing to store\".to_string()\n                })\n                .unwrap_or(e)\n            })?;\n            out.push(item);\n        }\n        return Ok(Expected::Checked(Tir::new(want.clone(), Kind::VecLit(out))));\n    }\n\n    synth(ctx, expr).map(Expected::Synthesised)\n}\n\n/// The synth arms refuse a stream inside a record or Vec with a message that teaches why\n/// (nothing can store or re-read one); the checked fast paths above would otherwise demote\n/// that refusal to a bare \"expected T, found Stream<T>\" mismatch. When a pushed field or\n/// element fails, ask synthesis whether a stream was the reason and keep the lesson.\nfn stream_refusal(ctx: &Ctx, value: &Expr, msg: impl FnOnce() -> String) -> Option<Error> {\n    let found = synth(ctx, value).ok()?;\n    found\n        .ty\n        .contains_stream()\n        .then(|| Error::new(value.span(), msg()))\n}","lineStart":3694,"lineEnd":3839,"totalLines":3839,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/check/mod.rs","time":{"start":1788705389648,"end":1788705389656}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_077263c4e001kBfZbfv2MM8lDh","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_0772634a7001HDMUXvHxQpFX8G"}}
