{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705393894,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_662f0f21eaf74890baffec72","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/check/mod.rs","limit":70,"offset":3239},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/check/mod.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n3239: enum Expected {\n3240:     Checked(Tir),\n3241:     Synthesised(Tir),\n3242: }\n3243: \n3244: /// The checking direction: an expected type goes in, and the expression is verified against it\n3245: /// rather than asked what it is. Most forms answer both questions, but not all do.\n3246: fn expect(ctx: &Ctx, expr: &Expr, want: &Type) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n3247:     match expect_inner(ctx, expr, want)? {\n3248:         Expected::Checked(tir) => Ok(tir),\n3249:         Expected::Synthesised(found) => {\n3250:             if &found.ty != want {\n3251:                 return Err(Error::new(\n3252:                     expr.span(),\n3253:                     format!(\"expected {want}, found {}\", found.ty),\n3254:                 ));\n3255:             }\n3256:             Ok(reorder_record(ctx, found, want))\n3257:         }\n3258:     }\n3259: }\n3260: \n3261: /// `found`, rebuilt to `want`'s field order when the two already agree (kantord/toylang#60) --\n3262: /// a no-op when they don't, leaving a real mismatch for the caller's own comparison to catch\n3263: /// and name.\n3264: fn conform(ctx: &Ctx, found: Tir, want: &Type) -> Tir {\n3265:     if &found.ty == want {\n3266:         reorder_record(ctx, found, want)\n3267:     } else {\n3268:         found\n3269:     }\n3270: }\n3271: \n3272: /// Whether `a` and `b` are not just the same type (kantord/toylang#60: a record's fields are a\n3273: /// set, not a sequence) but physically identical -- same fields, same order, all the way into\n3274: /// any field that is itself a record. Every non-nominal backend reads a record by name and does\n3275: /// not care; the native backend and the columnar Vec layout read it by position, off whichever\n3276: /// type a value is currently checked against, so a value crossing into a differently-ordered\n3277: /// but equal position needs rebuilding before those readers see it. This is what decides\n3278: /// whether that rebuild is needed.\n3279: ///\n3280: /// Also recurses into an enum's variant payloads (kantord/toylang#66): two instantiations of the\n3281: /// same generic enum are one `Type` (`Type::Enum`'s `PartialEq` compares `name` and `args`\n3282: /// only, and a generic argument's own order-insensitivity comes from `Type::Record`'s), so a\n3283: /// payload written in a different field order at each call site is exactly the same hazard a\n3284: /// bare record or a Vec element is. The variant list itself is never out of order -- it comes\n3285: /// from one declaration -- so zipping the two enums' variants positionally is safe, *except*\n3286: /// when one side's list is a recursive self-reference's placeholder (kantord/toylang#76): the\n3287: /// zip then runs zero pairs and this reports \"no reorder needed\" rather than crashing, which is\n3288: /// conservatively correct (nothing gets left mis-ordered that this function could have fixed)\n3289: /// though not necessarily complete (a reorder a full expansion would have found stays undone).\n3290: fn same_field_order(a: &Type, b: &Type) -> bool {\n3291:     match (a, b) {\n3292:         (Type::Record(x), Type::Record(y)) => {\n3293:             x.len() == y.len()\n3294:                 && x.iter()\n3295:                     .zip(y)\n3296:                     .all(|((n1, t1), (n2, t2))| n1 == n2 && same_field_order(t1, t2))\n3297:         }\n3298:         (Type::Vec(x), Type::Vec(y)) | (Type::Stream(x), Type::Stream(y)) => same_field_order(x, y),\n3299:         (Type::Enum { variants: vx, .. }, Type::Enum { variants: vy, .. }) => {\n3300:             vx.iter().zip(vy).all(|((_, px), (_, py))| match (px, py) {\n3301:                 (Some(tx), Some(ty)) => same_field_order(tx, ty),\n3302:                 _ => true,\n3303:             })\n3304:         }\n3305:         _ => true,\n3306:     }\n3307: }\n3308: \n\n(Showing lines 3239-3308 of 3839. Use offset=3309 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"enum Expected {\n    Checked(Tir),\n    Synthesised(Tir),\n}\n\n/// The checking direction: an expected type goes in, and the expression is verified against it\n/// rather than asked what it is. Most forms answer both questions, but not all do.\nfn expect(ctx: &Ctx, expr: &Expr, want: &Type) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    match expect_inner(ctx, expr, want)? {\n        Expected::Checked(tir) => Ok(tir),\n        Expected::Synthesised(found) => {\n            if &found.ty != want {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    expr.span(),\n                    format!(\"expected {want}, found {}\", found.ty),\n                ));\n            }\n            Ok(reorder_record(ctx, found, want))\n        }\n    }","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/src/check/mod.rs","text":"enum Expected {\n    Checked(Tir),\n    Synthesised(Tir),\n}\n\n/// The checking direction: an expected type goes in, and the expression is verified against it\n/// rather than asked what it is. Most forms answer both questions, but not all do.\nfn expect(ctx: &Ctx, expr: &Expr, want: &Type) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    match expect_inner(ctx, expr, want)? {\n        Expected::Checked(tir) => Ok(tir),\n        Expected::Synthesised(found) => {\n            if &found.ty != want {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    expr.span(),\n                    format!(\"expected {want}, found {}\", found.ty),\n                ));\n            }\n            Ok(reorder_record(ctx, found, want))\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// `found`, rebuilt to `want`'s field order when the two already agree (kantord/toylang#60) --\n/// a no-op when they don't, leaving a real mismatch for the caller's own comparison to catch\n/// and name.\nfn conform(ctx: &Ctx, found: Tir, want: &Type) -> Tir {\n    if &found.ty == want {\n        reorder_record(ctx, found, want)\n    } else {\n        found\n    }\n}\n\n/// Whether `a` and `b` are not just the same type (kantord/toylang#60: a record's fields are a\n/// set, not a sequence) but physically identical -- same fields, same order, all the way into\n/// any field that is itself a record. Every non-nominal backend reads a record by name and does\n/// not care; the native backend and the columnar Vec layout read it by position, off whichever\n/// type a value is currently checked against, so a value crossing into a differently-ordered\n/// but equal position needs rebuilding before those readers see it. This is what decides\n/// whether that rebuild is needed.\n///\n/// Also recurses into an enum's variant payloads (kantord/toylang#66): two instantiations of the\n/// same generic enum are one `Type` (`Type::Enum`'s `PartialEq` compares `name` and `args`\n/// only, and a generic argument's own order-insensitivity comes from `Type::Record`'s), so a\n/// payload written in a different field order at each call site is exactly the same hazard a\n/// bare record or a Vec element is. The variant list itself is never out of order -- it comes\n/// from one declaration -- so zipping the two enums' variants positionally is safe, *except*\n/// when one side's list is a recursive self-reference's placeholder (kantord/toylang#76): the\n/// zip then runs zero pairs and this reports \"no reorder needed\" rather than crashing, which is\n/// conservatively correct (nothing gets left mis-ordered that this function could have fixed)\n/// though not necessarily complete (a reorder a full expansion would have found stays undone).\nfn same_field_order(a: &Type, b: &Type) -> bool {\n    match (a, b) {\n        (Type::Record(x), Type::Record(y)) => {\n            x.len() == y.len()\n                && x.iter()\n                    .zip(y)\n                    .all(|((n1, t1), (n2, t2))| n1 == n2 && same_field_order(t1, t2))\n        }\n        (Type::Vec(x), Type::Vec(y)) | (Type::Stream(x), Type::Stream(y)) => same_field_order(x, y),\n        (Type::Enum { variants: vx, .. }, Type::Enum { variants: vy, .. }) => {\n            vx.iter().zip(vy).all(|((_, px), (_, py))| match (px, py) {\n                (Some(tx), Some(ty)) => same_field_order(tx, ty),\n                _ => true,\n            })\n        }\n        _ => true,\n    }\n}\n","lineStart":3239,"lineEnd":3308,"totalLines":3839,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/check/mod.rs","time":{"start":1788705393877,"end":1788705393890}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK so `expect_inner` has fast paths for checked-only forms, and falls through to `synth(...)` for ordinary synthesis. For `@(path)`, the dispatch can be handled in `synth_inner` (where I add an arm). `expect` would then compare the entry's ret against the want (via `conform`... Actually, `expect` calls `expect_inner` → falls to `synth` → `synth_inner` gets the dispatch Tir → returns `Expected::Synthesised(tir)` → `expect` checks `if tir.ty != want → conform/error`... Let me check expect:","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_077264cd0001RI4Z3ltWjTnDRt","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_077263c6c001Yb4pYbdTeRoFrd"}}
