{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705405391,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_da3aa680bdb04843b2d9f83a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"fn value_name|fn is_lower\" src/check/types.rs src/*.rs"},"output":"src/check/types.rs:23:fn value_name(name: &str, span: Span, what: &str) -> Result<(), Error> {\n","metadata":{"output":"src/check/types.rs:23:fn value_name(name: &str, span: Span, what: &str) -> Result<(), Error> {\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n \"fn value_name|fn is_lower\" src/check/types.rs src/*.rs","time":{"start":1788705405387,"end":1788705405389}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"`Sig` is Clone. Good.\n\nNow let me design the module loading. The tricky parts:\n1. Path resolution: `base_dir.join(path)`. `base_dir` is Option<&Path>. If None, what? For `run_on(src, None base)` — programs with `@(` from a string. The natural fallback: resolve relative to the current working directory. `std::env::current_dir()`. Hmm, but for `compile(src)` (the common test path), if a program uses `@(`, it'd resolve relative to CWD. That's a reasonable default (\"relative to the referring file\"; with no file, the referring file's directory is the CWD). Actually main.rs will pass the file's parent. For compile(src) callers (tests etc.), base=None → CWD. OK.\n\n2. Load: `std::fs::read_to_string(resolved_path)` → parse_module. Errors → `Error::new(span, ...)` naming the path. But `Error` has `span` — a span in the calling file. Good: point at the `@(path)` node.\n\n3. Rename defs: prefix per module. What prefix? Use the path string sanitized. E.g. path `route-groups/baz.toy` → prefix... Let me think about uniqueness: two paths could sanitize to the same prefix (e.g., `a-b.toy` and `a_b.toy`). Rare, but for safety use... hmm, just accept the risk (document?). Actually, could make the prefix include a counter index: `__m{idx}_`. But then names are unstable... fine, they're internal. Let me use a per-module index: the Nth distinct module gets prefix `__module_{n}_`. Clean, unique, no path-derived collisions. And the emitted code has names like `__module_0_handle`. Hmm, but wait, `value_name` casing check: function names start lowercase — `__module_0_handle` starts with `_`... is `_` allowed as first char? `value_name` checks... let me check.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0772679c1001Qmxd3fH4m4rQF3","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_0772662ba001QbuPXwZxeSRUQG"}}
