{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705425743,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_bbd5ccb8cda24332b6491805","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"Origin::|match.*Origin|origin:\" src/ --glob '!src/ast.rs' | head -30"},"output":"src/prelude.rs:31:        def.origin = Origin::Prelude;\nsrc/parse.rs:682:                origin: crate::ast::Origin::Program,\nsrc/parse.rs:710:            origin: crate::ast::Origin::Program,\nsrc/check/mod.rs:179:        file: Origin::Program,\nsrc/check/mod.rs:204:        file: Origin::Program,\nsrc/check/mod.rs:571:        file: Origin::Prelude,\nsrc/check/mod.rs:591:        file: Origin::Prelude,\n","metadata":{"output":"src/prelude.rs:31:        def.origin = Origin::Prelude;\nsrc/parse.rs:682:                origin: crate::ast::Origin::Program,\nsrc/parse.rs:710:            origin: crate::ast::Origin::Program,\nsrc/check/mod.rs:179:        file: Origin::Program,\nsrc/check/mod.rs:204:        file: Origin::Program,\nsrc/check/mod.rs:571:        file: Origin::Prelude,\nsrc/check/mod.rs:591:        file: Origin::Prelude,\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n \"Origin::|match.*Origin|origin:\" src/ --glob '!src/ast.rs' | head -30","time":{"start":1788705425738,"end":1788705425740}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"`value_name` only refuses capital first letters. `__module_0_handle` starts with `_` — fine. But wait, `__module_0_handle` — would that be confused with user names? User names can't contain `__`... actually, names are `[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*`. So `__module_0_handle` is a valid ident a user could write. Collision risk: a user defines a function literally named `__module_0_handle` — astronomically unlikely but possible. Better to use a prefix that can't be written by users? There's no such thing since all idents are the same charset. Hmm. The check for duplicates would catch collisions (if a user defines `__module_0_handle` in the program,and a module also produces that name, `check::check`'s `check_defs` would... actually, the module funcs aren't in `sigs`; a user call to `__module_0_handle` would... `sigs` wouldn't contain it (module funcs aren't in sigs), so a user calling it gets \"not a function\". Only the dispatch calls it (via `Kind::Call { func }` referencing the func name directly). So a user could never accidentally call it (it's not in sigs). The name collision concern is only between two modules — solved by the per-module counter. And between a module and the program: the program can't call module funcs (not in sigs), and the dispatch only calls the entry name from the cache. So collisions are harmless! Great — counter prefix `__module_{n}_` is safe.\n\nHmm wait, one more: `prune_unreachable` walks `Program.body` and reachable calls by func name. Module funcs reference each other (handle calls private helper),and the dispatch's `Kind::Call { func: \"__module_0_handle\" }` reaches the module's funcs. Good.\n\n\n\n4. Check the module: `check::check_module(&module, origin)`. But `check_module` returns `Vec<tir::Func>` for ALL defs. And its `sigs`/enums are module-local. Good. But wait, do I need to modify `check_module` to (a) take an origin param, (b) thread a `modules` cache (for recursion), (c) thread the base dir (for resolving nested `@(` paths)? \n\nRecursion: if a module's `handle` body contains `@(sub.toy)`, then during `check_module`'s `check_defs`, the dispatch arm in synth would call `resolve_module` needing the modules cache + base dir. So `check_module`'s Ctx must carry `modules`, `base_dir`, `next_local`. Currently `check_module` builds its own Ctx without those fields. I'll add them. `check_module(module, base, modules, next_local, origin)`. Hmm, that's a lot of params. Maybe a struct. Let me see how check_module is used: only build.rs (for prelude).. I can extend its signature with a context struct or just more params.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider recursion complexity vs. scope. Supporting nested dispatch in modules is natural (the machinery falls out), but adds: check_module must accept (base_dir, modules cache, next_local)and thread through. That's maybe 3 extra params + struct changes. Not huge. But there's a subtlety: the module's funcs (checked via check_module) may themselves dispatch, loading further modules, whose funcs also go into the same `Modules.funcs` cache. Since each module's dispatch resolves relative to ITS OWN dir, the cache is keyed by absolute path — works. And `check_module` must pass its own base dir into the Ctx for checking its defs' bodies. OK.\n\nLet me also reconsider: do I need origin param for check_module? check_module's `file: Origin::Prelude` hardcoded. For a submodule, file should be its own origin so its private helpers are callable from within it. If I use `Origin::Module` for ALL modules, then check_module(module) sets file: Prelude — mismatch! Private helpers (origin Module) wouldn't be callable (origin != file). So yes, check_module needs an origin param. Alternatively, make submodules' defs origins Prelude too?? That would make them visible... no, visibility: a Prelude-origin def called from file=Program is refused unless pub. For the module's own check, file=Prelude matches. Hmm, actually, using Origin::Prelude for submodules would work for the within-module check (file=Prelude, origin=Prelude→private allowed! And pub handle is pub so callable from program. But — but then, the module's private helpers would have origin Prelude, and... during the program's check (file=Program), if anything called them (they can't — not in sigs... so irrelevant.). So reusing Origin::Prelude for submodules would be a hack that conflates \"from the prelude file\" with \"from some module file\". Since privacy is enforced only within each check's own `file`, and each module check sets file to its origin, using Prelude works but is dishonest. Add `Origin::Module` — clearer. The enum doc says \"Only two exist today\" — I'm adding a third.\n\nActually wait — even simpler: could submodule defs keep `Origin::Program`? Then check_module(module) with file: Program... but file: Program for the program's own check too... no, each module check is a separate call with its own ctx; file: Program inside it means \"this module's own file\". Since the ctx is rebuilt per check, `Origin::Program` for module defs + file: Program in the module's check would allow private calls within the module. But then, `Origin::Program` means \"the file being checked\" — the module check's file IS Program. Hmm, that's actually semantically consistent: Origin::Program = \"the current file\" (the program's own file, or the module file being checked alone). But then how do we distinguish a module def from a program def for visibility at the dispatch site? The dispatch site checks the entry is pub (we look up the module's defs directly, not through visibility map). And program defs can't be called from module dispatch anyway. So `Origin::Program` could be reused... but it's confusing. The `origin` field's doc says \"Which source file a definition came from. Only two exist today:the program's own file and the single prelude module.\" Adding a third value `Module` is honest and costs one enum variant + a few matches. Do any matches on Origin need updating? `Origin` is compared for equality (==); no exhaustive matches I think. Let me check.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_07726c933001bo8fEdQbM1Tc3H","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_0772682000012rN0T9tLs0T1h0"}}
