{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705476723,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_ac0bae8eed8a452ba3b1fce7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/prelude.toy"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/prelude.toy</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: pub enum Opt<T> { some(T), none }\n2: \n3: pub enum Result<T, E> { ok(T), err(E) }\n4: \n5: fn join_parts(v: Vec<Str>) -> Str =\n6:     v | length(v) == 0 -> \"\" or v[0]! + join_parts(tail(v)!)\n7: \n8: pub fn join_lines(v: Vec<Str>) -> Str =\n9:     v\n10:         | length(v) == 0 -> \"\" or\n11:               length(v) == 1 -> v[0]! or\n12:               v[0]! + \"\\n\" + join_lines(tail(v)!)\n13: \n14: pub fn join(v: Vec<Str>) -> Str = join_parts(v)\n\n(End of file - total 14 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"pub enum Opt<T> { some(T), none }\n\npub enum Result<T, E> { ok(T), err(E) }\n\nfn join_parts(v: Vec<Str>) -> Str =\n    v | length(v) == 0 -> \"\" or v[0]! + join_parts(tail(v)!)\n\npub fn join_lines(v: Vec<Str>) -> Str =\n    v\n        | length(v) == 0 -> \"\" or\n              length(v) == 1 -> v[0]! or\n              v[0]! + \"\\n\" + join_lines(tail(v)!)\n\npub fn join(v: Vec<Str>) -> Str = join_parts(v)","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-167/prelude.toy","text":"pub enum Opt<T> { some(T), none }\n\npub enum Result<T, E> { ok(T), err(E) }\n\nfn join_parts(v: Vec<Str>) -> Str =\n    v | length(v) == 0 -> \"\" or v[0]! + join_parts(tail(v)!)\n\npub fn join_lines(v: Vec<Str>) -> Str =\n    v\n        | length(v) == 0 -> \"\" or\n              length(v) == 1 -> v[0]! or\n              v[0]! + \"\\n\" + join_lines(tail(v)!)\n\npub fn join(v: Vec<Str>) -> Str = join_parts(v)","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":14,"totalLines":14,"truncated":false}},"title":"prelude.toy","time":{"start":1788705476718,"end":1788705476720}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"`ty::variants`/`variants_of` looks up `enums[name]` — panics if missing. So if a module func's body carries `Type::Enum { name: \"Request\" }` and `Request` isn't in program.enums, the backend would panic. So YES, module enums must be merged into program.enums (whenever a module func's types reference them. Simplest: merge ALL module enums (pub and non-pub alike, since privacy for types isn't a thing yet). The check::check's enum-map for the program... wait, hmm. But hold on: are module enums used by the PROGRAM's own checking? No. But they must be in program.enums for backends. Merge all.\n\nBut also: could a module enum collide with a program enum name? Then program.enums would have two entries with the same name... check::check builds program.enums from file.enums (program's enums); I'd add module enums after, with duplicate-check (error naming both. If a module declares `Route` and the program also does — collision error. Reasonable (matches the prelude's inject, which... actually prelude::inject merges prelude enums into file.enums BEFORE check, so collisions are caught by `enum_map` duplicate check at check() start. For modules, loaded mid-check, I'll check duplicates when inserting into modules.enums collection — hmm, but the program's enums is already built by then. Let me instead insert module enums into a collection and at the end of check(), merge into program.enums with duplicate errors.\n\nHmm, wait. There's another wrinkle: module enums that are ALSO prelude enums ( e.g., a module referencing `Opt`! The module's `handle` might return `Opt<...>` — `Opt` is a prelude enum, resolved against... wait, check_module's enum_map has NO prelude! check_module resolves enums against module.enums only! So a module that uses `Opt` (via `tail(...)`, `[]`, `!`)... would check_module resolve it? `opt_of` in the checker requires `ctx.enums.get(\"Opt\")` — check_module's ctx.enums has only module enums! So `Opt`-producing forms in a module body would PANIC (`expect(\"the prelude declares Opt\")`)! \n\nHmm! So check_module doesn't support the prelude's Opt at all. A module body can't use `tail`/`[]`/`!` that produce Opt... Because check_module's enums lacks Opt. That's a limitation of check_module TODAY (prelude itself doesn't use Opt-producing forms?). So a module whose handle uses `!` or `[]` would break under my design. \n\nHmm. To fix: check_module should ALSO see the prelude's enums (Opt). How does the program's check get Opt? `prelude::inject` merges prelude enums into file.enums before check(). For check_module, there's no inject. build.rs passes only prelude.toy's own module (enums=Opt's declaration). So prelude's Opt IS in check_module's enums ( since it's declared in prelude.toy. For a submodule, its check needs Opt too — from the prelude.toy file. So when checking a submodule, I should inject prelude enums into its module enums (prepend, like inject does for programs. That means loading prelude.toy as a module and merging its enums (pub enums: Opt, Result...). Actually, prelude::module() returns enums filtered to pub. I can call `crate::prelude::module()` which parses prelude.toy each time... that's a parse per module load — fine (cheap. Then inject its enums into the submodule's enums before check_module. Hmm, wait, but does check_module need prelude DEFS too? A module body calling a prelude function (e.g., `str(...)`, `length(...)`? Predule defs are ordinary functions merged into programs. Can a submodule's handle call a prelude function? The prelude functions are \"always available to every program\". A module file is a file of declarations; when run as a module via dispatch, should it see the prelude? I'd say yes — consistent with programs. But check_module's sigs comes from module.defs only. So prelude defs aren't in sigs → a call to `join_lines` in a module body fails \"not a function\". Hmm!\n\nSo to fully support modules using the prelude, check_module would need prelude defs merged (as sigs) and their checked funcs... but then the prelude funcs would also need emitting... they're already in the program's funcs (via prelude::checked() in check()). But their names are unprefixed... and the module's call to them is `Kind::Call { func: \"join_lines\" }` — matching the program's funcs entry. Fine! But check_module's sigs needs prelude defs' sigs. That means check_module needs the prelude's defs merged into its module.defs (for signatures)... and check_defs would re-check them?! That duplicates and their bodies would be checked in the module context (file=Module)... visibility: prelude defs origin Prelude, file=Module → private prelude helpers refused from... wait, a prelude PUB function called from the module body: pub, so allowed. But prelude pub functions' bodies call private prelude helpers → origin Prelude != file Module → REFUSED! So prelude pub defs can't be rechecked in a Module-file context. \n\nUgh. This is the same issue the prelude's build-time check solves: prelude defs are pre-checked with file=Prelude. So for submodules, if I merge prelude defs into module.defs for sigs ONLY (not recheck bodies...), the sigs lookup needs the prelude sigs. But check_module's check_defs iterates ALL defs and rechecks each. Merging would recheck. \n\nAlternative: in check_module, build `sigs` from (module defs + prelude sigs),but check_defs ONLY on module defs. I.e., prelude defs are injected for signature resolution only, not re-checked, and the resulting funcs from prelude are... not produced by check_module (the program already has them via prelude::checked(). Fine. \n\nBut wait — check_module is also used by build.rs for prelude.toy ITSELF. In that case, merging prelude defs into itself would duplicate (and prelude.toy obviously shouldn't see itself... hmm, it would: prelude defs origin Prelude... sigs would have self-references...). build.rs passes the prelude module; the prelude shouldn't get prelude defs merged (it IS them. So the prelude-merge should apply only for NON-prelude modules. \n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me step back and think about SCOPE. \n\nThe task: implement `@(path)` module-as-function routing-arm syntax. The definition of done: implementation complete, just check green, fmt/clippy clean. \n\nThe minimal honest implementation:\n- `@(path)` parses.\n- The checker loads the module, finds `pub fn handle`, checks its signature against the subject and expected return, and emits a call.\n\nFor the module's OWN checking, I need check_module to handle the module's own defs. What do module bodies legitimately need?\n1. Their own defs (sig resolution, private helpers...).\n2. Builtins (str, length, map, ... — check_module's synth handles these; no enums needed).\n3. The prelude's enums ( `Opt` — needed by `[]`, `!`, `tail` in module bodies).\n4. The prelude's functions (e.g., `join_lines`...).\n\nFor scope, I could support 1-3 and defer 4. But 4 failing would be an arbitrary gap (\"a module cannot call prelude functions\")... Hmm, but is that even coherent? The prelude is \"always available to every program\". Is a submodule a \"program\"? It's a module (declarations only. When dispatched, its handle runs as a function within the program. A user would expect `str(...)` etc. — wait, those are builtins (Kind::Builtin, always available. `join_lines` is the only prelude function?? Let me check prelude.toy.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_07727906c001k4NoXtbbFwSCa2","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_077274312001ESe4Bs9hakd2xG"}}
