{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705510246,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_e6a65b10ffd644a895cbc853","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"module-as-function-routing|routing-design|@path|option C\" research-log/ plans/opencode-rollout.md | head -30"},"output":"plans/opencode-rollout.md:578:2026-09-02 (option C, \"drop the poisoned issue-159 lane, re-board under a fresh lane id rather\nplans/opencode-rollout.md:1011:time. Maintainer picked option C (drop and re-scope smaller), with a freeText addendum:\n","metadata":{"output":"plans/opencode-rollout.md:578:2026-09-02 (option C, \"drop the poisoned issue-159 lane, re-board under a fresh lane id rather\nplans/opencode-rollout.md:1011:time. Maintainer picked option C (drop and re-scope smaller), with a freeText addendum:\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n \"module-as-function-routing|routing-design|@path|option C\" research-log/ plans/opencode-rollout.md | head -30","time":{"start":1788705510239,"end":1788705510243}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The prelude has `Opt`, `Result`, `join_parts` (private), `join_lines` (pub), `join` (pub).\n\nIf a submodule's handle wanted to call `join` or `join_lines`... it would fail under check_module (sigs lacks them. And `Opt` would fail (enums lacks it unless injected.\n\nHmm. Supporting full prelude access in modules would require:\n- check_module's enums: prepend prelude pub enums ( `Opt`, `Result`).\n- check_module's sigs: prepend prelude defs' sigs (without rechecking bodies... but check_defs checks all defs...). Hmm, unless I add a mechanism to check only own defs.\n\n Actually, wait. There's an existing mechanism: `check::check` (the program check) does exactly: prelude::inject merges prelude defs+enums, sigs built from ALL (prelude + program), check_defs skips precompiled_names (prelude defs already checked at build time). So the pattern exists: merge prelude in, build sigs from all, and check only the non-prelude defs!\n\nSo for check_module (checking a submodule), I could do the same: build sigs from (prelude defs + module defs, enums from (prelude enums + module enums, and check_defs only on module defs (skipping precompiled_names). And the module's own defs that call prelude functions resolve. \n\nBut wait — the prelude defs' sigs: `signatures(&file.defs, ...)` resolves types against the env. If I merge prelude defs into module.defs, their sigs resolve (enums include Opt...). Good. And precompiled_names skip prevents rechecking. So check_module can mirror check()'s approach.\n\n.\n\nAnd build.rs's use of check_module (prelude itself): there I'd NOT merge prelude (it IS the prelude... but merging prelude defs into itself would make sigs include all prelude defs — that's actually FINE (self-references resolve, e.g., `join` calls `join_lines`, `join_lines` calls itself recursively — those currently resolve via sigs already). Actually, hold on: in build.rs, check_module checks ALL defs (including the pub ones; precompiled_names would be... empty (build.rs passes a stand-in prelude::checked() returning empty!). So for build.rs, no merging (it's already the prelude's own defs. Merging prelude defs into itself = no-op anyway (same defs. And precompiled_names empty → checks all. So build.rs unchanged behavior. \n\nHmm wait, but for build.rs, if I now make check_module merge prelude defs, it would duplicate the defs (module.defs already ARE prelude defs; prepending prelude defs again → duplicate name errors! \"defined twice\"!). So I must NOT merge prelude defs when the module IS the prelude. So check_module needs to know whether to inject the prelude. \n\nSimplest: only inject the prelude for the submodule path, i.e., do the merging in the module-loading code (resolve_module), not inside check_module itself. That keeps check_module unchanged (still \"checks a module's own defs in isolation\")... but then check_module needs prelude enums+sigs for the module's bodies...\n\nHmm. OK let me reconsider. Actually, let me look at what `check_module` would need for the submodule use case, holistically:\n\nFor a submodule, I want its defs checked with:\n- sigs = prelude defs' sigs ∪ module defs' sigs\n- enums = prelude pub enums ∪ module enums\n- visibility/file: module's origin\n- check: module defs only (not prelude).\n\nThe cleanest way to get that: modify check_module to do the prelude-merge internally, with a flag. OR: do it in the caller (resolve_module): build a `Module` struct = prelude_defs + module_defs, prelude_enums + module_enums, pass to check_module... but check_module checks ALL defs (including prelude defs → duplicate check → the prelude defs' bodies rechecked in wrong file context → visibility refusals as discussed). \n\nSo I need check_module to skip precompiled names, like check() does. Modify check_module:\n\n```rust\npub fn check_module(module: &Module) -> Result<Vec<tir::Func>, Error> {\n    // Existing: enums from module.enums...\n}\n```\n\nAdd: merge prelude defs/enums into the module (for sigs/env resolution), skip them in check_defs. But build.rs's prelude case: it would merge prelude defs into prelude module → duplicates. So I need the caller to say \"don't inject prelude\". \n\nHmm, what if instead: prelude injection happens in `resolve_module` (the loader), by constructing the merged Module AST:\n- `let prelude = crate::prelude::module();` (gives prelude defs + enums, origins=Prelude.\n\n Actually, `prelude::module()` returns `Module { defs, enums }` with def origins set to Prelude. I'd build:\n```rust\nlet mut merged = Module { defs: prelude.defs (cloned), enums: prelude.enums };\nmerged.defs.extend(module.defs);\nmerged.enums.extend(module.enums);\n```\nAnd pass merged to check_module. But check_module would check ALL defs (prelude included(→ wrong. So I modify check_module to skip precompiled_names (the same filter check() uses:\n\n```rust\nlet mut funcs = crate::prelude::checked();\nlet precompiled_names: HashSet<String> = funcs.iter().map(|f| f.name.clone()).collect();\nlet own_defs = module.defs.iter().filter(|d| !precompiled_names.contains(&d.name));\nfuncs.extend(check_defs(own_defs, &ctx)?);\n```\n\nThat mirrors check(). And build.rs's prelude case: merged defs = prelude.defs + prelude.defs (duplicate!! — extend duplicates the defs. So for build.rs, merging is wrong. \n\nSo the merge must be conditional. Where's the condition? build.rs calls check_module directly on the prelude. resolve_module calls it on a merged module. If the merging is done by resolve_module (not check_module), then check_module stays \"checks a module's own defs\" but needs the precompiled-skip so the merged prelude defs aren't rechecked. Wait, but if resolve_module builds merged (prelude+module, then check_module's own defs ARE merged (including prelude. If check_module skips precompiled_names, good. For build.rs (prelude alone, no merge): precompiled_names (from prelude::checked() — in build.rs, the stand-in returns empty!, so no skips; all prelude defs checked. Good — build.rs behavior unchanged. \n\nBut wait — build.rs includes a `mod prelude { pub fn checked() -> Vec<...> { Vec::new() } }` stand-in (returns empty). So in build.rs's check_module call, precompiled_names empty, all defs checked. In the library's check_module (submodule case), prelude::checked() returns real funcs, so prelude defs are skipped. \n\nAnd in resolve_module, I merge prelude defs+enums into the submodule before calling check_module. The prelude defs' sigs resolve module bodies' calls to them. Their funcs are already in the program via prelude::checked() (names unprefixed — matches the module bodies' Kind::Call names! Since module bodies' calls to prelude functions emit `Kind::Call { func: \"join_lines\" }` which matches the program's funcs entry \"join_lines\". The rename pass (prefixing module-internal calls) must NOT prefix calls to prelude functions! \n\nUgh — the rename pass: after check_module returns funcs, I prefix every `Kind::Call { func }` in module bodies. But calls to prelude functions ( `join_lines` etc.) must stay unprefixed. How to distinguish? The module's own def names (the set to prefix: module defs' names). Calls to those names → prefix; calls to anything else (prelude functions, or... can a module body call anything else? Only own defs + prelude defs (sigs has both. So: prefix `Kind::Call` funcs only when the func name is one of the MODULE's OWN def names. So rename pass needs the set of the module's original def names.\n\nBut hmm — there's a subtle collision: a module def named `join_lines` shadowing the prelude's? signatures() duplicate check would flag (module.defs has both prelude's and the module's `join_lines` → \"defined twice\". Hmm — actually, is that desirable? In programs, `prelude::inject` prepends prelude defs and a program redefining `join_lines` is flagged \"defined twice\" (per prelude.rs doc: \"Prepend... so a program that redefines the same name is the one flagged as the duplicate\"). So modules match programs. Good.\n\nOK this is workable. Let me also handle the enums collision: merge prelude enums+module enums → check_module's enum_map catches duplicates. And the module enums need to go into program.enums for backends. But careful: prelude enums (Opt, Result) are already in program.enums. So when collecting module enums for program.enums, I should only add the module's OWN enums (not prelude ones — already there. And I must avoid double-adding if a module... it's fine: collect module.enums only (the module file's own), and merge into program.enums at the end, checking duplicates (prelude enums already in program.enums; a module declaring its own `Opt` would collide with the prelude's → error. Good (matches programs).\n\n\n\nNow, let me also double-check: check_module's `sigs` — I'm merging prelude defs into module.defs before signatures(). But prelude defs' TYPE references (Opt<T> in `join`'s signature... wait, `join`/`join_lines` take `Vec<Str>` — no enums. `Opt`/`Result` enums are declared in prelude.toy — merged into module.enums. So prelude sigs resolve. Good.\n\n.\n\n And — one more: `signatures()` refuses `BUILTIN_NAMES` and dups. Fine.\n\n.\n\n\n\nHmm, wait — one more consideration: the prelude enums I merge into the module check must include `Opt` for `opt_of` etc. `prelude::module()` filters enums to `is_pub`. Opt and Result are pub. Good.\n\n. And `ctx.enums.get(\"Opt\")` in opt_of works. And `variant_owners`... build from merged enums. Good.\n\n\n\nNow — do I merge prelude DEFS too (for sigs, yes. For `join_lines` call from module body. Yes.\n\n. OK.\n\n\n\nLet me now also handle: `check_module` currently returns `funcs` in def order. For the submodule, I then prefix names and rename internal calls. And find the entry: the Func whose... hmm, I renamed funcs. I need the entry's SIG and renamed name. The entry def is `handle` (name as written`. After checking, find func with original name \"handle\" (before renaming). Then rename all funcs. Entry name = prefixed handle. Sig from func.param_ty + func.body.ty.\n\n\n\nWait, actually, I realize there may be a subtlety with `handle` being `pub`: the visibility. `check_module` checks defs with file=module origin. A non-pub handle... hmm, actually check_module doesn't enforce that a module's PUB functions can only be called... no visibility rule is about call sites. `check_module`'s own defs call each other. A `pub fn handle` in the module's file: pub marking. At the dispatch site (program file), I must verify handle is pub — since a non-pub def is \"callable only from within its own file\". So in resolve_module, find the def named `handle`, require `is_pub`, error otherwise (\"`handle` is not `pub`, so it cannot be dispatched into from another file\"...). Good.\n\nAlso, what if a module has NO handle? Error: \"`@(path)` needs a `pub fn handle` entry point in the module, but none is declared\" or similar.\n\n\n\nNow — the subject requirement & signature check. Let me reconsider the payload-arm question once more. I earlier worried about the doc's unit-variant example. Let me settle: `@(path)` reads `.` (the arm body's subject, which for a payload arm is the payload, for guard/default arms is the subject. For a unit variant arm, `.` is unbound → the dispatch errors \"`.` is not bound here\"... \n\nHmm, actually wait. Let me reconsider. Is that really acceptable? The doc's canonical example:\n\n```\nfn router(r: Route) -> Response =\n    r | FooBar -> @(route-groups/foo-bar.toy)\n```\n\nFor this to typecheck, `.` in the FooBar arm body must be... the subject `r` (Route). But current semantics: unit variant arm → `.` unbound. So this example FAILS. \n\nMaybe the design intends `@(path)` to apply to the *arm chain's subject* regardless of the arm's rebinding? That would require the dispatch to access the chain's subject (not the arm's `.`. The match_chain knows the subject; an arm body is checked with `arm_ctx`. The dispatch only sees `.`. To dispatch on the chain's subject, we'd need to pass it down... that's a bigger change (arm bodies would need access to the original subject, or the dispatch node would need the chain's subject threaded through).\n\nHmm. Alternatively, maybe the intended semantics for module dispatch is that it does NOT go through the arm's `.` rebinding at all — `@(path)` is applied to the **match subject** (the value the pipe delivered to the match chain). For a router, that makes total sense: the Route value (or the Request...) flows to the submodule. For guard/default arms, `.` = subject too — consistent. For payload arms, `.` = payload — INCONSISTENT (dispatch would receive the payload, not the whole subject...\n\nHmm, hmm. Which is it?\n\nLet me think about the most defensible reading. The research doc candidate C: \"`@path` is a new arm-body kind reserved for module dispatch\". And \"The subject arrives through the pipe, so the submodule is applied to `.` in every case.\"\n\n\"applied to `.` in every case\" — `.` in an arm body. In a payload arm, `.` is the payload (per the match docs).. In a unit arm, `.` isn't bound. So \"in every case\" — hmm.\n\n, the doc's sketch uses unit variants, which under current semantics gives an unbound `.`. Contradiction.\n\n.\n\nI think the most likely intent: the maintainer's sketch is loose (`FooBar -> $(route-groups/foo-bar.toy)` — maybe intending that `.` there is... hmm.\n\n.\n\n\n\nLet me look at the actual erlang round notes if they exist. The board title mentions \"(module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question module-as-function-routing-design)\". Maybe there's a rounds log. Let me search for \"grilling round\" or \"module-as-function-routing-design\" in research-log or plans.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_077281348001l9TdL6ohL3U32o","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_077279083001LEfTuEhh810jh7"}}
