{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705523262,"sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_a2a06d05e4af4d7da89fc442","state":{"status":"error","input":{"command":"cat .gitignore && git check-ignore -v ESCALATION.md || true"},"error":"The user rejected permission to use this specific tool call.","time":{"start":1788705523254,"end":1788705523260}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"No round notes available. The issue body and research doc are all I have.\n\nLet me make a pragmatic decision:\n\n**Decision:** `@(path)` applies the module's entry `handle` to the arm's `.` — whatever the arm's body `.` is bound to (the payload for a payload variant arm; the subject for guard/default arms. This is the literal reading of \"applied to `.`\" given the documented match-arm `.` semantics. The doc's sketch with unit variants would not typecheck under this reading — an honest limitation recorded in the commit. Actually hold on... let me reconsider once more. \n\nHmm, actually, wait. Let me re-read the doc's candidate C body text: \"`@path` is a new arm-body kind reserved for module dispatch, distinct from ordinary function application. The checker treats it specially:the path must resolve to a module whose entry signature accepts the subject's type and returns what the arm expects\"\n\n\"the subject's type\" — the subject `.`. For a unit variant arm, there is no subject. So the sketch is just inconsistent with its own text... The sketch was from the maintainer's note (candidate 1's sketch).,the refined final form is candidate C. I'll go with the doc's prose (apply to `.`), which matches \"applied to `.` in every case\".\n\nAnd actually — hmm, one more thought: maybe for unit-variant arms, the maintainer intends the *whole chain subject* because there's nothing else to pass. But that would require new machinery (threading the outer subject into arm bodies...). and conflicts with payload arms. No — keep it simple: `.`. The doc's example needs payload variants to work. Fine.\n\nSo my test cases will use payload variants or guard/default arms where `.` is bound.\n\n. Actually, hmm, wait — what about a default arm `any() -> @(baz.toy)`? `.` stays the subject. So the router could be written as:\n\n```\nfn router(r: Route) -> Response =\n    r | FooBar -> @(route-groups/foo-bar.toy) ...\n```\n\nwhere Route's FooBar is a payload variant? Hmm, but the maintainer's example clearly shows unit variants. \n\nAlternatively, use guard arms:\n\n```\nr | FooBar -> @(foo-bar.toy) ...\n```\n\nWait — `FooBar -> ...` where FooBar is a variant pattern. If FooBar is a unit variant, `.` unbound. Hmm.\n\n\n\nOK, let me look at this from yet another angle: maybe the intended usage is that the enum's variants carry the request:\n\n```\nenum Route { foo(Request), bar(Request), ... }\n```\n\nWith lowercase constructors? No — variant names in patterns are capitalized matchers. So:\n\n```\nenum Route { FooBar(Request), Baz(Request) }\nfn router(r: Route) -> Response =\n    r | FooBar(req) -> @(route-groups/foo-bar.toy)\n```\n\nHmm, with a field pattern `FooBar(req)`: `.` = payload (Request). Then handle(Request) -> Response. And the arm's `req` binding... could be unused? No — unused pattern binding is an error (field_used check. But the arm body is `@(...)`, which reads `.` not `req`. So `FooBar(req) -> @(...)` would error \"`req` is bound here but never used\". So the pattern should be `FooBar( -> @(...)`? A payload arm with no field pattern: `FooBar -> @(...)`. `.` = the payload. YES — that's the \"bare payload arm\" documented in match.md: \"`text -> .body`: a bare payload arm; `.` rebinds to the payload\". So:\n\n```\nfn router(r: Route) -> Response =\n    r | FooBar -> @(route-groups/foo-bar.toy)\n```\n\nwhere `FooBar` is a PAYLOAD variant (declared `FooBar(Request`)), `.` in the arm body = the payload Request. `@(route-groups/foo-bar.toy)` applies handle to Request. That typechecks! And it reads identically to the maintainer's sketch (which just drew the enum with unit variants loosely**. So the maintainer's sketch works if Route's variants are payload variants. \n\n\n\nSo my design decision holds: `@(path)` reads `.`; for the router example to work, the Route variants carry payloads. I'll write test cases accordingly, and note the constraint. If the maintainer intended unit-variant dispatch on the whole subject, that's a follow-up. But I should flag this ambiguity in ESCALATION.md or the commit. Hmm. Actually, per the brief: \"If something needs deciding that this brief does not settle, write ESCALATION.md ... take the most conservative continuation, and keep going\". This is a genuine design ambiguity. But I've made a defensible decision (the literal reading of \"applied to `.`\"),and it doesn't block work. I'll note it in the commit message as a decision rather than ESCALATION (since ESCALATION is for things that block continuation or need human weigh-in... The brief says escalate if something \"needs deciding that this brief does not settle\". Hmm. The doc's prose settles it (\"applied to `.`\"). I'll proceed with `.` and note in commit. Actually, let me write a brief ESCALATION.md noting the interpretation choice so the maintainer sees it — but continue working. The brief says: write ESCALATION.md, COMMIT it, take the most conservative continuation, keep going. That's compatible. Hmm, but also `.gitignore` now ignores ESCALATION.md (per the git log \"gitignore ESCALATION.md: stop stale escalation writes from riding onto main\"). Let me check .gitignore.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_077284628001rIzeFRuzUKTObl","sessionID":"ses_f88de0697ffeJyEgaxJWCC7F93","messageID":"msg_0772813a1001OdeR1k3yP624Si"}}
